digraph c64 {
  rankdir=LR;
  splines=ortho;
  node [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fontname="Helvetica" fontsize=9 penwidth=0.6];
  edge [color="#333644" arrowsize=0.5 penwidth=0.5];
  "c64" [label="Commodore 64 (breadbin, ASSY 250407)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A home computer sold from 1982. This tree traces every physical component back through the industrial processes of the early 1980s to the ore, crude oil and quartz sand it began as. Roughly 320 discrete parts from about twenty distinct manufacturing industries. The chips were designed in Pennsylvani"];
  "c64.bottoms-out" [label="Where every branch terminates" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Everything above ends in one of three trunks: refined metals, petrochemicals, and the plants and utilities that supply both. Most component subtrees carry their own shorter copy of the relevant chain - a capacitor really does carry its own aluminium, and an ABS case really does carry its own acrylon"];
  "unlinked.catalogue" [label="Published but unlinked materials" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="These 10 nodes were written as canonical, reusable subtrees — a fragment's \"made from\" chains in full detail — but no other branch ended up referencing them, so nothing linked them into the tree. They are collected here rather than deleted. Where a component subtree inlines its own shorter version o"];
  "si.polysilicon.gangue" [label="Feldspar and mica gangue, hand-picked away" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The pegmatite that hosts the quartz veins contains albite and K-feldspar with muscovite and biotite. Alumina in feldspar is the enemy - it survives all the way to the aluminium metallisation - so the lump is sorted by hand and by hydrofluoric-acid washing before it is even shipped."];
  "si.polysilicon.coke.crude" [label="Sour crude residue, vacuum distilled" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Heavy Venezuelan or Middle East crude sent to a vacuum tower at 350-400 C and 30-50 mmHg; the bottoms are a dense black pitch of asphaltenes and metal porphyrins, and are the feed for the coke. Vanadium and nickel concentrate in this residue, so coke made from it carries trace vanadium that the sili"];
  "si.polysilicon.coke.calciner" [label="Delayed coking and calcination of petroleum coke" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Vacuum residue from crude oil distillation is heated in a battery of delayed-coking heaters to 500 C and held in a large coking drum for 12-24 hours; the green coke is then calcined in a rotary kiln at 1200-1350 C to drive off volatiles and leave needle coke at 0.5-2% sulphur. Silicon furnaces blend"];
  "si.polysilicon.coke.anthracite" [label="Anthracite coal as a coke substitute" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other traditional reductant for the submerged arc furnace, mined from the hard coal fields of Pennsylvania, Wales and Shanxi. Lower carbon, higher ash and higher iron than petroleum coke, so it was used as a blend component."];
  "si.polysilicon.brine" [label="Chlor-alkali brine electrolysis" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Salt dissolved in water and electrolyzed in a membrane or mercury cell at 3-4 V to make chlorine and hydrogen. The hydrogen is what reduces the trichlorosilane, and the chlorine is one half of the hydrochloric acid that chlorinates the metallurgical silicon in the first place."];
  "metal.salt-brine" [label="Rock salt and solar salt brine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two very different-looking things that are the same feedstock. Rock salt is a bed of halite pushed up by evaporation of a restricted sea and then mined; solar salt is the same chemistry done in the sun, in shallow evaporation ponds that turn a square mile of bay into a crystalliser. The chlor-alkali"];
  "metal.salt-solar-pan" [label="Evaporate brine in a solar salt pond" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A series of shallow ponds, from 2 m down to 30 cm, through which seawater is stepped down in salinity, precipitating gypsum first and then halite. A hectare of pond evaporates tens of thousands of tonnes of salt a year. The leaf is a solar pond on a coastal lagoon."];
  "metal.salt-rock" [label="Halite bed: Wieliczka, Michigan, the saltbelt" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Salt deposited in an evaporating sea and buried later; mined by room-and-pillar, with the salt crushed and screened for the brine saturator. The Wieliczka mine in Poland is the oldest deep salt mine still working, and Polish rock salt was the classic feed for the eastern European chlor-alkali indust"];
  "metal.salt-evaporite" [label="Halite evaporite: the rock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the sodium branch: a bedded evaporite sequence of halite with interbedded gypsum and clay, laid down when a restricted sea evaporated. Nothing chemical about it - just a dried-up ocean."];
  "si.cz.seed" [label="Seed crystal for CZ pulling" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A short (100)-oriented sliver cut from a previous ingot, used to set the orientation and the defect-free growth of the new boule. Its diameter has to match the finished boule, so a seed is cut and ground for each crystal-pulling station."];
  "si.cz.crucible-sand" [label="Quartz sand melted into crucible glass" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="High-purity quartz sand, acid-leached to remove aluminium and iron, is melted in a flame or arc furnace around a rotating graphite mandrel at about 2000 C into a clear, annealed vitreous silica blank. The graphite is then burnt off in air at 800-1000 C."];
  "si.cz.atmosphere" [label="Helium over the CZ melt" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="High-purity helium, bled over the melt to carry off the intense blackbody heat and to sweep volatile boron compounds away. Argon, or a helium-argon mix, was the cheaper alternative and produced more oxygen precipitation in the finished boule."];
  "chem.silane" [label="Silane and organosilane chemistry" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Monosilane, SiH4, is a gas made by redistributing trichlorosilane, and it is the precursor for the polysilicon films deposited inside the fab that made the 6510 and the 4164s. Organosilanes are the opposite: a methyl- or ethylchlorosilane that has been reacted with methanol or ethanol instead, so th"];
  "chem.silane.coupling-agent" [label="Organosilane coupling agents" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Bifunctional organosilanes: a hydrolysable alkoxy or chloro group that bonds to the silanol groups on a glass surface, and an organic group - vinyl, amino, epoxy or methacrylate - that reacts with the resin. Aminopropyltriethoxysilane and vinyltrimethoxysilane are the workhorses; methacryloxypropylt"];
  "chem.silane.monosilane" [label="Monosilane (SiH4)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The polysilicon precursor: a colourless, pyrophoric gas that decomposes on a hot surface to lay down a conformal silicon film. A fab in 1982-84 that used it ran a small silane plant, because the gas is both too dangerous and too cheap to ship far. It goes into the machine's own logic: the polysilico"];
  "chem.silane.monosilane.lpcvd" [label="Deposit polysilicon by low-pressure CVD" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Silane decomposes on hot silicon in a diffusion tube at 600-850 C, laying down a conformal film of polycrystalline silicon. This is the step that changed how the C64's own chips were made. A self-aligned gate needs a gate material that survives ion implantation into the source and drain on either si"];
  "chem.silane.monosilane.tcs-route" [label="Disproportionate the trichlorosilane" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The industrial route to monosilane: trichlorosilane redistributes into silane and tetrachlorosilane, catalysed, with the whole reaction run in a hydrogen atmosphere: 4 HSiCl3 -\> SiH4 + 3 SiCl4. It suits a fab because the trichlorosilane it consumes is the same material the polysilicon plant already "];
  "chem.glass-fiber" [label="E-glass fibre" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Continuous glass filaments about 5 microns across, drawn in thousands from a tank furnace and woven into cloth. The C64 needs it twice on the board side of the machine: as the reinforcing cloth in the FR-4 mainboard laminate, and as the laminate core of the keyboard matrix board. E-glass is a lime-a"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.cloth-sizing" [label="Size the woven cloth with silane" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="For laminate grade the woven cloth is desized and then treated with an organosilane coupling agent - sprayed or dipped, dried and cured at 100-150 C - before it is impregnated with epoxy. The molecule has one end that hydrolyses and bonds to the silanol groups on the glass surface and an organic end"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.packaging" [label="Spin, rove and weave the cloth" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The attenuated strand is wound onto a rotating package at high traverse speed, then a few hundred filaments are gathered into a roving and woven on an air-jet loom into plain-weave cloth. The loom is the reason a laminate is isotropic: a plain weave with a square fill puts equal glass in both direct"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.attenuation" [label="Attenuate and size the strand" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A blast of air at constant pressure across the wicket pulls the emerging cone out to about 5 microns before it has travelled half a metre, and the filament is wound onto a rotating package at 10-25 m/s. As it passes the sizing apron it picks up a thin film - starch or oil for the textile trade, and "];
  "chem.glass-fiber.bushing" [label="Forehearth and bushing plate" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The glass leaves the furnace through a refractory forehearth and arrives at a bushing plate: a small alloy block, a couple of hundred millimetres across, drilled with 200 to several thousand holes of 10-40 microns. Gold-platinum-rhodium is the usual alloy because nothing else survives 1400 C in cont"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.melt" [label="Melt, refine and condition the batch" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The batch is charged into a regenerative tank furnace held at 1400-1500 C - hot enough to keep the viscosity low enough to draw a filament through a 10 micron hole, and then held in that narrow window for tens of hours. What comes out is not merely molten: it is refined until every bubble larger tha"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.borax" [label="Borax (sodium borate)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The boron source, and the reason E-glass is a borosilicate at all. Borax decahydrate, Na2B4O7.10H2O, goes into the batch either as the decahydrate or as boric acid made from it; either way the boron ends up in the glass network as B2O3. A few per cent of B2O3 lowers the liquidus and widens the worki"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.borax.ore" [label="Colemanite and borate ore" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The borates are mined from evaporite deposits and altered limestone-tuff beds, in Turkey (Bigadiç, Emet) and California's Boron and Kramer districts. Ore is 25-35% B2O3 and is shipped as run-of-mine lump, so this is as close as the boron in a C64 mainboard gets to the ground."];
  "chem.glass-fiber.borax.acid" [label="Boric acid from colemanite" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Colemanite ore is crushed and boiled with sulfuric acid, which drives the calcium out as gypsum and leaves boric acid in solution: Ca2B6O11.5H2O + 2 H2SO4 + 2 H2O -\> 2 CaSO4 + 6 H3BO3. The liquor is filtered hot and crystallised by cooling, and the crystals are dried at low temperature because boric"];
  "chem.glass-fiber.batch" [label="Mix the batch" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The raw materials are weighed and dry-blended to about +/-1% on each constituent before a single tonne is melted. The tolerance matters more than it looks: the melt fixes the viscosity curve, and the viscosity curve decides whether 5 micron filament can be drawn from the bushing at all. A modern bat"];
  "facility.power.kaolin" [label="Kaolin (china clay)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A white kaolinite clay from a kaolin pit, washed and beneficiated for the ceramic and paper trades. Ends at the clay pit."];
  "metal.soda-ash" [label="Soda ash (Na2CO3)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sodium carbonate, the second half of the sodium story. It goes into glass (a huge 1980s user, though the glass agent owns that branch), into detergents, and into the Bayer plant as the carbonate that lime converts back into caustic. The Solvay process, still the dominant route in 1982, makes it from"];
  "metal.soda-ash.salt-recovery" [label="Causticise the spent brine to recover the salt" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Solvay process does not consume the salt: the ammonium chloride in the spent brine is causticised with lime back to ammonia and sodium chloride, and the brine is re-saturated. The plant therefore closes its salt loop, and its only real consumption is limestone and energy and the ammonia. That cl"];
  "metal.soda-ash.lime-kiln" [label="The lime kilns that feed the Solvay tower" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Solvay process needs carbon dioxide and it gets it by burning limestone in a shaft kiln beside the tower, in the same way a sugar mill burns bagasse. The kiln gas, after the dust is knocked out and the gas is cooled and scrubbed, is about 40-45% carbon dioxide on a dry basis - lower than the 98%"];
  "metal.limestone" [label="Limestone (CaCO3) quarry" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="High-calcium limestone for the Solvay towers, the blast furnaces, the Bayer plant, the lead sinter and the lime kilns. Quarried by bench mining with a face shovel or a chain dragline, crushed, and graded; a blast furnace wants roughly a third of a tonne of limestone per tonne of hot metal. The same "];
  "metal.limestone.quarry" [label="Limestone quarry and crushing plant" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 5-15 m bench taken out of a 10-40 m face with a face shovel or a chain dragline, drilled and blasted where the rock is competent, then a primary jaw or gyratory crusher to 50-150 mm and a crushing and screening plant that produces three fractions: 0.5-5 cm for the sinter plant, 5-40 mm for the bla"];
  "metal.limestone.bed" [label="Carbonate platform limestone" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a fossiliferous carbonate rock, mostly calcite, laid down on a shallow warm sea floor and now quarried a few metres at a time out of a 10-40 m face."];
  "metal.soda-ash.ammonia-source" [label="Make ammonia from coke in the gasworks" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="In 1982 most ammonia in the soda industry still came from the gasworks: coke plus steam and iron passed over a hot carbonaceous catalyst, then cracked to nitrogen and hydrogen. Natural gas-based steam reforming had largely displaced this in North America and Western Europe, but coke-based ammonia re"];
  "metal.soda-ash.ammonia" [label="Carbonation tower and the rotary calciner" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 20-30 m steel tower with a conical bottom, fed at the top with ammoniated brine and in the side with CO2 from the lime kilns. Sodium bicarbonate precipitates as a fine white slurry in the upper third and settles into the cone; the temperature is held near 40 C because below that the ammonium chlor"];
  "metal.silica-sand" [label="Industrial silica sand (quartz dune sand)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Siliceous sand from a dune or a beach, washed, screened and often magnetically separated to take out the heavy mineral concentrates that would be a nuisance. This is a distinct node from the silicon-grade quartzite: the purity required here is far lower, and this sand is a commodity that the foundry"];
  "metal.silica-sand.dune" [label="Aeolian dune sand and beach placer" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: quartz grains rounded by wind or wave, in an unconsolidated surface deposit a few metres to tens of metres thick sitting on older sediment. Dunes along the Mississippi valley, the Great Lakes shorelines and the Atlantic and Pacific coasts of the United States were the standard sources, min"];
  "industry" [label="Foundries, factories, utilities and logistics" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="The plant and the plumbing behind the Commodore 64: who made the chips, who assembled the box, what burned to light the lights, where the water came from, where the waste went, and how many hands and hours it took. A C64 is an artefact of eleven industries, not one. The eleven branches below are the"];
  "facility.test-equipment" [label="Factory and engineering test equipment" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Every C64 that left the Hong Kong line had been through a functional test, and the boards had been tested electrically before population and after soldering. In-circuit testers and flying probes were the technology of the era; the automated ATE industry of the 1990s did not exist yet. The capital eq"];
  "facility.test-equipment.bare-board" [label="Bare-board test and flying probe" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Bare PCB test came first: a bed-of-nails grid fixture to prove every net was etched and plated correctly, catching a shorts-heavy board before it was populated and therefore before the parts were on it. The flying probe - a robot or CNC head with a moving needle that touched nets individually withou"];
  "facility.test-equipment.needle-probe" [label="Spring probe and tungsten needle" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The bed-of-nails is a field of sprung tungsten-rhenium or beryllium-copper pins on a 2.54 mm grid, each pressing a few grams onto a pad to make contact. The 1980s fixtures were drilled and wired by hand or on a numerical-control machine from the Gerber artwork - which is why a board layout change me"];
  "facility.test-equipment.burn-in" [label="Burn-in oven and sockets" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A forced-air oven at 70-100 C with burn-in sockets, holding hundreds of boards for 24-72 hours so that early-life component failures - bad solder joints, marginal ICs, leaky capacitors - showed up on the line instead of in the customer's house. Commodore's 1982-83 quality record suggests it was not "];
  "facility.test-equipment.test-rom" [label="Test ROM in the final-test fixture" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A dedicated test ROM - the '501' diagnostic ROM is the one most C64 owners meet - loaded into the machine's own ROM socket during factory final test so the machine could self-test its own RAM, character ROM, CIA timers and VIC registers. It is the same object a user later used to prove the machine w"];
  "facility.test-equipment.functional" [label="Functional tester" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A powered-up test station running a test program from ROM that exercised the VIC-II registers, checked the RAM, drove the keyboard matrix and read back the RAM. On a Commodore line this was a dedicated fixture with a monitor and a keyboard, and the operator's job was to load, run, read the result, b"];
  "facility.test-equipment.ict" [label="In-circuit tester (ICT)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A bed-of-nails fixture pressing 1000 or so spring-loaded probes onto the bare board's pads, measuring resistance, capacitance, diode voltage and, at an increasing share, functional signatures at each node - all in a few seconds. By 1983 the analogue resistance-only tester was giving way to the analo"];
  "logistics.people" [label="The labour content of a C64" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Two labour populations with almost nothing in common: a tiny, very senior, very expensive American engineering team in Pennsylvania and Japan, and a large, low-paid, mostly female production workforce in Hong Kong and Japan. The design labour is amortised over millions of units and is invisible in t"];
  "logistics.people.qa-defects" [label="The labour cost of the quality problems" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Commodore's labour did not just build machines, it found and reworked other people's mistakes. The 'sparkle' defect was traced after three weeks to a ROM - not the VIC-II - with a pre-charging circuit that was sensitive to a bus contention spike; three million such ROMs were already in service. A lo"];
  "logistics.people.production-engineering" [label="The production engineers who fixed it" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The unsung labour in Commodore's story: David Ziembicki (production engineer, then Commodore West Chester), Bruce Crocket and Charles Winterble, who had to debug the project and bring it into production, and Robert H. Lane, who became president of Commodore Business Machines Inc in November 1982 and"];
  "logistics.people.development-cost" [label="Nobody knows what the C64 cost to develop" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Winterble's answer to the question was direct: 'I had no formal budget accountability, other than Jack watching me. Jack said that budgets were a license to steal.' The reason the answer was 'nothing much' is that the fab was not running at full capacity and the design chips were absorbed into exist"];
  "logistics.people.c64-system-team" [label="The C64 system design team" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="After Tramiel decided in late November 1981 that the VIC-II and SID would go into a 64 KB home computer rather than a game console, the machine itself was designed in about five weeks. The architecture was laid out on paper in two days; five working prototypes were finished just before the new year."];
  "logistics.people.design-team" [label="The C64 chip design team" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A team of about seven people, working in parallel on two chips, in nine months. Al Charpentier designed the VIC-II with two draftsmen and one CAD operator. Bob Yannes designed the SID with two draftsmen and one CAD operator. Charles Winterble, then director of worldwide engineering, authorised and m"];
  "logistics.people.wave-solder-worker" [label="The worker at the wave-solder line" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The single most recognisable job in a 1980s electronics factory. She stood or sat at one end of a 2-3 m wave-solder machine, fed pre-inserted boards into the conveyor, watched for bridging and dry joints, pulled the rejects, and often did the visual inspection and the component-insertion handwork at"];
  "logistics.people.hk-wages" [label="Hong Kong electronics wages, 1983" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The Hong Kong Census and Statistics Department's Quarterly Report of Wages, Salaries and Employee Benefits Statistics carried explicit occupational breakdowns for the electronics industry in 1983 - assembler, soldering worker, tester/aligner, quality control operator, packer, machine operator - with"];
  "logistics.people.direct-labour" [label="Direct assembly labour per machine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Roughly 0.5-1.5 person-hours per machine across population, insertion (automated), wave-solder, assembly, test and packing, plus a share of the test and rework. At 1983 Hong Kong and Japanese electronics wage levels of roughly HK$9-14 or JPY 500-700 per hour, that is about US$1-3 per machine of dire"];
  "logistics.people.bom" [label="The bill of materials (1983 estimate)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Brian Dougherty of Berkeley Softworks gave the most-cited contemporaneous breakdown, used as a production cost of $35-50 by 1985: three ROMs at $1 each; eight 64K DRAMs at $1.85 each; SID and VIC-II at $4 each; RF modulator $3; 6510 at $1-2; TTL, buffers, regulators and capacitors together $5; keybo"];
  "logistics.shipping" [label="Moving 100,000 C64s" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="The C64 was assembled in East Asia and sold in North America and Europe. In 1983 that was containerised cargo on liner services, not break-bulk: a 40-foot box holds 250-350 C64s depending on how they were packed, and the whole industry's economics were shaped by the 1966-1980s transition from weight"];
  "logistics.shipping.customs-valuation" [label="Customs valuation and the paper trail" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="US customs value was the transaction value - the price actually paid or payable - which is why Commodore's transfer-pricing between Commodore Electronics Ltd (Bahamas), Commodore Japan Ltd and the Hong Kong branch became the subject of a US Tax Court case that ran from 1991 and shifted $116 million "];
  "logistics.shipping.commercial-documents" [label="Commercial invoice, packing list and certificate of origin" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The three documents that travelled with the container besides the bill of lading. The invoice stated the price actually paid or payable, which is the number customs used; the packing list was the count and weight the carrier checked against the manifest; the certificate of origin was what made the 8"];
  "logistics.shipping.import-duty" [label="US import duty on a computer in 1983" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="The C64 entered the United States under the Tariff Schedules of the United States (the TSUS - the HTS did not exist until 1989), classified as an accounting, computing and other data processing machine incorporating a calculating mechanism, item 676.15, dutiable at 5.5% ad valorem. Separately, item "];
  "logistics.shipping.demurrage" [label="Demurrage, detention and the inventory cost" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Two separate costs. Demurrage is the charge for leaving a container on the terminal beyond the free time; in 1983 free time was commonly four to five days and the daily charge escalated. Detention is the charge for keeping the container beyond the time allowed at the other end. On top of both sat th"];
  "logistics.shipping.air-freight" [label="Air freight for the high-value parts" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Customs values tell the story: a box of 300 C64s entered at, say, $150-200 declared value each would carry $45,000-60,000 of electronics inside a container whose ocean freight was a few hundred dollars. The ICs were the opposite case: a 6510 and a VIC-II at internal transfer value were a tiny fracti"];
  "logistics.shipping.bill-of-lading" [label="The bill of lading" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The negotiable document that made containerised trade possible: a carrier-issued contract of carriage naming the shipper, the consignee, the port of loading and discharge, the freight terms, and the description of the goods. Without it the goods did not move; with it, the bank paid on presentation. "];
  "logistics.shipping.routes" [label="Route times: transpacific and Europe-Asia" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Kaohsiung or Hong Kong to Long Beach is about 4,500-5,000 nautical miles via the Luzon Strait and the great circle; at 16-18 knots that is roughly 12-14 days port to port, plus 3-7 days in the terminal on each end. Hong Kong to Rotterdam via the Suez Canal is about 11,000 nautical miles, 22-26 days,"];
  "logistics.shipping.ports" [label="The ports" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Kaohsiung was Taiwan's largest container port and was ranked the world's fifth-largest container port in 1982; its first container terminal was built 1969-70 and Container Terminal No. 4 in the Zhongxing Commercial Harbor Area was built from 1983. Keelung, on the northern approach to Taipei, opened "];
  "logistics.shipping.c64-count" [label="The volume Commodore was moving" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="By March 1983 the C64 was shipping at about 25,000 units a month and had passed the Apple II in monthly unit sales; roughly half a million had sold in the first six months. At 300 units a 40-foot box, that is about 83 TEU a month - about four to five ships' worth of containers a month, which sounds "];
  "logistics.shipping.containerisation" [label="Containerisation (40-foot box)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="ISO 40-foot general purpose container: 12.192 m x 2.438 m x 2.591 m internal, holding up to about 32-36 tonnes. One TEU is a 20-foot box; a 40-foot box is two. Box rates had replaced weight rates on the main east-west lanes by the end of the 1970s, so a low-density, high-value cargo like a C64 paid "];
  "facility.foundries" [label="The Commodore supply-chain geography" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Where a C64 physically came from, and the only place in this tree where the map is Commodore-specific rather than industrial. Commodore owned one fab, in Pennsylvania. The boards were printed in Hong Kong. The boxes were assembled in Hong Kong, Japan, West Germany, the United States and - from 1984 "];
  "facility.foundries.subcontract-network" [label="The sub-contractor geography: Japan, Korea, Taiwan, the Philippines" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Commodore's own factories were few; its supply base was enormous. Japan supplied the disks and a great deal of the precision mechanics and passives. Taiwan and Korea supplied the display-grade passives, the transformers, the coils, the keyboards and the power supplies, through the export processing "];
  "facility.foundries.other-sites" [label="The rest of the manufacturing map, 1983" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="By 1983 Commodore described itself as an international company with manufacturing in Japan, Hong Kong, West Germany, the UK, Pennsylvania and California in the United States, and Scarborough in Metropolitan Toronto. C64 units carried the MADE IN HONG KONG, West Germany or England marks, and were ass"];
  "facility.foundries.corby" [label="Commodore Business Machines (UK), Corby" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The British end. Commodore UK's head office was in Slough and it built calculators at a factory on Teesside; in 1983 it announced a new GBP 20 million home computer factory at Corby, Northamptonshire, as the European manufacturing and distribution centre for the C64 and VIC-20, expecting 300 jobs by"];
  "facility.foundries.braunschweig" [label="Commodore Buromaschinen GmbH, Braunschweig, West Germany" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The European production base. Commodore launched production for the European market in Braunschweig in 1980 and it once employed up to 2,000 people. Commodore GmbH also designed products. The PET had been imported from Braunschweig, and Commodore UK in the 1970s bought calculators at a factory on Te"];
  "facility.foundries.japan-vendors" [label="Japanese assembly vendors for the VIC-20" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The VIC-20 - the machine whose case, board size and BASIC the C64 inherited - was first built in Japan by Shinwa Electric Company, then by Japanese Cash Register (JCR), both assembling and manufacturing to Commodore Japan Limited's specifications and under its direction. Japanese Cash Register also "];
  "facility.foundries.kentron" [label="Kentron Co., Ltd., Ina, Japan" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The small Japanese production-engineering company that Commodore Japan acquired in October 1982 and that actually fixed the C64. Kentron had about 150 employees at its height and expertise in production engineering, quality control, work standards, purchasing and materials control. Commodore Japan s"];
  "facility.foundries.hong-kong" [label="The Hong Kong operation (CE(HK)L and the CEL Hong Kong branch)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Where the C64 really got built. Commodore Electronics (Hong Kong) Ltd had been making watches and calculators since 1979, and manufactured VIC-20 units from April 1982; later the Hong Kong branch of Commodore Electronics Ltd took over. Mass production of the production-redesigned 'C-64A' began at th"];
  "facility.foundries.psu-maker" [label="Power supply / transformer sub-contractor" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The linear power supply inside the C64 - the big orange or black transformer, the rectifier and the linear regulators - came from outside suppliers, cost an estimated $5-10 including connectors, and one of Ziembicki's complaints was that a supplier that had passed engineering approval then quietly c"];
  "facility.foundries.hk-injection" [label="Hong Kong / Taiwan injection moulding sub-contractor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ABS case, the keyboard keycaps and the cassette-port door were moulded by a sub-contractor, not by Commodore. Hong Kong and Taiwan each had hundreds of small toolrooms and presses in 1983, and Commodore used both. The case cost an estimated $1-2 in 1983 prices."];
  "facility.foundries.hk-pcb" [label="Hong Kong PCB fabricator" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="All sixteen versions of the C64 motherboard were manufactured in Hong Kong. Hong Kong in the early 1980s was a genuine centre of bare-board fabrication - dozens of small plants doing gold-impregnated or tin-lead plated FR-4 double-sided boards on a 0.3-0.4 mm grid with the pads drilled and plated by"];
  "facility.foundries.santa-clara" [label="CBM Santa Clara, California" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Commodore's US assembly line, where production of the C64 began in late summer 1982. It had automated component-insertion equipment. It went badly: excessive video and audio noise, excessive heat, and semiconductors incompatible with the design, so yield was low, cost high and returns came back. Onl"];
  "facility.foundries.board-layout-dispute" [label="The automated-insertion board layout fight" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Commodore's US plant used automatic component insertion; the Japanese plant did not, and the two wanted different component spacing. Charpentier's group won: the C64 board was laid out for automated insertion, and production was moved to a Hong Kong plant that had the automated tools. This single de"];
  "facility.foundries.mos" [label="MOS Technology / Commodore Semiconductor Group fab, Norristown PA" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The fab that made the 6510, the 6567/6569 VIC-II, the 6581 SID, the 6526 CIA and the ROMs. MOS Technology was founded in 1969 by John Paivinen, Mort Jaffe and Don McLaughlin as a second source for Texas Instruments' calculator chips, was majority-owned by Allen-Bradley from 1970, and built its own p"];
  "facility.foundries.mos.csg-successor" [label="After Commodore: GMT Microelectronics" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The fab was bought out of bankruptcy in December 1994 for about $4.3 million plus $1 million of liabilities (including the EPA licence), reopened in 1995, ran a 1-micron process for third parties, and was shut down by the EPA in 2000-01. 183 employees and $21 million of revenue in 1999. It is a rare"];
  "facility.foundries.mos.captive-prototyping" [label="The captive fab as a prototyping advantage" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The reason Commodore could design a chip in nine months: the fab was not full, the mask shop and the processing line were on site, and a designer could pull a test chip in a few weeks - or, in an emergency, four days. Ziembicki's line is the whole story of Commodore's speed: 'We were using people wh"];
  "facility.foundries.corporate-structure" [label="The corporate chain that owned all of it" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="From the US Tax Court record: Commodore Holding B.V. (Netherlands) at the top; Commodore International Ltd (Bahamas); Commodore Electronics Ltd (CEL, Bahamas) as the principal operating corporation; Commodore Business Machines Inc (CBM, West Chester PA) as the US sales subsidiary and the site of the"];
  "facility.foundries.vertical-integration" [label="Vertical integration as a strategy" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Tramiel's thesis, and the reason the whole tree looks the way it does: if you own the chip, you do not pay a merchant supplier's margin, you control the schedule, and nobody can raise your price. The concrete results: a $135 initial production cost against a $595 retail price, and a production cost "];
  "facility.cleanroom" [label="The semiconductor fab building" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Where the C64's five custom chips were made. A 1-micron NMOS fab: a raised access floor over a service plenum, a ceiling of HEPA filters feeding laminar-flow hoods and open cleanroom, Class 100 in the process bays and Class 1000 in the support areas, and a yellow-lit room for photolithography. Air c"];
  "facility.cleanroom.workforce" [label="Fab workforce, 1980s" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A large 1983 fab ran 5,000-10,000 employees, of whom the majority working on the wafer lines were women - the largest single female industrial workforce in many of the regions where the plants went, because the tool operators and the wafer handling were culturally coded as women's work even though t"];
  "facility.cleanroom.bobbin-and-tools" [label="Wafer carriers and handling hardware" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="In 1983 a wafer still travelled in a quartz boat inside a plastic carrier, or hand-carried in a PTFE-wafer cassette from process to process. The SMIF FOUP - the sealed nitrogen-purged single-wafer container that made the whole modern fab possible - arrived with SEMI E10 in the early 1990s. Before th"];
  "facility.cleanroom.quartz-boat" [label="Fused-quartz wafer boat" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A slotted quartz holder that carries 25-50 wafers through the diffusion furnace. Fused quartz is used because it survives 1000 C, expands predictably, and is chemically inert to the dopants. Made from high-purity synthetic quartz rod, ground and flame-polished."];
  "facility.cleanroom.gowning-supplies" [label="Garment and consumable supply" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Garments were not laundryable in the era - a hooded coverall was worn once and destroyed. The plant bought enormous quantities of spun-bonded polypropylene garments, hoods, overshoes, gloves, wipes and tacky mats, and washed nothing. One 1980s 5,000-person fab burned something like a million garment"];
  "facility.cleanroom.spunbond-pp" [label="Spun-bonded polypropylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polypropylene melt-spun into continuous filaments and laid down in a random web, then bonded by heat or needle punching. Cheapest possible cleanroom garment material, and the raw material for the 'tacky mat' that caught shoe-borne particles at every airlock."];
  "facility.cleanroom.prefilter" [label="Pre-filter bank" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Bag or panel filters (2-5 micrometre) ahead of the HEPA units, on the make-up air ducts and on the return air. The 1980s standard pre-filter was a 5-ply disposable bag of coarse glass fibre in a steel frame, or a V-bank of 1-inch washable aluminium-header panels. It existed because a HEPA that gets "];
  "facility.cleanroom.prefilter-media" [label="Coarse glass-fibre filter media" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Glass melted and blown into coarse fibres and felted into a mat. The same glassfibre technology as the HEPA medium but 100-1000x coarser and far cheaper. Ends at the glass batch."];
  "facility.cleanroom.cost" [label="What the building cost (1983 dollars)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="For scale: a small 4-inch fab cost about $4 million in 1970 (about $31 million in 2024 money); a full production fab in 1983 ran from roughly $100 million to several hundred million dollars - National Semiconductor announced a $100 million, 290,000 sq ft plant in August 1983, and UMC spent about $20"];
  "facility.cleanroom.airlock" [label="Gowning and airlock" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A two-stage airlock - a 'dirty airlock' for incoming components and personnel in street clothes, and a 'clean airlock' with a cross-over bench for gowning. The costume of the era was a hooded coverall plus boots; in the amber room a yellow-coated garment as well."];
  "facility.cleanroom.hvac" [label="Temperature and humidity control" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="20-22 C and 40-60% relative humidity, tighter than any other building of the era, and the single most expensive thing in the building to get right. The 1980s control was a system of pneumatic controllers and a mechanical chart recorder; a modern fab uses thousands of sensors and a BMS."];
  "facility.cleanroom.makeup-air" [label="Make-up air and air change rate" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The fab's single largest utility load. Because the Class 100 rooms were almost entirely recirculating, the make-up air was only the leakage plus the exhaust: the acid and solvent fume hoods, the furnace exhaust, the ion-implant exhaust. Even so, a 1980s fab typically ran 100-1000 air changes per hou"];
  "facility.cleanroom.passivation" [label="Passivation and packaging areas" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Passivation (the final silicon nitride or phosphosilicate glass over the completed aluminium) and then the back-end: wafer probe, dice, die attach, wire bond, and the moulding of the ceramic or plastic package. The back end in 1983 was Class 10000 to Class 1000, not Class 100 - the contaminating wor"];
  "facility.cleanroom.litho-area" [label="Photolithography area" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Mask aligners, coat/develop tracks, bake ovens and the mask storage cabinets. In a 1980s 1-micron NMOS fab the lithography bay was Class 100 with the amber lighting, the operators wore yellow-room coats and face shields, and the photoresist was spun from a solvent (Shipley AZ1350, based on a novolak"];
  "facility.cleanroom.amber-room" [label="Amber (yellow) room for photolithography" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The lithography bay was lit only through yellow filters - either yellow-lens fluorescent fixtures or, later, a laminated film - so that no light below about 500 nm reached the photoresist. The 1980s specification was that irradiance from 350 to 500 nm should stay below roughly 0.5 W/m2 at the work s"];
  "facility.cleanroom.classes" [label="Class 100 and Class 1000 zoning" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Federal Standard 209E Class 100 in the lithography, etch and metallisation bays; Class 1000 in the diffusion, cleaning and packaging areas; Class 10,000 in the corridors, the airlock and the material store. The class was maintained by air pressure differentials - the Class 100 room slightly positive"];
  "facility.cleanroom.ffu" [label="Fan filter unit and laminar flow" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 2.4 x 1.2 m fan-filter unit or a fan coil with an integral HEPA, blowing filtered air down through perforated ceiling tiles at 0.3-0.5 m/s to give unidirectional ('laminar') flow. Open cleanroom used ceiling FFUs over the whole floor; the 1980s move was to a 'minienvironment' - a small FFU box ove"];
  "facility.cleanroom.hepa" [label="HEPA (absolute) filter" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Deep pleated borosilicate glass-fibre media in a galvanised steel casing, rated 99.97% efficient at 0.3 micrometres - the most penetrating particle size, hence 'most penetrating diameter'. A 2.4 m x 1.2 m filter was the ceiling module of every 1980s fab, ceiling and laminar-flow bench alike."];
  "facility.cleanroom.hepa-glass-fibre" [label="Borosilicate glass microfibre" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Glass melted at 1400 C and blown into fibres a few micrometres across, then laid into a paper and fired at 800-900 C to set a bonding agent, and chopped into slivers. Every 1980s fab consumed tens of thousands of these filters a year and changed them on a schedule rather than on condition."];
  "facility.cleanroom.silica" [label="High-purity silica" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The same raw material as the fused-quartz diffusion tube, the photomask substrate and the ceramic package: high-purity SiO2. In 1983 most came from Brazil or from the US inland deposit, and by the mid-80s the industry moved to synthetic vapour-deposited silicon tetrafluoride because natural high-pur"];
  "facility.cleanroom.access-floor" [label="Access floor panel" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 600 mm square steel or aluminium-frame panel with a steel or cast-iron skin, on adjustable pedestals with seismic and electrical bonding. The panel had to be load-rated for a 1000 kg diffusion furnace cart and still be light enough for two people to lift."];
  "facility.cleanroom.raised-floor" [label="Raised floor and plenum" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 600 mm or 900 mm void under a load-rated pedestal floor, with all the process exhaust, the cold water, the nitrogen, the vacuum and the electrical cabling running underneath. The whole architectural idea of the fab is that the floor is a duct. This is also why the 1980s fabs needed full basements "];
  "facility.hazardous-waste" [label="Hazardous and industrial waste, 1980s practice" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The part of the story usually left out. A 1980s electronics plant generated, per fab, tens of thousands of gallons of spent acid, spent solvent, cyanide-bearing rinse, and metal-bearing sludge a year, plus a smaller amount of contaminated equipment and packaging. What happened to it was loosely gove"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.commodore-site" [label="Commodore's own solvent site: the CSG Superfund case" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The clearest documented example in this whole story. A 250-gallon underground concrete tank at the MOS Technology site at 950 Rittenhouse Road, Norristown, Pennsylvania - installed with the building in 1970 - leaked in 1974. It stored the trichloroethene and other solvents used to clean silicon wafe"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.trichloroethene" [label="Trichloroethene (TCE)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A chlorinated solvent, 1,1,2-trichloroethene, once the standard vapour-degreasing and wafer-cleaning solvent in the semiconductor industry. A suspected carcinogen; a common groundwater contaminant; the specific reason the Norristown site is on the National Priorities List."];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.tce-feedstock" [label="Ethylene from the steam cracker" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The chlorination of ethylene, or the oxychlorination of 1,2-dichloroethane, which is itself from the chlorination of ethylene. The ethylene came from naphtha steam cracking - the same petrochemical chain as everything else on this tree. Ends at the crude oil still."];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.ocean-outfall" [label="Ocean outfall" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="In the coastal Far East - Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore, the Philippines - dilute effluent was and is discharged through a permitted outfall diffuser into the sea, often at a designated ocean disposal zone beyond the territorial limit. The dilution argument is real; so is the fact that the 1980s outf"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.manifest" [label="The RCRA manifest" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A multi-part paper form - the shipping paper, the generator certification and the transporter and facility certifications - carried with the load from generator to treatment, storage or disposal facility, with signed copies returned to the generator. This is the paper trail that later made it possib"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.incineration" [label="Incineration of solvent vapours" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Vapour incineration (afterburner / thermal oxidiser) for the xylene, toluene and trichloroethane vapour from the resist track and the degreaser, plus a wet scrubber or a carbon absorber on the cool side. By the time the air came out it passed an oxidiser at 800-1000 C with a residence time of about "];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.neutralisation" [label="Acid neutralisation with lime" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The cheapest and by far the most common treatment: slaked lime or limestone slurry is dosed into a reaction tank, the acid is neutralised, the metal hydroxides precipitate out, and the tank is then settled and the supernatant discharged. It converts an acid into a sludge - which is still a hazardous"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.landfill" [label="Hazardous waste landfill" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="In 1983 a hazardous landfill was typically a clay-lined cell with a leachate collection system and groundwater monitoring wells, licensed under RCRA or operating under interim status. Because the Land Disposal Restrictions did not yet exist, most of it received untreated sludge and incinerator ash."];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.settling-pond" [label="Settling pond / lagoon" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An unlined or clay-lined earthen basin into which treated effluent was discharged and solids allowed to settle, with the supernatant decanted to a stream or a municipal sewer. Thousands of these existed in 1983; the liner requirements came later. The pond was often the plant's cheapest and worst ide"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.metal-hydroxide-sludge" [label="Heavy-metal hydroxide sludge" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A wet, low-solids sludge of iron, aluminium, chromium, copper, nickel, zinc and cadmium hydroxides, filtered on a plate-and-frame or belt press to 15-25% solids and trucked to a hazardous landfill in a lined, covered cell. In 1983 this was the default destination and the reason so many US sites are "];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.generation" [label="Spent acid, solvent and plating waste streams" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The three physical waste streams that carried almost all of a 1980s electronics plant's hazardous load: the spent acids and alkalis from etching and cleaning, the spent organic solvents and resist stripper from photolithography and degreasing, and the cyanide- and metal-bearing rinses from gold and "];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.plating-rinse" [label="Cyanide- and metal-bearing plating rinse" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Rinse water carrying gold, tin, copper, nickel and chromium out of the plating line. The cyanide-bearing stream needed alkaline chlorination before discharge; the acid-bearing stream went to the neutralisation tank. Both ended up in the same hydroxide sludge."];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.spent-solvent" [label="Spent organic solvent and resist stripper" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Acetone, xylene, toluene, isopropanol and cellosolve acetate from the degreasers, plus the spent developer (tetramethylammonium hydroxide from positive photoresist) and stripper from the coat-develop track. Recovered by activated-carbon adsorption and distillation where that was economical, and othe"];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.spent-acid" [label="Spent acid and alkali" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sulfuric and hydrochloric acid exhausted by the etchers and the wet benches, plus the caustic and the alkaline develop and strip baths. Neutralised with lime into a metal hydroxide sludge; the arsenic-containing liquor from the diffusion furnaces went the same way."];
  "facility.hazardous-waste.rcra" [label="RCRA: the cradle-to-grave regime" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The Resource Conservation and Recovery Act became law on 21 October 1976. Its Subtitle C hazardous waste regulations were promulgated 26 February and 19 May 1980 and took effect 19 November 1980 - so in 1983 the whole American electronics industry was three years into the most consequential change i"];
  "facility.compressed-air" [label="Instrument air and plant air" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Every factory ran on compressed air. Instrument air was dried, oil-free air fed to the air-line and to the logic valves; plant air was for the pneumatic tools, the automated insertion machines and the ejectors. The classic 1980s split was a dedicated compressor for instrument air and one or more dir"];
  "facility.compressed-air.fab-exhaust" [label="Process exhaust (fab wet-bench and furnace)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A separate, larger system from the instrument air: a scrubbed chemical exhaust ducting the acid, base, solvent and furnace exhaust out of the building, usually to an alkaline scrubber on the roof. The neutral brine it discharges and the metal-bearing sludge it produces are the liquid and solid waste"];
  "facility.compressed-air.scrubber-packing" [label="Alkaline scrubber packing" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A packed column of plastic Raschig or saddle rings on a support grid, fed with caustic soda or sodium carbonate, stripping acid mist and the base-mist equivalent out of the exhaust. Discharge is a neutral brine sent to the effluent system."];
  "facility.compressed-air.pvc-packing" [label="PVC / PP packing media" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Moulded plastic ring or saddle packing, chemically inert to acid and alkali where ceramic or metal would be attacked. The same vinyl chloride monomer chain that makes the conduit and the cable in the plant."];
  "facility.compressed-air.nitrogen-purge" [label="Nitrogen purge of the air system" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Before opening a vessel, a line, or a tank that had held flammable or reactive material, it was purged with nitrogen and tested with an oxygen analyser. The purge itself was fed from the nitrogen header or, in a small plant, from a bottled cylinder."];
  "facility.compressed-air.ring-main" [label="Ring main and distribution" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Black steel pipe - galvanised or, better, painted steel - in a ring around the plant with drop-legs to each machine. A ring rather than a spine so that a single closed valve could isolate a section for maintenance. Drops went through a service unit with a tap, a pressure regulator, a filter and a fl"];
  "facility.compressed-air.receiver" [label="Air receiver (accumulator)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A vertical ASME code air vessel, 1-10 m3, acting as a buffer so the compressor could unload on a pressure switch. It also stored enough air to ride through a power cut for a few minutes - which in a fab meant keeping the wet-bench exhaust and the compressors together."];
  "facility.compressed-air.dryer" [label="Air dryer" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Two chemistries in 1983. Refrigerant (absorption or adsorption, e.g. a zeolite unit chilled to 1-3 C) gave a pressure dew point of about 2-10 C, good enough for most pneumatic machinery and marginal for instrument air. Desiccant (two alternating towers of silica gel or molecular sieve with a heatles"];
  "facility.compressed-air.separator" [label="Water (and oil) separator" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An automatic drain pot or cyclone knock-out that removes the condensed water and, in an oil-injected screw machine, the injected oil. This vessel was the source of most of the 'the air is wet' complaints in the shop."];
  "facility.compressed-air.aftercooler" [label="Aftercooler" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A water-cooled or finned-air heat exchanger that takes the discharge air from ~100 C down to ~40 C. Without it the machine would not hold pressure in a hot climate and the separator would not work."];
  "facility.compressed-air.compressor" [label="Air compressor" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Reciprocating or, from the 1960s onward, oil-injected rotary screw machines. A large electronics plant ran several 100-300 kW screw units; a small sub-contractor ran one 30 kW reciprocating machine under a concrete acoustic enclosure."];
  "facility.compressed-air.motor" [label="Drive motor" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A TEFC or, in a corrosive coastal plant, an explosion-proof motor. 400 V three-phase induction motor with a direct-on-line or star-delta starter; speed control only by pole changing."];
  "facility.compressed-air.intake-silencer" [label="Intake silencer and inlet filter" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An intake filter-silencer combination that kept the machine from eating the dust and the unfiltered outside air. In a coastal site (Kaohsiung, Hong Kong, Long Beach) the sea salt went straight onto the rotors if this was neglected."];
  "facility.gases" [label="Industrial gas supply" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The bulk gases a 1980s electronics plant bought by the tanker load: nitrogen, oxygen, argon and hydrogen, plus helium and carbon dioxide. Supplied either by an on-site air separation unit, an on-site PSA/membrane/electrolyser, or delivered as liquid from a merchant producer in a road tanker. Everyth"];
  "facility.helium.supply-constraint" [label="The 1980s helium shortage" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="US Bureau of Mines and then BLM administered the supply; the Cliffside field ran out in the mid-1970s and production was rationed, so helium became genuinely rationed and expensive for laboratories and for leak testing. This is a real constraint on the 1980s helium supply chain, not a later phenomen"];
  "facility.argon.uses" [label="Where argon went in a 1980s plant" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="TIG welding of the sheet metal and the transformer laminations, the inert blanket over the 1800 C silicon melt in the crystal puller, and - increasingly - argon sputter deposition of the aluminium interconnect in a fab. Almost all of it went into joining metal, not into making silicon."];
  "facility.gases.hydrogen.uses" [label="Where hydrogen went in a 1980s electronics plant" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Hydrocarbon and silicone greasing, hydrogen annealing of the copper and the metallisation, the reducing atmosphere in the diffusion furnace and the epitaxial reactor, and - for the tie-in with this story - the hydrogen atmosphere in the case of a lamp or a nickel/plated through-hole fill was not yet"];
  "facility.gases.carbon-dioxide" [label="Carbon dioxide (dry ice)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Used in the plant for refrigerant cargo, for the dry-ice shipping of perishables, and - in the electronics plant - for nothing much; the two dry-ice applications in a 1980s C64 supply chain were chilling the shipped goods and cleaning moulds of frost. A large part of the 1980s merchant supply came n"];
  "facility.gases.hydrogen" [label="Hydrogen" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Three routes in 1983: (a) bulk hydrogen from a merchant plant by tube trailer or by liquefaction; (b) on-site steam methane reforming of natural gas, the standard for anything above about 100 Nm3/h; and (c) electrolysis of demineralised water for the last, and highest, purity."];
  "facility.gases.hydrogen.electrolysis" [label="Electrolysis of demineralised water" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="In a diaphragm or, from the 1970s, an alkaline cell, a nickel-plated or activated-titanium cathode makes hydrogen while a nickel anode makes oxygen. Fed with conductivity-grade water at 20-25 C. Output purity is 99.99% once the cell gas is passed over a palladium diffuser or an oxygen getter - the '"];
  "facility.gases.hydrogen.reforming" [label="Steam methane reforming" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Natural gas is desulphurised to under 0.5 ppm sulphur, mixed with steam at 3:1 and passed over a nickel-molybdenum or chromite catalyst at 800-850 C and 15-25 bar. This gives hydrogen plus CO, and the CO is shifted over an iron-chrome catalyst to CO2 and more hydrogen. Then CO2 is removed by amine s"];
  "facility.helium" [label="Helium" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="In 1983 helium was a genuinely scarce, genuinely odd commodity. Most of the world's supply came from a handful of natural-gas fractionation plants in Texas, Wyoming, Kansas and New Mexico, which extracted the 0.0005-0.005% helium in the gas as a crude 50-75% mixture. The rest came from four dedicate"];
  "facility.helium.hug-gas" [label="Hug gas from an underground helium reservoir" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The Permian Basin of west Texas and the southwest Kansas gas fields hold helium that never migrated out of the crust, trapped under impermeable salt and anhydrite since geological time. These are drilled specifically for helium, at tiny volumes - Amarillo field production was only tens of millions o"];
  "facility.helium.cryo-separation" [label="Cryogenic helium separation from natural gas" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The gas is chilled to -190 C through a train of plate-fin heat exchangers; helium, having the second-lowest boiling point, stays in the vapour phase and is drawn off from the top of the cold box as a crude 50-75% helium stream. Then purified by cryogenic adsorption, catalytic dehydrogenation of the "];
  "facility.helium.liquid-helium" [label="Liquid helium dewar (4.2 K)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A double-walled stainless vessel with a superinsulating vacuum jacket, holding 4.2 K liquid helium for a cryostat or a superconducting magnet. Expensive, evaporating at about 0.5-1% a day, and topped up by truck in a dewar that looked like a scaled-down rocket stage."];
  "facility.argon" [label="Argon" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Argon is not made; it is separated out. It is the third component of air and comes off the top of the ASU's nitrogen column, either as an argon-nitrogen mixture from a simple unit or as 99.99% pure from a dedicated argon column. Most 1980s plants bought it by the tanker as a by-product of someone el"];
  "facility.argon.argon-column" [label="Argon recovery column" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A second, smaller column that separates the nitrogen-argon mixture from the top of the nitrogen column into pure argon bottoms and nitrogen tops. Installed when the oxygen plant needed the argon value or the customer paid for it - usually in a semiconductor or welding-wire plant, not in a steelworks"];
  "facility.gases.oxygen" [label="Oxygen supply" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Supplied as liquid oxygen from a merchant AS U or from the site unit. Used for the oxy-acetylene flame of the mechanical and maintenance shops, the gas-shielded welding of the metal frames, and increasingly for the oxygen plasma strip in a fab."];
  "facility.gases.acetylene-cylinder" [label="Acetylene cylinder" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Dissolved acetylene at 15 bar in a porous monolithic mass (calcium carbide + acetone), in a steel shell. Calcium carbide came from the electric arc furnace carbide plant, i.e. ultimately from coal coke and quicklime."];
  "facility.gases.oxygen-acetylene" [label="Oxy-acetylene flame for tool and frame welding" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Oxygen plus a locally generated acetylene from calcium carbide (in the Far East, imported cylinder acetylene was common). This is the flame that welded every sheet-metal chassis, rack and jig in the factory."];
  "facility.nitrogen" [label="Nitrogen supply" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The single largest bulk gas. Used for inerting storage tanks and vessels, blanketing, purging, and as the carrier gas in diffusion furnaces and plasma etchers. Small plants bought a few tonnes a month of liquid N2; a furnace-loaded fab wanted tens of tonnes a day and built its own ASU."];
  "facility.nitrogen.tube-trailer" [label="Tube trailer (bulk gas road delivery)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A tractor-trailer carrying 30-40 high-pressure cylinders or a single multi-compartment tube bundle of 6,000-15,000 litres, delivering liquid nitrogen, oxygen or argon from a merchant plant. In 1983 this was how a factory without an on-site ASU got its gases; the driver's unload was a vent line that "];
  "facility.nitrogen.liquid-storage" [label="Bulk cryogenic liquid storage" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A double-walled vacuum-insulated stainless tank on a skirt, with a vacuum-insulated transfer line (a double pipe with perlite or multilayer reflective insulation between) from the ASU or the delivery tanker. The tank is a pressure vessel rated to about 700-1000 kPa on the inner shell."];
  "facility.nitrogen.membrane" [label="Membrane nitrogen generator" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Hollow-fibre asymmetric polyimide or cellulose acetate membranes in a pressure vessel, 20-40 bar feed, with N2 diffusing through the wall faster than O2. Cheap, silent, no regeneration heat, and limited to about 90-95% nitrogen - which is fine for blanketing and not fine for a furnace. By 1983 membr"];
  "facility.nitrogen.membrane-polyimide" [label="Hollow-fibre polyimide membrane" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A thin-walled asymmetric fibre, about 0.1-0.2 micrometre wall, with a selectively permeable outer skin. Making a defect-free fibre over a 1 m length was, and is, the hard part."];
  "facility.nitrogen.psa" [label="PSA / molecular-sieve nitrogen generator" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Pressure swing adsorption: compressed air is adsorbed on beds of zeolite molecular sieve at 7-10 bar, then dropped to near atmospheric and purged with a slice of product to release the nitrogen. Gave 90-99% nitrogen cheaply at small scale; the 1980s workhorse for factories of under a few hundred kW "];
  "facility.nitrogen.zeolite" [label="Zeolite molecular sieve (synthetic)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Crystalline hydrated aluminosilicate with a uniform 3-5 angstrom pore, made by hydrothermal crystallisation of bentonite, kaolin or synthetic gels. Uniform aperture size is what makes PSA possible - a zeolite with 3A pores excludes N2 and adsorbs only O2 and H2O; the 4A and 5A grades are used elsewh"];
  "facility.nitrogen.cryogenic-asu" [label="Cryogenic air separation unit (ASU)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Air is filtered, compressed in a large multi-stage compressor to about 6-7 bar, cooled against the cold product streams until it liquefies, then fractionally distilled in a low-temperature column to make oxygen and a nitrogen-argon mixture. A unit also making argon has a separate argon column fed fr"];
  "facility.nitrogen.expander" [label="Expansion turbine (expander)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A turbine in the cold box that recovers the enthalpy drop of the expanding nitrogen and uses it to generate power, offsetting part of the ASU's compression energy. Standard from the 1960s onward."];
  "facility.nitrogen.asu-tower" [label="Fractionating columns" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Insulated stainless-steel columns 5-10 m tall with trays or packing, holding the low-temperature distillation that separates oxygen (bottoms, warmer) from nitrogen and argon (top, colder). A common 1980s arrangement was a single low-temperature column producing oxygen plus a nitrogen-argon mixture, "];
  "facility.nitrogen.asu-heat-exchanger" [label="Cold box (plate-fin heat exchanger)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A stack of aluminium brazed plate-fin exchangers, some 10-20 m tall, in which the compressed air is progressively cooled to -196 C by the outgoing oxygen and nitrogen. The heart of every ASU."];
  "facility.nitrogen.atmospheric-air" [label="Atmospheric air" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="78.08% nitrogen, 20.95% oxygen, 0.93% argon, plus water and CO2. The single source of every bulk industrial gas. Delivered to an ASU intake through a windowless, acoustically baffled multi-bay intake so it arrived at a steady temperature, humidity and free of the dust that would plug a cryogenic pla"];
  "facility.nitrogen.atmospheric-air.intake" [label="Air intake stack" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A tall concrete or brick stack with louvres facing away from the prevailing wind, feeding a plenum and the air compressor. Intakes were sited so that no two of them - for air, for cooling water, for the coal yard - were close enough to contaminate each other."];
  "facility.water" [label="Water for a factory" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Every 1980s electronics and metals plant drank more water than it realised, and it arrived from one of two places. Two distinct demands sit on top of it: ordinary industrial water for cooling, rinsing, process and human use, and, in the fab, water pure enough to grow an oxide on silicon without intr"];
  "facility.water.industrial" [label="Ordinary industrial water" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The 99% of the plant's water use: cooling, washing, rinsing, boiler feed, human use. Deliberately not ultrapure, deliberately recycled. This branch is what a factory that needs cooling towers, a degreasing line or boiler make-up should point at; it draws the same raw intake as the ultrapure plant. E"];
  "facility.water.chiller" [label="Reciprocating chiller" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A compressor, condenser and evaporator in a packaged unit, cooling water or air cooled. The alternative to evaporative cooling wherever the wet-bulb was too high. 1980s chillers were almost all ammonia or R-12/CFC-11, and therefore both a refrigerant-ozone problem and a safety problem."];
  "facility.water.evaporative-cooling" [label="Evaporative (swamp) cooler" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A finned coil with water sprayed over it and an induced-draft fan. Far cheaper than a chiller and it worked well in Hong Kong, Taiwan and Singapore, where the wet-bulb temperature was already low; useless in a humid July in Pennsylvania."];
  "facility.water.cooling-tower" [label="Induced-draught cooling tower" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 20-60 m concrete or steel shell packed with fill, with fan blades in the base drawing air up through the falling film of circulating water. Water evaporates, concentrates the dissolved solids, and is purged to the drain or the recovery plant."];
  "facility.water.chem-treatment" [label="Cooling-water chemical treatment" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Chromate-based corrosion inhibitors (the dominant, and environmentally toxic, 1980s chemistry), or the newer phosphate/phosphonate/zinc formulations, plus biocide (chlorine or an organomercurial/isothiazolinone blend) and an antiscalant acid. This is where a large share of the plant's chemical consu"];
  "facility.water.chromate" [label="Sodium chromate inhibitor" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A dichromate/chromate film former that passivates the steel of the cooling system. Enormously effective, and the reason thousands of 1980s cooling towers are a chromate source in the groundwater."];
  "facility.water.chromite" [label="Chromite ore (FeCr2O4)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The only economic source of chromium. Mined by underground stoping in the Bushveld Complex of South Africa and elsewhere, then smelted to ferrochrome."];
  "facility.water.chromite-mine" [label="Chromite mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A stratiform chromite seam (UG2 in the Bushveld) worked by stoping or by open pit. Ends at the chromitite layer in the ground."];
  "facility.water.tower-filling" [label="Tower fill and eliminator" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Cellulose (Kraft), PVC or polystyrene film fill media in a lattice or dendritic pack, plus a drift eliminator of PVC chevron sheets at the top. Cellulose fill was being replaced by flame-retardant PVC in the mid-1980s for fire reasons."];
  "facility.water.ultrapure" [label="Ultrapure water" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The water that rinses a silicon wafer between every process step, and the water in which the thermal oxide grows. Target in 1983 was 10-15 MOhm-cm (roughly 1 ppb total ionic impurities) held at 20-25 C, distributed in 304 stainless or PVDF-lined piping. The yellow-lit photolithography bay and the we"];
  "facility.water.di-loop" [label="Ultrapure distribution loop" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Slotted 316L stainless or PVDF-lined pipework in a continuous recirculating loop, running hot at 60-80 C to suppress bacteria, with take-off points at each wet bench. A loop that is not constantly moving will grow biofilm within a day - the single hardest problem in 1980s ultrapure water. Modern fab"];
  "facility.water.pp" [label="Polypropylene piping" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polypropylene was the cheapest acid- and chemical-resistant pipe for wet-bench waste lines and reclaim, and (as spun-bonded fabric) the material of the 1980s 'class 100' air filter pre-filter."];
  "facility.water.pvdf" [label="PVDF (polyvinylidene fluoride)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A fluoropolymer from vinylidene fluoride, chemically inert to ultrapure water and to most process acids, used as a liner or as solid pipe in ultrapure service. Melting point about 170 C, and it will not support microbial growth."];
  "facility.water.uv" [label="UV sterilisation" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 254 nm germicidal lamp in a stainless vessel, dosing typically 30-40 mJ/cm2, to kill bacteria and oxidise the last of the organics. Added in the late 1980s onward, mostly for the reclaim loop, because a 1970s fab did not sterilise its ultrapure water at all."];
  "facility.water.uv-lamp" [label="Low-pressure mercury lamp" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A quartz envelope with a low-pressure mercury vapour discharge emitting 254 nm, immersed in a quartz sleeve in the water. Same lamp chemistry as the germicidal fittings in hospitals."];
  "facility.water.ro" [label="Reverse osmosis" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Cellulose acetate or thin-film composite spiral-wound elements in 6-inch pressure vessels rejecting 90-99% of dissolved ions, 95-98% of organics and, importantly for the 1980s plant, most of the silica and hardness. The first RO units were installed at plants like this from the mid-1970s; by 1983 th"];
  "facility.water.ro-membrane" [label="Cellulose acetate / TFC membrane" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 0.2-1.5 micrometre-thick selective layer on a polysulphone support, wound into a spiral and potted with epoxy. This is the same membrane family as the 1541 floppy's read/write head gap in spirit - a nanoscale selective barrier - but the RO element was 1 m long."];
  "facility.water.mixed-bed" [label="Mixed-bed deioniser" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A single resin bed with the cation and anion beads thoroughly mixed, polishing the effluent of the two-bed demineraliser down to a few tens of parts per billion of the sodium-chloride equivalent. It was the top of the 1980s purity curve and was standard offtake to the wet benches; the resin was rege"];
  "facility.water.ion-exchange" [label="Demineralisation by ion exchange" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Strong-acid cation then strong-base anion exchange, then a mixed-bed polish. Removed \>95% of the ionic load of the filtered water and got to 10-15 MOhm-cm. It could not remove silica, organics or colloids, and the resin was a huge organic load in itself."];
  "facility.water.pretreatment" [label="Water pretreatment plant" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Coagulation, clarification, multimedia filtration and activated carbon, all in one reinforced-concrete block that dominated the plant footprint. The purpose is to strip the water of the things that would poison an ion-exchange resin or foul an RO membrane: suspended solids, colour, hardness, chlorin"];
  "facility.water.activated-carbon" [label="Activated carbon adsorption" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A tall bed of coconut-shell, coal or lignite carbon catalytically activated in a furnace, removing humic colour, trihalomethane precursor, pesticide and industrial organics. In the ultrapure plant it was the last chance to remove organics before the ion-exchange resin and the RO membrane."];
  "facility.water.multimedia-filtration" [label="Multimedia (sand) filtration" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Pressure filters in lead/catalysed iron tanks, 3.5-4.5 m diameter, containing 600-700 mm of graded media - typically 100 mm anthracite on 50 mm sand on 25 mm garnet. Backwashed with air scour plus water at 20-40 m/h."];
  "facility.water.anthracite" [label="Anthracite filter coal" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Hard, coarse anthracite crushed to 1-2 mm as the coarse top layer. Same material as the boiler fuel, just graded differently."];
  "facility.water.filter-sand" [label="Silica filter sand" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Washed, graded, nearly pure SiO2 sand with effective size 0.5-0.7 mm. A large, unglamorous, and genuinely scarce mineral input to any waterworks."];
  "facility.water.sand-quarry" [label="Sand and gravel extraction pit" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A pit worked by river dredging or by bank excavation beside a watercourse, screening the outwash into a concrete-aggregate fraction and a finer silica fraction. Filter sand is scarce: it has to be nearly pure SiO2 with an effective size of 0.5-0.7 mm, and most river gravel is too dirty to wash up to"];
  "facility.water.coagulation" [label="Coagulation and clarification" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Alum or ferric chloride plus lime and a coagulant aid is flash-mixed, then the floc is grown in a flocculation bay and settled out over 2-4 hours in a clarifier. Residual turbidity had to be under about 1-2 NTU for the sand filters to be worth having."];
  "facility.water.alum" [label="Alum (hydrated aluminium sulfate)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The workhorse coagulant. Made from bauxite (or domestic low-grade bauxite/clay) digested in sulphuric acid and crystallised. Ended at the bauxite mine by way of the same chain that makes the aluminium in the grid conductor."];
  "facility.water.raw-intake" [label="Raw water intake" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Either a surface intake from a river or reservoir, or a deep well into a confined aquifer. Surface water for cooling, well water for the ultrapure plant - because groundwater is already filtered by geology and low in organics."];
  "facility.water.surface-intake" [label="River / reservoir intake and pump house" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A screened intake structure with travelling-bar screens, coarse screens and a low-lift pump house on the bank. In practice many 1980s plants - including Commodore's Taiwanese subcontractors in the export-processing zones - bought municipal or industrial-zone water from the local authority and paid a"];
  "facility.water.river-intake" [label="The river or reservoir" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Turbid, hard, seasonally variable. The Schuylkill at Norristown, the Sham Chun at Hong Kong, the Tamsui at Taipei, the Keelung river, and the many dams that supplied Hong Kong from the 1960s (Plover Cove, 1973)."];
  "facility.water.well" [label="Deep well and well pump" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 150-300 mm steel or PVC casing to 100-300 m with a deep-turbine or submersible pump. In the Lehigh Valley the sand-and-gravel aquifer gave soft, iron-bearing water that had to be degreased; in Taiwan the wells hit 100-300 m of Pleistocene gravel."];
  "facility.water.well-screen" [label="Well screen and gravel pack" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Wire-wound or slotted screen with a graded silica-sand or garnet gravel pack, wrapped in geotextile. Stops the well pumping sand and the aquifer silting up the pump."];
  "facility.water.aquifer-gravel" [label="Aquifer sand and gravel" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Glacial outwash and alluvial gravel forming the confined aquifer; the raw material of the well itself and the source of the hardness and iron."];
  "facility.water.aquifer" [label="Confined sand-and-gravel aquifer" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The buried glacial outwash and alluvial gravel bed, laid down in the Pleistocene and now saturated under pressure by an impermeable cap. It is the rock the well is drilled into, the source of the hardness and dissolved iron that the treatment plant has to remove, and the reason groundwater was prefe"];
  "facility.steam" [label="Industrial steam plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Process steam for soldering, cleaning, degreasing, autoclaving and heating the building. Most industrial steam plants of 1983 were coal- or oil-fired package/field-assembled water-tube boilers, often with their own turbo-alternator so that the site exported some of its own electricity."];
  "facility.steam.feedwater-treatment" [label="Boiler feedwater treatment" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Two chemistries: lime-soda softening for the low-pressure plant, and ion-exchange demineralisation for anything above about 20 bar. By 1983 the whole industry standard was sodium-cycle cation plus strong-base anion demineralisation with a mixed-bed polish."];
  "facility.steam.demineralisation" [label="Ion-exchange demineralisation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A cation exchanger in the sodium form swaps Ca/Mg for Na; a strongly basic anion exchanger then swaps the bicarbonate/sulphate/chloride anions for OH; CO2 is degassed between the two beds by a packed or bubble column, because skipping the degasser overloaded the anion resin with carbonic acid and sh"];
  "facility.steam.ion-resin" [label="Ion-exchange resin (polystyrene-DVB)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A gel-type cation resin (sulfonic acid groups) or an anion resin (quaternary ammonium groups) on a cross-linked polystyrene-divinylbenzene bead. Beads of 0.3-1.0 mm, sieved into size fractions so the fine resin could be kept out of the top of the bed."];
  "facility.steam.divinylbenzene" [label="Divinylbenzene crosslinker" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small fraction of a C10H10 comonomer added to the styrene so the bead does not dissolve in the caustic used for regeneration. This crosslinking is what makes the resin insoluble and gives it its capacity."];
  "facility.steam.styrene" [label="Styrene monomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Produced by dehydrogenating ethylbenzene or by cracking benzene, both from the aromatic fraction of an oil refinery. A C8H8 molecule - the same feedstock family as the styrene in the ABS case of the C64."];
  "facility.steam.lime-soda" [label="Lime-soda softening" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Slaked lime Ca(OH)2 and soda ash Na2CO3 are dosed into a hot reaction basin, converting the scale-forming calcium and magnesium hardness into calcium carbonate and magnesium hydroxide sludge, which settles in a clarifier. This is the classic 1900-1960 process, still running at small industrial plant"];
  "facility.steam.hardness-sludge" [label="Calcium carbonate / magnesium hydroxide sludge" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The lumpy waste from lime-soda softening, pumped to a thickener and then to a drying bed or trucked to landfill wet. This is the sludge that fills the lagoons around old steel and chemical works."];
  "facility.steam.lime" [label="Burn and hydrate lime" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Quicklime CaO is burnt from limestone in a vertical or flash calciner at about 1000 C, losing its carbon dioxide as CO2 and leaving a caustic porous solid; it is then hydrated, an exothermic reaction that turns it into slaked lime Ca(OH)2 for the lime-soda process. Ends at the limestone quarry face."];
  "facility.steam.condensate-return" [label="Condensate return" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Steam traps in every steam-consuming process returned hot condensate to the hotwell. Recovering it saved both fuel and water-treatment chemical, and the copper condensate pipework of a 1980s plant was a continuous, weeping mess of unions and gaskets."];
  "facility.steam.trap" [label="Steam trap" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A thermostatic (bimetallic or liquid-expansion) or mechanical bucket trap with a strainer upstream. Failed-open traps cost fuel; failed-closed traps flooded the plant. Every steam system of the era leaked at a few percent of its steam load, largely from these."];
  "facility.steam.header" [label="Steam header and distribution" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A large-diameter steel pipe - 300-800 mm - with flanged or socket-welded branches to each user, insulation lagged in magnesium-block or calcium-silicate, and a trap at every low point. Steam was distributed at constant pressure from a header rather than generated at each machine."];
  "facility.steam.insulation" [label="Steam pipe insulation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Moulded magnesium-block sections, calcium silicate or, on hot surfaces, an asbestos-based rope and canvas wrap. Asbestos lagging was utterly routine on a 1980s steam line and is the reason so many power and chemical plants have mesothelioma claims decades later."];
  "facility.steam.asbestos" [label="Asbestos (chrysotile) mineral" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Fibrous magnesium silicate mined from open pits and shafts in Quebec, South Africa, Italy and the Soviet Union, and spun, carded and woven into rope, cloth and board. This is the single most under-acknowledged raw material in any 1980s industrial plant."];
  "facility.steam.asbestos-mine" [label="Asbestos mine and mill" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A serpentinite or chrysotile orebody worked by open pit or underground, then crushed, graded and carded. Ends at the serpentinite rock in the ground."];
  "facility.steam.turbo-alternator" [label="Industrial turbo-alternator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Where a plant burned coal continuously it made sense to take steam past 300 C and put it through a small turbine to make some of its own electricity. A 2-6 MW pass turbine driving a 480 V alternator, exporting a few hundred kW and buying back the rest at peak rates, was a standard arrangement in Tai"];
  "facility.steam.turbine-governor" [label="Speed governor and overspeed trip" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A flyball or hydraulic governor on the turbine shaft plus a mechanical overspeed trip and, in larger sets, a turbine protective device that tripped the generator breaker. The overspeed trip was the last line of defence against a runaway."];
  "facility.steam.boiler" [label="Water-tube boiler" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Two-drum or three-drum evaporators with a superheater. Industrial boilers of 4-20 t/h fired coal at about 6-9 bar saturated, directly into the plant's heating and process network. Economisers and feedwater heaters recovered heat; a fraction of the boiler water was blown down to keep total dissolved "];
  "facility.steam.safety-valves" [label="Safety valves and drum level control" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Two spring-loaded safety valves per drum, a feedwater level regulator on the drum, and the refractory-lined sight-glass port that operators actually looked at. A steam plant is a place where the concrete is usually older than the manager."];
  "facility.steam.fuel" [label="Industrial boiler fuel" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Anthracite, bituminous coal, fuel oil or - very commonly in Hong Kong, Japan and Taiwan - coal briquettes pressed from imported anthracite fines in a ring-die or pillow press."];
  "facility.steam.briquette" [label="Coal briquette (hongsha / pressed coal)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Binderless or starch-bound anthracite fines pressed at 200 bar into egg-shaped briquettes about 100 g each, fired on a grate. Hong Kong's thermal power and much of its industrial boiler load ran on exactly this fuel."];
  "facility.steam.anthracite" [label="Anthracite fines" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Hard, high-carbon coal, largely imported to the Far East from South Wales, Vietnam and China. Ends at the anthracite seam."];
  "facility.steam.boiler-house" [label="The boiler house" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A tall, heavily built single-storey steel-framed building with a travelling crane, the boilers in a row, coal bunkers or an oil day tank alongside, and the chimney outside. Even in 1983 it was the tallest building on most industrial sites."];
  "facility.steam.fuel-handling" [label="Fuel handling and bunker" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A conveyor, a hopper and a boiler feed screw, or an oil tank and a pump skid. Manual barrow-and-shovel coal handling was still normal at plants below about 10 t/h."];
  "facility.power" [label="Industrial electricity supply (1983)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The electricity that ran every press, oven, wave-solder machine, conveyor and test rig. In 1983 the world's grids were coal, oil, gas and hydro - not the gas-turbine-dominated grids of today - and industrial customers bought it on a two-part tariff with a demand charge. This node is the utility's ow"];
  "facility.power.tariff" [label="The industrial electricity tariff (1983)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Industrial customers never paid a simple price per kWh. The tariff was two-part: an energy charge per kWh plus a demand charge per kW of measured maximum demand over a 15 or 30 minute interval. The demand charge rewarded flat load and punished a factory that ran its ovens and its wave-solder line to"];
  "facility.electricity" [label="Electricity at the factory wall" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The chain from the utility's service drop to the machine tool. This is the branch other subsystems should reference whenever they need 'mains power': the substation, the transformer, the low-voltage switchboard, the motor control centre, and finally the cord and plug."];
  "facility.electricity.distribution-busbar" [label="Busbar trunking and cable" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Copper or aluminium busbar in an epoxy-impregnated sandwich ('busway') for high-current runs, and PVC/PVDF-insulated copper or aluminium cable in conduit or tray for the rest. Flexible conduit was still common above machine tools."];
  "facility.electricity.substation" [label="Customer substation" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Where a large factory bought at transmission or sub-transmission voltage: a fenced yard with the utility's metering, an incoming circuit breaker, a main transformer, a secondary switchgear line-up and the plant's own protection relays."];
  "facility.electricity.switchgear" [label="Low-voltage switchboard" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Air-circuit-breaker switchboards at 480/415 V, with bolted busbar, fuses/MCCBs per outgoing way and a transformer tap-selector. Most factory power for small machines was tapped off the board at 120/230 V through a plug."];
  "facility.electricity.earthing" [label="Earthing grid and lightning protection" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Copper rod or bare conductor bonded under the floor and up the steelwork, plus air terminals and down conductors to earth rods. The grid is not decoration: it is the reference for every protective device on the switchboard, and in a fab with a raised floor the earth grid and the electrostatic-dissip"];
  "facility.electricity.power-factor" [label="Power factor correction capacitors" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Motor loads are inductive, so a big factory runs at 0.7-0.85 power factor and gets penalised by the utility. Banks of switched capacitor kVAr corrected it. A capacitor is the cheapest industrial electronics ever made - a polypropylene film between two aluminium foils."];
  "facility.electricity.mcc" [label="Motor control centre" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A vertical lineup of combination starters and contactors with a fused disconnect per motor, feeding the compressors, injection-moulding presses, conveyors and extract fans. The 1980s unit had no variable-frequency drives at all - speed control was a pole-changing or slip-ring motor."];
  "facility.electricity.contactors" [label="AC contactors and overload relays" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Silver-alloy contacts on a moulded phenolic base with a shading ring to stop contact chatter, plus a bimetal or electronic overload. The bimetal overload relay, unchanged since the 1920s, was the industrial motor's main protection."];
  "facility.electricity.transformer" [label="Distribution transformer" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A laminated silicon-steel core with copper or aluminium windings on pressboard and paper insulation, immersed in mineral oil or Askarel. Oil-immersed units dominated until the 1980s-90s fire codes and the economics of dry-type began to change the picture in Europe and Japan."];
  "facility.electricity.porcelain-bushing" [label="Porcelain bushings and standoff insulators" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Glazed porcelain sheds, cemented with epoxy onto the winding ends, to bring the winding out to a terminal. These are the tall ribbed 'sheds' you see on any pole-top transformer."];
  "facility.electricity.feldspar" [label="Feldspar" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A potassium-sodium aluminosilicate used as a flux in porcelain and in the electrolytic alumina bath. Ends at the pegmatite quarry."];
  "facility.electricity.insulating-oil" [label="Transformer insulating oil" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Mineral oil (naphthenic or paraffinic), a by-product of vacuum distillation of crude. Also Askarel (PCB-filled chlorinated dielectric) in older units - a PCB source that explains part of the PCB contamination of the Hudson/Superfund sites."];
  "facility.electricity.askarel" [label="Askarel (PCB-filled transformer dielectric)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A chlorinated biphenyl dielectric used from the 1930s until the 1980s ban. Ends at the chlorinated benzene/aromatics stream."];
  "facility.electricity.chlorobenzene" [label="Chlorinated aromatic feedstock" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Chlorobenzene and biphenyl feedstock for the askarel blend, itself from the chlorination of benzene over a chloride catalyst. Ends at the aromatic hydrocarbon still."];
  "facility.electricity.electrical-steel" [label="Grain-oriented silicon steel (GOES)" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="3.2% silicon steel rolled so its crystallographic grain lies parallel to the flux direction, giving a core loss an order of magnitude lower than hot-rolled steel. Made in a special continuous-annealing line. This is the same material used in the C64's switch-mode transformer and in every mains trans"];
  "facility.electricity.grain-oriented-mill" [label="Grain-oriented steel rolling mill" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Hot band rolled to 2 mm, cold rolled to 0.3 mm, decarburised in a hydrogen atmosphere and given a final magnetic anneal. Ends at the ore and the coke oven."];
  "facility.electricity.service" [label="Utility service and metering" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A pad-mount or pole-mounted transformer on the utility side of the fence, with revenue metering and a fused cut-out. The customer owned everything downstream of the meter."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant" [label="Coal-fired steam power plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The workhorse baseload of the 1980s. Coal from a mine or a rail/barge unloading dock is crushed, pulverised to talcum fineness, blown into a furnace with preheated air, burned under a boiler, and the resulting steam drives a turbine-generator. Net efficiency typically 33-38% thermal to electricity o"];
  "facility.power.grid" [label="Transmission and distribution network" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="High-voltage transmission from the plant's switchyard to the city, then medium-voltage distribution to the industrial park, then the service drop to the customer. This is the piece almost nobody thinks about: roughly 8% of generated electricity is lost in transmission and distribution before it reac"];
  "facility.power.grid.distribution" [label="Medium-voltage distribution feeder" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Overhead or underground 11-13.8 kV feeders with reclosers, sectionalisers and fuses. Underground feeders were standard in the new Hong Kong industrial estates and in Japanese and Korean industrial parks; overhead was normal in the US outside cities."];
  "facility.power.grid.substation" [label="Utility substation" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Transformer bays, circuit breakers, buswork and the control house that steps transmission voltage down to the 11-34.5 kV distribution level. Utility substations outnumbered the plants; a 1980s industrial park typically had one primary substation feeding a ring of unit substation transformers."];
  "facility.power.grid.line" [label="Overhead transmission line" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Steel lattice towers or wooden H-poles carrying three phases on aluminium conductor, with shield wires and porcelain or glass suspension insulators. The thermal rating of the conductor, not the voltage, is the limit."];
  "facility.power.insulator-porcelain" [label="Porcelain suspension insulator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Glazed (glassy) or unglazed (ceramic) insulator strings, 10-20 discs to hold a 230-500 kV line up in the air. Replaced later by composite polymer strings, which is why the classic transmission silhouette disappeared after 1990."];
  "facility.power.acsr-conductor" [label="Aluminium conductor steel-reinforced (ACSR)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Aluminium strands around a steel core for tensile strength, twisted and stranded on the line. Ends at an aluminium smelter fed by alumina."];
  "facility.power.nuclear-plant" [label="Nuclear power plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Not a 'coal/oil/gas/hydro' machine, but it was 10.2% of Taiwan's electricity in 1980 - the first reactor at Chinshan came on line in 1978, followed by Chinshan 2 (1979), Chinshan 3, Feitsui and Lungmen. Any 1983 power bill in northern Taiwan could legitimately be traced to a uranium fuel rod."];
  "facility.power.uranium" [label="Uranium fuel" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Uranium concentrate converted to UF6 and enriched from natural 0.71% U-235 to 2.5-3.5%, then fabricated into ceramic UO2 pellets 8 mm across, sintered, ground to length, clad in zircaloy and made into a fuel assembly. In 1983 the US HEU was being phased down and the world's enrichment market was swi"];
  "facility.power.uranium-ore" [label="Uranium ore (U3O8)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Graded and leached in the 1970s-80s, largely from the Colorado Plateau (Uravan) and New Mexico, plus Namibia and Canada. Ends at the orebody."];
  "facility.power.uranium-vanadium-ore" [label="Vanadiferous uraninite ore" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The US Colorado Plateau ore was processed mainly for vanadium with uranium as a by-product, in mills that roasted the ore with salt and soda ash and leached it with chlorine."];
  "facility.power.gas-plant" [label="Gas-fired power plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Two different things share this name. For the 1983 industrial economy the honest answer is that almost none of the grid came from natural gas - gas turbines ran mainly for peak load, standby and cogeneration. Taiwan's first LNG cargo arrived in 1983 and natural gas did not become a grid fuel until t"];
  "facility.power.gas-plant.turbine" [label="Gas turbine" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A compressor, a combustor at 1400-1600 C and a turbine in a single shaft or two. Open-cycle efficiency only 25-35% in 1983 - the poor number that made gas peakers expensive until combined cycle became standard in the 1990s."];
  "facility.power.oil-plant" [label="Oil-fired steam power plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The 1980s default in much of Asia and the Middle East, and the reason Taiwan's grid was 58.8% oil in 1980. Residual fuel oil and fuel oil burned in a boiler instead of coal - cheaper to handle, dirtier, and dead on arrival once the 1979 oil shock and OPEC reset prices."];
  "facility.power.oil-plant.stack" [label="Tall chimney" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 150-250 m concrete or steel chimney to get the plume above the inversion layer and disperse sulphur dioxide over a large area. Not a pollution control device - a dilution device."];
  "facility.power.oil-plant.boiler" [label="Oil-fired boiler" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Same water-tube boiler as the coal plant, but with oil burners instead of a pulveriser and an induced-draft fan sized for the lower flue-gas volume. Two to four boilers typically share one stack."];
  "facility.oil" [label="Crude petroleum and fuel oil" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The liquid fuel of the whole industrial world in 1983. Kuwait and Saudi Arabian Light for the Far East, plus North Sea Brent and US domestic crude for Europe and the US East Coast."];
  "facility.oil.refinery" [label="Refinery" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Atmospheric and vacuum distillation plus hydrotreating to make fuel oil and, in a complex refinery, the naphtha feed for the hydrogen and ethylene crackers. Ali Qaido in Kuwait (1945) or Amuay in Venezuela (1962) typified the scale."];
  "facility.oil.lpg" [label="LPG (propane/butane)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The liquid the Hong Kong and Japanese industrial estates burned for process heat and factory heating, and the feedstock for the 1980s alkylation route to isobutylene. Ends at the well or the refinery."];
  "facility.oil.well" [label="Oil and gas well" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A rotary or cable-tool hole completed into sandstone or carbonate. In 1983 the Far East's oil was largely onshore (Gawair in Kuwait, Ghawar-equivalent fields) with growing offshore production."];
  "facility.oil.reservoir" [label="Sandstone or carbonate oil reservoir" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Porous sedimentary rock (typically Tertiary sandstone or Jurassic carbonate) saturated with oil under pressure. This is where the story actually ends for oil."];
  "facility.power.hydro-plant" [label="Hydroelectric power plant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Genuinely important for the 1980s Far East: Taiwan took 13.9% of its supply from hydro in 1980 and 24.5% by 1990 (Sun Moon Lake, Tachia, Feitsui, Wujie), and Taiwan's run-of-river stations are exactly the kind of small plant Commodore's Taiwanese subcontractors drew from. Elsewhere hydro was the che"];
  "facility.power.hydro-plant.tailrace" [label="Tailrace and reservoir" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Where the water goes back to the river - and, downstream of the dam, the river itself is the raw water intake for the industrial park that the dam's power attracted."];
  "facility.power.river" [label="The river itself" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A glacial or monsoonal river carrying suspended sediment, hardness and organic colour. This is the actual origin of most of the ultrapure water in this tree."];
  "facility.power.hydro-plant.turbine" [label="Francis turbine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The standard medium-head machine: water enters the spiral casing, turns a runner on a vertical or horizontal shaft at 100-750 rpm, and exits into the draft tube."];
  "facility.power.hydro-plant.penstock" [label="Concrete penstock and headrace tunnel" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 3-6 m diameter steel or concrete conduit carrying water from the intake at head to the turbine, usually several hundred metres and dropping 50-300 m of head."];
  "facility.power.cement-portland" [label="Portland cement" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Limestone plus clay, fired in a rotary kiln at 1450 C, ground with gypsum. The universal structural binder of the 1980s industrial plant: dams, cooling towers, substation foundations, effluent tanks, the fab's raised floor."];
  "facility.power.cement-kiln" [label="Rotary cement kiln" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 60-150 m inclined steel tube rotating 1-2 rpm, burning coal or oil at 1450 C to drive off CO2 and drive the reaction forward. Ends at the limestone and clay pits."];
  "facility.power.rockfill" [label="Rockfill dam and riprap" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Dams of the era were built of quarried rock tipped into the river and compacted, faced with concrete or asphaltic membrane. Ends at the quarry face."];
  "facility.power.aggregate-quarry" [label="Aggregate and riprap quarry" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Granite/gneiss quarried, crushed and screened for dam rock, concrete aggregate and filter material. Ends at the rock in the hillside."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.turbine" [label="Steam turbine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A multistage expansion turbine: high-pressure, intermediate-pressure and low-pressure sections, the last with the long bladed rotor of a wet-steam half-pressure machine. Exhaust goes to the condenser at about 0.05 bar. Steam rate for a 1980s unit was roughly 9-11 MJ/kWh."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.switchyard" [label="Switchyard and circuit breakers" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Outdoor busbars, disconnectors, dead-tank and live-tank oil circuit breakers and surge arresters, on a steel lattice gantry. The step-up point where the plant connects to the transmission network."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.stepup-transformer" [label="Generator step-up transformer (GSU)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A single-phase or three-phase bank of 240-500 kV oil-filled transformers that takes 21 kV from the alternator to the transmission line. This is the piece of iron that makes the whole grid possible, and it is why every power station has a lumpy, oil-filled, bunded yard next door."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.generator" [label="Generator (turbo-alternator)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The turbine shaft turns a 2- or 4-pole alternator at 3000/3600 rpm. A 500 MVA water-cooled unit is about 12 m long and 6 m wide. Output was 13.8 kV or 21 kV - far too low to transmit, hence the step-up transformer next door."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.condenser" [label="Surface condenser" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Thousands of stainless-steel tubes with condensing exhaust steam outside and cooling water inside. The condensate is the plant's own feedwater; cleanliness here directly sets boiler-tube life."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.turbine-steel" [label="Nickel-alloy rotor and casing steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="For a 540 C steam turbine the high-pressure rotor needed 0.5-0.8% Cr-Mo-V steel and the last stages austenitic stainless; the whole thing was forged, normalised, stress-relieved and then creep-rupture proof-tested."];
  "facility.power.coke" [label="Metallurgical coke" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Bituminous coal coked in batteries of 30-45 ovens to make the fuel and the reductant for the blast furnace. Ends at the coking plant."];
  "facility.power.coking-coal-seam" [label="High-Bituminous coking coal seam" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A very specific subset of bituminous coal - the seam that swells and fuses in the coke oven, distinct from the steam coal the power station burns. The Pennsylvania/Kentucky/West Virginia 'High Bituminous' field."];
  "facility.power.iron-ore" [label="Iron ore (hematite/magnetite)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The blast-furnace feed, and the reason a power station's turbine steel has a supply chain at all. US supply was Mesabi Range hematite and taconite in the Lake Superior district, shipped by rail to the blast furnaces of the Great Lakes steel belt; Europe and Japan imported Brazilian pellets and ore. "];
  "facility.power.iron-ore-pit" [label="Iron ore open pit (Mesabi)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Taconite and hematite are dug by 15-tonne payload shovels and hauled in 90-tonne trucks from a strip 60 m deep. Ends at the orebody."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.boiler" [label="Water-tube boiler and evaporator" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Drum-type water-tube boiler, typically 300-900 MW, drum at ~15 MPa and 340-370 C. Feedwater circulates through downcomers and up risers around a flame path several hundred passes long; saturated steam leaves the drum to be superheated. Superheaters raise that saturated steam to 540-565 C by hanging "];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.furnace" [label="Boiler furnace and combustion" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A refractory- and water-wall-lined firebox. Coal and preheated air enter at the burners, burn at 1300-1600 C, and give up radiation to the water walls lining the furnace walls."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.scrubber" [label="Wet flue-gas desulphuriser (scrubber)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Required in the US from about 1978 onward once new-source performance standards took effect. Slaked lime or limestone slurry absorbs SO2 to calcium sulphite, which is then forced-oxidised to gypsum; the stack gas is reheated so the plume still rises."];
  "facility.power.limestone" [label="Limestone (calcium carbonate) slurry" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Crushed and slaked to calcium hydroxide for wet scrubbing, or used directly in dry/semi-dry scrubbers. Ends at the quarry."];
  "facility.power.air-preheater" [label="Ljungstrom / regenerative air preheater" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The flue gas gives up its heat to the incoming combustion air through a slow-rotating bank of corrugated steel plates - exhaust gas at ~300 C, air at ~300 C. This alone recovered several percent of the coal's energy."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.pulveriser" [label="Coal pulveriser (mill)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A ball or roller mill grinds run-of-mine coal to a powder with at least 70% through 200 mesh (75 micrometres) so that it will burn in suspension. This was the technology that made the 1950s-80s boiler possible at all."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.fan" [label="Hot-air fan and primary air" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A fan supplies the 60-80% of combustion air that dries and suspends the powder in the furnace and the remainder that sweeps it out between the boiler tube banks."];
  "facility.power.coal-plant.receiving" [label="Coal receiving and stockyard" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Unload from rail unit trains or self-unloading barges into a yard. In the 1980s the East Coast plants were fed by unit trains; Japan's and Korea's coal came by Panamax and post-Panamax bulker."];
  "facility.coal" [label="Steam coal (run-of-mine to sized)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The fuel. Bituminous coal, 20-35 GJ/t, 0.5-3% sulphur, bought on the international steam coal market or delivered by rail from the field. Its ash and sulphur content set the flue-gas desulphuriser and the bottom-ash grinder bill."];
  "facility.coal.prep-plant" [label="Coal preparation plant" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Crushed, screened, washed and dried at the mine mouth. 1980s plants were dense-medium washers plus hydrocyclones; the resulting 2-inch x 0 coal with 8-12% moisture is what a boiler of that era was designed to burn. Thermal drying was the last step - a screen-deck or flash dryer brought the fuel down"];
  "facility.coal.washing" [label="Dense-medium coal washing" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Raw coal is floated on a magnetite/water slurry of specific gravity ~1.35; the light coal floats, the ash-rich shale sinks. This is where the mountaintop-removal debate's 'coal refuse' piles come from."];
  "facility.coal.magnetite" [label="Magnetite sand for dense-medium fluid" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Fe3O4, a natural iron oxide sand used as the density medium because it is dense, cheap, recoverable magnetically and chemically inert."];
  "facility.coal.magnetite-ore" [label="Magnetite ore body" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Magnetite is rare as a bulk ore and common as a concentrates: the dense-medium coal washer only needs a few grams of Fe3O4 per litre of circulating slurry, so the ore was mined in small tonnages - often lake-shore or gabbro deposits - and concentrated by flotation and magnetic separation. Ends at th"];
  "facility.coal.mine" [label="Coal mine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="In 1983 the relevant supply was room-and-pillar underground mining in the Appalachian basin (Pennsylvania, West Virginia, Kentucky) for the US East Coast grid, and open-pit truck-and-shovel or bucket-wheel in the Powder River Basin, Silesia, the Ruhr, the Bowen Basin and the Witbank coalfield."];
  "facility.coal.opencut" [label="Open-cut coal mine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Overburden stripped by dragline or shovel, coal ripped by bulldozer, loaded into 100-tonne rear-dump trucks. Dominant in the Powder River Basin, the Ruhr, Limburg, New South Wales and Limpopo."];
  "facility.coal.overburden-strip" [label="Overburden strip of shale and clay" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Burden stripped in benches to reach the coal seam - often 10-60 m of rock and dirt per metre of coal in the Powder River Basin. The overburden itself becomes a backfill and a landfill-pond liner."];
  "facility.coal.room-pillar" [label="Room-and-pillar mining" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Still the dominant method for Appalachian metallurgical and steam coal: rooms driven to the pillar, pillars left behind to hold the roof. Cheap but leaves 30-50% of the seam underground."];
  "facility.coal.room-pillar-seam" [label="Seam between shale and sandstone beds" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The same Carboniferous coal-bearing sequence as the longwall face, mined from a shaft or slope rather than a portal. Ends at the rock in the ground."];
  "facility.coal.longwall" [label="Longwall face" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A mechanised shearer cuts a 2-3 m slice across the panel; the roof is allowed to cave into the goaf and the coal flows to a shearer loader. High recovery, high output, and a lot of subsidence."];
  "facility.coal.seam" [label="Bituminous coal seam in sedimentary rock" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The raw input, full stop. In the northern Appalachian basin the commercial coal is in the middle and upper Pennsylvanian (Conemaugh, Monongahela, Kittanning, Freeport) formations - Carboniferous, roughly 300 million years old, laid down in swamp forests and buried under Pennsylvanian sediment."];
  "chem" [label="Petrochemicals and process chemistry" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Everything on the C64 that began as crude oil or natural gas: the polymers of the case and the keyboard, the solvents of the assembly plant, the wet chemicals and photoresists of the silicon fab, the laminate resin and flux of the board, and the fuels that moved all of it. Traces roughly a tonne of "];
  "chem.petroleum" [label="Petroleum: light sour crude oil" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The dig-it-out endpoint of the whole petrochemical chain. Crude is a mixture of thousands of hydrocarbons from methane to asphaltenes, plus sulphur compounds, nitrogen compounds, resins, asphaltenes and trace metals. The grades that feed the 1980s steam crackers are light, low-sulphur: Arab Light at"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals" [label="Solder, flux and rosin" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A wave-soldered through-hole board of 1983 is assembled with a 60/40 tin-lead solder and a rosin flux core. The two facts that make this branch worth tracing are that the flux is pine rosin - a forestry product, the clearest non-fossil material in a C64 - and that the solder is 40% lead by weight, w"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.reflow-profile" [label="The thermal profile is the process" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The reason the soldering chemistry is mostly temperature management: a through-hole joint sees 250-260 C for one to three seconds in the wave, which is enough to make the intermetallic and not enough to damage the flux or the laminate; a surface-mount joint sees a 220-245 C peak for 30-120 seconds i"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.cleaning" [label="Board cleaning after soldering" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The flux residue had to come off, and the 1980s answer was either an organic solvent (1,1,1-trichloroethane, or isopropanol after 1983, or a mixture) or an aqueous detergent wash. The solvent was the same molecule as the degreaser, and its replacement by isopropanol is one of the cleanest single sub"];
  "chem.isopropanol" [label="Isopropyl alcohol (propan-2-ol)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="C3H8O, boiling 82.6 C, and by the mid-1980s the solvent that had taken over most of the cleaning work the chlorinated solvents used to do. It is flammable, it is cheap, it evaporates cleanly, it dissolves flux residue and oil, and it does not dissolve the ABS or the polycarbonate the way a chlorinat"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.solder-alloy" [label="60/40 tin-lead solder" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The eutectic at 183 C, which is why a wave-solder bath sat at 250-260 C and a hand iron at 300-350 C: 70-80 C of superheat above the melting point, which keeps the bath molten at the top and fluxes the joint as it passes through. In 1983 about 60-70% of the world's solder was 60/40 tin-lead for elec"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.solder-alloy.tin-source" [label="Where the tin came from" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The tin in a 60/40 solder was smelted from cassiterite, the tin ore, and cassiterite is one of the rarer tin deposits in the world: Bolivia's placer and vein cassiterite, the Cornish tin belt, the Nigerian and the Indonesian deposits, and the alluvial cassiterite of Malaysia and Thailand. In 1983 th"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.solder-alloy.tin-source.cassiterite" [label="Cassiterite (SnO2)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Tin oxide, 78.8% tin by mass, the principal tin ore. Recovered from placer deposits by panning and gravity concentration and from hard-rock veins by flotation, then smelted or reduced to tin. The C64-scale observation is the interesting one: a single soldered joint holds a few milligrams of tin, and"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.rosin-flux" [label="Rosin flux (RMA)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The flux core of the solder wire, or the liquid flux of the wave-solder machine: 25-40% rosin in a flux base with an activator. The rosin's job is to dissolve the surface oxide of the copper and the solder - the abietic acid is a mild acid and a mild chelant - and its virtue is that it is a good flu"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.rosin-flux.rosin-flux-base" [label="The flux base and its solvent" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A liquid flux is rosin, activator and solvent, and the solvent is what makes it a liquid. Through the 1980s the solder wire's core was a rope of activated rosin flux in an alcohol or glycol ether solvent (isopropyl alcohol, or a glycol ether), and the core was drawn into a wire of solder by a wire-d"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.rosin-flux.activator" [label="Flux activator" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Rosin alone is a weak flux. The activator is what makes the flux work, and it is also what makes the residue corrosive, so the whole specification of a flux is a trade: enough acid to remove the oxide, not enough ion to eat the board. The 1980s activators were carboxylic acids (stearic acid, abietic"];
  "chem.solder-chemicals.rosin-flux.activator.amine-halide" [label="Amine halide activator (the halide source)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A triethanolamine or an alkylamine neutralised with a mineral acid, giving a halide ion in the flux. It is the reason a halide flux is corrosive if the residue is left on, and it is the reason the halide-free no-clean flux of the mid-1980s removed them rather than replacing them: the halide was neve"];
  "chem.rosin" [label="Pine rosin (colophony)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The sticky amber solid left when turpentine is distilled out of pine resin. Most rosin is made from pine stumps - the resinous wood left behind when a tree is cut for pulp - by destructive distillation: the stump is chipped, heated in a still to about 150-200 C, turpentine and pine oil come over, an"];
  "metal.rosin.activator" [label="The halide activator in the flux core" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Plain rosin is a weak flux. The electronics industry improved it by adding a small amount of acid activator - in 1982 almost always zinc chloride, or in the more aggressive grades a zinc chloride-ammonium chloride mixture - so the flux can remove oxides that rosin alone would leave. The trade is rea"];
  "metal.pine-resin" [label="Pine resin from a plantation or a pulp mill" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The leaf: resin bled from the trunks of Pinus species in managed plantations in the American Southeast, Brazil, India, Malaysia, Portugal and China. In the 1980s most rosin came not from tapping but from distilling pine stumps and pulping residues left over from the paper industry - a pulp-mill resi"];
  "metal.turpentine" [label="Turpentine from the same stump" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="When pine stumps are distilled for rosin, the first thing off is turpentine - alpha-pinene, beta-pinene and camphene - and it is the solvent the flux paste is made with. So the same 5 kg of pine stump that ends up as the flux in a C64's solder joint first gives up its turpentine, and the two are the"];
  "chem.rosin.distillation" [label="Stump distillation and grading" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Rosin is made by distilling the resin out of pine stumps. The stump is chipped into 5-10 cm pieces, charged to a still, and heated; turpentine comes over first and is condensed and separated by distillation into alpha-pinene, beta-pinene and dipentene, and then pine oil and a heavy residue come over"];
  "chem.rosin.distillation.stump-chipping" [label="Chipping and the still" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A whole-tree distillery burns about 1.5-2.5 tonnes of dry pine stumps per tonne of rosin plus its turpentine, and yields about 5-8% rosin, 3-4% turpentine and 1-2% pine oil by mass of dry wood. The stumps come from pine killed by beetle attack, from pine cut for pulp, and from the thinning of a plan"];
  "chem.rosin.distillation.stump-chipping.retort-and-still" [label="Retort and the batch cycle" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The classic stump still is a cast-iron or steel retort holding one to two tonnes of chipped wood, charged, closed, and fired with the turpentine vapour going to a condenser and the rosin staying in the pot. A cycle takes 12-20 hours and the still is charged by hand - which is why the business has ba"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals" [label="The chemistry of making the board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 1983 bare board goes through about eleven wet operations, and each one is a chemical plant: laminate impregnation and pressing, copper foil treatment, dry-film lamination, alkaline or ferric etching, drilling, electroless copper, electroplating, solder mask, silkscreen, and a final clean. The C64'"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.chlorine-free" [label="Chlorine-free chemistry, and why it took twenty years" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Nothing in the 1983 process list was unusual except that it was chlorinated: ferric chloride, trichloroethane, a chlorinated epoxy and a brominated one. By 1995-2000 the industry had moved to alkaline etching, alkaline dry-film development, and epoxy laminates with a non-halogenated flame retardant "];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.plating" [label="Plating baths" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 1983 double-sided board is copper plated in an acid copper sulphate bath (with levelers and brighteners), then tin plated or gold plated. The chemistry is a small industry of its own: a copper electroplate with organic additives at 2-6 A/dm2 and 25-30 C; a tin or tin-lead bath; and, for the connec"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.plating.immersion-gold" [label="Immersion gold over electroless nickel" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Electroless nickel-phosphorus (hypophosphite reducer) gives a hard, corrosion-resistant, solderable surface, and immersion gold in a cyanide-complexed bath gives the contact surface its low contact resistance. The gold bath contains a complexing agent (EDTA, a phosphate or a cyanide) whose job is to"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.plating.immersion-gold.electroless-nickel" [label="Electroless nickel-phosphorus and its reducer" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The nickel under a gold surface is deposited by a chemical reduction: hypophosphite gives up a hydrogen atom to the nickel ion on the surface and the nickel comes down as metal, with phosphorus co-deposited at 3-9 wt% to make the layer hard and amorphous. The bath is nickel chloride or sulphate at 5"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.plating.immersion-gold.electroless-nickel.hypophosphite" [label="Sodium hypophosphite and white phosphorus" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The reducing agent is made from yellow phosphorus, caustic soda and sodium carbonate. So the nickel under the gold on a connector - the part that made a C64's edge connector and its I/O socket reliable in a humid climate - was plated out of phosphate rock, coke, silica and an electric furnace, and t"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.etching" [label="Copper etching" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Ferric chloride at 35-42 degrees Baume, or cupric chloride, dissolves the exposed copper in 30-60 minutes. Ferric chloride was the 1980s default: cheap, fast, and it etches copper at a rate that keeps the undercut manageable. Its problems were regeneration (the spent etchant is worthless unless it i"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.etching.ferric-chloride" [label="Ferric chloride etchant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="FeCl3 at 35-42 degrees Baume (about 3-5 molar), which is an oxidising acid: the ferric ion takes an electron from the copper and the cupric ion falls back to ferrous. Regeneration is done either with chlorine (the industrial way, in a cell) or with hydrogen peroxide and acid. The byproduct is copper"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.etching.ferric-chloride.regeneration" [label="Regenerating the ferric chloride etchant" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Spent ferric chloride is ferrous chloride and copper chloride, and it has to be oxidised back to ferric before it can etch again. The industrial way was a chlorination cell: chlorine into the ferrous solution at 60-80 C, converting ferrous back to ferric and precipitating the copper as cuprous chlor"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.dry-film-resist" [label="Dry-film photoresist" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 25-40 micron dry film laminated hot onto the copper - a polyester or polyethylene terephthalate base carrying an acrylate photopolymer and an acrylated monomer that crosslinks on exposure. The exposure crosslinks the acrylate, so the exposed areas become insoluble and the unexposed areas are devel"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.dry-film-resist.lamination" [label="Laminating the dry film" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The dry film comes off a protective polyester or polyethylene release liner, is laid on the cleaned and dried copper, and is squeezed onto it by a hot laminator at 110-150 C and 3-5 bar with a silicone rubber roller. The rollers are silicone because the film must not stick to them and the liner must"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.dry-film-resist.lamination.release-liner" [label="Release liner and its disposal" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 25 micron polyester or polyethylene film with a slightly adhesive surface, peeled off and wound onto a take-up reel. In 1980s board plants the liners went into the waste stream by the kilogram - a 500 mm square panel of dry film consumes about a tenth of a square metre of liner - and a solvent-fre"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.laminate.copper-foil" [label="Electrolytic copper foil" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A 35-70 micron copper foil, electrodeposited on a rotating drum from a copper sulphate-acid bath and then treated on the bonding surface. The treatment is the interesting part chemically: the foil's teeth are made mechanically or electrochemically, and the surface is then covered with a nodular copp"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.laminate.copper-foil.benzotriazole" [label="Benzotriazole coupling agent" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="BTA, a seven-membered ring with three adjacent nitrogens, adsorbs onto copper and stops it reacting with anything else - it is the corrosion inhibitor and the adhesion promoter in one. It is a beautiful small molecule for this tree because the same triazole ring chemistry appears in the corrosion in"];
  "chem.pcb-chemicals.laminate" [label="Epoxy-glass laminate (FR-4)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Woven glass cloth impregnated with a brominated epoxy resin at 50-60% resin content, then pressed at 170-180 C and 0.3-1.5 MPa in a multi-daylight press. The cloth comes from the mineral chain (chem.glass-fiber); the resin is chem.epoxy; the hardener in a high-temperature board is the DDS of the epo"];
  "chem.epoxy.brominated-epoxy" [label="Brominated epoxy for FR-4" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Tetrabromobisphenol A based epoxy, brominated to about 20-40% bromine, blended into the resin at 10-30% so the laminate meets UL 94 V-0. The bromine works by releasing hydrogen bromide in the flame and smothering it, and it works so well that the industry continued using it through the 1980s even as"];
  "chem.epoxy.branched-epoxy" [label="Branched and novolac epoxies" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Reacting DGEBA with a dihydroxy or trihydroxy compound - trimethylolpropane, bisphenol A again, or an epoxy cresol novolac - builds a molecule with more than two epoxide groups and higher functionality, which raises the glass transition temperature and lets a laminate hold a solder temperature. The "];
  "chem.fab-chemicals" [label="The wet chemicals of a 1980s silicon fab" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 1983 wafer fab consumed tens of thousands of gallons a day of ultrapure water and a few thousand gallons of five electronic-grade acids, and its entire chemistry was aimed at one thing: keeping a few hundred metallic atoms per square centimetre off the wafer. This branch traces those chemicals - s"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.ammonium-hydroxide" [label="Electronic-grade ammonium hydroxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Aqueous ammonia at 20-29 wt% in ultrapure water, made by sparging anhydrous ammonia into the water under a scrubbed laminar-flow hood, then filtered to sub-micron and to low sodium. It is the alkaline clean and the alkaline developer companion, and it is the one wet chemical that arrives as a gas: a"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.spec-purity" [label="The purity ladder" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 1980s specification ladder, from technical grade to the electronic grade that a wafer demanded: a technical acid at about 5N with metals at tens and hundreds of parts per million; an electronic acid at about 8N; and the 9N-10N grade with a total metal content under 10 parts per billion. The reas"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.process-gases" [label="Bulk process gases" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The three gases a fab inhaled by the thousand: nitrogen for inerting, purging and sparging; argon for the sputtering cathodes and for the anneal; and helium for the leak detector, the cryopump and some of the photolithography optics cooling."];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.process-gases.argon" [label="Argon" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A by-product of the air separation unit at 1-3% of the air, purified to 99.999%. It fills the sputtering cathodes - the argon ions are accelerated across the gap and knock atoms out of the aluminium or titanium target - and it is the inert atmosphere of the annealing and ashing steps. The sputtering"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.process-gases.helium" [label="Helium" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Almost all helium is recovered as a by-product of the cryogenic separation of natural gas liquids - 0.05-0.7% of a helium-rich gas stream, separated to 99.999%. It is the cleanest gas in the plant and it is used in the helium leak detector that is how a fab proves a chamber is sealed, and in the cry"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.process-gases.nitrogen" [label="Nitrogen" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="99.999% and, for the very best, 99.9999%. Produced by cryogenic air separation for the large users and by pressure swing adsorption for the small ones. Its uses were the furnace tube atmospheres, the photoresist spin-coat bowl, the drying cabinets, the solvent flush and the packaging seal. A large C"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.process-gases.nitrogen.cryogenic-air" [label="Cryogenic air separation" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Air is filtered, compressed to 30-100 bar, cooled and expanded through a cold box to fractionate it into oxygen, nitrogen and argon at -180 C. A unit producing 5,000-30,000 tonnes a day of nitrogen was a large industrial plant in its own right, and it shared its cold box technology with the natural "];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants" [label="Dopant sources" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The chemicals that put boron, phosphorus and arsenic into the silicon. The chemistry here is a closed loop back to the ammonia, the chlorine and the fluorspar of the rest of this tree, and the whole business is about the fact that a dopant source is gaseous, toxic and must be delivered to a few cubi"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.diborane" [label="Diborane (B2H6) and boron trifluoride" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The p-type sources. Diborane is made from boron trifluoride and sodium hydride or from boron trichloride and a hydride, and it arrives at 0.5-5% in hydrogen; boron trifluoride was delivered neat from a cylinder. In 1983 p-type implantation was moving from boron to BF2+ (a molecular ion made in the i"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.diborane.borane-from-fluoride" [label="Boron trifluoride from fluorspar" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="BF3 is made by treating fluorspar with sulphuric acid or with fluosilicic acid, so the p-type dopant of a 1983 NMOS process is, three steps back, the same rock as the HF that etched the oxide. This is the tightest single chain in this whole tree from a mineral to a transistor."];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.arsine" [label="Arsine (AsH3)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The other n-type source, used for heavier and shallower implants and for the arsenic source oxide. It is made by reducing arsenic trichoxide with zinc and hydrochloric acid or with hydrogen, and the arsenic comes from arsenic metal or from gallium arsenide production. Arsine is the most acutely toxi"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.phosphine" [label="Phosphine (PH3)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The n-type dopant source. In 1983 it was supplied two ways: as an ion-implantation source, and as a dopant gas diluted to 5-15% in hydrogen or nitrogen for the dopant gas deposition reactor. Phosphine is pyrophoric in the pure state and is one of the most acutely toxic gases in a fab, which is why i"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.phosphine.phosphorus-source" [label="Where the phosphorus comes from" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The phosphine of a 1983 fab has a longer ancestry than most people assume. Elemental phosphorus is made in a submerged arc furnace from phosphate rock, coke and silica at 1,400-1,500 C, condensed as white phosphorus under water, and then either burned to phosphorus pentoxide (for fertiliser and for "];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.phosphine.phosphorus-source.phosphoric-acid" [label="Phosphoric acid (the wet-process acid)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wet process beats the phosphorus furnace, and it beats it because the furnace is only economical at a very large scale: ground phosphate rock is digested in 30-40% sulfuric acid, the calcium sulphate is filtered off as gypsum, and the phosphoric acid is concentrated to 54% P2O5. The C64-scale re"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.phosphine.phosphorus-source.phosphate-rock" [label="Phosphate rock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A sedimentary rock of calcium phosphate - apatite - mined from the Florida phosphate belt and the Moroccan and Chinese deposits. It is one of the four rock types this whole tree ends in, alongside crude oil, natural gas and sand. The C64-scale fact is absurd but true: the quantity of phosphate rock "];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.phosphine.phosphorus-source.electric-furnace" [label="The submerged arc phosphorus furnace" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 30-100 MW furnace in which phosphate rock, coke and silica are heated to 1,400-1,500 C by three graphite electrodes dipped into the charge, and the phosphorus vapour - P4 - is taken off, condensed under water and stored under water because it ignites in air. A 1980s US plant made 100,000-150,000 t"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases" [label="Plasma etch gases" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The gases that a barrel or planar plasma etcher used to etch silicon dioxide, polysilicon, aluminium and organic films. They are the fluorine and chlorine family, and they are the most expensive part of the etch cost after the electricity."];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.barrel-etcher" [label="The barrel plasma etcher" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A quartz or alumina tube with electrodes at the top and bottom, gas fed in at the top, RF at 13.56 MHz, and wafers standing vertically in slots around the tube wall. It was the workhorse of the 1980s for silicon dioxide and aluminium etching and for resist ashing, and it was cheap, simple and gently"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.nf3" [label="Nitrogen trifluoride" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="An emerging chamber-cleaning gas in the 1980s, and later a feedstock for the NF3-based etch chemistry. It is a very strong oxidiser, it is a gas at room temperature, and the reason it took so long to industrialise in the United States was a supply constraint rather than a performance one - the produ"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.cl2" [label="Chlorine and boron trifluoride" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A chlorine-based plasma - Cl2, or BCl3 with Cl2 - is used for aluminium, because fluorine does not etch aluminium cleanly. BCl3 also supplied boron, so the etch gas doubled as the p-type dopant source, which is one of the reasons the boron trifluoride and boron trichloride business ended up as one b"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.cf4" [label="Tetrafluoromethane" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="CF4, the workhorse oxide etch gas and the fluorine chemistry's workhorse generally. It is made from fluorine (from fluorspar) and carbon, or from a chlorofluorocarbon feedstock. In a plasma etcher it dissociates to fluorine radicals that etch silicon dioxide - it does not etch silicon, which is what"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.cf4.fluorine-from-fluorspar" [label="Fluorine from fluorspar" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Every fluorine atom in a fab's chemistry started as calcium fluoride. Fluorspar is treated with concentrated sulphuric acid at 200-250 C in a kiln or a retort to make anhydrous hydrogen fluoride, and the HF is either sold as the aqueous etch acid or chlorinated with chlorine to make the CFCs and the"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.etch-gases.cf4.fluorine-from-fluorspar.anhydrous-hf" [label="Anhydrous hydrogen fluoride" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Fluoride plus sulphuric acid at 200-250 C gives anhydrous HF, which is distilled off and shipped in a fluorine-lined tanker or a nickel tank. It is a strong acid, a violent poison, and it will dissolve most metals; the 1980s storage practice was a tank with a fluorine polymer lining and an emergency"];
  "chem.photoresist" [label="Photoresist chemistry" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The organic photoresists of 1983 were almost all positive-working diazonaphthoquinone resins on a novolac: the light-sensitive group is a diazonaphthoquinone sulphonate ester on a phenolic novolac made from phenol and formaldehyde. Exposing the resist converts the ester to a carboxylic acid, which m"];
  "chem.photoresist.plasma-resist" [label="Polysilane and plasma-polymerised resists" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The silicon-containing resists, in which a Si-Si backbone absorbs the deep ultraviolet and generates a silyl radical that crosslinks the exposed area. In 1983 they were in development for the 1 micron and sub-micron layers, and they are the reason the industry needed a deep-ultraviolet lithography t"];
  "chem.photoresist.negative" [label="Negative resists and image reversal" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small share of 1980s production was negative-working - the exposed areas crosslink and stay - and image-reversal resists, where a photoacid generator makes a carboxylic acid in the exposed areas and an amine in the top layer diffuses into them and makes the exposed areas soluble, so a positive ima"];
  "chem.photoresist.hard-bake" [label="Dehydration bake and hard bake" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A dehydration bake at 150-200 C to drive the water out of the spin-coated film before exposure, and a post-exposure hard bake at 120-180 C to crosslink or densify the remaining image. The hard bake is what makes the resist resistant to the plasma etch and to the buffered HF, and it is also what make"];
  "chem.photoresist.diazonaphthoquinone" [label="Diazonaphthoquinone sulphonate" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The photosensitive ester: 1,2-naphthoquinone-2-diazide-5-sulphonic acid esterified onto the phenolic hydroxyls of the novolac. The 1,4-isomer was the one the 1980s photoresist industry settled on because it gives the higher photospeed and less outgrowth. The whole resist is about 20-30% diazo compou"];
  "chem.photoresist.novolac" [label="Novolac resin from phenol and formaldehyde" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Phenol condensed with formaldehyde under acid to a thermoplastic, low-molecular-weight resin with no epoxide or methylol functionality left. The resin is not the photosensitive part; it is the part that gives the film its mechanical integrity and its dissolution behaviour in the developer. Its pheno"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.tmah" [label="Tetramethylammonium hydroxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="TMAH, sold at 25 wt% in ultrapure water, used to develop the positive photoresists and - from the late 1970s - to wet-etch the aluminium interconnect of a dynamic random access memory. It arrived as a photoresist developer in the single most consequential materials change of the early 1980s: a chemi"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.nitric-acid" [label="Electronic-grade nitric acid" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="70% HNO3 at about 10N. Nitric acid was the oxidising component of the older cleaning recipes and a component of some of the early aluminium etchants. It was the least-used of the four big wet chemicals by the early 1980s because it had been largely replaced by hydrogen peroxide."];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.nitric-acid.ostwald" [label="Ostwald process" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Ammonia oxidised over a platinum-rhodium gauze at 800-900 C to nitric oxide, then to NO2 in excess air, then absorbed in water. Nitric acid is therefore an ammonia product, which means it is a natural-gas product: the Haber-Bosch ammonia of the 1980s was the source of the nitric acid and of the ammo"];
  "metal.cyanide-precursor" [label="Ammonia (NH3)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The nitrogen source for the cyanide that dissolves the gold. Ammonia is made in enormous tonnage for fertiliser and the cyanide plant buys a small part of it: natural gas and steam are reformed to hydrogen and nitrogen, the mixture is shifted, and about 15-20 MPa of hydrogen with nitrogen passes ove"];
  "metal.habers-catalyst" [label="Iron catalyst for ammonia synthesis" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The hydrogen in the cyanide comes from ammonia, and the ammonia's Haber-Bosch reaction runs over a promoted iron catalyst made from magnetite or from mill scale. So the cyanide that leaches the gold, and the chlorine that makes the hydrochloric acid, and the aluminium in the case all hang off an iro"];
  "metal.magnetite" [label="Magnetite (Fe3O4) and the taconite route" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other main ore. Magnetite is magnetic, so it can be separated from a low-grade run of rock by magnetic separation, and that changes the economics of the whole business. The Mesabi taconite of Minnesota at 25-32% Fe is ground to 30-45 um and concentrated to 65-70% Fe pellets on a concentrator wit"];
  "metal.magnetite.roasting" [label="Fluidised-bed roasting of magnetite concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Krivoy Rog and Finnish magnetite concentrates were roasted before magnetic separation and before the blast furnace, because a magnetite concentrate that has been oxidised to hematite sinter is far easier to reduce - hematite is the reducible oxide, magnetite is not. A fluidised bed of concentrat"];
  "metal.magnetite.speciality" [label="Oxide pellets and the direct reduction route" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A different branch of the same ore: magnetite concentrate is the only ore that can be reduced to sponge iron directly, without coke, by a hydrogen-nitrogen mixture at 1100-1200 C in a shaft furnace. That is the MIDOR and HYL processes, which by 1982 were the strategic reserve of the steel industry i"];
  "metal.magnetite.underground" [label="Deep underground magnetite mining" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Soviet iron ore industry - Krivoy Rog, the Kursk Magnetic Anomaly, and the Khibiny - mined massive magnetite bodies at 300-1000 m with sublevel stoping, sublevel caving and room-and-pillar, on a scale with no real comparison anywhere else. The magnetite ore was magnetic enough that the mine was "];
  "metal.magnetite.separators" [label="Magnetic separation and the taconite concentrator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The separator is a rotating drum 1-2.5 m in diameter with a 4-10 pole magnet of 400-1,200 gauss at the drum surface, or a high-gradient can of ferrite pole pieces that achieves 10,000-20,000 gauss on the tips. Slurry flows past and the magnetic grains are held to the drum and carried around until th"];
  "metal.magnetite.ferrite-magnets" [label="Ferrite magnets in the separator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A short and important cross-link: the permanent magnet inside a magnetic separator is a ferrite - barium ferrite or strontium ferrite, sintered like the power ferrites but with a different composition and without the permeability requirement. So the ore concentrator that feeds the blast furnace cont"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.hydrochloric-acid" [label="Electronic-grade hydrochloric acid" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="20-37% HCl purified to about 10N. It was the second half of the piranha mix in some recipes, the dilute etch for the native oxide, the source of the chloride chemistry in the plasma etchants, and the chemical that keeps a fab's chemical room smelling of a swimming pool. It is made by absorbing the H"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.hydrogen-peroxide" [label="Electronic-grade hydrogen peroxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="30-50% aqueous H2O2 purified to 99.999% with a total metal content in the tens of parts per billion. It was the other half of the piranha mix and the workhorse cleaner and oxidiser of the wet bench, and by the 1980s it had largely displaced the older sodium hydroxide and nitric acid cleaning recipes"];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.sulfuric-acid" [label="Electronic-grade sulphuric acid" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="96-98% H2SO4 purified to 9N-10N, with a total metal content under 10-100 parts per billion depending on the grade. It was delivered in a fluoropolymer-lined tanker or in a 2,000-litre high-purity container and was used for the piranha clean, for the dehydration bake, for the sulphuric acid-hydrogen "];
  "chem.fab-chemicals.sulfuric-acid.contact-process" [label="Contact process and sulphur burning" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sulphur is burned to SO2 in a 900-1,200 C furnace, converted over a vanadium pentoxide catalyst at 420-450 C to SO3, and absorbed in 98% acid to make oleum, which is then diluted. The feedstock in 1983 was overwhelmingly recovered elemental sulphur, because the Claus plant had made it cheap; earlier"];
  "chem.sulfur" [label="Elemental sulphur (brimstone)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Bright yellow solid sulphur, one of the four commodities - alongside crude, natural gas and coal - that a 1980s chemical plant could buy. It is used in vulcanisation, in the manufacture of sulphuric acid (which is then used to make titanium dioxide, fertiliser and phosphates), in 2,4-dichlorophenoxy"];
  "chem.solvents" [label="Solvents and degreasers in a 1980s plant" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Between half and three quarters of the organic solvent used in a 1980s electronics plant went into degreasing, mostly in a hot vapour degreaser. The solvent was almost always a chlorinated hydrocarbon - 1,1,1-trichloroethane or trichloroethylene in Europe and Asia, perchloroethylene in the United St"];
  "chem.solvents.regulation" [label="Regulation: why the solvent list changed" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Three forces rewrote a 1980s solvent shelf. The US Clean Air Act's 1970 and 1977 amendments and the resulting state implementation plans constrained volatile organic compound emissions, and because a chlorinated degreaser is an emission source, they hit the degreaser directly. The 1983-1989 federal "];
  "chem.solvents.regulation.occupational" [label="Occupational exposure limits" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The other half of the story. In 1980s practice every one of these solvents had a 1980s-era recommended limit, every one of them was under active review by the ACGIH, and the direction of travel through the decade was downward. Perchloroethylene's limit fell from 400 to 100 ppm, trichloroethylene fro"];
  "chem.solvents.regulation.montreal" [label="Montreal Protocol" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Signed in 1987, in force from 1989, and the most effective environmental treaty ever agreed. It phased out CFCs and halons, and it is the reason expanded polystyrene packaging lost its CFC blowing agent and the electronics plant lost its CFC cleaning gas. A C64 in 1983 was packed in a CFC-blown foam"];
  "chem.solvents.regulation.epa" [label="US EPA VOC and solvent rules" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The 1970 Clean Air Act amendments listed chlorinated solvents as hazardous air pollutants, and the states' implementation plans in the early 1980s put specific limits on degreaser emissions. New Jersey's solvent programme in the early 1980s was the strictest of them and effectively banned chlorinate"];
  "chem.solvents.regulation.epa.substitutes" [label="The 1980s substitution: isopropanol, NMP, hydrocarbons" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="What actually replaced the chlorinated degreasers, and why it took about ten years rather than two. The candidates were isopropyl alcohol, N-methylpyrrolidone, and increasingly hydrocarbon solvents and aqueous detergents. Each was available, each worked, and none of them was free: isopropanol is fla"];
  "chem.solvents.aromatic-hydrocarbon" [label="Xylene, toluene and hexane" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The hydrocarbon solvents. Xylene is the classic silkscreen and coatings solvent and it is what the screen printing ink of a PCB is thinned with. Toluene was the coatings and adhesive solvent. Hexane (extracted from the light ends by solvent extraction with furfural or NMP) was the degreaser for prec"];
  "chem.solvents.aromatic-hydrocarbon.white-spirit" [label="White spirit and mineral spirits" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A petroleum distillate boiling 150-200 C - roughly C8-C12 - sold as the standard paint and stencil solvent. It was the cheapest coating solvent there was and it was used in the silkscreen shop, in the stencil shop and in the assembly shop's fixture cleaning. Its defect was the same defect as xylene'"];
  "chem.solvents.other-oxygenated" [label="Ketones, esters and glycol ethers" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The rest of the 1980s solvent shelf: methyl ethyl ketone and methyl isobutyl ketone (the latter made by condensing acetone to diacetone alcohol and then hydrogenating), ethyl acetate and isobutyl acetate, propylene glycol ethers, and dimethylformamide. Each had a niche - the ketones for coatings, th"];
  "chem.solvents.acetone" [label="Acetone" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Propanone, boiling 56 C. It was the general cleaning solvent for glass, for the epoxy residues of a coil winding operation, and for some stencil and screen operations, and it is the solvent in the acetylene absorption step of the acetylene process and in the electrophotography of copying. It came ou"];
  "chem.ethanol" [label="Ethanol" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="C2H6O, boiling 78.4 C. In 1980s electronics it was a solvent and a cleaning fluid for a few specialist applications, and it was the fuel additive competing with MTBE for the octane that lead had given up. Chemically it can be made either by hydrating ethylene over phosphoric acid at 300 C and 60-70 "];
  "chem.isopropanol.kpo" [label="KPO process (from propylene)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Propylene plus acetone gives propylene oxide; propylene oxide isomerises to acetone over a base catalyst; acetone plus hydrogen is hydrogenated to isopropanol. The isomerisation-hydrogenation loop is the unit that makes the process economical, and the 1970s introduction of the low-temperature isomer"];
  "chem.solvents.cfc" [label="CFCs and HCFCs (halons and freons)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Trichlorofluoromethane (CFC-11, boiling 23.8 C) and trichloro-1,2,2-trifluoroethane (CFC-113, boiling 48 C) were the 1980s cleaners, refrigerants and foam blowing agents, and CFC-11 was the blowing agent in expanded polystyrene packaging until the late 1980s. In 1983 there was no regulation on them "];
  "chem.solvents.cfc.cfc-synthesis" [label="CFC synthesis from chloroform" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="CFC-11 (trichlorofluoromethane) is chloroform plus hydrogen fluoride in an antimony halide catalyst at 50-100 C; CFC-12 (dichlorodifluoromethane) is carbon tetrachloride plus hydrogen fluoride. Both therefore start from the chlorination of methane - the same methane the plastics industry buys - and "];
  "chem.solvents.cfc.cfc-synthesis.methane-chlorination" [label="Methane chlorination" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Chlorine plus methane at 250-400 C gives chloromethane, and further chlorination gives chloroform and carbon tetrachloride. The chlorination is a free-radical chain reaction in a fluidised bed, and the product is a mixture that has to be distilled. The chloroform route to CFC-11 is therefore a natur"];
  "chem.solvents.methylene-chloride" [label="Dichloromethane (methylene chloride)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="CH2Cl2, boiling at 40 C, made by chlorinating methane to chloromethane and then to dichloromethane, or by the chlorination of formaldehyde. It was the fastest-evaporating chlorinated solvent and was used for paint stripping, for the epoxy and flux residues that a slow solvent would not remove, and i"];
  "chem.solvents.perchloroethylene" [label="Tetrachloroethylene (perchloroethylene)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Cl2C=CCl2, boiling at 119 C, made by chlorinating ethylene to 1,2-dichloroethane and then to tetrachloroethane followed by chlorination and dehydrochlorination, or by the oxychlorination route. It was the American electronics degreaser of choice because it dissolves grease and flux residue and does "];
  "chem.solvents.trichloroethylene" [label="Trichloroethylene (1,1,2-TCE)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Boiling at 87 C, made from acetylene by chlorination to tetrachloroethane followed by dehydrochlorination. It has excellent solvency for grease and for the epoxy and phenolic residues of an electronics line, and it is also a solvent for the vapour degreaser that handles the cleaned parts. It is a su"];
  "chem.solvents.trichloroethane.vinylidene-chloride" [label="1,1,1-Trichloroethane from acetylene" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The synthesis that links a degreaser to a natural gas field. Acetylene plus HCl over a mercuric chloride catalyst gives vinylidene chloride, CH2=CCl2, and chlorinating that with chlorine gives 1,1,1-trichloroethane. Both steps are the same chemistry, and the same catalyst, as the vinyl chloride proc"];
  "chem.solvents.trichloroethane.vinylidene-chloride.mercuric-cell" [label="Mercury catalyst and its recovery" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="HCl is adsorbed onto a mercuric chloride-charcoal catalyst and acetylene adds across it. The catalyst is what makes the chemistry work and what makes the plant a mercury emitter, and the 1980s answer was a mercury recovery system: the reactor effluent was stripped with an acid stream, the mercury co"];
  "chem.solvents.trichloroethane.vinylidene-chloride.mercuric-cell.charcoal-support" [label="Activated charcoal support" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 2-4 mm charcoal pellet, impregnated with mercuric chloride and dried. Activated charcoal is made by carbonising wood or coal at 800-1,000 C and steam activating it, so it is - like the rosin in the solder flux - a forestry product. The charcoal support in the degreaser's ancestor chemistry is ther"];
  "chem.solvents.trichloroethane" [label="1,1,1-Trichloroethane (methyl chloroform)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="CH3CCl3, boiling at 74 C, a non-flammable solvent that was the standard degreaser for precision metalwork and electronics through the 1950s to the 1980s. It was made from acetylene by hydrochlorination to vinylidene chloride and then chlorination to 1,1,1-trichloroethane - so it shares its whole ups"];
  "chem.solvents.vapour-degreaser" [label="The vapour degreaser" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A tank of boiling solvent with a cold condenser coil above it. Parts are lowered into the vapour, the vapour condenses on the part and washes the grease off, the condensate runs back into the boil tank, and the distilled clean solvent is pumped to the sump. It was astonishingly effective and astonis"];
  "chem.solvents.vapour-degreaser.condenser-coil" [label="The condenser and the tank" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The machine has two working parts: a boiling sump at the bottom that supplies the vapour, and a water-cooled coil above it that condenses the vapour and drips it back clean. The whole design depends on the vapour being kept out of the room, so the machine had a lid and a draught, and the lid was ope"];
  "chem.solvents.vapour-degreaser.ultrasonic" [label="Ultrasonic cleaner" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A tank of hot solvent or water with a 20-80 kHz transducer. In 1983 the electronics plant used ultrasonic cleaning before the vapour degreaser for fine work - the watch crystals, the gears, the small press-fit parts - because a vapour degreaser will not clean a crevice and an ultrasonic tank will. I"];
  "chem.abs" [label="ABS resin plant, and the C64 case" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene is not a copolymer but a polymer blend: a SAN matrix with a grafted polybutadiene rubber phase dispersed in it as 0.1-10 micron particles. Making it is four separate unit operations - make the rubber, graft styrene and acrylonitrile onto it, make the matrix, blend and"];
  "c64.case.abs-resin.phthalo-blue" [label="Phthalocyanine blue pigment" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Copper phthalocyanine (PB15), an intensely coloured, heat-stable, lightfast blue used for the cool grey-blue tones on some cases. Copper plus four phthalonitrile rings, assembled in a violent exotherm."];
  "c64.case.abs-resin.phthalic-anhydride" [label="Phthalic anhydride" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by air oxidation of o-xylene over vanadia-titania catalysts at ~450 C. It is the classic route to phthalocyanine pigments and to glycidyl ester epoxy resins."];
  "c64.case.abs-resin.o-xylene" [label="o-Xylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="One of the three xylene isomers separated out of the mixed aromatics from the reformer by adsorption (UOP Parex-type) or by distillation. The ortho isomer is the one that gets oxidised to phthalic anhydride."];
  "c64.case.abs-resin.c8-aromatics-separation" [label="C8 aromatics separation (UOP Parex)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Para-xylene is selectively adsorbed from the mixed C8 aromatics onto a zeolite molecular sieve, desorbed with a purge, leaving o- and m-xylene. It is a swinging adsorption unit with hundreds of adsorbent beds and rotating valves."];
  "chem.abs.other-polymers" [label="Other polymers in and around a C64" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 1983 consumer electronic device used perhaps eight different engineering plastics and two rubbers. This branch traces each of them to its monomer, because they all come out of the same crackers, the same benzene and the same phenol. Each is a node with its own subtree; chem.pvc, chem.epoxy, chem.r"];
  "chem.thermosets" [label="Phenolic and amino thermosets" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The two oldest synthetic plastics, still in a 1983 machine in the places where a thermoplastic will not do: phenolic for the switch and socket housings and the old-style bakelite knobs, and urea-formaldehyde and melamine-formaldehyde for the white insulating parts, the plug bodies and the coil-forme"];
  "chem.thermosets.urea-formaldehyde" [label="Urea-formaldehyde and melamine-formaldehyde" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Urea condensed with formaldehyde gives a thermoset used for the white insulation of plugs, sockets and coil formers, and melamine gives the tougher, heat-resistant version used for the panel fittings. Both are white, both are cheap, and both are moisture-sensitive, which is the defect that kept them"];
  "chem.thermosets.phenolic" [label="Phenolic resin (phenol-formaldehyde)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Phenol condensed with formaldehyde under acid (novolac, with hexamine as the curing agent) or under base (resol, self-curing). Bakelite, patented in 1907, was the first wholly synthetic plastic. In 1980s electronics it was in the switchgear, the relay covers and the phenolic laminate (paper or cotto"];
  "chem.thermosets.phenolic.hexamine" [label="Hexamethylenetetramine (hexamine)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Formaldehyde plus ammonia, and it does double duty: it is the curing agent for novolac phenolic and it is a slow accelerator and a decomposition source of formaldehyde in rubber compounds. It is one of the few molecules in this file that reaches into three unrelated chemistries."];
  "chem.thermosets.phenolic.formaldehyde" [label="The formaldehyde in a phenolic" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Methanol oxidised to formaldehyde over silver at 600-700 C. The same formaldehyde as the acetal plastics, from the same methanol, from the same synthesis gas. Phenolic resin and POM sitting a few hundred metres apart in a 1980s chemical park is not a coincidence; both are formaldehyde chemistry."];
  "chem.pom.formaldehyde" [label="Formaldehyde from methanol" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Methanol plus a controlled amount of oxygen over silver at 600-700 C, giving 25-35% formaldehyde in air (the Formox process), then concentrated to 60-70%. The methanol comes from synthesis gas, so this is a coal-or-gas-to-methanol-to-formaldehyde chain rather than a direct petroleum one - although i"];
  "chem.pom" [label="POM (acetal homopolymer and copolymer)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polyoxymethylene, made by polymerising formaldehyde - from the oxidation of methanol - either with an acetic anhydride chain transfer (giving the homopolymer, Delrin) or with a small amount of a diacetate comonomer (giving the Celcon-type copolymer). It is the 1980s precision engineering plastic: ge"];
  "chem.pom.uses" [label="Where POM sat in a 1983 machine" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Small precision parts: the gear and cam of a fan, the hinges and catches, the insulator of a switch mechanism, the arm of a relay. In a 1983 home computer it was present in tens of grams at most, but it was there because nothing else moulded to a 0.05 mm tolerance with a low friction coefficient."];
  "chem.rubber" [label="Elastomers: natural rubber, neoprene, EPDM" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Three rubbers for three different jobs in and around a 1983 machine: natural rubber for the feet and plinths and the moulded grips, neoprene for gaskets and the flexible cable jacket, and EPDM for the cable's weather-resistant insulation. The C64's case feet are elastomer pads stuck to the underside"];
  "chem.rubber.epdm" [label="EPDM" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethylene copolymerised with a diene (norbornene or a cyclic diene), the diene content giving the vulcanisable double bonds. It has no unsaturation in the main chain, so it is ozone-resistant and will not crack in sunlight, which is why it replaced natural rubber in weather-exposed cable insulation a"];
  "chem.rubber.neoprene" [label="Neoprene (polychloroprene)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Chloroprene polymerised in emulsion at 40 C. It is oil-resistant, weather-resistant and flame-resistant, which makes it the rubber for gaskets, for the flexible jacket of a power cord, and for the cable's outer sheath when the cable is not PVC. It is made from acetylene and chlorine through vinylace"];
  "chem.rubber.natural" [label="Natural rubber (cis-1,4-polyisoprene)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Field latex tapped from Hevea brasiliensis, coagulated, smoked and pressed into bales. In 1983 world production was of the order of 3.0-3.5 million tonnes a year, two thirds of it in Malaysia, Thailand and Indonesia. It is cis-1,4-polyisoprene with a glass transition near -70 C, so it is the most re"];
  "chem.silicone" [label="Silicone (polydimethylsiloxane)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The only engineering polymer whose backbone is not carbon. Built from chlorosilanes made by the Rochow direct process - silicon plus methyl chloride over copper at 280-350 C - then hydrolyzed and condensed. In 1983 silicones were everywhere in electronics: as RTV sealing gaskets, as conformal coatin"];
  "chem.silicone.polymer" [label="Silicone rubber and fluids" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The chlorosilane is hydrolyzed to a silanol and condensed with a silanol-terminated chain stopper, giving a silicone oil (low viscosity) or, with reinforcing silica and peroxide cure, a silicone rubber (elastomer). The 1980s mix in electronics was dominated by room-temperature-vulcanising silicone -"];
  "chem.silicone.polymer.key-domes" [label="Conductive elastomer key domes" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A switch contact made of a silicone or neoprene elastomer filled with carbon black - or with metal-plated hollow glass spheres - so that the rubber itself conducts. When the dome is pressed it shorts to a printed conductor beneath; the dome's force curve (about 0.5-2 N operating, 8-12 mm travel) is "];
  "chem.silicone.polymer.conformal-coating" [label="Conformal coating" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 20-100 micron film of room-temperature-vulcanising silicone brushed or dipped over a populated board, cured in humid air, to keep water, dust, sulfur dioxide and salt off the conductors. In 1980s practice it was used on power supplies and on automotive electronics rather than on consumer boards, a"];
  "chem.silicone.rochow" [label="Rochow direct process" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Metallurgical silicon plus methyl chloride over copper at 280-350 C gives dimethyldichlorosilane. The silicon comes from the quartz and coke reduction that the whole semiconductor industry depends on, so the silicone business and the chip business share an upstream - and share the same four-nines pu"];
  "chem.polyester" [label="Polyesters: PET, PBT and unsaturated polyester" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Three families that share the same two feedstocks - ethylene glycol or propylene glycol, and a terephthalic or maleic/fumaric acid. PET in 1983 was overwhelmingly a fibre and bottle business, PBT was the engineering thermoplastic, and unsaturated polyester was the resin of the glass-reinforced mould"];
  "chem.polyester.unsaturated" [label="Unsaturated polyester resin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Maleic anhydride plus propylene glycol condensed to about 1,000-2,000 molecular weight and dissolved in styrene; the crosslinker is styrene itself and the cure is a peroxide (usually methyl ethyl ketone peroxide) at 20-80 C. In the 1980s it was the resin of glass-reinforced moulding compound and of "];
  "chem.polyester.pet" [label="PET from ethylene glycol and dimethyl terephthalate" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Dimethyl terephthalate plus ethylene glycol transesterified at 180-280 C over an antimony or titanium catalyst, then polycondensed at 270-290 C under 1-50 mbar. The bottleneck is the transesterification methanol coproduct, which is recovered and returned to the terephthalate plant, so the plant is a"];
  "chem.polyester.pet.ethylene-glycol" [label="Ethylene glycol" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About 2.2 tonnes of ethylene glycol per tonne of PET resin, from the ethylene oxide hydration branch. The glycol is purified to 99.9% because the water it carries ends up as a hydrolysis chain terminator and it is the single largest cause of high-acid-value polyester."];
  "chem.polyester.pet.terephthalic-acid" [label="Para-xylene and terephthalic acid" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Para-xylene is separated from the C8 aromatics by adsorptive separation (UOP Parex, 4A zeolite) or by crystallisation, then oxidised with air over a cobalt-manganese-acetate catalyst at 175-225 C and 15-30 bar in acetic acid. The para isomer must be more than 99.5% pure because the meta and ortho is"];
  "chem.polyamide" [label="Nylon 6 and nylon 6,6" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The two polyamides that a 1980s machine used for its precision parts. Nylon 6,6 from adipic acid and hexamethylenediamine, or nylon 6 from caprolactam which itself comes from phenol. Both are condensation polymers of a diamine and an acid or of a lactam, and both need the phenol-aromatic chain, so b"];
  "chem.polyamide.nylon-6" [label="Nylon 6 from caprolactam" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Caprolactam is opened with water at 250-270 C in a hydrolysis-polymerisation reactor under 1-15 bar, giving a nylon 6 with a very narrow molecular weight distribution because the chemistry is a step growth from a single monomer. Nylon 6 is the form used where a low melt viscosity matters, and it was"];
  "chem.polyamide.nylon-6.caprolactam" [label="Caprolactam from phenol" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Phenol hydrogenated to cyclohexanol, then dehydrogenated to cyclohexanone; cyclohexanone oximation to cyclohexanone oxime; the oxime rearranged with oleum (the Beckmann rearrangement, which expands the ring from six to seven members) to caprolactam, and the ammonium sulphate byproduct stripped and r"];
  "chem.polyamide.nylon-66" [label="Nylon 6,6 from adipic acid and HMDA" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Adipic acid plus hexamethylenediamine condense to nylon salt at 250-270 C and 10-20 bar in a batch or continuous process, then polymerise at 270-280 C under a small nitrogen pressure. Adipic acid in 1983 came mostly from the oxidation of cyclohexane or of a C4 route with nitric acid; hexamethylenedi"];
  "chem.polycarbonate" [label="Polycarbonate" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Bisphenol A polycarbonate, made by the interfacial phosgene route from BPA and phosgene in dichloromethane, then melt polycondensed at 250-290 C. It is the transparent, tough, dimensionally stable engineering plastic of the 1980s, and in electronics it went into connector housings, terminal blocks, "];
  "chem.polycarbonate.properties" [label="Why it was chosen" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Amorphous at 100-150% linear retention, glass transition near 145 C, tensile strength 60-70 MPa, and a notched Izod of 8-15 kJ/m2. It is the transparent tough polymer of the 1980s: sunglasses lenses, camera and instrument housings, the disc of a compact disc from 1982, and the dome of the keyboard o"];
  "chem.polycarbonate.phosgene" [label="Phosgene" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Carbonyl chloride, COCl2, made by oxidising carbon monoxide with chlorine over activated carbon at 100-150 C. It is acutely toxic, and it is one of the reasons the polycarbonate industry of the 1980s was moving toward the non-phosgene route based on bisphenol A carbonate and on the methylene chlorid"];
  "chem.polycarbonate.phosgene.chlorine" [label="Chlorine and carbon monoxide" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Chlorine from brine electrolysis plus carbon monoxide from a reformer or from coke. This is the point at which the chlor-alkali industry - which needs hydrogen to sell its chlorine, and therefore needs natural gas - touches the plastics industry through a molecule with two chlorine atoms and one oxy"];
  "chem.epoxy" [label="Epoxy: bisphenol A and epichlorohydrin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="DGEBA epoxy is made by hydrolyzing bisphenol A with epichlorohydrin, then dehydrochlorinating to close the epoxide ring. About 800,000-1,000,000 tonnes of epoxy resin were made in 1983, of which the largest single use was the glass-reinforced laminate for printed circuit boards. A C64 has epoxy in i"];
  "chem.epoxy.uses-c64" [label="Epoxy in a C64" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Four places. The board's laminate resin. The power supply's transformer bobbin and coil potting, and the mains filter capacitor's encapsulation. The die attach inside the VIC-II, the SID and the CPU packages, where the chip was bonded to a small ceramic or epoxy header with an epoxy that had to surv"];
  "chem.epoxy.hardeners" [label="Curing agents" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="An epoxy is useless until something cross-links it, and the curing agent decides the properties. Amines (aliphatic triethylenetetramine, cycloaliphatic diamines) give fast cures at room temperature and good mechanical properties; anhydrides give high-temperature cures and excellent electrical insula"];
  "chem.epoxy.hardeners.anhydride" [label="Anhydride cure" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A cyclic anhydride (a methyl substituted tetrahydrophthalic or nadic anhydride) catalysed by an amine or a tertiary amine. The cure is slower and needs 150-200 C, but the network is very tight and the dielectric properties are excellent - which is why anhydride epoxy was the potting and the chip-att"];
  "chem.epoxy.hardeners.ddd" [label="4,4'-Diaminodiphenylmethane (DDM/MDA)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by condensing aniline with formaldehyde, it is the first-generation hardener: cheap, and it is the amine that showed up in the early carcinogenicity work on epoxies in the 1970s, which pushed the industry toward DDM's less toxic relatives. It is still in laminates and in the rubber industry as "];
  "chem.epoxy.hardeners.dds" [label="4,4'-Diaminodiphenyl sulfone (DDS)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The high-temperature laminate hardener: about 80 parts per 100 of DGEBA, cured at 180-200 C. DDS is made by sulphonating diphenyl sulphone, nitrating it to the dinitro compound and reducing the nitro groups - an aniline and chlorobenzene route for the aniline part. It is the hardener in the aerospac"];
  "chem.epoxy.dgeba.bisphenol-a" [label="Bisphenol A from cumene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Two phenol molecules plus acetone, condensed over a strong acid to give 4,4'-isopropylidenediphenol, with the water stripped. In 1983 most BPA was made this way from the cumene process's phenol, and the rest from the older two-step route using bis(4-chlorophenyl) ketone. The 1980s were the period wh"];
  "chem.epoxy.dgeba.bisphenol-a.acetone" [label="Acetone as the BPA co-reactant" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The BPA reaction is where the case polymer meets the laminate resin: the phenol for BPA comes out of the cumene oxidation that also yields the acetone for the reaction, so the two halves of bisphenol A come from one reactor 20 km apart. The same bisphenol A went into the polycarbonate and the unsatu"];
  "chem.epoxy.dgeba.epichlorohydrin" [label="Epichlorohydrin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="1-chloro-2,3-epoxypropane: the C3 building block. In 1983 most of the world's epichlorohydrin was made by the chlorohydrin route - propylene chlorohydrin from propylene plus hypochlorous acid, dehydrochlorinated with a lime slurry - which is the same chlorohydrin chemistry that makes propylene oxide"];
  "chem.epoxy.dgeba" [label="DGEBA (diglycidyl ether of bisphenol A)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse epoxy: bisphenol A condensed with two moles of epichlorohydrin, giving a resin with an epoxide group at each end of the molecule and epoxide number 172-190 equivalents per 100 g. It is a solid at room temperature (melting 4-12 C for the liquid grades) and it cures into a cross-linked n"];
  "chem.pvc" [label="PVC: vinyl chloride and suspension polymerisation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About 3.5 million tonnes of PVC in 1983, of which maybe 5-8% was flexible and the rest rigid. Two-thirds of the monomer came from the oxychlorination of ethylene and the rest from acetylene and HCl. The C64 used it in the power cord's jacket and insulation, in the moulded cable accessories, and in t"];
  "chem.pvc.uses-c64" [label="PVC in a C64" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The power cord: a two-core or three-core flexible PVC jacket, typically 1.5-3 mm of wall, with PVC insulation on the conductors and a PVC or PVC/rubber strain relief at the moulded connector. A C64 power cord is a couple of hundred grams of PVC. The keyboard keys and the side vents of the earlier ca"];
  "chem.pvc.plasticiser" [label="Plasticisers" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A flexible PVC is PVC with 30-60 parts per 100 of a liquid plasticiser, and the plasticiser is where most of the toxicology lived. Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP, DOP) was the standard from the 1950s into the 1990s, made by esterifying phthalic anhydride with 2-ethylhexanol - itself from butyralde"];
  "chem.pvc.plasticiser.dehp" [label="Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="C8H17OOC-C6H4-COOC8H17, 391 g/mol, a colourless liquid with a very slight odour. About 200,000-250,000 tonnes of DEHP a year were made in 1983, most of it for PVC. The C8 alcohol comes from propylene and from ethylene via butyraldehyde, so a PVC cable jacket is a direct consumer of the cracker."];
  "chem.pvc.stabilisation" [label="Heat stabilisation, and the lead problem" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="PVC is unstable above about 140 C: the dehydrochlorination produces HCl, which catalyses more dehydrochlorination, which is why unstabilised PVC cannot be processed at all. The 1980s stabiliser systems were lead salts - dibasic lead phosphite, tribasic lead sulphate, lead stearate - which are excell"];
  "chem.pvc.stabilisation.ca-zn" [label="Calcium-zinc stabilisers" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Calcium and zinc carboxylates (stearate, hydrostearate) with an organic co-stabiliser such as a beta-diketone or a polyol. Zinc carboxylates are the primary stabilisers and calcium carboxylates are the co-stabilisers that exchange zinc for calcium at the right moment. The 1980s engineering problem w"];
  "chem.pvc.stabilisation.lead-stabiliser" [label="Tribasic lead sulphate and lead phosphite" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The lead salts are cheap, opaque, and superb at scavenging HCl and chelating chlorine. The cost is that lead is a cumulative neurotoxin and PVC was, until recently, a major route of human lead exposure through pipes, and in 1983 the limits on lead in consumer products were tightening. The chemistry "];
  "chem.pvc.polymerisation.initiator" [label="Initiator and chain transfer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Acyl peroxide or a persulphate at 0.05-0.5%, with a chain transfer agent (often chloroform, tetrachloromethane or an ester) to control molecular weight. The transfer agents are chlorinated for a reason: they also terminate the radicals that would otherwise go on to make chlorinated by-products, and "];
  "chem.pvc.polymerisation.suspending-agent" [label="PVA suspending agent" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Partially hydrolysed polyvinyl alcohol at 0.5-2% of the water phase, with a second colloid stabilizer if needed. It is the same PVA chemistry as the suspension polystyrene suspending agent - one more appearance of a polymer from the vinyl acetate chain doing structural work in a different polymer's "];
  "chem.pvc.vcm.storage" [label="VCM storage and inhibition" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Bulk VCM in a refrigerated atmospheric tank at 10-15 C with a nitrogen blanket, or in a pressure vessel. It is inhibited against polymerisation with a phenolic or an organic peroxide, and it is kept below 50 C because vinyl chloride is a powerful inhibitor of its own polymerisation - a fact the indu"];
  "chem.pvc.vcm.acetylene-route" [label="Acetylene plus HCl (the mercury route)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Acetylene and anhydrous hydrogen chloride over a mercuric chloride catalyst on charcoal at 20-60 C and 1-2 bar absolute, at 15-25% conversion per pass with 90-95% selectivity. This is the route that ties PVC to natural gas, because acetylene is made from methane. It survives where there is no cracke"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.to-vinyl-chloride" [label="Acetylene to vinyl chloride" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The reason the whole acetylene industry existed. Acetylene plus anhydrous hydrogen chloride over a 0.3-0.5% mercuric chloride catalyst on charcoal at 20-60 C and 1-2 bar absolute gives vinyl chloride at 15-25% conversion per pass and 90-95% selectivity, the low conversion forced by the HCl ratio and"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.to-vinyl-chloride.mercury-catalyst" [label="Mercuric chloride catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Anhydrous HCl is adsorbed onto a mercuric chloride-charcoal catalyst and ethyne adds across it. The catalyst is a genuine environmental liability: mercury lost from a large acetylene plant was tens of kilograms a year, and by the 1980s both West Germany and the Netherlands were restricting mercury e"];
  "chem.pvc.vcm.acetylene-route.mercury" [label="Mercury in the PVC plant" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The mercury chloride catalyst is a genuine environmental liability. A 1980s European PVC plant was a mercury emitter at tens of kilograms a year, and by the mid-1980s both the German and the Dutch authorities were requiring either catalyst recovery or a switch to the oxychlorination route. The engin"];
  "chem.pvc.vcm" [label="Vinyl chloride monomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="C2H3Cl, boiling at -13.4 C, so it is a liquid only under pressure. It is a proven human carcinogen (angiosarcoma of the liver), it is flammable from 4-33% in air, and it is the reason the PVC industry of the 1980s had an unusual public health profile. In 1983 world VCM capacity was of the order of 9"];
  "chem.abs.c64-case" [label="The Commodore 64 case, in ABS" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About 350-400 g of ABS: a two-shot moulding, the beige or brown textured skin over the structural frame, with the moulding texture itself - a fine leather grain applied by the tool's EDM process - as the only decoration. Behind the front bezel there is a metal RF shield and a plastic shield can, and"];
  "chem.abs.grade" [label="The ABS grade the C64 case used" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A high-impact, general purpose injection moulding grade in the family of the low-cis, medium-graft commercial ABS sold as Cycolac 2800 or equivalent, with a notched Izod impact strength of 150-250 J/m and a moulding shrinkage of 0.4-0.7%. The UL 94 flammability rating would be HB or 2A: a home compu"];
  "chem.abs.regrind" [label="Regrind" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Runner, sprue and rejected mouldings are chopped and fed back. A 1980s injection moulder typically ran 5-10% regrind in the case and up to 20% in less critical parts, on the grounds that a second heat history costs some molecular weight and some impact strength. Commodore's own case moulding ran reg"];
  "chem.abs.additives" [label="Compounding additives" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Colour and processing aids, dosed into the twin screw before the vacuum vent so that anything volatile is stripped there: 1-3% of a pigment masterbatch, 0.1-0.5% of an external lubricant (a metal stearate, usually zinc or calcium stearate or an EBS wax), and a UV stabiliser. The beige of the C64's c"];
  "chem.abs.additives.stearate" [label="Metal stearate release agent" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Calcium stearate or zinc stearate, 0.1-0.5%, dosed to stop the ABS sticking to the screw and the die, and to help the mould release. The stearates come from stearic acid (from tallow or from a C18 fraction) neutralised with lime; the metal itself is the only heavy metal routinely added to a consumer"];
  "chem.abs.additives.pigment-masterbatch" [label="Pigment masterbatch" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A pre-dispersed concentrate - typically titanium dioxide (rutile, for weather resistance), carbon black, ultramarine blue, phthalocyanine green, or an iron oxide - at 20-60% loading in an ABS or LDPE carrier, dosed at 1-3% into the compounding line. A masterbatch exists because a pigment dropped int"];
  "chem.abs.drying.desiccant" [label="Molecular sieve desiccant" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 3A or 4A zeolite in a heated bed at 200-350 C that adsorbs water to below 0.1% of its own weight, with a purge cycle on a hot air or nitrogen sweep. The same zeolite family as the cracker drier and the FCC catalyst, and in the 1980s this was the standard way every plastic was dried before moulding"];
  "chem.abs.drying.desiccant.molecular-sieve" [label="The 3A molecular sieve itself" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The desiccant in the dryer is a synthetic zeolite with a 3 angstrom pore opening, which admits the water molecule (kinetic diameter about 2.65 angstrom) and excludes anything bigger. That single geometric fact is why a molecular sieve can dry a plastic to 200 ppm: it is a molecular filter, not an ab"];
  "chem.abs.drying" [label="Drying" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="ABS is hygroscopic: its equilibrium moisture content is about 0.3-0.4% by weight, and a wet pellet in a mould produces a part with silver streaks on the surface, voids, and a lower impact strength than the design assumed. The moulding shop therefore dried the pellets at 80-90 C for 2-4 hours in a de"];
  "chem.abs.pelletising" [label="Compounding and pelletising" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The dry blend is melted in a twin-screw extruder at 230-260 C, the volatile xylene and the residual styrene are pulled out under vacuum, and the melt is cut into pellets. This is the operation that turns a powder into a product a moulding machine can eat, and it is where the ABS gets its second heat"];
  "chem.abs.pelletising.static-mixer" [label="Static mixer in the melt line" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A motionless packed element in the transfer pipe or the die adapter that blends the pigment and the regrind into the melt without shear. It matters because the impact property depends on the particle size distribution surviving, and every unnecessary shear element costs you 0.5-1 micron of particle "];
  "chem.abs.pelletising.cutter" [label="Die plate, water bath and rotary cutter" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The melt is extruded through a die plate as a number of strands into a circulating water bath at 20-40 C, solidifies, is pulled by a first calender and chopped by a rotary knife mounted on the extruder die. The alternative is underwater pelletising, where the die is submerged and the pellets are cut"];
  "chem.abs.pelletising.vacuum-vent" [label="Vacuum venting" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A vent at 10-100 mbar with a steam ejector or a liquid ring pump, sized to remove the residual monomer and solvent. The residual styrene specification matters: a high-styrene ABS smells, and the monomer content of a 1980s pellet was typically under 500 ppm."];
  "chem.abs.pelletising.twin-screw" [label="Twin-screw extruder" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Two intermeshing screws 50-90 mm in diameter at 10-30 rpm, length to diameter ratio 30-40:1, with an L/D feed zone, a melting zone, a vacuum vent and a second vent for the devolatilisation. In 1983 the counter-rotating or co-rotating twin screw was the standard compounding machine for ABS because it"];
  "chem.abs.blending" [label="Blending the graft into the matrix" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The graft is mixed into the SAN at 25-50 parts graft per 100 parts SAN for a general purpose grade and up to 50-70 parts for a high-impact grade. That ratio - not the chemistry - is what a plastics buyer specifies: rubber content sets the impact strength, the heat distortion temperature and the pric"];
  "chem.abs.san-matrix" [label="The SAN matrix" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The continuous phase: styrene with 24-32% acrylonitrile, made separately by bulk, emulsion or suspension polymerisation. It has to be made separately because it has to be a continuous phase, not a grafted one. In 1983 a large ABS plant bought its SAN in, or made it on site in a bulk reactor and then"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere" [label="SOVERE ammoxidation process" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A mixture of propylene, ammonia, oxygen and steam is passed at 400-460 C and 15-25 bar (1.5-2.5 MPa) over a bismuth molybdate or antimony-phosphorus-boron oxide catalyst in a fixed-bed or fluidised-bed reactor. The reaction is a partial oxidation plus an ammonia substitution in one step: propylene +"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.distillation" [label="Recovery and purification train" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The reactor effluent is cooled and partly condensed, the unreacted propylene and ammonia are stripped and recycled, the acrylonitrile and acetonitrile are separated by distillation (acrylonitrile boiling 77 C, acetonitrile 81.6 C, which is a genuinely difficult separation and was the reason for the "];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.distillation.stabiliser" [label="Acrylonitrile stabiliser" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A phenolic or amine stabiliser (a small percentage of a phenol such as hydroquinone, or a hindered amine) is added to prevent anionic polymerisation of the monomer in storage. Acrylonitrile has a third-order rate constant for anionic polymerisation that is high enough that a drum of uninhibited mono"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.byproducts" [label="By-products: acetonitrile, hydrogen cyanide, acrylic acid" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Acetonitrile is the biggest by-product at 3-8% of the acrylonitrile and it had a real market - it went into solvent and into acrylonitrile copolymer fibres. Hydrogen cyanide was a small but lethal by-product, and its formation was the reason for the halide promoter. Acrylic acid came from the hydrol"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst" [label="Ammoxidation catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Three families existed. Bismuth molybdate on silica (Bi2Mo3O12 or Bi2(MoO4)3 on silica) was the original and still the industry standard for selectivity in fixed beds. Antimony oxide with phosphorus and boron oxides on a silica support (Sb2O3/P2O5/B2O3) was the workhorse of the fluidised-bed designs"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst.catalyst-manufacture" [label="Making the ammoxidation catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ammoxidation catalyst is a shaped, robust porous solid, and making it is a small ceramics industry. A bismuth molybdate catalyst is prepared by impregnating precipitated bismuth molybdate or by sol-gel methods onto a silica support, then shaping it into 3-5 mm extrudates or spray-dried microsphe"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst.catalyst-manufacture.silica-support.soda-silica" [label="Soda silica from sand" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Quartz sand is fused with sodium carbonate at about 1,300 C to make sodium silicate, which is dissolved in hot water to make the silicate solution, which is acidified to gel. This is the same soda-silica chain that gives the silica sol for the polysilicon ingot's crucibles and the quartz for the fus"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst.catalyst-manufacture.silica-support" [label="Silica gel support" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A porous amorphous silica of 300-700 m2/g surface area and a 6-20 nm pore diameter. The pore diameter matters twice over in this file: in the ammoxidation catalyst a small pore keeps the diene from cracking, and in the FCC catalyst a large pore lets the bulky alkylated intermediates in. The silica g"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst.antimony" [label="Antimony-phosphorus-boron oxide catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The fluidised-bed catalyst: a silica support promoted with phosphorus and boron oxides so that the phosphorus does not volatilise away at 450 C. It was less selective but far more active and far more mechanically robust than the bismuth catalyst, which matters in a fluidised bed."];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.catalyst.bismuth-molybdate" [label="Bismuth molybdate on silica" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The standard catalyst, chosen for its selectivity to acrylonitrile and its resistance to reduction by ammonia. The 1980s development targets were the vanadium- and tungsten-promoted versions and, later in the decade, the bismuth-molybdenum-phosphorus systems aimed at raising the acrylonitrile yield "];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.reactor" [label="Fixed-bed or fluidised-bed ammoxidation reactor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The original SOVERE design was a series of fixed beds with salt bath or oil bath cooling, giving 90-93% conversion and 85-92% selectivity. The 1980s industry had largely moved to fluidised-bed or moving-bed designs, which were easier to heat and to strip of coke, at the cost of a lower selectivity ("];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.reactor.heat-removal" [label="Heat removal from the ammoxidation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ammoxidation gives out roughly 1,000 kJ per kilogram of acrylonitrile, and the reactor has to be taken out at a rate the metallurgy can live with. That is the single hardest engineering problem in the process, and it is why the original SOVERE design was a salt-bath reactor and why the later flu"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.reactor.heat-removal.salt-bath" [label="Molten salt bath reactor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A tubular reactor immersed in a bath of a molten nitrate-nitrite salt (the working point is around 300-330 C), with the bed of catalyst on an inert support inside the tube. It is a beautiful heat-transfer arrangement and a nasty maintenance one: the salt is oxidising, the reactor is at 400-450 C ins"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.sovere.reactor.heat-removal.salt-bath.molten-salt" [label="The molten salt itself" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A mixture of potassium nitrate, sodium nitrite and sometimes calcium nitrate, chosen for a melting point a little below the operating temperature and for a decomposition temperature a good deal above it. It is a beautiful heat-transfer medium and an oxidising one, and a small leak from the reactor p"];
  "chem.abs.san-matrix.acrylonitrile-raw" [label="Acrylonitrile monomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="99.5%+ acrylonitrile with less than 45 ppm of the stabiliser, from a SOVERE ammoxidation plant. Because acrylonitrile is acutely lethal through the skin, it arrived in the ABS plant in tankers and was handled in a closed system with an outside storage tank; the 1980s industry norm was glove boxes an"];
  "chem.abs.grafting" [label="Graft copolymerisation: growing SAN on the rubber" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The heart of the process. 20-40 parts of a low-cis polybutadiene rubber dissolved in xylene or toluene is charged to a reactor, and styrene plus acrylonitrile is added at 60-80 C in the presence of a persulphate initiator, a chelating agent and a mercaptan chain transfer agent. The radicals abstract"];
  "chem.abs.grafting.chain-transfer-agent" [label="Chain transfer agent (modifier)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A mercaptan - diethyl dithiocarbamate, tetraethyl thiuram disulphide, or a dodecyl mercaptan - at 0.1-0.5 parts. It transfers the growing radical to the sulphur and caps the chain, which limits molecular weight and prevents the gelation that would otherwise turn a linear rubber into a useless networ"];
  "chem.abs.grafting.chelant" [label="Chelating agent" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A polycarboxylic acid or its salt - trisodium citrate, ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid disodium salt, or a polyacrylate - at 0.05-0.3 parts. It chelates the trace transition metals that would otherwise decompose the persulphate catalytically and produce a wild, uncontrolled radical rate. In the 1980"];
  "chem.abs.grafting.initiator" [label="Potassium persulphate initiator" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="K2S2O8 or the cheaper sodium persulphate, thermally decomposed to sulphate radical, generating the first polymer radicals at the aqueous droplet surface. Persulphate initiators leave a residue of sulphate groups on the chain ends, which is why an emulsion SBR cannot be re-polymerised as a living pol"];
  "chem.abs.grafting.rubber-phase" [label="The polybutadiene rubber phase" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A low-cis (40-60% cis, therefore glassy rather than rubbery on its own) butyllithium-polymerised polybutadiene at 0.5-2% residual oil, with the whole chain ending in a couple of reactive sites. The low cis content is deliberate: a high-cis rubber dissolves in its own polymer at reactor temperature a"];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation" [label="How butadiene is polymerised" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Three chemistries, chosen for what you were making. Emulsion polymerisation gives cis-1,4, trans-1,4 and vinyl-1,2 structures mixed - a rubber but not a specific one. Anionic polymerisation with lithium butyllithium gives cis-1,4 selectively and controls chain ends. Coordination polymerisation with "];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.neodymium-catalyst" [label="Neodymium-catalysed cis-polybutadiene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The high-cis technology of the 1980s: a neodymium (or lanthanide) compound with an alkylaluminium in a hydrocarbon diluent gives 97-99% cis-1,4, and because the cis content is so high the rubber does not need to be plasticised with oil. Linear low-cis polybutadiene was the growth product of the deca"];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.neodymium-catalyst.neodymium-source" [label="Neodymium from bastnasite" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The high-cis polybutadiene catalyst needs a rare earth metal, and the rare earths come from bastnasite, a light-brown carbonatite ore mined in Mountain Pass, California, in Bayan Obo in China, and in Australia and India, concentrated and then separated by a long solvent-extraction cascade. The separ"];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.neodymium-catalyst.neodymium-source.bastnasite" [label="Bastnasite ore" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A light rare earth carbonate fluorosilicate, about 6-9% rare earth oxides, discovered in 1878 at Bastnas in Sweden and mined in quantity from 1949. It is the last dig-it-out node of the ABS branch's rubber phase: a C64's rubber comes from butadiene from a cracker, but the catalyst that gives that bu"];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.lithium-catalyst" [label="Lithium butyllithium polymerisation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The living anionic process: butyllithium in a hydrocarbon diluent (diesel oil or hexane) at 0-60 C, with the cis content set by solvent and temperature. Living means the chain end stays active, so the polymer can be chain-stopped with an electrophile - water, carbon dioxide, epoxide - and the whole "];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.lithium-catalyst.butyllithium" [label="Butyllithium" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A pyrophoric organolithium, always supplied as a 10-20% solution in hexane and never in bulk. In 1980s practice it was the initiator for the solution polymerisation of polybutadiene and for the SBR made by anionic polymerisation, and its manufacture from lithium metal and butyl chloride or butane wa"];
  "chem.butadiene.polymerisation.structures" [label="The four polybutadiene microstructures" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="cis-1,4 (the elastomer; the chain is flexible below its glass transition), trans-1,4 (the chain is regular and crystalline, so it is a plastic not a rubber), 1,2-vinyl (a pendant vinyl group, also rubbery but with a high glass transition because of the pendant group), and 3,4-vinyl (rare). The rubbe"];
  "chem.abs.grafting.rubber-phase.butadiene-raw" [label="Butadiene monomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="99.0-99.7% butadiene from a cracker C4 extraction unit. The chain is why the C64's rubber phase is a petrochemical and not a natural rubber: the natural rubber alternative is cis-1,4-polyisoprene from latex, and the graft chemistry that works on polybutadiene's pendant vinyl group does not work on i"];
  "chem.styrene" [label="Styrene: benzene plus ethylene, and the plastics of it" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Benzene is alkylated with ethylene to ethylbenzene, and the ethylbenzene is dehydrogenated to styrene. About 12-14 million tonnes of styrene were made in 1983, of which roughly two thirds went to polystyrene and the rest to SAN, ABS and SBR. Styrene is a C64 material in a way that is easy to miss: t"];
  "chem.styrene.divinylbenzene" [label="Divinylbenzene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 40/60 to 80/20 mixture of the two divinylbenzene isomers made by the same ethylbenzene route with a second vinyl group. About 1-2% of the styrene monomer, and essentially all of it goes into ion exchange resins and into the crosslinking monomer of unsaturated polyester. The cation exchange resin i"];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation" [label="Styrene polymerisation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Styrene polymerises readily by free-radical mechanisms because the benzyl radical it makes is resonance stabilised. Almost all commercial polystyrene is made by free-radical polymerisation; the ionic routes that give syndiotactic and isotactic polystyrene existed in the 1980s but were curiosities. S"];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps" [label="General purpose polystyrene, bulk and suspension" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Bulk polymerisation at 140-200 C and 20-40 bar in a stirred or plug-flow reactor with a benzoyl peroxide or a hindered phenol initiator, the classic route for general purpose and crystal grade. Suspension polymerisation, in which styrene is dispersed as 0.1-2 mm beads in water with a suspending agen"];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps.finishing" [label="Finishing: devolatilisation and pelletising" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polystyrene has to be devolatilised after the bulk polymerisation, because the monomer left in the melt is both a smell and a peroxide risk. A 1980s finishing line was a twin-screw extruder at 200-240 C and 5-50 mbar with two vacuum vents, then a strand die into a water bath, then a rotary cutter - "];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps.finishing.pelletiser" [label="Strand die, water bath and cutter" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Extruded through a die with 10-30 holes of 2-3 mm, quenched in a water bath at 20-30 C, drawn by a first calender and cut by a rotary knife. The alternative - underwater pelletising - cuts the strands in the water and was becoming the 1980s choice for polystyrene as it had been for ABS, for the same"];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps.finishing.devolatiliser" [label="The devolatiliser" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A falling-film or wiped-film evaporator with two stages of vacuum, taking the residual styrene from a few per cent down to a few hundred parts per million. The polymer is deliberately kept above its own melting point but below its decomposition point, which for polystyrene is a window of about 40 C "];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps.suspending-agent" [label="Suspending agent for suspension polystyrene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Gelatin, methylcellulose or polyvinyl alcohol, at 0.1-1% of the water phase. It has to hold the bead together without letting the bead coalesce, and the whole engineering of suspension polystyrene is the engineering of that balance: too little and the beads fuse, too much and the bead has a hard ski"];
  "chem.styrene.polymerisation.bulk-gpps.benzoyl-peroxide" [label="Benzoyl peroxide initiator" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Dibenzoyl peroxide, dosed at 0.05-0.5 wt%, thermally decomposed at 60-90 C to give a benzoyloxy radical and then a phenyl radical. It leaves a benzoic acid residue in the polymer and a trace of styrene in the reactor bottoms, which is why polystyrene made with it has a characteristic faint smell and"];
  "chem.styrene.dehydrogenation" [label="Dehydrogenation to styrene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethylbenzene is dehydrogenated to styrene at 600-700 C and 1-3 bar over an iron oxide catalyst (the classic Feeders-Rudd, now UOP ST-110, a shell-and-tube bed of promoted magnetite with potassium and copper promoters). Single-pass conversion is 40-50% and the selectivity to styrene 90-93%. The conve"];
  "chem.styrene.dehydrogenation.fired-furnace" [label="The dehydrogenation furnace" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The dehydrogenation of ethylbenzene is strongly endothermic - about 0.22 GJ of heat per tonne of ethylbenzene - and it is carried out in a bank of shell-and-tube reactors with the heat applied from the outside. A 1980s styrene plant's furnaces were its largest energy consumers and its largest single"];
  "chem.styrene.dehydrogenation.reactor" [label="ST-110 dehydrogenation reactor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A bank of shell-and-tube reactors, 20-50 tubes 4-6 inch in diameter by 20-30 m long, at 1-3 bar, fired on the outside. A 1980s styrene plant had 8-20 such reactors per train and several trains. The furnace is the plant's energy hog, which is why the styrene industry was the largest single consumer o"];
  "chem.styrene.dehydrogenation.iron-catalyst" [label="Promoted iron oxide catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Magnetite or hematite promoted with potassium oxide, copper oxide and sometimes molybdenum or chromium, formed as extruded rings or as a granular bed. Steam is used as a diluent to keep the partial pressure low and to suppress coking, and a trace of a polymerisation inhibitor (sulfolane or a chlorin"];
  "chem.styrene.ethylbenzene" [label="Ethylbenzene from benzene and ethylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Benzene plus ethylene in a large molar excess of benzene (3-6 to 1) over a zeolite or a phosphoric acid catalyst at 250-400 C and 10-30 bar. The 1980s breakthrough was the Mobil process using a shape-selective zeolite (the ZSM-5 family) at about 350 C, which gives over 97% selectivity to ethylbenzen"];
  "chem.styrene.ethylbenzene.zeolite-catalyst" [label="Shape-selective alkylation catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A zeolite whose pore geometry lets a benzene molecule in, lets ethylbenzene out and does not let diethylbenzene in - the same shape selectivity that let ZSM-5 crack gasoline to high-octane and propylene, and that later let MFI zeolites make para-xylene. One catalyst family, three jobs, all in this f"];
  "chem.styrene.benzene" [label="Benzene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The feedstock. In 1983 world benzene production was of the order of 15-17 million tonnes a year, and it came from three sources: catalytic reforming (about half), hydrodealkylation of toluene (roughly a quarter to a third, and the growth share of the 1980s), and coal tar from coke ovens (10-20%, and"];
  "chem.styrene.benzene.coal-tar" [label="Coke oven benzene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Byproduct benzene from the coking of coal in an integral coke plant: the coke oven gas is cooled and the aromatics stripped out, giving a crude benzole with toluene and xylenes. In 1983 coal tar benzene was still a major share of world benzene - more so in Europe and Asia than in the United States -"];
  "chem.styrene.benzene.aromatics-recovery" [label="Aromatics recovery from reformer naphtha" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The C6 to C8 aromatics have to be extracted from a naphtha stream where they are only a few percent by weight. The 1960s and 1980s workhorse was the UOP EdCo process: extractive distillation with sulfolane (a four-plus-four ring sulfone, 218 C boiling point) followed by azeotropic distillation with "];
  "chem.styrene.benzene.aromatics-recovery.sulfolane" [label="Sulfolane" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Tetrahydrothiophene-1,1-dioxide, a polar aprotic solvent that dissolves aromatics preferentially to paraffins and is thermally stable and non-corrosive. It is also the inhibitor chemistry's solvent cousin: sulfolane is used as a polymerisation inhibitor in the styrene dehydrogenation reactors, and i"];
  "chem.styrene.benzene.aromatics-recovery.sulfolane.sulfolane-synthesis" [label="Sulfolane from butadiene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The solvent that extracts benzene out of a naphtha stream is itself made out of the cracker's C4. Butadiene is reacted with sulfur dioxide and water to give tetrahydrothiophene 1,1-dioxide. So the styrene chain - benzene, ethylbenzene, styrene - is fed by butadiene twice over: once as the rubber in "];
  "chem.butadiene" [label="Butadiene and the elastomers of it" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Butadiene is the C4 diene that makes synthetic rubber, and in 1983 about 5-6 million tonnes a year of it was made, roughly 60% from cracker C4 by extractive distillation and the rest by dehydrogenation of butane. It is the molecule behind the rubber in the C64's case feet, the plinths, and the elast"];
  "chem.butadiene.styrene-butadiene-rubber" [label="Styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The largest synthetic rubber by a wide margin: of the order of 6-7 million tonnes a year in 1983, in a handful of grades. It is not a C64 material, but it is the material whose emulsion polymerisation recipe - cold, water, persulphate, rosin acid soap, chain transfer agent - is the same recipe the A"];
  "chem.butadiene.styrene-butadiene-rubber.emulsifier" [label="Rosin acid soap emulsifier" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Sodium or potassium salts of pine rosins (about 60% abietic acid) and of tall oil fatty acids, mixed 50/50. Two jobs: the rosin part stabilises the polymer against oxidation, and the fatty acid part controls the particle size and the rate. Rosin emulsifiers also give the rubber a slightly tacky surf"];
  "chem.butadiene.styrene-butadiene-rubber.emulsifier.fatty-acid-emulsifier" [label="Fatty acid emulsifier and tallow" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About half of a cold SBR or ABS emulsifier system is a fatty acid salt, and the fatty acid came from tallow - the rendered fat of cattle and sheep - split into oleic and stearic acid and neutralised with sodium or potassium hydroxide. So part of the emulsifier that keeps the rubber particles apart i"];
  "chem.butadiene.styrene-butadiene-rubber.emulsifier.fatty-acid-emulsifier.tallow" [label="Tallow" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Rendered animal fat, roughly 50% palmitic, stearic and oleic acid. Historically a by-product of the meat industry and increasingly, through the 1980s, a rendering industry of its own. Its oleic acid was the emulsifier and its stearic acid was the release agent, and both ended up in the polymer. The "];
  "chem.butadiene.sources" [label="Butadiene sources" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Two industrial routes and a third that was still just about alive in 1983: extractive distillation of the steam cracker's C4 stream, dehydrogenation of n-butane, and thermal dimerisation of acetylene (the old Reppe route, dead by 1960). The choice between them is a function of whether a cracker was "];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.houdry" [label="Houdry butane dehydrogenation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="n-Butane dehydrogenated endothermically at 480-550 C over platinum on alumina or chromia at 1-5 bar to butenes, which are then extracted and the butadiene purified separately. It was the on-purpose route, it made isobutylene for MTBE as a co-product, and it suffered from the thermodynamics of an end"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.houdry.dehydrogenation-furnace" [label="The butane dehydrogenation furnace" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Dehydrogenation of butane to butenes is endothermic, so the reactor is a bank of fired tubes - cast iron or cast alloy, several hundred tubes, with the furnace gas at 500-700 C. The catalyst inside is platinum on alumina or chromia, and the giveaway of a fouled furnace is the pressure drop across th"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.sounsby" [label="Sounsby extractive distillation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The standard butadiene recovery unit from 1962 onward: an extractive distillation with a selective polar solvent that dissolves butadiene and the butenes but not the saturated C4s, followed by a second stripper to recover the pure diene. A 1980s unit processing 100,000-400,000 tonnes a year of C4 st"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.sounsby.butadiene-purity" [label="99%+ butadiene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The polymerisation-grade monomer has to be 99.0-99.7% with less than 100 ppm of inhibitor residue, less than 100 ppm water and less than 50 ppm carbonyls. The spec is enforced because the polymerisation catalysts of the era - both the persulfate emulsion ones and the lithium butyllithium ones - were"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.sounsby.nmp" [label="N-methylpyrrolidone as the extractive solvent" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="NMP (a strongly polar, high-boiling amide, 202 C) replaced furfural because it does not polymerise and needs less make-up. NMP was the 1980s solvent of choice and it is the same molecule that later arrived in the electronics plant as the chlorinated-solvent replacement for 1,1,1-trichloroethane. NMP"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.sounsby.furfural" [label="Furfural as the extractive solvent" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Furfural (furan-2-carbaldehyde) made from furfural-bark or from pentoses in bagasse hemihydrate pulping, with a boiling point of 161 C and a high selectivity for diene over alpha-olefin. It was the original Sounsby solvent and its dominant drawback is that it polymerises in the presence of acid, so "];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.cracker-c4" [label="Cracker C4 stream" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A NGL stream of 1-butene (15-25%), cis-2-butene (10-15%), trans-2-butene (30-35%), isobutylene (15-25%) and isobutane/isopentane, with butadiene at 20-30% of the C4 - which is 4-6% of the cracker's total hydrocarbon output. The butadiene has to be taken out by a separate, very large unit."];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.cracker-c4.c4-recovery" [label="C4 recovery and the depentaniser" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Before the butadiene extractor there is the C4 recovery section: a depentaniser that takes the C5+ out of the whole cracker liquid so the C4 unit does not have to handle pentanes and heavier, and then a C3/C4 splitter that separates propane from the butanes. Both columns are ordinary fractionations,"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.cracker-c4.c4-recovery.c3-c4-splitter" [label="The C3/C4 splitter" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 60-80 tray column separating propane overhead from the butanes. It is a good example of a column that has nothing to do with the product of interest: the butadiene extractor's feed comes off the bottom of this splitter, and the splitter's job is only to keep the propane out of the extractor so the"];
  "chem.butadiene.sources.cracker-c4.c4-recovery.depentaniser" [label="The depentaniser" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 40-60 tray column operating at 25-40 bar, taking the C5 cut point at about 50-60 C. Its overhead is the C4 stream that goes to the extractor; its bottoms are the C5+ gasoline fraction that goes to the aromatics recovery or to the gasoline pool. It is the least glamorous column in the plant and the"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile" [label="Acrylonitrile and the ammoxidation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The SOVERE process - SOx-free reductive ammoxidation - oxidises propylene with ammonia and air in one step to acrylonitrile. It was invented at Standard Oil of Ohio and licensed worldwide from 1960-1965, and by 1983 essentially all the world's roughly 1.2-1.5 million tonnes a year of acrylonitrile w"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.downstream" [label="Downstream acrylonitrile" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="What the acrylonitrile was for in 1983: acrylic fibre (Orlon, Dralon, and in Japan the acrylics), nitrile rubber (butadiene copolymer), SAN and ABS copolymer, and the polyacrylonitrile precursor for carbon fibre. More than half the acrylonitrile made in the early 1980s went into acrylic fibre, and a"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.downstream.methyl-methacrylate" [label="Methyl methacrylate (acetone cyanohydrin route)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A separate molecule with the same nitrile: acetone plus hydrogen chloride gives acetone cyanohydrin, which is esterified with methanol and then dehydrated with sulphuric acid to methyl methacrylate. The acetone cyanohydrin route was introduced from 1938 and became dominant in the 1950s-60s. The hydr"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.downstream.methyl-methacrylate.acetone-cyanohydrin" [label="Acetone cyanohydrin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A compound that made the C64 possible twice over: it is the intermediate for methyl methacrylate, and the HCN it carries is the feedstock for methyl methacrylate and for the older butadiene-adiponitrile route to nylon 6,6. It is acutely toxic and it has killed people by being absorbed through the sk"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.downstream.acrylic-acid" [label="Acrylic acid" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by hydrolysis of acrylonitrile in an 85-93% sulphuric acid solution at 90-120 C in a batch or continuous reactor in the 1980s (the later propane-oxidation route came later and took over). The acrylonitrile had to be purified first because the hydrolysis is irreversible and the by-product ammoni"];
  "chem.acrylonitrile.downstream.acrylic-acid.ammonium-sulphate" [label="Ammonium sulphate by-product" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="One tonne of acrylic acid from acrylonitrile hydrolysis throws out about one tonne of ammonium sulphate, which is a low-grade fertiliser that is expensive to dry and transport. It is the classic penalty of the hydrolysis route and the reason the later oxidation route won."];
  "chem.propylene" [label="Propylene and its derivatives" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Propylene is the C3 that the cracker makes whether you wanted it or not, and for two decades the propylene splitter process existed purely to turn that unwanted C3 into more of it. By 1983 most propylene came from the cracker and from FCC, and it went into polypropylene, propylene oxide, cumene for "];
  "chem.propylene.cumene" [label="Cumene route to phenol and acetone" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Benzene plus propylene gives cumene; cumene plus oxygen gives cumene hydroperoxide; the hydroperoxide is cleaved with acid to give phenol and acetone. About a million tonnes a year of phenol in 1983 came out of this route, and phenol went to bisphenol A (and therefore to the C64's epoxy laminate res"];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.cleavage" [label="Acid cleavage to phenol and acetone" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The cumene hydroperoxide is cleaved with sulphuric acid (or with a zeolite in later plants) at 50-80 C, giving phenol and acetone with 85-95% selectivity. The 1980s safety concern was the rearrangement of the cumene hydroperoxide to the phenol and acetone, which is violently exothermic: a small hold"];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.cleavage.acetone" [label="Acetone" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The co-product of the cumene process and, from 1960s, a major product in its own right from the acetone cyanohydrin process. Acetone was the electronics plant's solvent of choice for degreasing and photoresist stripping in 1980s practice, and it is also the feedstock for methyl methacrylate and for "];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.cleavage.phenol" [label="Phenol" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The molecule that goes into bisphenol A for epoxy, into phenol-formaldehyde for the phenolic plastics of switches and sockets, and into caprolactam for nylon 6. 1983 world phenol production was of the order of 3.5-4 million tonnes a year, essentially all of it from cumene."];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.oxidation" [label="Cumene oxidation to the hydroperoxide" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Cumene with air at 120-140 C, either with a hydroperoxide initiator or with a cobalt or manganese catalyst, gives cumene hydroperoxide at 20-40 wt%. This is a liquid-phase autoxidation, and it has the classic autoxidation hazard: the reaction is exothermic, it runs away above 150 C, and the product "];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.alkylation" [label="Cumene alkylation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Benzene with excess benzene over phosphoric acid on silica at 140-180 C and 20-30 bar in an adiabatic bed, or over a solid acid. The large excess of benzene (3-5 mol per mol of propylene) is deliberate: it suppresses the diethylbenzene and triisopropylbenzene by-products and stops the reactor polyme"];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.alkylation.phosphoric-acid-catalyst" [label="Phosphoric acid on silica" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The classic cumene alkylation catalyst is orthophosphoric acid on a silica carrier: the acid is absorbed into the silica's pore structure and the pore structure holds it there, and the catalyst's whole life is the slow loss of that acid to the reactor walls. The 1980s industry answer was a solid aci"];
  "chem.propylene.cumene.alkylation.phosphoric-acid-catalyst.silica-carrier" [label="Silica carrier (the same silica as the cracker drier)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A silica gel of 200-400 m2/g surface area, and a strong family resemblance to the 3A molecular sieve's carrier and to the FCC catalyst's matrix and to the ammoxidation catalyst's support. One amorphous silica, made from sand by the soda-silica route, appears in this file as a support four different "];
  "chem.propylene.flame-retardants" [label="Chlorinated flame retardants from propylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polymers and elastomers for electronics and furniture were made to burn slowly by chlorinating a paraffinic or a phosphate ester. Chlorinated paraffins, and the phosphorus esters derived from propylene oxide and phosphorus oxychloride, went into the flame-retardant grades of ABS, polypropylene and p"];
  "chem.propylene.flame-retardants.tdcpp" [label="Tris(2,3-dichloropropyl) phosphate" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The organophosphate flame retardant made from propylene oxide and phosphorus oxychloride. It is an effective flame retardant for ABS, rigid polyurethane foam and polyester resins, and its precursor, tris(2,3-dibromopropyl) phosphate, is the classic story of a flame retardant that was banned in the U"];
  "chem.propylene.flame-retardants.chlorinated-paraffin" [label="Chlorinated paraffin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Paraffins chlorinated to 40-70% chlorine, used as a secondary flame retardant and, in the 1970s, as a plasticiser for PVC. It is a cheap and effective flame retardant and a persistent environmental contaminant, which is why the 1980s trend in electronics housings was towards phosphorus systems and t"];
  "chem.propylene.propylene-oxide" [label="Propylene oxide" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Propylene epoxidised, then made into propylene glycol, polyether polyols for polyurethane foam, and propylene chlorohydrin. World production was of the order of 0.7-0.9 million tonnes in 1983. Because the epoxidation of propylene was historically done with hypochlorous acid, a chlorinated by-product"];
  "chem.propylene.propylene-oxide.hydrogen-peroxide" [label="Hydrogen peroxide epoxidation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Propylene plus hydrogen peroxide over a titanium-on-silica catalyst at 40-80 C and 1-10 bar, with the propylene chlorohydrin recycled as the phase-transfer catalyst. It makes no chloride, it is nearly quantitative, and it depends on a cheap supply of hydrogen peroxide - which in 1983 was the reason "];
  "chem.propylene.propylene-oxide.chlorohydrin" [label="Chlorohydrin route" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Propylene plus hypochlorous acid gives 1-chloro-2-propanol (about 90%) and 2-chloro-1-propanol (about 10%); the mixture is dehydrochlorinated with a lime or caustic slurry to propylene oxide and the chlorides are recycled as chlorine. The 10% isomer is the problem: it goes on to make propylene chlor"];
  "chem.propylene.propylene-oxide.chlorohydrin.dichloropropane" [label="1,2-dichloropropane" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The unwanted isomer stream, made when propylene chlorohydrin is treated with excess caustic. It is a solvent and a flame-retardant intermediate, and it is toxic to the liver and kidney. In the 1980s the industry reduced its production by selling propylene oxide as a propylene glycol monomethyl ether"];
  "chem.propylene.propylene-oxide.chlorohydrin.propanol" [label="1-chloro-2-propanol" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The intermediate whose dehydrochlorination is propylene oxide. It is mildly toxic, it is the reason propylene oxide workers were exposed to a chlorinated alcohol, and it is the direct chemical relative of propylene chlorohydrin, the skin sensitiser that caused occupational dermatitis in the plastics"];
  "chem.propylene.polymerisation" [label="Polypropylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About 7 million tonnes of polypropylene in 1983, the fastest-growing major plastic. It needs a stereospecific catalyst to be anything but glue: isotactic polypropylene is crystalline and rigid, atactic is a sticky amorphous gum, syndiotactic is a curiosity in the 1980s. The 1980s advances were the h"];
  "chem.propylene.polymerisation.loops-and-slurry" [label="Slurry loop, gas phase and slurry reactor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The 1980s reactor competition: the slurry loop (heptane diluent, polymer as a slurry, then solvent recovery and devolatilising), the loop slurry with 30-50% solids, and the gas-phase fluidised bed with no solvent at all. The gas-phase process was the 1980s newcomer and its whole commercial case was "];
  "chem.propylene.polymerisation.stereospecific-catalyst" [label="Stereospecific titanium catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Titanium trichloride or titanium tetrachloride on magnesium chloride with an aluminium alkyl, refined in the 1970s and 1980s to give activities high enough that no catalyst removal was needed. The MgCl2-supported catalyst with an internal donor (a phthalate or a diether) was the technical developmen"];
  "chem.propylene.separation" [label="Propylene purification" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The propylene has to be at 99.5-99.9% with less than 10-20 ppm of carbonyl compounds, because propanal and acetone stop an ionic polymerisation dead. In 1980s practice the purified propylene was made by distillation plus an alkaline wash, and by the later 1980s by hydrazine or hydrazone treatment wh"];
  "chem.propylene.sources" [label="Where propylene comes from" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Three sources, in order of size in 1983: the steam cracker (roughly 40-45% of world propylene supply), catalytic cracking (roughly 30-35%), and dedicated processes. A naphtha cracker makes propylene at about 15% of its hydrocarbon product, so a cracker and a propylene splitter were bolted together i"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.depolymerisation" [label="Depolymerisation of polypropylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Reheated polypropylene was fed back to a reactor to recover the propylene it was made from. This was developed as a recycling route in the early 1970s when the word recycle meant something and it survived as a niche process for decades, mostly because the chemical plant could not discharge a plastic"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.propylene-splitter" [label="Propylene splitter (the older dedicated unit)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A dedicated thermal cracker that re-cracked propane and the cracker's propylene-rich C3 stream at 500-600 C to make more propylene, at 15-25% selectivity, with a big furnace and a bad energy balance. It was the standard propylene route of the 1960s, it was still running in 1983 in a few places, and "];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3" [label="Cracker C3 stream" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The propylene and propane that distil out of the cracker's quench separator. Naphtha crackers make roughly 0.15 t of propylene per tonne of feed, which on a 500,000 t/yr cracker is about 75,000 t/yr of propylene - more than a small polypropylene plant needs."];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery" [label="C3 recovery and the propylene column" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="How the propylene actually leaves the cracker. The C3 and C4 streams are separated in a deethaniser, then the propane and propylene go to a propylene splitter column where the propylene is taken overhead. Before that column, the stream is caustic washed to remove the carbonyls and the oxygenates; in"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery.propylene-column" [label="The propylene splitter column" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 50-70 tray column 50-70 m tall that separates the propylene overhead from the propane that has to be recycled back to the furnace. Propylene and propane have a relative volatility of about 1.1, so this is a distillation with a reflux ratio of 15-25 and a lot of energy; the 1980s development was a "];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery.caustic-wash.chlor-alkali" [label="Chlor-alkali (brine electrolysis)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Salt water electrolyzed in a membrane or mercury cell at 60-90 C and 3-4 V DC to give chlorine, sodium hydroxide and hydrogen. It is the source of every chlorine molecule in this file - the PVC monomer, the epichlorohydrin, the chlorinated solvents, the hypochlorous acid of the propylene chlorohydri"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery.caustic-wash.chlor-alkali.brine" [label="Brine (salt)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A saturated sodium chloride solution, made from a salt mine or a solar evaporation pond, purified to remove calcium and magnesium because they foul the membrane and foul the diaphragm. Salt is the one raw material in this branch that is dug out of the ground rather than taken from a refinery, and it"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery.caustic-wash.chlor-alkali.membrane-cell" [label="Membrane cell" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The cell type that replaced the mercury cell through the 1980s: a perfluorosulfonate membrane separating the anode and cathode, giving chlorine at 99.5%+ without the mercury the older cell had to manage. The conversion of the world's chlor-alkali from mercury to membrane was one of the largest and f"];
  "chem.propylene.sources.cracker-c3.c3-recovery.caustic-wash" [label="Caustic wash" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 10-20% sodium hydroxide wash column that neutralises the acidic oxygenates - propanal, acetone, propionic acid, and the phenol and cresols from the C9+ - that the cracker makes. The spent caustic is a refinery effluent problem: it is a phenolic wastewater with a high chemical oxygen demand, and in"];
  "chem.ethylene" [label="Ethylene: steam cracking and the polymers of it" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Steam cracking naphtha at 800-900 C over cast alloy coils is the largest single chemical reaction in the world by tonnage. World ethylene capacity in 1983 was about 27-30 million tonnes a year, and roughly half of it went into polyethylene and the rest into ethylene oxide, styrene and solvents. Ever"];
  "chem.ethylene.uses-c64" [label="Where the C64's ethylene ends up" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Concretely: the styrene monomer for the ABS case and the SAN matrix, the ethylene oxide route to ethylene glycol for anything polyester in the power supply, the polyethylene in the power cord's insulation and in the moulded cable accessories, and the C2 in the vinyl chloride monomer for the PVC jack"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene" [label="Polyethylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The largest use of ethylene: about 12-14 million tonnes of polyethylene were made in 1983, split roughly between low-density (free radical, high pressure) and high-density (coordination catalysis, low pressure). The C64-scale uses are the power cord's insulation and jacket, the moulded cable accesso"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.additives" [label="Polyethylene additives" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Antioxidants (hindered phenols such as BHT, and phosphites), stabilisers, and pigment. The antioxidant is the reason a polyethylene cable in a C64's power supply does not go brittle: a radical from the mains or from a metal ion attacks the chain and causes scission or cross-linking, and a phenol tra"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.additives.bht" [label="BHT (2,6-di-tert-butyl-4-methylphenol)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse antioxidant, dosed at 0.05-0.2 wt%. It is made by alkylating para-cresol with isobutylene over a solid acid or an aluminium chloride catalyst, and it was also in the C64's power supply as a capacitor-filling fluid (as the antioxidant of the impregnant)."];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.hdpe" [label="HDPE: coordination catalysis at low pressure" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethylene at 20-60 bar and 150-260 C over a transition-metal catalyst gives linear polyethylene with almost no long-chain branching, so it crystallises tightly and has a density of 0.94-0.97. Because the polymer comes out of the reactor as a powder or a slurry rather than a melt, HDPE in 1983 was mad"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.sllpe" [label="Linear low density polyethylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Copolymers of ethylene with a small amount of alpha-olefin - 1-butene or 1-octene - which gives a linear chain with short branches: low density with high stiffness. LLDPE was the fastest-growing polyethylene family of the 1980s (about 10-15% of PE production by 1983 and climbing), and the switch fro"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.hdpe.ziegler-natta" [label="Ziegler-Natta polymerisation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Titanium tetrachloride or titanium dichloride with an aluminium alkyl (triethylaluminium or diethylaluminium chloride) at 50-100 bar and 50-80 C in a hydrocarbon diluent. Ziegler was the 1953 discovery that started the whole low-pressure polyethylene industry; by the 1960s it had become Ziegler-Natt"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.hdpe.ziegler-natta.aluminium-alkyl" [label="Triethylaluminium" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The cocatalyst. It alkylates the titanium and reduces Ti(IV) to Ti(III), and it scavenges the water and oxygen that would kill the catalyst. It is pyrophoric, and the 1980s industry response to that fact was the standard practice of diluting it to 5-10% in a hydrocarbon before it ever left the cylin"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.hdpe.ziegler-natta.titanium-catalyst" [label="Titanium-magnesium chloride catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="TiCl4 supported on finely divided MgCl2, activated with an aluminium alkyl. The MgCl2 support is the whole trick: it generates about a hundred active titanium sites per gram of catalyst with a range of stereospecificity, and that is what turned HDPE from a family of blends into a graded set of grade"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.hdpe.ziegler-natta.titanium-catalyst.tetrachloride" [label="Titanium tetrachloride and the sponge route" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The titanium tetrachloride of the Ziegler-Natta catalyst is made from titanium ore - rutile or ilmenite - by chlorinating it with coke and chlorine at 900-1,000 C, then distilling the TiCl4 out at 136 C. Titanium metal comes back by reducing the tetrachloride with magnesium (the Hunter process, whic"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.ldpe" [label="LDPE: free-radical high-pressure process" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethylene is polymerised at 200-300 C and 1,000-2,000 bar in a jacketed or tubular reactor with a peroxide initiator, in the liquid phase (below about 280 C) or the gas phase (above it). The density of 0.91-0.93 comes entirely from chain branching: the very high pressure creates radicals on the growi"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.ldpe.autoclave" [label="Autoclave versus tubular reactor" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The autoclave process (Union Carbide, 1930s) stirred the gas-phase reaction; the tubular process (Sons, 1950s) had plug flow with injection quills and much better temperature control. By the 1980s the tubular reactor was the standard large unit because it gave a narrower molecular weight distributio"];
  "chem.ethylene.polyethylene.ldpe.initiator" [label="Peroxide initiator" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Di-tert-butyl peroxide, or in the autoclave process a mixture of di-tert-butyl peroxide with di-tert-butyl percarbonate. The initiator decomposes at temperature to give the two tert-butoxy radicals that start the chain, and the choice of half-life at operating temperature sets the run length."];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-glycol" [label="Ethylene glycol and the glycols" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Monoethylene glycol, diethylene glycol and triethylene glycol, made by hydration of ethylene oxide in a 1:1 to 1:20 mole ratio of oxide to water, with the ratio chosen to steer the product distribution towards the wanted glycol. In 1980s practice monoethylene glycol was about half the output and wen"];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-glycol.vacuum-distillation" [label="Glycol finishing by vacuum distillation" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Water is removed in a multi-column vacuum system because the glycols boil at 197, 245 and 285 C and water outranks them everywhere; a set of vacuum columns at 30-100 mbar separates mono-, di- and triethylene glycol and recovers the unreacted oxide. This is one of the clearest cases in the whole tree"];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-oxide" [label="Ethylene oxide" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The oxidation of ethylene over silver at 200-300 C and 20-30 bar, in which every eighth or tenth ethylene molecule is burned to carbon dioxide and water instead of being oxidised. It was about 1.5-2.5 million tonnes a year in 1983, most of it going to ethylene glycol for polyester and to surfactants"];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-oxide.safety" [label="Ethylene oxide safety" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Ethylene oxide boils at 10.7 C, so it is a liquid only just below ambient and a gas in a warm tank; its flammable range in air is 3-80% by volume and its ignition energy is about 0.06 millijoule. It is acutely toxic, it polymerises without catalyst into a runaway, and it is kept in equilibrium by a "];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-oxide.air-oxidation" [label="Air-oxidation process (the industrial one)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethylene and air over silver on low-surface-area alpha-alumina in a multi-tubular reactor at 200-300 C and 20-30 bar. Per-pass conversion is only 10-15% and the selectivity to ethylene oxide is 80-90%, so the unreacted ethylene is recycled with the inert diluent. The alternative process - the chloro"];
  "chem.ethylene.ethylene-oxide.air-oxidation.silver-catalyst" [label="Silver on alpha-alumina catalyst" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="10-20% silver on low-surface-area alpha-alumina, promoted by chlorine. Silver is the only catalyst that works: it is the only metal that both activates oxygen and lets ethylene through, and the geometry matters as much as the chemistry, because the low surface area keeps the pore diameter small enou"];
  "chem.ethylene.purification" [label="Ethylene purification" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Before the ethylene can be polymerised it has to be dry and essentially free of oxygen and carbon dioxide, because both water and oxygen poison a radical or coordination polymerisation catalyst irreversibly. A cracker therefore had a molecular sieve drier taking water to under 1 ppm and a CO2/CO rem"];
  "chem.ethylene.quench" [label="Quenching and acetylene control" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The cracked gas leaves the coil at 800-900 C and has to come down to under 40 C in a second or two before anything can be separated, or the reactor metallurgy melts. The choice of quench is a real fork in the road: a gas quench to 350-400 C followed by selective hydrogenation, or a direct spray quen"];
  "chem.ethylene.quench.decanter" [label="Decanter and quench oil circuit" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The condensed liquid is settled and decanted, separating the heavy C9+ tar from the gasoline and C4 streams. The heavy fraction - about 5-10% of the cracker feed - was the benzene, toluene and xylenes rich C9+ cut in the 1980s, and it was the aromatics feed for the styrene plants and the solvent pla"];
  "chem.ethylene.quench.gas-quench" [label="Gas quench to 350-400 C" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The cracked gas is quenched with recycled process gas rather than directly with liquid, stopping at 350-400 C. This keeps the gas hot enough that the acetylene survives, so the acetylene can be destroyed deliberately afterwards in a selective hydrogenation reactor (palladium on alumina at 130-180 C,"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking" [label="Steam cracking" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A hydrocarbon feed mixed with steam is brought to 800-900 C in a few tenths of a second in a serpentine coil inside a radiant furnace, at 1-3 bar. The chemistry is brutal free-radical chain cracking: the large molecules fall apart into smaller ones and a large hydrogen-rich tail gas. What decides th"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.expander" [label="Cracked-gas expander turbine" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The cracked gas at 800-900 C is not simply cooled; in the 1980s it was let down through a turboexpander that recovered most of the sensible heat as work, doing the job of the quench exchanger. The expander recovered 10-20% of the cracking energy and was one of the reasons the energy crisis of the 19"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.decoking" [label="Coke and decoking" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Coke builds up on the coil, which insulates it, raises the skin temperature and finally blocks the tube. 1980s practice was to shut the furnace and decoke in place by burning the coke out with steam and air (off-line decoking), taking several hours and costing several thousand dollars a cycle; a shi"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.decoking.coke" [label="Cracker coke" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A granular, high-carbon, low-hydrogen solid with vanadium and nickel that came out of the feed. It is a nuisance rather than a product in the 1980s - the FCC coke market was far bigger and the cracker coke was dirty - and a large cracker made a few hundred to a few thousand tonnes a year of it."];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.furnace.epoxy-coating" [label="Ceramic (CHA) coil coating" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Cracking alumina or silicon carbide coated onto the coil inside the furnace. The industry spent the 1970s and early 1980s trying to reduce coil metal temperature by 50-100 C; silicon carbide coatings (the CHA process, from Combustion Heat Exchanger) were the commercially successful route and let a f"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.furnace.coiled-coil" [label="Cracker coil metallurgy" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The coil is cast alloy, not steel: 25Cr-35Ni (Incoloy 800, 800H, 802) or the HP-modified 35Cr-45Ni alloys of the 1980s. The radiant fire gas is oxidising and its metal skin runs 30-50 C above the process temperature, so at 850 C process the skin is 880-900 C; a carbon steel coil crept and failed. In"];
  "chem.ethylene.steam-cracking.furnace" [label="Cracker furnace and radiant section" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A refractory-lined box 20-40 m long with 40-80 coiled coils hung side by side, each coil 30-80 m of 4 in (100 mm) cast tube in ten passes of 10-20 m. The fire is gas- or oil-fired at 30-50 MW per furnace, and the radiant duty is the whole point: roughly 70% of the process heat has to arrive by radia"];
  "chem.ethylene.feedstock" [label="Cracker feedstock" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Naphtha was 70-80% of world cracker capacity in 1983, ethane and propane 20-30%. The choice is regional and historical: US Gulf crackers run on ethane piped from the Gulf's gas fields because the ethane price under a gas contract made it unbeatable; European and Japanese crackers run on naphtha beca"];
  "chem.ethylene.feedstock.gas-oil" [label="Gas oil feedstock (the third choice)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The heavier option, chosen when naphtha and gas oil were both dear: a 300-700 F cut cracked harder to get ethylene, coking heavily, and yielding the most propylene of the three. Gas oil cracking existed in the Middle East and in Japan through the 1970s and mostly disappeared in the 1980s because its"];
  "chem.ethylene.feedstock.ethane" [label="Ethane feedstock" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Ethane cracks cleanly with little coke and needs almost no steam dilution, so an ethane cracker is cheaper to run and its ethylene yield (50-55%) is much higher. But it makes almost no propylene and no butadiene, so a pure ethane cracker has to buy its co-products. The 1980s US Gulf argument about e"];
  "chem.natural-gas.composition.ethane-propane" [label="Ethane and propane (LPG)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The C2 and C3 that make natural gas liquids, recovered as LPG at 1.5-8 bar in the field and as a debottlenecking liquid at the gas plant, then split for heating or fed to a cracker. LPG was the preferred cracker feedstock precisely because it is already light and needs no naphtha handling. A dedicat"];
  "chem.ethylene.feedstock.naphtha" [label="Naphtha feedstock" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A C5 to 200-220 F hydrocarbon cut, hydrotreated to under 0.5 wt% sulphur and under 0.5 ppmw nitrogen. Beyond its hydrocarbon content what matters is the coniocarbon and the asphaltene content: those are what coke the coil. The naphtha from an Arabian light crude is comparatively clean, which is one "];
  "chem.ethylene.scale" [label="The ethylene industry in 1983" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About 27-30 Mt/yr of world ethylene capacity in 1983, of which the United States held roughly 19 Mt (about two thirds) and Western Europe about 8 Mt, with the Gulf Coast of the US, the Rhine-Ruhr, Antwerp, the Suez Gulf and the Japanese and Korean coasts as the clusters. A 1980s cracker cost $150-40"];
  "chem.refining" [label="Refining: turning crude into fuels and feedstocks" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Atmospheric and vacuum distillation to cut crude into fractions, then conversion and upgrading processes to make the specific products industry wants. For the C64 the interesting products are not the fuels but the naphtha that gets steam-cracked to ethylene, the benzene for styrene, the LPG for crac"];
  "chem.refining.polymer-feedstocks" [label="Refinery to polymer: the handover" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Three handovers matter. Naphtha plus steam into a cracker gives ethylene and propylene. C4 from the same cracker gives butadiene and isobutylene. Reformer naphtha into the aromatics complex gives benzene, which with ethylene gives styrene. This node is the place where the fuel industry stops and the"];
  "chem.refining.polymer-feedstocks.naphtha-to-ethylene" [label="Naphtha to ethylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The handover. Naphtha plus steam at 800-900 C over a cast alloy coil, giving 25-35% ethylene by weight with propylene, butenes and a benzene-rich C9+ residue. Roughly 70-80% of world cracker capacity in 1983 was naphtha-based, with the rest ethane and propane; the Gulf Coast and the Far East were na"];
  "chem.refining.products" [label="Refinery products and by-products" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Fuels, lubricants, waxes, coke and sulphur. For this tree the interesting list is short: naphtha to the crackers, benzene to styrene, LPG to crackers, propylene and butylene to the older chemical plants, and sulphur to rubber and to sulphuric acid."];
  "chem.refining.products.fuel-oil" [label="Fuel oil" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The bottom of the barrel: a 1-3% sulphur residue sold to power stations and ships. After 1973, and especially after the 1980s tanker accidents, the political life of fuel oil in Europe and the US got hard, and the refinery's answer was more conversion. In 1983 fuel oil still took about 25-30% of wor"];
  "chem.refining.products.waxes" [label="Petroleum waxes and slack wax" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Waxy distillate from solvent deasphalting, deoiled on a centrifuge train to give hard, medium and soft paraffin wax and microcrystalline wax. Slack wax goes into paper, board and packaging film; the microcrystalline waxes are the petroleum fraction that was blended into the first generation of hot-m"];
  "chem.refining.products.lubricants" [label="Lubricant base oils" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Distillate fractions solvent-extracted (2,700-5,000 Ns at 100 C) and hydrofinished, or in the 1980s increasingly hydroisomerised. The C64 link is not the lubricant in the gearbox but the fact that the same hydrofinishing catalysts and the same additive chemistry (zinc dialkyldithiophosphate, made fr"];
  "chem.refining.octane" [label="Lead phase-down and the unleaded problem" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Tetraethyl lead was phased out of most industrialised markets between 1978 and 1985 as catalytic converters spread. The lead phase-down is the single most consequential fuel specification change of the decade and it is the reason MTBE, alkylate, isomerate and the ZSM-5 additive were all developed on"];
  "chem.refining.octane.tetraethyl-lead" [label="Tetraethyl lead" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Pb(C2H5)4 blended at a few grams per litre, discovered in 1921 and the cheapest octane technology for fifty years. It was the cheapest route to octane and the most expensive in externalities: it destroyed the lead and phosphorus catalysts that were the whole point of the catalytic converter. Teledyn"];
  "chem.refining.isomerisation" [label="Isomerisation and octane boosting" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The C5 and C6 straight-chain paraffins in naphtha have terrible octane numbers; isomerising them to the branched isomers does something about that with almost no yield loss. This is the cheap half of the unleaded-gasoline story; MTBE and alkylate were the expensive half. C5/C6 isomerisation over pla"];
  "chem.refining.isomerisation.mtbe" [label="MTBE (methyl tert-butyl ether)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Methanol plus isobutylene over an acidic ion-exchange resin (Amberlyst, sulphonic acid polystyrene, about 100 C, 5-10 bar), giving a 100+ octane component. MTBE was the big gasoline technology story of the late 1970s and early 1980s in the United States, and its feedstock - isobutylene - came from t"];
  "chem.refining.isomerisation.mtbe.ion-exchange-resin" [label="Ion-exchange resin catalyst" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A macroporous or gel-type sulphonic-acid polystyrene resin, the same chemistry as the ion exchange resins used in water treatment. MTBE resins had to be replaced every 10,000-20,000 hours in the 1980s as they aged."];
  "chem.refining.catalytic-reforming" [label="Catalytic reforming and the aromatics complex" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Reforming naphtha over platinum at 500-540 C and 15-30 bar converts paraffins and naphthenes into aromatics, releasing about 0.2-0.3 tonnes of hydrogen per tonne of feed and upgrading the octane number. It is where the world's benzene comes from, and therefore where the styrene in the C64's ABS come"];
  "chem.refining.catalytic-reforming.platinum-catalyst" [label="Platinum-rhenium on chlorided alumina" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Platinum crystallites of about 1 nm on gamma-alumina promoted with chlorine to keep the metal and the acid function in balance. Platinum is one of the most expensive catalysts in the plant, so catalyst regeneration and platinum recovery are real economics: the platinum in a 1980s reformer was worth "];
  "chem.refining.claus" [label="Claus process: recovering elemental sulphur" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Three-quarters of a mole of oxygen is used to burn a third of the hydrogen sulphide to sulphur dioxide, then the two react over an alumina catalyst to make sulphur and water: 2 H2S + SO2 -\> 3 S + 2 H2O. The Claus unit turns the refinery's or gas plant's worst stream into a saleable commodity, and it"];
  "chem.refining.claus.catalyst" [label="Claus catalyst (activated alumina)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="High-surface-area alumina, sometimes promoted with titanium, in two or three fixed beds at 200-350 C with interstage reheating. Because the reaction is exothermic and reversible, a multi-bed converter with controlled quench keeps the equilibrium favourable; a single-bed converter cannot get above ab"];
  "chem.refining.claus.catalyst.alumina-catalyst" [label="Claus alumina catalyst and its support" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The catalyst is not a mystery material: it is activated alumina, and the alumina is refined from bauxite. A Claus unit's catalyst life is measured in years and its loss rate is what sets the unit's sulphur recovery, so a good 1980s unit used a promoted, high-surface-area alumina with a shape (extrud"];
  "chem.refining.claus.furnace" [label="Claus furnace" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A refractory-lined furnace at 250-400 C where a third of the H2S is burned with oxygen or air. The burn has to be kept on the rich side: too much oxygen and the H2S is oxidised all the way to SO2, which wastes the sulphur; too little and soot forms and plugs the catalyst."];
  "chem.refining.hydrotreating" [label="Hydrotreating and desulphurisation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The refinery's chemistry of taking things out: sulphur, nitrogen, oxygen and metals, plus saturation. It is the answer to sour crude, and it is the process that made Arab Light and Mexican crudes usable in 1980s refineries. It also produces the acid gas from which elemental sulphur is recovered. Nap"];
  "chem.refining.hydrotreating.acid-gas" [label="Acid gas to the Claus unit" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Every hydrotreater and every amine unit makes an acid gas of mostly H2S with some CO2. In 1983 that stream was worth real money: recovering its sulphur turns a disposal liability into a product, and about 80-90% of world sulphur production was elemental sulphur recovered this way. A 20-35 wt% MEA or"];
  "chem.refining.vacuum-residue-processing" [label="Converting the vacuum residue" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The 2-5% of crude that will not distil is the hardest material in the refinery. It must be converted - into coke, into gasoline, or into distillate - or it is sold as fuel oil. This is where the refinery's biggest single units and its biggest capital decisions sit. Hydrocracking cracked heavy oil an"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking" [label="Fluid catalytic cracking" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Vacuum gas oil is contacted with a zeolite catalyst at 500-530 C and 1-3 bar in a riser, cracked into gasoline, light ends and coke, and the catalyst is continuously regenerated in a fluidised-bed regenerator at 650-750 C. FCC was the largest refinery process by capacity in the early 1980s - a large"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.cracked-gasoline" [label="Cracked gasoline and the propylene route" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="FCC gasoline is high in olefins, aromatics and isoparaffins, with an octane number well above the reformer's, and it is the blending component that let the industry drop lead. Its propylene stream - 2-4% of FCC feed - fed the old chemical industry: by 1983 propylene from FCC and from crackers togeth"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.zeolite-catalyst" [label="FCC zeolite catalyst" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A synthetic zeolite (USY, made by dealuminating Y zeolite) dispersed in a silica-alumina or silica matrix, rare-earth exchanged by lanthanum and neodymium. The zeolite gives shape-selective acid sites; the matrix gives attrition resistance and heat capacity. This is the same family of materials as t"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.zeolite-catalyst.zeolite-manufacture" [label="Making the FCC zeolite" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A synthetic zeolite is not mined; it is crystallised. The reagents are an alumina source, a silica source, an alkali and a template, and the product is a 3-5 micron crystal of a lattice so regular that it was named after the mineral zeolite. The FCC catalyst's lattice is what makes it a shape-select"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.zeolite-catalyst.zsm-5" [label="ZSM-5 octane additive" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A small-pore zeolite (mordenite framework, 10-membered rings) blended at 3-15% into the USY catalyst to crack the C7-C10 straight chains that were killing octane after the lead phase-down. This is the direct ancestor of the propylene and isobutylene it also made: ZSM-5 selectively shapes the product"];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.zeolite-catalyst.silica-alumina" [label="Silica-alumina matrix" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Amorphous silica-alumina, precipitated from sodium silicate and aluminium sulphate and caustic-gelled, then spray dried as 40-90 micron microspheres. The matrix is what gives the catalyst its attrition resistance and its heat capacity; it also carries a good part of the cracking, because the matrix "];
  "chem.refining.fluid-catalytic-cracking.riser" [label="Riser and regenerator" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The riser reactor is a vertical pipe 10-20 m long with the catalyst and feed entering at the bottom; the cyclone separators strip entrained catalyst back; the regenerator burns the coke off the catalyst with air. A 1980s unit's two-stage regeneration was the standard answer to how to get more heat i"];
  "chem.refining.delayed-coking" [label="Delayed coking" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Vacuum residue is heated to 350-400 C and held in large coke drums for 12-24 hours, during which it cracks to 15-25% petroleum coke while the vapours are continuously drawn off and fractionated. Delayed coking was the dominant residue conversion process of the 1960s and remained so through the 1980s"];
  "chem.refining.delayed-coking.green-coke" [label="Green coke" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The unreformed coke straight out of the drum: 85-92 wt% carbon, 8-15% volatiles, with all the feed's sulphur and metals still in it. Green coke is the intermediate between the coker drum and the calciner, and it is also what the plastics industry noticed in the 1980s: green coke is a needle-coke pre"];
  "chem.refining.delayed-coking.coke-drum" [label="Coke drum" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 6-11 m diameter vessel 20-35 m tall holding 100-400 tonnes of coke per cycle, switched in parallel with a fractionator overhead. Switching a drum is the highest-risk operation in a refinery: the drum is water-quenched and de-coked with a full-diameter drill and coke cutter while the neighbouring d"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation" [label="Atmospheric distillation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Fractional distillation of crude at atmospheric pressure in a 35-50 m fractionating tower, separating a barrel of crude into naphtha, kerosene, gas oil, and a 700 F residue. It is the first process every molecule of the polymer industry passes through, and it is where the difference between a 30 and"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.vacuum-column" [label="Vacuum distillation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A second column at 25-50 mmHg absolute (740-760 mmHg vacuum) that distils the 700 F plus residue down to a vacuum gas oil and a vacuum residue of 2-5% of the crude, without cracking it. Lowering the pressure raises the boiling points' relative spacing so heavy molecules can be distilled instead of d"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.vacuum-column.ejectors" [label="Steam ejectors and vacuum" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Three-stage steam ejectors holding 25-50 mmHg, using 10-25 t of steam per hour for a mid-size unit. Ejectors are simple but they cost a lot of steam; by the 1980s mechanical vacuum pumps (rotary or liquid-ring, with a surface condenser) were being installed to save that steam, and most plants still "];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower" [label="Fractionating tower" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 35-50 m column with 40-50 trays, at about 1.1-1.5 bar overhead and 2.5-3 bar at the bottom, condensing a vapour-liquid staircase in which each tray separates hydrocarbons by boiling range. A large atmospheric tower is a pressure vessel of 100-200 tonnes, and it is still the cheapest separation dev"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.naphtha-hydrotreating" [label="Naphtha hydrotreating before the cracker" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The cracker feedstock was almost never straight-run naphtha. It was hydrotreated to under 0.5 wt% total sulphur (often 0.05-0.3 wt%) and under 0.5 ppmw nitrogen, because sulphur poisons the reforming and cracking catalysts and nitrogen poisons the polymerisation catalysts downstream. Naphtha hydrotr"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.naphtha-hydrotreating.sour-water" [label="Sour water stripper" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The hydrogenating reactors make water and ammonia, and the separator water carries dissolved H2S and NH3. A stripper removes them, otherwise the recycled hydrogen carries ammonia into the cracker, where ammonia poisons the polymerisation catalysts and shows up as a colour defect in the polymer."];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.naphtha-hydrotreating.cobalt-molybdenum" [label="Cobalt-molybdenum / nickel-molybdenum catalyst" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sulphided Co-Mo or Ni-Mo on alumina in a fixed bed at 300-400 C and 20-50 bar hydrogen, with an H2/hydrocarbon ratio of 200-1,000. Lumping several reactors in series gave 0.2-0.5 wt% sulphur naphtha; a naphtha cracker in 1980s practice wanted 0.5 wt% S or less."];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.cut-points" [label="Cut points and the product slate" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The fractions a refinery aims for, by boiling range: naphtha (C5 to about 200 F end point, roughly 30-40% of crude), kerosene/jet (200-300 F, 8-15%), gas oil (300-700 F, 20-30%), and the atmospheric residue (700 F plus, 25-40%). Naphtha is the fraction that matters to this tree: it is the steam-crac"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.overhead" [label="Overhead condenser and gas recovery" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The naphtha overhead is condensed in a shell-and-tube condenser with a reflux drum, and the non-condensable gas (methane, ethane, hydrogen, H2S) goes off to the fuel gas header. In 1980s practice the gas was usually simply burned, although the recovery unit for ethane and propane (an absorber) was s"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.reboiler" [label="Reboilers and side strippers" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A fired or steam reboiler supplies the vapour to the bottom of the tower, and side strippers remove the light ends from the kerosene and gas-oil draws so the side columns are not loaded with vapour. Kerosene is stripped with a few hundred kPa of steam; diesel often went straight to the bottom becaus"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.fractionating-tower.trays" [label="Distillation trays" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Valve, sieve, bubble-cap or proprietary proprietary-type trays at 0.4-0.6 m spacing. In 1980s practice a new refinery would install valve or sieve trays, and an old one would often be kept and given a new tray-packing section on the vacuum side."];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.crude-preheat" [label="Crude preheat train" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A train of shell-and-tube exchangers that takes crude from ambient to 220-300 C by recovering heat from every hot stream in the refinery, then the fired heater takes it the rest of the way to 350-370 C. Preheating was the classic refinery heat-integration project: a crude preheat train alone recover"];
  "chem.refining.atmospheric-distillation.crude-preheat.crude-heater" [label="Fired crude heater" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A radiant box furnace with 150-400 coils of cast alloy or wrought-alloy tube (Cr-Mo, 5 in outside diameter, 0.3 m apart), firing gas at 30-80 MW. Tube metallurgy matters: the coil sees a 30-40 C metal temperature above the oil temperature, and the 1980s move to coiled tubing and to higher radiant ef"];
  "chem.refining.scale" [label="The refining industry in 1983" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Around 750-850 refineries existed worldwide, of which more than 300 were in the United States alone (US capacity about 19 million bbl/d). The largest refining regions in 1983 were the US Gulf Coast, the US Midwest, Western Europe (Rotterdam, Antwerp, Fos-sur-Mer), the Caribbean (USVI, Curaçao), Japa"];
  "chem.natural-gas" [label="Natural gas: the gas field" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Methane, mostly: either the gas cap sitting on an oil reservoir or a gas-only field. In 1983 world gas production was roughly 2.9-3.0 trillion cubic metres a year, of which about 70% was still associated with oil and much of it was flared or burned at the wellhead. Gas matters to a C64 three ways: a"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.gas-reservoir" [label="Gas reservoir" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Gas fills a porous sandstone or a fractured carbonate just as oil does, but with a lower density and a higher compressibility, so gas in place is quoted in trillions of standard cubic feet rather than billions of barrels. The largest gas fields in the world in the early 1980s were the giant Permian "];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.gas-reservoir.gas-in-place" [label="Gas in place" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Trillions of standard cubic metres of gas in the pore space. The C64-scale fact: all the natural gas used worldwide in 1983 was of the order of 3,000 billion cubic metres a year, so one machine's plastics and energy are far below a thousandth of a cubic metre."];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.fertiliser-ammonia" [label="Fertiliser ammonia" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="About 80% of ammonia goes to fertiliser, a demand that sets the size of the hydrogen industry and therefore, indirectly, the amount of ammonia available for the acrylonitrile ammoxidation. The link to the C64 is indirect but real: the same ammonia plants that fertilised the fields also supplied the "];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.hydrogen" [label="Hydrogen by steam methane reforming" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="CH4 + H2O -\> CO + 3H2 over nickel at 750-850 C and 20-30 bar, followed by the water-gas shift (iron oxide/chromia, 350-450 C) to raise the hydrogen fraction, then CO2 removal and cryogenic or PSA purification. A world-scale 1980s plant made 50-100 million m3 of hydrogen a day. Almost all of it went "];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.hydrogen.to-ammonia" [label="Hydrogen to ammonia (Haber-Bosch)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="N2 + 3H2 -\> 2NH3 over promoted iron or ruthenium at 400-500 C and 150-250 bar, in a 1,000-2,500 tonne a day synthesis loop fed from a purge cascade. Ammonia is the single largest use of hydrogen, and it matters to the C64 in two places: the nitrogen for the polybutadiene and SAN polymerisations, and"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.hydrogen.to-ammonia.catalyst" [label="Ammonia synthesis catalyst" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The Haber-Bosch catalyst is promoted iron: magnetite reduced to alpha-iron, promoted with potassium oxide and with alumina or calcium oxide. Ruthenium is better by an order of magnitude but was, in 1983, still a research curiosity as far as ammonia plants were concerned. The catalyst is made by melt"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.hydrogen.to-ammonia.catalyst.magnetite" [label="Magnetite from iron ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Fe3O4, the reduction product of hematite, and the raw material for the ammonia catalyst. It is also the mineral in the Claus catalyst's neighbourhood and in the polishing lapidary industry. A tonne of ammonia carries about 0.3-0.4 tonnes of iron in its catalyst, and the catalyst is replaced every fe"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.hydrogen.nickel-catalyst" [label="Nickel reforming catalyst" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Nickel on alumina, or the older nickel-chromium used in the pre-1957 Topsoe SMR-180. By the 1980s the modern multi-bed reformer ran 25-40 tubes of 10-16 inch diameter at 30-50 bar, with an inlet temperature near 550 C and an outlet at 850-870 C, burning gas in a process furnace alongside the tubes."];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene" [label="Acetylene from natural gas" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The Reppe process, commercialised from 1959-1965: a mixture of roughly 6-8% methane with 30-38% air and 55-60% hydrogen is fired through a water-cooled burner at 1,200-1,800 C and quenched to below 100 C within milliseconds. The acetylene yield per pass is only 2-3% of the feed, so the process has t"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.quench" [label="Quench and acetylene recovery" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The hot gas is quenched to below 100 C within milliseconds, then acetylene is absorbed selectively from the acetylene-hydrogen-methane-hydrogen-sulphide mixture by acetone at 10-20 C and 5-15 bar (about 2.2 volumes of acetylene per volume of solvent), stripped and the solvent recirculated. Recovered"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.quench.absorption-train" [label="The absorption and recovery train" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The acetylene is absorbed out of the cracked mixture in a solvent and taken off the top of the tower at high purity, which is the whole reason for doing the separation this way. Acetone's job is to hold acetylene - which at 20 C and 12 bar will dissolve to about 2.2 volumes of solvent per volume of "];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.quench.absorption-train.absorber" [label="Absorber and stripper" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 20-40 tray absorber at 10-20 C and 5-15 bar with the acetone circulated, and a stripper that takes the pure acetylene overhead at 3-5 bar and the solvent off the bottom for recycle. The solvent must be regenerated at intervals because it accumulates the hydrogen sulphide and the water from the fee"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.quench.absorption-train.absorber.acetone-solvent" [label="Acetone as the selective solvent" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The choice of solvent is a compromise. Acetone has a high acetylene solubility and a low methane solubility and a manageable vapour pressure, and it is made from cumene on the same site, which is the practical reason it was chosen. It is also flammable and it is a reproductive toxin, which is a mode"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses.acetylene.partial-oxidation" [label="Partial oxidation burner" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Methane is partially oxidised to CO and H2, which then combine to acetylene at flame temperature. The furnace runs at atmospheric to 2 bar with the flame held above the acetylene decomposition point; the burner is water-cooled because copper and steel both survive the flame while the mixture would o"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.methane-hydrate" [label="Gas hydrate (a hazard, not yet a resource)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Methane trapped as a clathrate of water, stable below about 20 C at surface pressure. Known since the 1930s and already recognised in the deep ocean and permafrost as a potentially enormous resource, but in the early 1980s it was a hazard - it plugged subsea pipelines and was one reason offshore gas"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas" [label="Non-associated (gas-only) field" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A gas reservoir with no oil at the contact. The North Sea fields of the late 1970s - Frigg (1977), Heimdal, Sleipner, Troll (discovered 1979) - turned Europe from an import market into a gas market, and the Norwegian and British gas grids of the early 1980s were built around them. In the US the equi"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling" [label="Discovering and drilling a gas field" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Gas fields are found by the same seismic and drilled with the same rig as oil fields, but the completion differs: the log response that proves a gas column is high resistivity with low neutron density, and the well must be tested for absolute gas flow rather than liquid rate. Gas viscosity is about "];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling.dehydration" [label="Dehydration to pipeline dew point" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Gas must arrive at the pipeline dew point or the higher-pressure steps downstream condense water out and the line fills with liquid. By 1980s practice that meant a glycol unit (triethylene glycol at 80-95 wt% purity, contactor at 20-50 C and 40-70 bar, reboiler 120-140 C) and increasingly a 3A or 4A"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling.dehydration.triethylene-glycol" [label="Triethylene glycol" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A heavy glycol made by reacting ethylene oxide with diethylene glycol; the standard dehydration absorbent because of its low vapour pressure at the contactor temperature. The 1980s trend was towards 95 wt% TEG purity, because glycol vapour carried upstream into a gas turbine or a carburettor was a r"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling.sweetening" [label="Sweetening to pipeline specification" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="H2S and CO2 are removed with a 20-35 wt% alkanolamine in an absorber at 30-45 C and 20-70 bar, with the rich amine regenerated at 120-130 C and 1.5-2 bar in a reboiler. By the early 1980s the trend was to diethanolamine for economy and MDEA for lower heat duty and no carbamate degradation."];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling.sweetening.amine" [label="Alkanolamine" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Monoethanolamine (MEA, primary), diethanolamine (DEA, secondary), methyldiethanolamine (MDEA, tertiary). MDEA was the growth amine of the 1980s because it does not form carbamate with CO2, so its degradation and reboiler duty are lower; the penalty is a slower absorption rate and a lower equilibrium"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.non-associated-gas.discovery-and-drilling.drill-fluid" [label="Drilling fluid for a gas well" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Gas wells were drilled with the same bentonite mud as oil wells, with two differences: a low filter loss was mandatory because filtrate invasion had to be reversed before logging, and a gas-cut mud had to be controlled because entrained gas reduced hydrostatic pressure and made the well hard to cont"];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources.associated-gas" [label="Associated (oil-associated) gas" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Gas from the gas cap of an oil field, produced at roughly 50-150 m3 of gas per barrel of oil. Around 70% of world gas production was associated gas in 1983, and a large share was still flared: world flaring ran at the order of 100-130 billion m3 a year in the early 1980s, several million tonnes of h"];
  "chem.natural-gas.composition.h2s" [label="Hydrogen sulphide (sour gas)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A lethal, corrosive, smelly component that must be taken to a few ppmv before the gas enters a steel pipeline, because H2S embrittles steel and attacks copper alloys. Sweetening it produces a concentrated acid gas stream whose H2S is the feedstock for the Claus sulphur plant in the refining branch."];
  "chem.natural-gas.composition.nitrogen" [label="Nitrogen in natural gas" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="1-15% N2 in many fields. It is the impurity that most affects heating value, that forces the Wobbe index (a measure of interchangeability between gas supplies) onto a pipeline tariff, and that makes a gas which is 90% N2 unsaleable as town gas."];
  "chem.natural-gas.composition.methane" [label="Methane" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="CH4, 80-98% of a dry gas, 39.8 MJ/m3 gross calorific value, -161.5 C boiling point. The single molecule most of this branch is about: because methane is hard to polymerise and hard to functionalise, the industry of the 1980s broke it into acetylene at extreme temperature or into hydrogen over a nick"];
  "chem.natural-gas.uses" [label="What the gas is for" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Three jobs in 1983: burn it, crack it, or reform it. Burning dominated volume; the petrochemical jobs - acetylene, hydrogen, ammonia - were small in tonnage but disproportionate in value, and they are the reason the polymer industry existed at all. The largest single use of gas is plain combustion: "];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing" [label="Gas processing and transport" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The plants that turn a wet, sour wellstream into a pipeline gas, a liquefied gas or a natural-gas-liquids fraction. A large 1980s gas plant handled 5-30 billion standard cubic feet a day; the North Sea, Gulf and Middle Eastern plants of that decade were among the largest chemical plants ever built. "];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.pipeline" [label="Gas pipeline" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Line pipe 0.6-1.4 m in diameter, API 5L grade X52-X70, laid by a lay barge or a trenching spread and internally coated with epoxy and polyethylene powder. Transmission lines run at 60-100 bar with compressors every 100-200 km. The 1980s were the years of the great gas transmission builds: the Centra"];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.pipeline.subsea" [label="Subsea pipeline and riser" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Concrete weight-coated line with a hot-water or electrical trace heater for hydrate prevention, and a wet-insulated riser with a buoyancy module for the first 100-300 m of water. Deepwater gas tiebacks of the 1980s established the standard development: subsea wellhead manifold, a dedicated line to a"];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.liquefaction" [label="LNG liquefaction" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Chilling gas to -162 C so it becomes a liquid at atmospheric pressure, cutting volume about 600-fold for the same energy. In 1983 world LNG trade was roughly 55-70 billion m3 a year, about 2-3% of world gas production, on a network of roughly 60-70 liquefaction trains. The dominant cycles were the p"];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.liquefaction.carrier" [label="LNG carrier" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="A 70,000-125,000 m3 membrane or independent-tank ship with a re-liquefaction plant, because LNG boils off at 0.1-0.3% a day and the load has to be reliquefied in transit. The world LNG fleet in 1983 was of the order of 90-130 ships at $40-90 million each."];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.liquefaction.terminal" [label="LNG storage and regasification terminal" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A vacuum-insulated cylindrical tank of 20,000-160,000 m3 with a nickel-alloy inner tank over perlite insulation, plus a jetty for a 125,000-165,000 m3 carrier. Regasification burns about 0.3 tonnes of fuel gas per tonne of LNG, which is where the C64-scale fact becomes visible: the fuel burned to re"];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.liquefaction.refrigerant" [label="Mixed refrigerant cycle" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Propane pre-cooling to -30 to -40 C, then an ethylene/ethane/propane mixture expanded through a series of exchangers to -120 C, then a nitrogen expander to -162 C. The hydrocarbons in the refrigerant are the same ones the petrochemical industry buys; the nitrogen expander cycle burns almost nothing "];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.cryogenic-separation" [label="Cryogenic separation (expander plant)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The JT-expander process that became the industry standard for large gas plants in the 1980s: the gas is chilled by expansion through a turbine to -30 to -80 C and the resulting cold box distils methane from ethane and heavier. The plant cost is dominated by the aluminium cold boxes, which remain one"];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.cryogenic-separation.cold-box" [label="Aluminium cold box" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A brazed plate-and-fin aluminium exchanger 10-30 m long, cryogenic to -80 C, containing dozens of channels. Aluminium-brazed cold boxes replaced copper wound exchangers in the 1970s and are one reason the gas plant business grew so fast in that decade."];
  "chem.natural-gas.processing.cryogenic-separation.expander" [label="Cryogenic turboexpander" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A radial or axial turbine in the cold box, handling 10,000-40,000 kW on a big plant, recovering 5-15% of the gas enthalpy and reaching -30 to -80 C. The expander train was the mechanical heart of the 1980s gas plant and its efficiency set the plant cost."];
  "chem.natural-gas.sources" [label="Where the gas comes from" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Associated gas from the gas cap of an oil reservoir, and non-associated (gas-only) gas. The distinction matters industrially: associated gas was a by-product that had to be flared or reinjected until the late 1970s made it commercial, while gas-only fields carry the pipelines and the LNG trains."];
  "chem.natural-gas.composition" [label="What natural gas is" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A mixture, not a compound. A sour gas well may deliver methane with 2-10% ethane and propane, 1-8% C4, 1-20% hydrogen sulphide, 0.5-5% carbon dioxide and up to 15% nitrogen. Everything downstream - the pipelines, the dew point specification, the hydrogen plant - is set by those five impurities."];
  "chem.crude" [label="Crude oil: finding it, drilling it, lifting it" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The upstream end of the chain. Seismic surveys pick a structure, a rig drills a 5-7 km hole through rock, casing and cement line the hole, the well is completed and perforated into the reservoir, then often artificially stimulated. An 8 kg C64 case corresponds to only 1-3 litres of crude oil, but th"];
  "chem.crude.c64-fraction" [label="One Commodore 64's share of the wellhead" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The plastic mass of a C64 is about 350-400 g of ABS in the case plus 100-200 g in the keys, and ABS comes from roughly 2 kg of crude oil per kilogram of polymer. Add the energy to make it, the fuel to ship it and the polymer's own energy in the burning, and one machine corresponds to roughly 1-3 lit"];
  "chem.crude.assay" [label="Crude assay" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="What the seller is paid for: a barrel of crude is specified by a handful of standardised numbers, and every downstream decision - which cracker, which desulphuriser, whether it can be shipped - is made from them. Density and API gravity set the transport economics, sulphur splits sweet from sour, th"];
  "chem.crude.separation" [label="Wellhead and terminal separation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The wellstream arrives at 15-100 bar and 60-100 C as an oil/gas/water froth. It is separated in stages: test separator, stock tanks, heaters and desalters, gas-oil separator, and finally a train of electrostatic and coalescing vessels that make a storable crude. Field separation is where the crude a"];
  "chem.crude.separation.oil-water-coalescer" [label="Oil-water coalescer" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A packed or membrane device that lets separated water droplets out of the crude without carrying oil. Produced-water disposal in 1983 was to a pit or a treatment plant; the offshore discharge limit under the 1983 amendments to the US Clean Water Act required 42 ppm oil and no visible sheen, which is"];
  "chem.crude.separation.electrostatic" [label="Electrostatic coalescer (electrogravitational)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Charged droplets are attracted into the water phase, collapsing the emulsion in seconds instead of hours. Small pipeline electrocoalescers were introduced in the mid-1980s for crude pretreatment, cutting the demulsifier dose; they were used in 1980s practice wherever the crude conductivity allowed ("];
  "chem.crude.separation.heater-treater" [label="Heater-treater and desalter" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A crude preheater to 150-200 C followed by a settler and then a desalter, where the crude is washed with recycled water to strip salts. Needed because sour Middle East crudes carry 1-3 wt% dissolved salts, and because naphthenic acids plus water at 200 C will corrode the hot exchanger on the way to "];
  "chem.crude.separation.three-phase" [label="Three-phase separator and stock tanks" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A horizontal or vertical vessel at 2-10 bar with a mechanical or electrostatic coalescing section that separates oil, gas and water, then atmospheric storage tanks that settle the remaining water and sediment. Stock tank bottoms and the settled water carry the production's salt and heavy ends, and t"];
  "chem.crude.separation.three-phase.demulsifier" [label="Demulsifier chemistry" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Nonylphenol- or alcohol-based surfactants dosed at 5-50 ppm to break the oil-water emulsion; in the 1980s a growing share of demulsifiers were polyalkoxyamine and polyglycol ethers. A stable water-in-oil emulsion (chocolate mousse) could hold 20-50% water into the crude and was the single largest qu"];
  "chem.crude.separation.test-separator" [label="Test separator" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The first vessel, sized 1-5% of the field's liquid capacity, where the wellstream is bled at 25-70 bar to a stable pressure and temperature. Its records are the raw data for reservoir performance, gas-oil ratio and oil assay. A field with 100,000 bbl/d of oil needed a test separator of a few hundred"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation" [label="Well stimulation: fracturing and acidising" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Almost no reservoir delivers its whole potential through a millimetre perforation. Stimulation makes the rock permeable by fracture or by dissolving the rock; it is one of the biggest single levers on oil and gas supply and it consumes enormous volumes of water, sand and, for acidising, corrosive ac"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.acidising" [label="Matrix acidising and fracture acidising" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Hydrochloric or hydrofluoric acid injected to dissolve or acid-fracture the rock, typically 15-28% HCl in sandstone and HF-containing mud acid in carbonate. Sandstone acidising only reached the late 1970s as a standard service; carbonate mud-acid fracturing with a 12% HCl-3% HF system had become the"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.acidising.mud-acid.corrosion-inhibitor.tubing" [label="Acid-resistant tubing" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Coiled tubing and the acid lines were, by the mid-1980s, routinely nickel alloy - a nickel-molybdenum alloy such as Alloy C-276 or a titanium grade - rather than carbon steel, because the inhibitor was never perfect and the steel was never quite good enough. Nickel alloys and titanium both come out "];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.acidising.mud-acid" [label="Mud acid (HCl + HF)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Hydrochloric acid to dissolve carbonate, with 1-3 wt% HF to attack the clay in sandstone and 2-5 wt% HF in carbonate muds. The reason for mud acid was a reserve shift: by the early 1980s mudstone was around 80% of world oil reserves, and mudstone cannot be treated with pure HCl because of its clay r"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.acidising.mud-acid.corrosion-inhibitor" [label="Acid corrosion inhibitor" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Hydrofluoric acid at 12% and hydrochloric acid at 12%, pumped down a steel coil, will remove a hundredth of an inch of steel a day. The inhibitors - a long-chain amine, thiourea, an acetylenic alcohol - adsorb on the steel and cut the corrosion rate by several orders of magnitude without touching th"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing" [label="Hydraulic fracturing" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A water-based or gel fluid pumped down the casing into the pay, then into the perforations at 40-100 bbl/min and up to 6,000-10,000 psi surface pressure to part the rock; a proppant is carried in to hold the fracture open when pressure is released. Born in 1947 (experiments) and industrialised in th"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing.frac-equipment" [label="Fracturing pumping equipment" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Blender trucks, sand trucks and a high-pressure pump unit (one pumping unit at 25,000 psi), laid out in a yard over the rig. A 1980s job mobilised 10-40 trucks and 100-200 people, and it was the largest single water consumer in the oilfield outside steam flooding."];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing.proppant" [label="Proppant sand" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Sized, round, silica sand or resin-coated sand, typically 20/40 or 30/50 mesh (0.4-0.6 mm), graded to carry a hundred thousand tonnes a year in a region. Ottawa River and Illinois Basin sands were the standard, with regional sources in Texas and Arkansas. It was a heavy, low-value, high-bulk freight"];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing.proppant.sand-processing" [label="Fracturing sand and its plant" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The proppant is a silica sand that has been sieved, washed, resin-coated and graded, and the plant that does it is a small mineral processing operation that exists in the Permian basin because the proppant has to be nearby. The oilfield sands of the Permian have a problem that glass sands do not: a "];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing.proppant.sand-processing.sand-mine" [label="Frac sand mine" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A sand quarry that happens to contain almost pure silica in a dune, worked at 1,000-10,000 tonnes a day. The last dig-it-out node of the crude branch's stimulation chain, and a reminder that a hydraulic fracture is largely a mineral supply chain: the water, the sand and the chemicals are all mining "];
  "chem.crude.stimulation.hydraulic-fracturing.frac-fluid" [label="Fracturing fluid and gel" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Water plus a viscosity builder (hydroxyethyl cellulose in water, or a hydrocarbon gel), often crosslinked with borate or an organometallic zirconium/titanium chelate to hold the proppant in suspension at high temperature. Waterfracs (no crosslinker, friction-reducer only) took a large share of the m"];
  "chem.crude.well-completion" [label="Well completion" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Turning a drilled hole into a producing well: set production casing, cement to the pay, run and perforate the tubing, pack the annulus, and install a tree. This is where the reservoir is deliberately connected to the borehole for the first time. A completed well was logged with spinner and turbine f"];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.christmas-tree" [label="Christmas tree (wellhead assembly)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The valve stack on top of the casing: casing spool, tubing spool, master valve, wing valves with gauges, and the flowline tee off the wing. Every stream the well ever produces has to pass through this steel gatework, which is why it is the most pressure-rated cluster of parts in the business."];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.packer" [label="Packer and completion hardware" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An elastomer-sealed packer run and set in the casing to isolate zones and provide a seat for the tubing hanger. In 1980s completions this was single- or dual-packer tubing with sliding sleeves or selective stimulation, increasingly mechanical rather than hydraulic."];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.packer.elastomer" [label="Packer elastomer (nitrile / HNBR)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A nitrile (acrylonitrile-butadiene) or HNBR element that swells or mechanically expands to grip the casing. Nitrile is the same chemistry as chem.butadiene in the polymer branch: acrylonitrile copolymerised with butadiene."];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.perforation" [label="Perforation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Shaped charges fired down the casing to punch 1-3 cm holes through steel, cement and formation in one shot, linking pay zone to borehole. A 1980s completion might be 4-6 perforations per metre of pay; perforation is done in stages so only the best footage is shot."];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.perforation.entry-hole" [label="Entry hole into the pay zone" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The first connection to the reservoir is itself a jet-perforated hole in cement, and it is the smallest diameter in the whole production system: a few millimetres through which the entire well's flow must pass. That single fact justifies the next node in the tree."];
  "chem.crude.well-completion.perforation.shaped-charge" [label="Shaped-charge perforator" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A copper or steel cone lined with a high explosive (RDX/HMX or HMX-wax, 5-30 g per shot in the 1980s) with a steel or ceramic liner; the detonated cone forms a copper or tungsten-alloy jet at 8-10 km/s which drills the hole. Charges were fired on a wireline in a gun barrel or in a TCP (tubing convey"];
  "chem.crude.rig" [label="The drilling rig" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Rotary drilling: the drill string is rotated from the surface, the bit mills a hole, mud circulates to carry cuttings out and to control formation pressure. Offshore this is a jack-up, a semisubmersible or a drillship; onshore it is a land rig with a derrick. 1980s rigs reached 6,000-7,500 m total d"];
  "chem.crude.rig.core-drilling" [label="Core drilling and coring" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Diamond core barrels cut 1-4 in core in a special drilling string, giving the reservoir rock, its porosity, its saturation and its capillary properties undamaged. A cored well is the most expensive way to get reservoir data, so it was used on 1-3 wells out of 20 and almost always on the discovery we"];
  "chem.crude.rig.core-drilling.core-barrel" [label="Diamond core barrel" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A steel or aluminium tube with a diamond-set reaming shell and a core lifter, run on the bottom hole assembly at low RPM (20-100) and low weight. Carbit tungsten-carbide bits dominated abrasive sandstone, PCD for softer beds, and set diamond for carbonate."];
  "chem.crude.rig.pressure-control" [label="Well control: BOP, diverter and kill" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The blowout preventer stack (annular preventer plus 3-5 ram preventers) and the choke manifold above the wellhead, rated 5,000-15,000 psi. Before the 1970s and 1980s safety emphasis, the classic profile was a vertical string, overbalanced mud and a diverter; 1980s practice added the above-the-bit di"];
  "chem.crude.rig.rig-types" [label="Rig types and their 1980s cost" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Jack-up rigs (4-6 legs, 60-90 m water-depth limit) and fixed derricks onshore; semisubmersibles (700-1,000 m, self-propelled, expensive); drillships (ice-strengthened, for deep water and harsh climates). Costs in the early 1980s: onshore rig $1-3 million, jack-up $20-45 million, drillship $30-70 mil"];
  "chem.crude.rig.casing-cement" [label="Casing and cementing" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Steel casing is run to isolate the hole and to carry the pressure; cement is pumped between casing and formation (and sometimes inside the casing) to seal the annulus. Without a good cement job the whole completion leaks and stimulation injects into the wrong zone. Cementing is one of the few oilfie"];
  "chem.crude.rig.casing-cement.casing-string" [label="Casing string and cement sheath" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Conductor, surface, intermediate and production casing, each cemented across its shoe. The C64-scale point: even a modest producing well consumes tens to hundreds of tonnes of cement and hundreds of metres of steel, dwarfing the machine it ultimately supplies."];
  "chem.crude.rig.casing-cement.dispersant" [label="Cement dispersants and additives" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sulfonated naphthalene formaldehyde condensates (BNS) or sulfonated formaldehyde melamine (SFM) to keep the slurry workable at low water; and bentonite as an extender. Early 1980s practice was to optimise with laboratory thickening-time tests before the job, because a primary cement job cost $0.2-1 "];
  "chem.crude.rig.casing-cement.api-class-g" [label="API Class G oilwell cement" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Portland-type cement with 40-60 wt% Class G sulphate-resisting blend, gypsum for set control, silica flour as a retarder for the thickening time, and additives for fluid-loss control. Mixed on location with water and spacers at 1.7-2.0 g/cm3 and pumped at 30-90 bbl/min."];
  "chem.crude.rig.bit" [label="Drill bits" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The cutting tool at the bottom of the string. In 1980s practice a soft-formation bit was a sealed journal bearing with a cone of natural diamond, a medium bit a sealed or unsealed mud turbine with tungsten-carbide nose buttons, and a hard rock bit a milled-steel body with tungsten-carbide inserts on"];
  "chem.crude.rig.bit.pdc-bit" [label="PDC (polycrystalline diamond compact) bit" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The emerging bit of the early 1980s: sintered diamond compact cutters brazed to a steel body with no cones and no bearings, turning rather than grinding. PDC began as a hard-rock bit, spread into soft rock in the mid-1980s and largely replaced roller cones in soft formations by the early 1990s. In 1"];
  "chem.crude.rig.bit.pdc-bit.cutter" [label="Polycrystalline diamond compact cutter" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Diamond powder sintered at 5,000-6,000 bar and 1,400-1,600 C with a cobalt metal catalyst from the diamond-graphite phase boundary. Over a 1980s field life a soft-formation PDC cutter could cut tens of kilometres of sandstone; hard-rock PDC arrived later with tungsten-carbide substrates and domed cu"];
  "chem.crude.rig.bit.natural-diamond" [label="Natural diamond (soft-formation bit)" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Gem-quality or bort diamond in a matrix of tungsten and cobalt sintered into a cone. Natural diamond bits dominate soft sandstone and were the standard drag-bit insert in 1980s drilling; synthetic (polycrystalline) diamond began to displace them in abrasive sandstone late in the decade."];
  "chem.crude.rig.bit.tungsten-carbide" [label="Tungsten carbide cutters and buttons" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="WC-Co cemented carbide: 6-12% cobalt binder, 900-1,600 HV, pressed and sintered in a vacuum furnace then brazed onto the cone or into a milled-steel body. Cemented-carbide rock-bit consumption was the largest single use of tungsten carbide in the world in the 1980s, comparable to carbide tooling."];
  "chem.crude.rig.rotary-drilling" [label="Rotary drilling" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A rotary table or top drive turns the string at 30-150 rpm with 10-40 kN-m torque while 200-450 kN of weight is set off the bit. Weight-on-bit and torque are the two variables the driller steers with; the drilling engineer watches torque and bit exponent while the driller watches penetration rate."];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud" [label="Drilling mud (drilling fluid)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A thixotropic clay-water or oil-water emulsion that removes cuttings, lubricates and cools the bit, carries hydraulic pressure to the formation, and crucially gels to hold formation pressure when the pumps stop. In practice it was a chemically engineered suspension with four control jobs: viscosity "];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.chelant-biocide" [label="Chelants, biocides and defoamers" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The 1980s mud chemist's standard additive cabinet: phosphate and polyphosphate sequestrants for calcium, lignosulphonate and polyacrylamide viscosifiers, benzyl alcohol defoamer, and glutaraldehyde or formaldehyde-based biocides against sulphate-reducing bacteria, which produce hydrogen sulphide and"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.weights-mix" [label="Mixed-weight additive (iron ore + barite)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Where barite was unavailable or too expensive, ground iron ore was blended in as a second weighting agent. It attacks steel components by corrosion and needs a passivation layer of lime or sulphide film, but it saved cost in a tight barite market."];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.oil-based-mud" [label="Oil-based (diesel) mud" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Diesel or mineral oil as the continuous phase with water as the dispersed phase, organophilic clay (amine-treated montmorillonite) for viscosity. Chosen for high-temperature deep wells and for formations where water filtrate would swell clay. Its environmental reputation in the Gulf in the 1980s was"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.chromate-lime-mud" [label="Chrome/lime mud (invert emulsion)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The classic high-temperature mud: a water-in-oil (invert) emulsion with oil as the continuous phase, bentonite in the aqueous phase, and calcium carbonate and a lime (Ca(OH)2) emulsion. Chrome/lime muds were standard to about 120-150 C but they fail in a cement job because the calcium in cement brea"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.barite" [label="Barite (barium sulphate) weighting agent" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Almost all the weight in a mud: API-grade barite at 4.28 g/cm3 (42.8 ppg) added to a base mud of about 1.03-1.2. Barite is also the standard drilling weighting because it is nearly insoluble in water. The same mineral appears as filler in the plastics and cable branches of this file, via metal.bariu"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.bentonite" [label="Bentonite (montmorillonite clay)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sodium montmorillonite from the Cretaceous Bentonite beds of Wyoming and South Dakota; the single most important mud component. It gives the thixotropy: at rest a high gel strength to suspend cuttings, under shear it drops to low viscosity so the bit can cut. Drilling-grade bentonite is API 13/14 wi"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.bentonite.montmorillonite" [label="Montmorillonite and the bentonite bed" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Sodium montmorillonite is a dioctahedral 2:1 smectite: two tetrahedral silica sheets sandwiching an octahedral aluminium sheet, with the negative layer charge balanced by exchangeable sodium. Between the layers is water, and that water is why the clay swells, gels and gives the mud its thixotropy. T"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.bentonite.montmorillonite.soda-ash.clay-pit" [label="The clay pit" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 10-20 m thick bentonite bed, opened up and worked as an open pit or a shallow underground mine, in a landscape that looks like a lunar plain because the clay is grey-white when fresh and weathers to a pale crust. It is the last dig-it-out node of the crude branch's drilling chain, and it is the po"];
  "chem.crude.rig.drilling-mud.bentonite.montmorillonite.soda-ash" [label="Soda ash conversion to sodium bentonite" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The pit clay is a calcium montmorillonite and has to be converted. The clay is blunged with water, treated with sodium carbonate so that the exchangeable calcium is replaced by sodium, and then dried and milled. That step is what turns a geological curiosity into an industrial material, and it is al"];
  "chem.crude.rig.rotary-drilling.mud-system" [label="Mud circulation system" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Active and reserve pits, the pump, the surface mixing hopper (mud pit) and the flowline over the shaker. 1980s volumes for a single rig were 400-1,500 m3 of active mud with 150-400 m3 of reserve, and the pit room needed chemical dosing, dilution and waste disposal for one well's worth of chemically "];
  "chem.crude.rig.rotary-drilling.drillstring" [label="Drill string" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Drill pipe, drill collars and a bit, threaded or coupled API 5-1/2 in and 6-5/8 in in the 1980s, with 3-1/2 in drill collar subs. Length is a queue of 5,000-7,000 m of 5 in pipe plus 200-400 m of collars; the string must be strong enough for tensile load at the maximum string weight yet remain withi"];
  "chem.crude.reservoir" [label="The reservoir: where the oil sits" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Oil exists in pore space between grains of sandstone or in fractures and vugs of carbonate, held down by an overburden of rock and by an overlying gas cap or water. This is the physical thing that the whole upstream industry is trying to reach. Oil squeezed out of the source rock moves updip along a"];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.oil-column" [label="Oil column in the pore space" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An oil column of tens to hundreds of metres stands between a gas cap and a water contact, with oil saturation in the pay zone often only 50-80% of pore volume. That gap, plus the reservoir rock and the seal, is why recovery factors are 15-35% and why hydraulic fracturing and acidising exist."];
  "chem.petroleum.asphaltene" [label="Asphaltenes and resins" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The heavy, aromatic, high-molecular-weight end of crude. Roughly 2-20% of crude, soluble in the light ends and precipitated by heptane in the standard n-heptane asphaltene test. Asphaltenes are the coke precursors in every thermal cracker, coker and reformer, and their content is what decides whethe"];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.oil-column.oil-in-place" [label="Oil in place" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The volume of hydrocarbons physically present in the rock, before any recovery factor. Multiply it by the C64 scale: the entire world shipped about 200,000 Commodore 64s in 1983, so the machine is a rounding error on a single wellhead's hourly output."];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.reservoir-rock" [label="Reservoir rock: sandstone and carbonate" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Porosity 15-35% and permeability 50-2,000 mD for a good sandstone; carbonate reservoirs have most of their porosity as vugs and fractures and typically 5-25% matrix porosity. Around 60% of world oil reserves and 80% of world gas reserves were in carbonate rock, most of it fractured Arab-type in the "];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.reservoir-rock.marine-carbonate" [label="Marine carbonate (Arab-type) reservoir" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Dolomitised limestone and dolomite of the Arabian shelf: high initial productivity, typically 4,000-10,000 bbl/d per well with strong water drive, but rapid flow assurance decline and high water cut. Saudi production was essentially all of this rock type in 1983."];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.trap" [label="Geological trap" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Anticline, fault, pinch-out or stratigraphic trap. The Ghawar field in Saudi Arabia, discovered in 1948, remained the largest oil field on earth for decades, holding some 100+ billion stock-tank barrels in a stacked series of Arabian carbonate reservoirs."];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.source-rock" [label="Source rock and kerogen" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Organic-rich marine shale. Type I and type II kerogen give oil; type III, humic and mainly terrestrial, gives gas. Maturation is measured by vitrinite reflectance, with the oil window at roughly 0.6-1.3% Ro and the gas window above about 1.3%. Between about 60 and 160 C the kerogen breaks down into "];
  "chem.crude.reservoir.sedimentary-basin" [label="Sedimentary basin" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A subsiding basin in which organic mud is buried, buried deeper, cooked and squeezed; the whole petroleum system is source, maturation, migration, reservoir, seal and trap, and it takes 1-100 million years of that history. By 1983 the world's producing basins were a few hundred in number, with the M"];
  "chem.crude.exploration" [label="Petroleum exploration" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Finding a commercial accumulation of oil before spending the exploration well. In 1980s practice this was reflection seismology over regional grids, closed by wildcat drilling. Cost per barrel discovered had fallen from tens of dollars to roughly $2-8 by the early 1980s. A mud-logging unit beside th"];
  "chem.crude.exploration.reserve-estimate" [label="Reserve estimation" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Volumetric and material-balance estimates, presented to a board and to governments as proved, probable and possible reserves. Rules of thumb: recovery factor 15-35% of oil-in-place for a sandstone reservoir, higher (35-60%) for a fractured carbonate. Reserve booking was and remains a commercial and "];
  "chem.crude.exploration.reserve-estimate.uncertainty" [label="Reserve uncertainty and contingency" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Undiscovered reserves behind existing fields were treated as of similar magnitude to proved reserves. The industry view of world ultimately-recoverable oil in 1983 was around 1,500-2,000 billion stock-tank barrels, versus about 700 billion produced or proved."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.exploration-well" [label="Exploration (wildcat) well" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The first hole into a prospect. Cheap success-failure logging with mud logging and core, drilled to the deepest prognosis rather than the shallowest pay. Exploration wells in the early 1980s averaged about 3,000-5,000 m of hole and took 30-90 days offshore."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.exploration-well.wildcat-cost" [label="Wildcat cost and success statistics" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A North Sea or Gulf of Mexico wildcat cost $3-20 million in the early 1980s; the historical industry success rate was about 1 in 10 wells dry, 1 in 15 total wildcats a commercial discovery, and about 1 in 8 wildcats found non-commercial hydrocarbons. Exploration cost per barrel discovered was roughl"];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey" [label="Reflection seismic survey" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A surface source sends a pressure wave down through the rock section; reflectors return it to hydrophones and the travel times are stacked into a two-dimensional depth section. Seismic is a time-domain method: it maps reflectivity contrasts (sandstone/shale, gas-sand amplitude dimming) and gives no "];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.recording-truck" [label="Seismic recording system" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Field recording on 1/2 in or 1 in nine-track magnetic tape (typically 2000 inch/s, 12.5-50 Hz bandwidth) or, from the early 1980s, 12-bit digital field recorders writing 3M/IBM-compatible cartridges. The whole array recorded simultaneously; it was common for the record to be demultiplexed and copied"];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.geophone-array" [label="Seismic geophone array" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="On land, 12 to 48 groups of 10-60 Hz moving-coil geophones per station, each on a spike planted in a levelled hole, cabled and buried. In 1980s 2-D the fold of coverage (usually 6-fold to 24-fold) was chosen by geophone group interval and source spacing, not by 3-D binning."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.geophone-array.takeout-cable" [label="Geophone takeout cable and junction box" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Polythene-insulated multi-pair takeout cable to a junction box, then a line-trap and telemetry radio link to the recording truck. Cable insulation here is polyethylene from the same steam-cracking chain as the C64's power cord jacket."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.geophone-array.geophone" [label="Moving-coil geophone" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 1-2 kg mass suspended in a magnetic field inside a case 3-5 cm across; coil movement induces a signal proportional to ground velocity. A second coil acts as a position feedback for the servo. Cost per channel in the early 1980s was several hundred to a few thousand dollars."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.streamer-cable" [label="Marine seismic streamer and hydrophone cable" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A towed cable up to 3 km long carrying hundreds of hydrophone elements at 12.5-25 m groups; it was a synthetic-fibre-wrapped, oil-filled or water-filled analogue/digital telemetry line whose integrity was the limiting factor on data quality. Hydrophone elements used piezoelectric ceramic (PZT-5) dis"];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.streamer-cable.pzt-element" [label="PZT-5 piezoelectric hydrophone element" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Lead zirconate titanate ceramic discs, typically 25-50 mm diameter, epoxied onto a diaphragm and back-electrode in a silicone-oil-filled housing; sensitivity about -190 dB re 1 V/uPa in the 10-60 Hz band. Silicone oil here is one of the petrochemical elastomers/fluids, unrelated to the case material"];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.explosive-shot" [label="Dynamite shothole source" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Still used for small onshore surveys in the early 1980s: 0.5-5 kg of packaged explosive in a borehole, fired from a firing truck, with the shot hole re-drilled for the next shot. Cheap per shot but slow, noisy and it polluted a producing field's drill cuttings."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.explosive-shot.ammo-case" [label="Seismic shot-hole explosive ammunition" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Cast or pressed explosive in aluminium or paper shells with electric or vacuum-fire detonators, supplied on the same chemical logistics as any blasting operation. This is a one-off consumable of a survey, not part of any C64 material."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.airgun-array" [label="Airgun and marine seismic source" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Offshore and in shallow water the source was a 1,000-10,000 cu-in (16-160 litre) airgun array fired every 10-15 s from the stern of a seismic vessel. Compressed air from compressor trucks on deck; a single 1980s 3-D crew vessel carried up to four sources."];
  "chem.crude.exploration.seismic-survey.vibrator" [label="Seismic vibrator truck" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A truck-mounted hydraulic vibrator sweeps 10-80 Hz into the ground under hold-and-hold conditions; on land it replaced explosive shot holes because it gave repeatable, higher-energy sources with no shothole cleanup. Regionally a 1,000-2,000 m shotpoint spacing was normal in 1980s 2-D work."];
  "metal" [label="Metals, ores and industrial minerals" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Every mineral stream behind a 1982 Commodore 64: the quartzite that becomes silicon, the bauxite and cryolite that becomes aluminium foil, the porphyry copper that becomes PCB track and transformer magnet wire, the tin and lead that become 63/37 solder, the iron ore and coke that become the steel ch"];
  "metal.barium" [label="Barium titanate: the ceramic capacitor dielectric" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The disk and multilayer ceramic capacitors on a 1982 C64 mainboard are barium titanate - BaTiO3 - and barium titanate is an industrial material made from barite, the same heavy white mineral that makes the drilling mud. The chain is barite to barium sulphide to barium carbonate to barium titanate, a"];
  "metal.barium.titanate" [label="Barium titanate powder (BaTiO3)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The solid-state reaction of barium carbonate with titanium dioxide: BaCO3 + TiO2 -\> BaTiO3 + CO2. It happens in a calcination at 1100-1350 C, in a rotary kiln or a batch furnace, and the outcome is a powder of perovskite-structured grains - a cubic cell with the titanium atom at the centre of an oct"];
  "metal.barium-titanate-capacitor" [label="The ceramic capacitor and its dielectric classes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The finished use. A 1982 C64 mainboard carries perhaps 30-60 ceramic capacitors, mostly small discs and a few multilayer blocks. The disc is a pressed or cast barium titanate body, sintered at 1250-1350 C, silver-coated on both faces and fired to make the electrodes, and dipped in an epoxy or phenol"];
  "metal.dielectric-grading" [label="Grade the barium titanate powder into a dielectric class" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The same powder cannot make a Y5V capacitor and an X7R one. Y5V is made from a fine, uniform, high-surface-area powder near the Curie point, where the dielectric constant is enormous and wildly temperature-dependent - a huge capacitance that changes by a factor of five over the working range. X7R is"];
  "metal.dielectric-grading.mlc" [label="Co-fire the internal electrodes in a low-oxygen atmosphere" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A multilayer capacitor's internal electrode is nickel or palladium, printed as a paste and co-fired with the ceramic. That will not work in air - the ceramic would reduce and become a lossy semiconductor - so the whole stack is fired in a low-oxygen atmosphere at 1200-1350 C in hydrogen or in a nitr"];
  "metal.titanium-dioxide" [label="Titanium dioxide from rutile sand" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The titanium dioxide half of the equation, and it comes from a beach sand. Ilmenite, a titanium-iron oxide, is dredged from the heavy-mineral beach sands of the Cap York coast in Queensland, of Kerala in India, of the Mozambique coast and of the Richards Bay area of South Africa; it is separated fro"];
  "metal.ilmenite-sand" [label="Heavy mineral beach sand and the dredge" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a beach or dune sand a few metres thick carrying 1-10% heavy minerals - ilmenite, zircon, rutile, monazite, tourmaline - in a strip along the shoreline. A concentrator dredges a cutter suction pump from the shallow water, washes and upgrades on a shaking table and a spiral, then on gravity"];
  "metal.barite.reduction" [label="Barite to barium sulphide, then barium carbonate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The chemical reduction of barite. Barite plus coke at 900-1100 C in a rotary kiln gives barium sulphide: BaSO4 + 4C -\> BaS + 4CO. That reaction needs about 1100 C to proceed at a useful rate, and the kiln is a long steel tube, 2-3 m diameter and 30-50 m long, rotating at 0.5-1 rpm, heated by a gas o"];
  "metal.hydrogen-sulfide" [label="Hydrogen sulphide from the carbonation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The barium carbonate process makes hydrogen sulphide as a by-product, roughly 0.2 t of H2S per tonne of barium carbonate, and H2S is an acutely toxic gas that a 1982 barium plant either burned in a flare or sent to a Claus plant for elemental sulphur. It is also, if you are unlucky, a gas that kills"];
  "metal.barium-carbonate" [label="Ceramic-grade barium carbonate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A fine white powder, 99%+ BaCO3, and the actual commodity that the barium titanate maker buys. Its iron and sulphur contents are the specification that matters, and both come back from the reduction step. It is also used directly - in the glass frit of the ferrite coat, in the barium ferrite magnet,"];
  "metal.barium-carbonate.calcination" [label="Calcine the carbonate to a reactive powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The barium carbonate that comes out of the carbonation tank is 99.0-99.5% pure but coarse and dense, and barium titanate needs a reactive powder. So the carbonate is calcined at 1000-1200 C to break it down to the oxide and then re-carbonated lightly, or it is ground extremely fine. Either way it is"];
  "metal.barium-carbonate.contamination" [label="Iron and sulphur in the ceramic grade" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ceramic-grade barium carbonate specification is essentially a statement about two impurities. Iron, carried through from the barite and from the flue of the reduction kiln, if it survives into barium titanate, acts as an acceptor and shifts the Curie point and the dielectric constant and hurts t"];
  "metal.barite" [label="Barite (BaSO4) ore, 90-98%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Barite is a very heavy, very white, chemically inert mineral - specific gravity 4.5 - which occurs as veins, as replacement bodies in limestone, and as a residual clay. It has two completely separate markets. The drilling-mud market is by far the biggest and takes barite that has simply been crushed"];
  "metal.barite.drilling-mud" [label="Drilling grade barite and its mud" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The larger half of the barite market, and the reason a chemical-grade barite supplier has to be selective. Drilling mud needs a barite that is dense, grindable to a very fine size, low in soluble salts and above all low in cadmium and other toxic heavy metals, because the drill cuttings go into a mu"];
  "metal.barite.mine" [label="Barite vein and replacement mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Barite is mined as a primary vein mineral - a gangue mineral of many lead-zinc hydrothermal deposits - or as a replacement of limestone. The processing is simple: crush, wash off the clay, and either float or just size and sell. A barite plant of the 1980s was a jaw crusher, a log washer and a desli"];
  "metal.barite.mud-system" [label="Barite, bentonite and lime: the drilling mud system" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A drilling mud is not one material; it is a designed composite. The barite is the weight - it is the only cheap, abundant mineral dense enough to hold back formation pressure at a density of 1.5-2.0. The bentonite is the viscosity and the filtration control, and it works because its sodium montmoril"];
  "metal.lime" [label="Lime (CaO) and calcium" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Lime is one of the most-used industrial chemicals there is, and this tree touches it in four places: the Bayer plant uses it to desilicate and to causticise, the blast furnace uses limestone directly as flux, the PCB fabricator regenerates ferric chloride etchant and neutralises with it, and the met"];
  "metal.lime.scavenger" [label="Lime as a slag flux in tin, lead and silicon smelting" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Beyond alumina and iron, lime shows up as a cheap flux wherever a siliceous slag has to stay liquid and the impurity has to be dumped: tin smelting adds lime to pull silica and some tungsten into the slag, the lead sinter and blast furnace charge roughly 15-25% limestone, and calcium compounds are a"];
  "metal.lime.pcb-etch" [label="Regenerate ferric chloride etchant with lime" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="At the board fab the copper etchant is ferric chloride: CuCl2 + FeCl3 -\> CuCl + FeCl2, and the ferric is regenerated by blowing air through the spent bath, which is where a great deal of the chlorine chemistry of PCB etching comes from. Lime then raises the pH and drops the copper out as copper hydr"];
  "metal.lime.blast-flux" [label="Flux the blast furnace slag with limestone and lime" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The furnace needs its slag liquid at 1450 C, and calcium is how you get there: CaO absorbs silica into a calcium silicate slag whose liquidus is around 1300-1400 C, so the slag runs off the tuyeres before the iron does. The burden carries roughly 0.25-0.35 t of limestone per tonne of hot metal, deli"];
  "metal.lime.bayer-uses" [label="Causticise and desilicate the Bayer liquor" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two unrelated jobs in one plant. Lime added during digestion converts reactive silica (kaolinite) into an insoluble calcium silicate, which would otherwise lock up alumina permanently - the single most important reason a high-silica bauxite is unprocessable. Lime also drives the causticisation step,"];
  "metal.lime.pre-desilication" [label="Pre-desilicate the ore by attrition washing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="By 1982 the more elegant answer to high-silica bauxite had arrived and it needs no lime at all: wash the ore. An attrition scrub and a desliming step take out the kaolinite clay before the ore ever meets the caustic, and a gibbsitic ore washed this way can digest without lime at all. The cost of the"];
  "metal.lime.causticisation" [label="Causticise the carbonate back to caustic soda" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Bayer liquor's sodium carbonate is converted back to caustic with lime: Na2CO3 + Ca(OH)2 -\> 2NaOH + CaCO3, and the calcium carbonate drops out as a filter cake that is a by-product of every tonne of alumina. The cycle is the industry's real control loop, because the caustic that leaves in the re"];
  "metal.lime.slaking" [label="Slaking: quicklime to hydrated lime" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="CaO + H2O -\> Ca(OH)2, releasing about 65% more mass and enough heat to boil water. Slaking is done in a pan with a rotating rake, or in a ball mill in the paper industry, and the product is a fine white powder or a milk. It is done at the steelworks, at the water treatment plant and at the board fab"];
  "metal.lime.cement" [label="Lime in Portland cement" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="By far the largest single use of lime in the world, and the reason the business had a global price: about 90% of all lime produced goes into cement, and cement is the second-largest industrial material after steel. The route is the one already in this branch - limestone, kiln, CaO - and then the cli"];
  "metal.lime.kiln" [label="Shaft and rotary lime kilns" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Limestone plus coke or coal in a vertical shaft 20-40 m tall, or on a rotary kiln, at 1000-1100 C. The vertical kiln - a stack of alternating layers of 250 kg limestone and 40 kg coke in a 2.5-4 m shaft, fired from the bottom - makes the good lime, because the counter-current gas gives a soft, well-"];
  "metal.lime.kiln-gas" [label="The lime kiln gas and its CO2" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A lime kiln makes carbon dioxide as its main product, roughly 0.44 t of CO2 per tonne of CaO, and in 1982 that gas was usually burnt or dumped because there was nowhere to put it. Its two real uses were the Solvay tower, which is built beside its lime kilns, and - much later - the emerging practice "];
  "metal.lime.rotary-kiln" [label="The rotary kiln and the preheater" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 1.5-2.5 m diameter, 40-60 m long rotary kiln, inclined a few degrees, turning 1-3 rpm, heated by a burner at the lower discharge end with coal, coke or gas, and fitted at the upper end with a preheater or a flash dryer for the limestone. The rotary kiln makes worse lime than the shaft kiln - the r"];
  "metal.lime.kiln-clinker" [label="Overburnt lime and the clinker problem" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="If a lime kiln burns a stone too hot for too long, the lime grains sinter together into hard 'clinker' and the lime is unusable: it slakes too slowly and lumps in the mixer. Overburnt lime is a real fraction of the output of a poor kiln and it has to be screened out or ground. The same failure happe"];
  "metal.lime.shaft-kiln" [label="The counter-current shaft kiln" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Because limestone goes in at the top at ambient temperature and lime comes out at the bottom at 1000 C, the shaft has to be a heat exchanger as well as a kiln, and that is why a shaft kiln makes a better lime than a rotary one. The gases go up, the stone goes down, and the last of the fuel burns out"];
  "metal.bentonite" [label="Bentonite binder from a clay pit" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A sodium bentonite with a high swelling capacity, mined from a montmorillonite clay bed and used as the binder that makes a pellet roll up instead of shatter. In 1982 the world bentonite market was dominated by Wyoming and by Italy and the Balkan sources, and it was also the drilling mud of the oil "];
  "metal.barite.rock" [label="Barite vein in limestone or as a hydrothermal gangue" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: white bladed or massive barite filling a fracture, or replacing a fossiliferous limestone bed. The barite has essentially no use as a rock - it is only ever interesting where it happens to be 90% or better and low in iron."];
  "metal.ferrite" [label="Ferrite: transformer and choke cores" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The C64's power supply transformer, the filter chokes, the RF modulator coil and the ferrite beads on the board are all ceramic magnetic materials, not metal. A power ferrite is a spinel - either MnO.Fe2O3.ZnO or NiO.Fe2O3.ZnO - pressed as a powder and fired to 1200-1300 C, which is powder metallurg"];
  "metal.ferrite.radio" [label="Radio-frequency and rod ferrite" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A third shape family: ferrite rod and toroid for antenna and RF applications, pressed with an anisotropic die so the ferrite is oriented in the pressing direction. An isotropic pressed ferrite has half the usable permeability along the pressing axis as across it, because the pores are flattened; a f"];
  "metal.ferrite.radio.aniso-die" [label="The anisotropic pressing die" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A ferrite pressed in an ordinary die has its pores flattened in the pressing direction, and flattened pores cut the permeability in that direction roughly in half - so a plain-pressed ferrite rod is a poor antenna core along its length. To avoid that, a rod core is pressed in a die with a fine centr"];
  "metal.ferrite.hard" [label="Hard ferrites and permanent magnet materials" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The same pressed-and-fired powder metallurgy as the power ferrite, but with no permeability requirement and a coercive force instead. Barium and strontium hexaferrite, BaFe12O19 and SrFe12O19, are sintered into magnet blocks for small motors and, in this tree, for the permanent magnets inside a magn"];
  "metal.ferrite.strontium-ferrite" [label="Strontium ferrite magnet" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="SrFe12O19 pressed and fired like a power ferrite, then cooled through the Curie point in a magnetic field so the hexferrite grains line up. It is brittle, it is an oxide, and its key property is that it does not need any rare earth at all - which is exactly why barium and strontium ferrite are the s"];
  "metal.strontium-carbonate" [label="Strontium carbonate" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The only geologically interesting part: strontium is the rarest of the three alkaline-earth metals used in ferrites and ceramics, because it has no ore of its own that anyone uses. Strontium sulfate (celestine) is mined in Spain, Mexico and Iran, and almost all of it is converted to strontium carbon"];
  "metal.ferrite.manufacture" [label="Ferrite powder metallurgy from blend to fired core" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Five steps, all of them inherited from the ceramic industry, none of them changed since the 1950s. Blend: the oxides are mixed wet in a ball mill or a vibrating mill to a few tenths of a percent. Calcine or pre-sinter: the blend is heated in air to about 900-1100 C, which drives off the moisture and"];
  "metal.ferrite.quality" [label="Control the firing atmosphere and the schedule" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The most closely guarded part of the whole ferrite industry is the firing curve: the ramp rate, the hold times, and the oxygen partial pressure through a 12-30 hour cycle. Too fast through the Curie point and the grain boundaries form in the wrong order and the core is lossy; too oxidising and Mn2+ "];
  "metal.ferrite.grinding" [label="Grinding the fired part to size" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Because the fired part cannot be held to a useful dimension by pressing, it is lapped and ground. An E core is face-ground and the bore is ground; a toroid is OD and ID ground. In 1982 this was a double-disc or a planetary grinder with a diamond wheel, and it was expensive enough that a great many f"];
  "metal.ferrite.sintering" [label="Firing at 1200-1300 C and the shrinkage" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The pressed part is fired on a belt or in a tunnel kiln in air, and in the last stage of the sintering it goes through the Curie temperature - about 200-250 C for MnZn - which is a magnetic transition, and above it the permeability of the material is irrelevant because it is paramagnetic. That is wh"];
  "metal.ferrite.pressing" [label="Dry pressing in a tool steel die" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The pressed shape is the finished shape - an E, an EE, a toroid, a drum core - and the tool is a hardened steel die in a 100-250 tonne press. Ferrite presses in 1982 were 100-250 t, usually with a single or double action upper punch, and the cycle time for a small E core was 0.3-2 s. The part is pre"];
  "metal.ferrite.calcination" [label="Calcination and the spinel formation" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The chemical heart of the branch. Below the calcination temperature the blend is a mechanical mixture; by 900-1100 C the oxides have reacted and the cubic spinel lattice - which has three cation sites and eight for every 32 oxygen atoms - has formed, with the zinc on the tetrahedral sites, the manga"];
  "metal.ferrite.mixing" [label="Wet mixing and the ceramic ball mill" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The oxide blend is mixed wet in a rubber-lined ball mill or a vibrating mill, because water is the only way to get all three oxides into intimate contact within half a percent. A 1982 MnZn blend ran in a 1-3 m vibratory mill at 25-50 Hz, or in a ball mill with a few hundred kilos of zirconia or rubb"];
  "metal.ferrite.granulation" [label="Granulation and the binder" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Free powder flows; a pressed ferrite part needs the powder to fill the die uniformly, so the powder is granulated with 0.5-2% of a polyvinyl alcohol or a PVA-polyethylene glycol binder and 8-12% water, on a spray granulator or a pan granulator, into 100-400 um granules. The granule has to survive be"];
  "metal.ferrite.raw-materials" [label="Ferrite raw materials: the oxide blend" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The three powders that get blended, and each one has its own industrial route. The iron oxide is either mill scale from the aluminium or the steel mill - washed, dried and milled, and the cheapest source - or a precipitated oxide made by acid leaching hematite. The manganese is manganese carbonate, "];
  "metal.ferrite.mix-house" [label="The mix house and the recipe book" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The place where a ferrite formula is actually executed. A blend of perhaps 300 kg of oxide is weighed into a mixer - a ribbon blender or a ball mill - to 0.1 kg, sampled, analysed, and then the result is used to correct the next batch. The correction logic is where the manufacturer differs: some mak"];
  "metal.nickel.nickel-oxide" [label="Nickel oxide (NiO)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The commercially traded intermediate. The pregnant solvent is stripped of ammonia and boiled, basic nickel carbonate NiCO3.2Ni(OH)2.4H2O drops out, it is filtered and washed, and it is calcined at 400-600 C to green-black nickel oxide at 99.0-99.5% Ni. In 1982 the overwhelming majority of nickel oxi"];
  "metal.zinc.oxide" [label="Zinc oxide sinter: the smelter's intermediate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The dead-roast sinter is the actual feed to the leach circuit, and its specification is a compromise between the roast and the leach. It wants to be as completely roasted as possible so that the zinc is all in the oxide, but it also wants to be porous enough for the sulphuric acid to reach it, which"];
  "metal.manganese-carbonate" [label="Manganese carbonate" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Not a mined material: a chemical precipitate. High-purity manganese sulphate solution is treated with sodium or ammonium carbonate at 60-90 C and pH 5-7, and manganese carbonate drops out as a pale pink powder. The iron must be kept out because MnCO3 with 1-2% iron pushes the ferrite's Curie tempera"];
  "metal.ferrite.iron-oxide-source" [label="Iron oxide from mill scale or from a precipitate" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The cheapest ferrite iron oxide is the mill scale from an aluminium rolling mill or a steel mill - but it comes as a mix of FeO, Fe3O4 and Fe2O3 plus the alumina or silica the original metal carried, so it is milled and then passed over a strong magnetic separator to take out the magnetite, and the "];
  "metal.ferrite.nizn" [label="NiZn ferrite" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The second family. Substituting nickel oxide for most of the manganese oxide raises the resistivity of the grain boundary by a factor of a hundred, so the eddy-current loss at high frequency collapses and NiZn is usable to about 100 MHz instead of 1 MHz. The price is a permeability of only 15-100 wh"];
  "metal.ferrite.nizn.doping" [label="NiZn dopants: copper, cobalt and calcium" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The NiZn family is doped with copper and cobalt to shape the grain-boundary physics, and with calcium to control the sintering. The NiZn product of the 1980s was deliberately split by composition into a high-permeability low-loss grade for transformers below 1 MHz and a very high-permeability grade "];
  "metal.ferrite.nizn.co-source" [label="The nickel and cobalt raw materials for NiZn" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The Ni-Zn family's nickel does not come as metal. It comes as nickel oxide, or as green nickel hydroxide precipitate from a laterite leach, and the cobalt comes either as cobalt metal or - much more cheaply, and this is the point - as a cobalt hydroxide carried out of the Sherritt-Gordon ammonia cir"];
  "metal.nickel.cobalt-carbonyl" [label="Dicobalt octacarbonyl, Co2(CO)8" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cobalt in the Mond residue is a genuine industrial source of cobalt: the residue is treated with copper at 200 C under hydrogen to reduce the cobalt and copper carbonyls, and then re-carbonylated to Co2(CO)8, which is filtered out and decomposed. In 1982 the two leading producers - INCO in Canad"];
  "metal.ferrite.modulator" [label="The RF modulator coil" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The C64's TV modulator, which puts the composite video onto the RF of channel 3 or 4 so a television could display the machine, contains a small ferrite-loaded coil - a few turns of enamelled copper wire on a ferrite core or threaded through a ferrite bead. This is the one place in the machine where"];
  "metal.ferrite.bead" [label="Ferrite bead: a small cylinder of NiZn or MnZn" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A bead is just a pressed and fired ferrite with a hole through it, made on a bead press with a die that forms the cylinder in a few milliseconds. A 1982 board might carry a dozen: one on each supply line to the analogue section, one on the video line, one or two on the mains lead of the supply. The "];
  "metal.ferrite.mnzn" [label="MnZn power ferrite" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The material of the C64's transformer core, the rectifier choke and the bulk filter chokes. Its job is to carry a large alternating flux at 15-65 kHz with as little loss and as little audible noise as possible, and that is a tight compromise: MnO raises the permeability and lowers the magnetostricti"];
  "metal.ferrite.dopants" [label="Dopants: calcium, silicon, cobalt and the grain boundaries" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The last half percent of the ferrite recipe does most of the work. A small calcium and silicon addition forms a grain-boundary phase that makes the grain boundaries insulating and therefore suppresses eddy-current loss. Cobalt raises the anisotropy and the permeability at low field, which matters fo"];
  "metal.ferrite.choke" [label="Filter chokes and the common-mode choke" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The bulk filter chokes in a 1982 linear-supply C64 are 470 uH to a few millihenry with a current rating of 1-3 A, wound on an EE or a bobbin core of the same MnZn material. The switch-mode version instead has a common-mode choke: two windings on a single core, arranged so the two currents are equal "];
  "metal.ferrite.e-core" [label="E/EI/EE core geometry and the transformer" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The shape is dictated by the winding, and the 1982 supply for small switchers was a pair of E cores with a centre leg and a window, assembled 'E-E' or interleaved 'EI' so that the joint is staggered and the effective air gap is halved. The window has to fit the magnetising inductance and the wire; t"];
  "metal.ferrite.gap-scrape" [label="Cutting the air gap in the centre leg" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="An ungapped ferrite E core has a very high inductance and would not regulate a switch-mode supply; the switcher's magnetising current would be wrong and the whole design fails. So the gap is made deliberately, and on a 1982 E core it was cut with a carbide-tipped scraper - literally a hand blade run"];
  "metal.iron" [label="Iron ore: the hematite and magnetite end of the chain" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The bottom of the steel branch. Iron ore is dug, concentrated, reduced and never appears in a C64, and this branch exists so that the steel branch terminates in a mine rather than in an abstraction. The 1982 iron ore trade was dominated by three names: Mount Whaleback and the Pilbara in Western Aust"];
  "metal.hematite" [label="Hematite (Fe2O3) and the Pilbara" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Hematite is a steel-grey to red mineral that occurs as specular crystals, as kidney ore, and - the mass of the world's supply - as itabirite and hematite replacements in a banded iron formation. The Pilbara ore bodies in Western Australia are the richest hematite in the world at 62-66% Fe with almos"];
  "metal.hematite.pilbara" [label="Pilbara open-pit iron mine and the ore loader" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Pilbara mines in 1982 were the mining engineering standard for the industry. A 15 m bench, 100-250 tonne haul trucks, 3-4 cubic metre shovels, and - this is the part everybody photographs - the huge travelling ore loaders that straddle the width of a haul truck and scoop up a 40-60 tonne load in"];
  "metal.hematite.stockyard" [label="The ore stockyard and the ship loader" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Because the ships are 180,000-250,000 dwt and the mine's output is lumpy, the ore goes into a 1-2 million tonne stockyard with a stacker and a travelling shiploader. The shiploader is a 100 m long gantry on rails with a slewing chute that trims the ship's hold as it loads, so a 250,000 dwt vessel is"];
  "metal.hematite.crushing" [label="Crushing, screening and the lump/fines split" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ore is crushed in a gyratory and then on a screen, and the product is split three ways: lump ore above 6.3 mm that goes straight into the blast furnace, fines below 6.3 mm that must be sintered or pelletised, and a reject stream of the banded iron formation that is too low grade to smelt. The lu"];
  "metal.banded-iron-formation" [label="Banded iron formation: a chemical precipitate from seawater" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the whole iron and steel branch, and one of the great ones. Around 2.0-1.8 billion years ago, the oceans contained dissolved ferrous iron and oxygen appeared in the atmosphere as a result of an earlier generation of photosynthetic organisms. Wherever the oxygen-rich surface water sank an"];
  "metal.bif-banding" [label="The silica and iron oxide banding" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf, described properly: a stack of alternating 1 mm to 10 m bands of chert (cryptocrystalline silica) and iron oxide, the whole thing metamorphosed, with the hematite in the oxidised upper part and magnetite in the reduced lower part and the P-rich band - the band that gives the Sudbury nickel"];
  "metal.steel" [label="Steel: sheet, stainless, tin plate, tool steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Steel is the machine's frame, its shields, its fasteners and its tooling, and in mass it dominates everything else in the C64. The route is: iron ore plus coke plus limestone into a blast furnace for hot metal; hot metal plus oxygen plus scrap into a steelmaking vessel; steel through a continuous ca"];
  "metal.die-steel" [label="Tool steel: SKD61 / H13 / D2, the moulds and dies" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64's case, the keycaps, the key plungers and the disk-drive and modulator housings were all made in injection-moulding dies, and every one of those dies and every aluminium extrusion die was cut from tool steel. SKD61 in Japan and H13 in the US and DIN X40CrMoV51 in Europe are the same alloy: 5"];
  "metal.die-steel.vacuum" [label="Vacuum melting and the purity of tool steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A tool steel that contains a hundred parts per million of sulphur will embrittle, and one with two hundred ppm of oxygen will pit. So the melt for a die is made in a vacuum induction or a vacuum arc furnace rather than in an air-melted crucible, and the ingot is remelted by the VAR or the EB process"];
  "metal.vanadium" [label="Vanadium: a small alloying element" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Included only because tool steel and the AOD of stainless steel both use it, and because the source is genuinely unusual. Most vanadium came from vanadiferous titanomagnetite - a magnetite containing 1-6% vanadium - which was a problem in the iron ore of the Panzhihua district in China and in the U."];
  "metal.vanadium.titanomagnetite" [label="Vanadiferous titanomagnetite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a magnetite with the iron partly replaced by titanium and vanadium, occurring as an iron ore in itself. At Panzhihua it is a magnetite-ilmenite ore in a vanadium-bearing magnetite gabbro; in the Mesabi it was a nuisance component of the taconite. Either way it is an iron ore, and the vanad"];
  "metal.molybdenum" [label="Molybdenum: a tool steel alloying element" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Molybdenum is in the tool steel, in the stainless and in the hydrodesulphurisation catalyst, so it belongs in this tree, but its own branch is short because in 1982 almost no molybdenum came from a molybdenum mine: about half came from the molybdenite recovered as a by-product of the porphyry copper"];
  "metal.molybdenum.scheelite" [label="Scheelite and wolframite: the limestone vein alternative" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Almost all molybdenum came from porphyry copper, but there is a second source: the molybdenite that sits in the tungsten skarn and vein deposits - the Climax and Henderson molybdenum orebodies in Colorado were actually a Colorado Clueca-type porphyry with a molybdenum-bearing skarn. The relevant geo"];
  "metal.molybdenite" [label="Molybdenite in a porphyry" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Soft, greasy, platy flakes of MoS2 in the quartz and feldspar of a granitic porphyry, associated with the copper minerals of the Climax and Henderson molybdenum operations or with the Bingham copper concentrate. The leaf is the same porphyry intrusion the copper branch ends in, which is a genuine sh"];
  "metal.stainless.304" [label="AISI 304 and 430 stainless steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The iron-chromium-nickel family, and a deliberate convergence point of three branches in this file. 304 is 18% chromium, 8% nickel, the balance iron - austenitic, non-magnetic, non-hardenable. 430 is 16-18% chromium with no nickel - ferritic, magnetic, and cheap. Both are made the way any steel is m"];
  "metal.stainless.jewel-audio" [label="Where a 1982 C64's stainless actually is" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Not much: the outer shell of the composite or RCA connector, the shield cans over the analogue sections, some of the tuner and modulator housings, the hinge hardware, and any stainless fastener. In practice much of what looks like stainless in 1982 consumer electronics was nickel-chrome electroplate"];
  "metal.stainless.cold-work" [label="Cold rolling, bright annealing and finishing of stainless" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Stainless is rolled and formed by the same equipment as mild steel - a Sendimir mill, a continuous annealing line, a Timesaver - but the tooling is different, because austenitic stainless work-hardens violently and needs much more force, and because a bright anneal must be carried out in hydrogen or"];
  "metal.electrical-steel" [label="Grain-oriented electrical steel (GOES)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64's power supply transformer is the one place where the metal of the machine is deliberately not stainless or mild steel. Grain-oriented electrical steel is a 3.2% silicon low-carbon steel whose grains have been given a preferred orientation - the classic route is a 700-1000 C anneal in hydrog"];
  "metal.electrical-steel.casting" [label="Continuous casting and the Goss texture formation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Electrical steel is continuously cast, hot rolled, and then given the two anneals that make it grain-oriented. The first, at 800-1100 C, is an ordinary recrystallisation anneal in wet hydrogen; the second, at 1100-1200 C, is the orientation anneal, in which the steel is held just below the Curie poi"];
  "metal.ferrosilicon" [label="Ferrosilicon in the submerged arc furnace" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Ferrosilicon at 45-75% silicon is made in a submerged arc furnace very like the one that makes metallurgical silicon, but with more coke and a much higher silica ratio in the slag: SiO2 + 2C -\> Si + 2CO, with the silicon going into the ferroalloy instead of condensing as dust. The largest producers "];
  "metal.coke" [label="Coke and coke oven gas: the reducing agent" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Coke is the black, porous, 90% carbon solid that makes the blast furnace possible, and it is also the reductant in the tin, lead, zinc, manganese, ferrochrome and ferrosilicon furnaces. A tonne of coke is roughly 70% of the fuel and 30% of the reductant in a blast furnace charge, and nothing works w"];
  "metal.coke.gas" [label="Coke oven gas and its by-products" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The gas is the profit centre as well as the fuel. After cooling it goes through a primary cooler and a benzol washer, which strips out benzene, toluene and xylenes; then a saturator removes tar and ammonia; then a Quinolining or electrostatic precipitator removes the last of the dust. What is left i"];
  "metal.benzene" [label="Crude benzene from the coke oven" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 1982 coke oven produced a very large fraction of the world's synthetic benzene, and benzene was the root of the styrene-polystyrene, phenol-formaldehyde and epoxy industries - so this node is where the coal branch and the plastics branch join. The benzol was drawn out of the oven gas with a wash o"];
  "metal.coke.quenching" [label="Quench the hot coke, wet or dry" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The coke comes out of the oven at 1000-1100 C and must be cooled fast or it will keep burning. Wet quenching sprays it with 3-4 m of water in a 30-60 s tower; the water flashes to steam, the coke is cooled to about 200 C and comes out wet with 3-8% moisture, which the blast furnace operator hates. D"];
  "metal.coke.quench-blow" [label="The coke quench blow tank and its drain" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Quenching has three products, not one: the coke, the water with the fine carbon in it, and the coke breeze - the 3-10 mm fraction that the blast furnace cannot use because it would be blown through the burden. Breeze is worth something: a coke plant sells it for sinter-plant fuel, for foundry mouldi"];
  "metal.coke.battery" [label="The battery, its flue and the chimney" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A coke battery is a long brick building - 50-100 ovens in a row, 40-60 m long, with a 60-70 m chimney at one end and a quenching tower at the other. Between every pair of ovens is a heating flue, so there are twice as many flues as ovens, and the gas from the ovens is burned in those flues and the h"];
  "metal.coke.cold-strip" [label="The cold-strip push and the bench" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The hottest, ugliest machine in the coking plant: a horizontal bar on a chain, 3-5 m long, permanently red, that pushes red coke out of the chambers onto the collecting bench. Its life is measured in weeks and its water-cooling is the operator's main concern. Everyone who worked on a battery in the "];
  "metal.coke.tar-pitch" [label="Coal tar and pitch: the binder under everything" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The tar that comes off a coke oven condenses as a dense black liquid, and it is the source of three of the most important carbonaceous materials in this file. Distilled, it gives crude tar, heavy creosote oil, and pitch - the binder in the carbon anode, the coke-battery electrode, the extrusion die "];
  "metal.coke.tar" [label="Crude coal tar and creosote" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The tar is separated into a light fraction (benzol wash oil and crude benzene), a middle fraction of heavy oils used as a coking-plant quench medium and a wood preservative, and a heavy pitch fraction. Creosote as a wood preservative is a genuinely large market and it is the same molecule family as "];
  "metal.pitch.from-pitch" [label="The coke oven feeds six branches of this tree" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Putting the carbon branch together as one loop. Coal is distilled for rosin and turpentine on one side and for tar and pitch on the other; the pitch becomes the binder in the carbon anode, which becomes aluminium; the coke from the same oven becomes the fuel for the silica reduction that becomes the"];
  "metal.soft-pitch" [label="Soft pitch and hard pitch" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Pitch is classified by softening point: soft pitch around 40-70 C, medium 70-110 C, hard pitch above 110 C. The carbon anode wants a medium-to-hard pitch for the extrusion binder; the pot lining wants a softer grade that can be rammed hot. Pitch is also a dense, black, aromatic, carcinogenic materia"];
  "metal.carbon.anode" [label="Carbon anode for the Hall-Heroult cell" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Calcined petroleum coke at 90-95% carbon, ground to 1-20 mm, mixed with 25-30% of crushed and baked coke returns and 25-30% coal-tar pitch as the binder, extruded through a die with a threaded stud on the end, and roasted at 1000-1100 C for 20-30 hours. The point of roasting rather than baking is to"];
  "metal.coke.oven" [label="Coking in a battery of by-product ovens" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two hundred and fifty horizontal chambers, 5-6.5 m long, 0.4-0.6 m wide and 3.5-5 m high, arranged in a brick battery 40-100 m long, heated in the flues between them by coke oven gas and reheated to 1100-1300 C. A charging car pushes 20-30 tonnes of crushed coal through a hopper into each chamber in"];
  "metal.coke.quench-tower" [label="The quench tower and the coke push" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The physical heart of the battery: a tall steel tower with spray nozzles fed by a 300 mm main, a receiving pan, and a surge hopper from which a 2-3 m pusher car pushes the cooled coke onto a belt. Behind it stands the battery, bricked with about 20,000 refractory bricks per oven chamber in the moder"];
  "metal.coking-coal" [label="Coking coal, caking and prime coking blends" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Not any coal: a blend of coals that fuse and solidify into a coherent cellular cake when heated. The caking power comes from the vitrinite, and the best coking coal is a low-volatile, medium-bituminous coal that goes plastic around 350-450 C. The 1982 sources were the Appalachian basin (the US steel"];
  "metal.coal-preparation" [label="Coal preparation: crushing, washing, blending" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Run-of-mine coking coal is crushed to under 50 mm and washed, because the ash that matters is the mineral matter - clay bands and pyritic nodules - that can be taken off by a dense-media wash at about 1.4-1.6 specific gravity. The washed coal then goes to a blending yard, because a coke oven wants a"];
  "metal.coal-tailings" [label="The wash plant tailings and the creek" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other end of the coal preparation. Dense-medium washing rejects 15-30% of the mined tonnage as a fine waste with a high ash content, and in the Appalachian coal country in the early 1980s that waste was routinely placed in the creeks. The valley fills and the stream failures - the Buffalo Creek "];
  "metal.valley-fill" [label="Rehabilitate the spoil by valley fill" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The deep end of coal preparation's waste problem. In a contour mine the seam outcrops along a hillside, the coal is taken off along the contour and the overburden is pushed into the valley below, so the terrain ends up as a stream of grey waste from the highwall to the toe. It is cheap mining - no s"];
  "metal.coal-mine" [label="Longwall coal mine at 300-1000 m" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A coking coal mine in 1982 was almost always a mechanised longwall: a shearer cuts a 1.5-3 m thick seam across the face, the roof is allowed to collapse, the coal is loaded onto a pan chain conveyor and a main belt carries it to a preparation plant and up the shaft. The seam is a few metres thick, s"];
  "metal.coking-coal.seam" [label="Bituminous coal seam (Middle and Lower Kittanning)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a 2-3 m band of bright, banded, caking bituminous coal lying within a cyclothemic sequence of sandstone, shale and fireclay - the same sequence that holds the Pennsylvanian iron ore, coal and limestone together in the Appalachian basin, and the reason Pittsburgh is the steel capital of Ame"];
  "metal.quartzite" [label="High-grade quartzite (97-99% SiO2)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A metamorphosed, recrystallised quartz sandstone that is quarried rather than deep-mined. The chemical silicon industry does not use beach sand at all - it uses very low iron, very low aluminium quartzite, because Al and Fe are the impurities that survive the entire chain into the crystal. Two regio"];
  "metal.quartzite.quarry" [label="Quartzite quarry, ~100,000 t/year" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Norwegian quartzite quarry is a bench mine in metamorphosed sandstone: drilling, blasting, a primary jaw crusher, and a sorting step where workers - and, later, optical sorters - remove the visibly iron-stained lumps by hand. The rock is then crushed, washed on a sluice or in a scrubber, dried a"];
  "metal.quartzite.rock" [label="Quartz sandstone protolith (metaquartzite)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The rock as a geologist would name it: a quartz-rich sandstone or siltstone, now a hard white metaquartzite of interlocking quartz grains with the bedding picked out by iron oxides. Some of the Norwegian material is a hydrothermal replacement body in a metasedimentary sequence. Either way the leaf o"];
  "metal.electrical-steel.silicon-source" [label="Make the ferrosilicon and add it to the steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="How does 3% silicon get into sheet steel at all? Ferrosilicon is added to the ladle, or - in the parent process - the ore is smelted with silica and coke in a submerged arc furnace to give 45-75% Si ferroalloy, and that alloy is added to the steel in the ladle or in the EAF. So the silicon steel bra"];
  "metal.electrical-steel.lamination" [label="E-E laminations and the stamping" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 0.3 mm strip is blanked and stamped into E and I shapes - or, in a cheaper 1982 supply, into E-E pairs and then stacked and interlocked - on a 400-800 tonne mechanical or hydraulic press with a compound die that does the whole lamination stack in one stroke. The stamping is heavily lubricated an"];
  "metal.tinplate" [label="Tinplate: the tin-coated steel sheet" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tinplate is 0.15-0.3 mm of low-carbon steel with 1.1-11.2 g/m2 of tin on both sides, made on a continuous electrolytic line at 4-8 m/s. The strip is degreased and pickled in hydrochloric or sulfuric acid, rinsed, then tinned in a stannous sulphate bath with a fluoroborate additive, rinsed, and eithe"];
  "metal.tinplate.tin-pots" [label="The tinning line: greening, tinning, soaking" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The line has four electrolytic tanks in series - a 'greening' or pretreat tank to strip the last oxide, two or more tinning tanks with soluble tin anodes, and a 'soaking' tank to heal any bare spots. The strip runs through as a ribbon, and the tin is applied at a controlled thickness by the current "];
  "metal.steel.sheet" [label="Hot and cold rolled sheet steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The slab goes to a reversing hot rolling mill at 1150-1250 C - first through a roughing stand, then a rail mill or a continuous mill - and comes out as strip 2-12 mm wide. Then the cold mill, a Sendimir or a multi-high cluster mill with a Sendzimir Z-High line upgrade, takes it to 0.2-0.5 mm, with t"];
  "metal.steel.surface" [label="Pickle, oil and specify the surface" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cold-rolled sheet is covered in mill scale and must be pickled in hydrochloric or sulfuric acid to remove it, or the next operation cannot stick to it. Then it is oiled or lacquered so it does not rust in storage. On the 1980s tinplate line the acid was hydrochloric, which put a genuinely dangerous "];
  "metal.steel.heat-treatment" [label="Anneal and temper the sheet to its specification" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cold-rolled low-carbon steel is hard and full of internal stress, so it is annealed in a continuous line - a few seconds to a few minutes at 700-850 C in a hydrogen-nitrogen atmosphere - to recrystallise it into soft equiaxed grain, and then, if it is going to be drawn rather than stamped, given a t"];
  "metal.rolling-oil" [label="Steel rolling oil: emulsion and hydraulic oil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Hot rolling of steel uses water emulsion; cold rolling uses a neat oil or a water-in-oil emulsion, chemically similar to the aluminium rolling lubricant but with a much higher base oil viscosity and a lot of boundary additives. So the C64's steel mill and its aluminium mill are the same kind of lubr"];
  "metal.steel.caster" [label="Continuous-cast the steel slab" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Steel is not poured into moulds one heat at a time; it is cast continuously. Liquid steel at 1600 C is fed from a tundish through a nozzle into a water-cooled copper mould 1-2.5 m long, where the shell freezes in seconds. The strand then passes through a series of spray headers that remove the rest "];
  "metal.steel.mould-wall" [label="The copper mould wall of the continuous caster" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The copper alloy plate - phosphor bronze in most designs - that does the single hardest job in the whole of steelmaking, withdrawing heat from 1600 C steel at a rate that makes a millimetre of metal freeze in a second, and doing it hundreds of thousands of times. The plates are thin, 5-30 mm, and ar"];
  "metal.steel.vessel-choice" [label="Choosing the steelmaking vessel in 1982" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Three vessels made the world's steel in 1982 and only one of them was winning. A short parent node so the basic oxygen furnace, the dying open hearth and the scrap-based electric arc furnace sit side by side instead of inside one another."];
  "metal.steel.ladle" [label="Ladle treatment, degassing and the steel's last chemistry" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="In 1982 the most important development in steelmaking was not the furnace but the ladle. A 1980s steel ladle is a 100-200 t vessel on a rotating turret, and it is where three things happen in sequence. Secondary metallurgy: argon is stirred in to homogenise the temperature and composition and to flu"];
  "metal.steel.eaf" [label="Electric arc furnace on scrap" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other route, and the one that was growing fastest in the 1980s. Three carbon electrodes are lowered into a 30-100 t shell furnace and an arc struck, melting scrap with 600-1,500 A at 30-50 V - a physically brutal heat transfer that lets a small furnace melt steel quickly. In 1982 the units were "];
  "metal.carbon-electrode" [label="Carbon electrodes from needle coke and pitch" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The EAF, the submerged arc furnaces of the ferroalloy industries, the aluminium pot anodes and the large carbon blocks of the blast furnace all consume the same two materials: calcined petroleum coke or needle coke, and coal-tar pitch as a binder. An electrode is milled green - coke, pitch and somet"];
  "metal.steel.open-hearth" [label="Open hearth furnace steelmaking, in decline" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 200-500 t rectangular basin of refractory with a hearth at 1300-1400 C, in which pig iron and scrap were melted while fuel gas or oil was burned above them and air was blown across the surface. It was dominant in 1920-1960 and it took 6-10 hours for a heat, produced a lot of nitrogen (so it could "];
  "metal.steel.bof" [label="Basic oxygen furnace steelmaking" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Hot metal is charged into a 40-60 t vessel - a pear-shaped converter with a refractory lining - together with about 20-30% scrap, and then a lance lowered into the bath on a boom blows 99.5% oxygen at 40,000-80,000 Nm3/h for 15-25 minutes. Three things happen at once: the carbon burns, the silicon a"];
  "metal.iron.hot-metal" [label="Blast furnace hot metal (4.3% C)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 25-30 m tall, 8-11 m wide stack, charged at the top with sinter, pellets, lump ore, coke and limestone, and blown at the bottom through 3-4 tuyeres with 1000-1300 C blast at a pressure of 2-4 bar. The iron oxide is reduced in stages on the way down - hematite to magnetite at 400-600 C, magnetite t"];
  "metal.iron.burden-shaft" [label="The furnace stack, throat and bustle pipe" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 20-30 m stack is lined with 1,000-2,000 mm of refractory and carries the burden down through a rotating throat seal into a stock column. The burden rests on a 'ledge' in the wall where the gas changes from upward to horizontal flow - the place every blast furnace operator watches, because if the"];
  "metal.iron.copper-cooler" [label="Copper stave hearth cooling" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The stave coolers that protect the hearth from 1500 C molten iron are made of copper or copper alloy with a water passage cast into them, because copper conducts heat far better than cast iron. They are a copper industry product, and they are one of the more elegant cross-links in this tree: the bla"];
  "metal.iron.burden" [label="Blast furnace burden: sinter, pellets, lump ore, coke and flux" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The furnace eats a precisely weighed blend, batched in 1-3 t bins and dumped in a rotating order around the bell-less throat so the burden descends evenly. Sinter is 55-65% Fe and 8-12% gangue; pellets are 62-66% Fe and are nearly always the majority of the iron-bearing burden in a modern furnace be"];
  "metal.pellet" [label="Pelletising the concentrate on a disc or a pelletiser" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Very fine concentrate - under 45 um - is mixed with 5-8% bentonite binder and 0.5-1% limestone, moistened, and fed to a rotating disc pelletiser or a drum pelletiser where 3-10 mm green pellets are rolled up by capillary action. The green pellets are then fired on a travelling grate at 1200-1350 C i"];
  "metal.sinter" [label="Sinter the fine ore on a travelling grate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Fine iron ore cannot be charged into a blast furnace - it would be blown away or it would pack. So it is sintered: mixed with 8-12% returning sinter fines, 5-7% coke and 10-20% lime, moistened to 6-8%, spread in a 0.7-1.0 m bed on a travelling grate, and ignited. At 1200-1400 C the bed self-fuses in"];
  "metal.coke-breeze" [label="Coke breeze: the sinter fuel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 3-10 mm fraction of coke, screened out at the coke plant, that would be too small for the blast furnace. A steelworks' sinter plant is therefore a consumer of its own coke plant's fines - a neat example of an internal recycling loop in the iron and carbon chain that closes from the coke oven's q"];
  "metal.tantalum" [label="Tantalum powder for electrolytic capacitors" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 1982 C64 has a handful of tantalum capacitors on the mainboard, in the analogue filter section, and tantalum beads in small packages. Each one is a sintered porous tantalum anode with a tantalum oxide dielectric and a solid manganese dioxide or a liquid electrolyte. The tantalum metal is won from "];
  "metal.tantalum.separation" [label="Separation of tantalum from niobium: K2TaF7" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="From the fluoride solution the tantalum is selectively precipitated as potassium heptafluorotantalate, K2TaF7, by adding potassium chloride and hydrofluoric acid at 70-90 C - and this is the step that separates it from niobium, because K2TaF7 is far less soluble than K2NbF7. The K2TaF7 crystals are "];
  "metal.tantalum.kcl-cycle" [label="Recycling the potassium and fluorine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The potassium in K2TaF7 and the fluorine in it both come back. The mother liquor from the precipitation is evaporated and the potassium is recycled, and the fluoride is recovered from the aluminium fluoride slag in the metal reduction by hydrolysing it to fluoride and re-fluorinating. A modern tanta"];
  "metal.tantalum.powder" [label="Tantalum powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The anode of a tantalum capacitor is a pressed and sintered tantalum pellet of porous sintered tantalum powder, sintered at 1200-1400 C under vacuum, then anodised - electrolytically grown Ta2O5 - to a formation voltage of about 2-4 times the working voltage. The dielectric is the anodised oxide its"];
  "metal.tantalum.hf-digest" [label="Hydrofluoric acid digestion at 100-200 C" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The concentrate is digested in a mixture of hydrofluoric acid, nitric acid and hydrochloric acid at 100-200 C in a Teflon-lined or Monel vessel with a condenser to keep the HF in the loop. Ta2O5 and Nb2O5 both dissolve as pentafluorides, while iron, tin, thorium and most of the rare earths stay as i"];
  "metal.tantalum.digest-vessel" [label="The fluoride digestion vessel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The digestor is the most aggressively corrosive piece of equipment in the metals tree. Anhydrous hydrofluoric acid at 100-200 C attacks glass, attacks most metals, and attacks concrete; so the vessel is Monel, nickel, or Teflon-lined, with the condenser and the piping also in Monel, and there is a c"];
  "metal.nickel-monel" [label="Monel: nickel with copper" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The alloy that made the fluorine chemistry of the 1980s possible: 60-70% nickel with 30% copper, and sometimes molybdenum, made in the same vacuum-melted, cast-and-rolled route as any nickel alloy. Monel resists hydrofluoric acid better than almost anything because the nickel fluoride film that form"];
  "metal.tantalum.thorium" [label="Thorium in the coltan: a radioactive by-product" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Columbite-tantalite carries thorium and uranium in solid solution, typically 0.01-0.5% Th as ThO2, and because thorium's chemistry is so close to the rare earths it follows them through the whole fluoride circuit and reports with the rare-earth product. It is an alpha emitter, so the refinery's thor"];
  "metal.monazite.beach" [label="The heavy-mineral beach and its dredge" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the tantalum branch's by-product chain: a beach on the east coast of Queensland or on the Malabar coast of Kerala where the wave has concentrated ilmenite, zircon, rutile, monazite and xenotime into a dark, 1-10% heavy-mineral band a metre or two thick in the sand. It is worked by a suct"];
  "metal.tantalum.monazite" [label="Monazite, the thorium and rare-earth carrier" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Monazite, (Ce,La,Nd,Th)PO4, is a heavy placer mineral from the same beach sands as the ilmenite and the rutile - the Kerala and Queensland and Cape Breton sands. It is the world's main thorium source and, after a very difficult separation, a rare-earth source. In the tantalum circuit monazite is an "];
  "metal.hydrofluoric-acid" [label="Hydrofluoric acid from fluorspar" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The corrosive reagent. Fluorspar plus sulfuric acid gives hydrogen fluoride in a kiln at 200-300 C; fluorspar plus concentrated sulfuric acid in a rotary kiln gives anhydrous HF directly, and the anhydrous route is what makes water-free fluorinations possible. HF is the reagent for silicon to trichl"];
  "metal.columbite" [label="Columbite-tantalite concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A hard, black, sub-conchoidal mineral - (Fe,Mn)(Nb,Ta)2O6 - that in practice is never a pure species but a solid solution, and that is the whole problem of the tantalum industry: tantalum and niobium are chemically almost identical and always occur together, with the tantalum content ranging from a "];
  "metal.columbite.concentrator" [label="Coltan concentrator: gravity, magnet and electrostatic" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The coltan upgrade in a small operation is a classic three-stage gravity and magnetic plant: a shaking table or a jig throws away the quartz and mica, a magnetic separator takes off the magnetite and ilmenite, and a second gravity stage with a dense-medium separator makes a 20-30% Ta2O5 concentrate "];
  "metal.columbite.tailings" [label="Coltan tailings" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A coltan concentrator throws away over 99% of the mass it treats, and in the artisanal operations of the 1970s and 1980s that was put into the creek bed without any attempt at settling, and the creek carried it away. The ecological consequence of that was small compared to what followed - the coltan"];
  "metal.columbite.mine" [label="Pegmatite open pit with sluicing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The tantalum ore is in a pegmatite dyke, a very coarse-grained granite intrusion, and the extraction in the small operations is almost artisanal: the dyke is blasted, the rock is crushed and hand-sorted, and the heavy tantalum minerals are concentrated on a sluice or a jig. The Greenbushes operation"];
  "metal.columbite.pegmatite" [label="Granitic pegmatite dyke" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a coarse-grained, quartz-feldspar-mica rock so coarse that individual crystals are centimetres across, with the tantalum-bearing minerals in small pockets of rare-metal enrichment. Pegmatites are the last-crystallising fraction of a granite magma, so they are the natural home of every inco"];
  "metal.pegmatite-crystals" [label="The crystals and the rare-metal pockets" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the tantalum branch. A pegmatite is the last thing a granite magma crystallises, and because it is water-rich and volatile-rich it can hold elements that have no business being in a granite at all - lithium, tantalum, niobium, beryllium, tin, the rare earths - at hundreds of times their "];
  "metal.silver" [label="Silver: contacts, solder paste, brazing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Silver is the most electrically conductive of any element, and it is used in a C64 where the contact has to carry current at low force: the contacts of the rocker switches and of many of the keyboard switches, the silver-bearing solder and brazing alloys for the heat sinks and chassis joints, the si"];
  "metal.silver.refining" [label="Refine the silver to 99.9-99.99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Most silver never gets a refinery of its own. It is separated inside the lead smelter and refinery, or the copper anode slime, and refined there. The copper route: the slime is dissolved in nitric acid or in an aqua-regia type circuit, silver precipitates as silver chloride which is then reduced wit"];
  "metal.silver.electrorefining" [label="Electrolyse the silver and the dore loop" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The silver from the Parkes cupellation or from the anode slime is cast as anodes and dissolved in a nitric acid electrolyte with a little silver nitrate, at 2-4 V with a pure silver cathode. The electrolyte's job is to hold the silver as the nitrate while the gold and the base metals either stay in "];
  "metal.silver.borax-flux" [label="Fire assay with borax and litharge" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A refinery that also assays needs a cupellation furnace and a borax flux, because silver is assayed by cupellation: the sample is mixed with borax and litharge, cupelled, and the silver bead that is left is weighed. So the assay and the refining process are the same process, which is why a small ref"];
  "metal.silver.contact-powder" [label="Silver contact powder: Ag-SnO2 and Ag-graphite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A switch contact is not silver metal. It is a powder metallurgy composite: 80-95% silver powder mixed with 5-15% of a refractory oxide - tin oxide, cadmium oxide or graphite - pressed at 200-400 MPa and sintered at 700-950 C. The oxide breaks up the silver grain boundaries and suppresses the metal t"];
  "metal.silver.ingot" [label="Silver ingot, 99.9%, and silver contact material" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 99.9% ingot is the standard commodity grade, cast as 0.5-1 kg bars or as a 15-30 kg or 1000 troy ounce bar. For the contacts of the C64's switches and keys the relevant alloy is not pure silver but a silver-cadmium-oxide or silver-tin-oxide electrical contact material - a silver matrix with 5-15% "];
  "metal.silver.ore" [label="Silver ore and the by-product sources" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Silver behaves chemically like lead, so it substitutes for it in galena, and the world's most productive silver mines are really lead mines. The primary ores are native silver, argentite Ag2S, pyrargyrite Ag3SbS3 and stephanite Ag5SbS4 - the last two are why silver is also a lead-bysulphide metallur"];
  "metal.silver.mill" [label="Silver mill: crushing, grinding and flotation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Ore at 300-1500 g/t is crushed to 10-25 mm, ground in a ball mill to 50-100 um, and floated with a xanthate or a thio compound collector at pH 8-10. Silver sulphides float with the lead and zinc sulphides they are intergrown with, so the circuit produces a bulk concentrate that the smelter treats as"];
  "metal.silver.concentrate-shipping" [label="Why silver concentrate goes to a lead smelter" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A silver concentrate is not a product anybody wants to buy; it is a concentrate to ship to a lead smelter 500 km away, because the only economical way to separate silver from lead is the Parkes process, and that needs a lead smelter. So the silver chain leaves the mine, joins the lead chain inside a"];
  "metal.lead.blast-furnace" [label="Lead blast furnace reduction to crude lead" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A lead blast furnace looks like an iron blast furnace but is only 4-7 m tall, because lead reduces so easily. Self-fluxing sinter, coke and limestone are charged at the top; a blast of 200-350 C air is blown in at the hearth; the PbO is reduced to lead by CO at 1000-1100 C and the metal runs out. Th"];
  "metal.lead.ingot" [label="Refined lead ingot, 99.99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cast into 25 kg pigs or into 500 kg slabs. About two thirds of all the lead refined in the world was never made into a product in the refinery - it went straight into an alloy, most of it lead-acid battery plate and cable sheath. The solder industry took a small fraction, on the order of five to ten"];
  "metal.lead.cupellation-lead" [label="Cupellation of the silver dross to silver and gold" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The dross from the Parkes process is mixed with lead and flux and cast into a 30-50 kg charge, which is placed in a cupellation furnace at 1000-1150 C and blown with air. The lead oxidises to litharge, PbO, which melts at 888 C and is absorbed by the porous cupel, while the silver and gold do not ox"];
  "metal.lead.bullion" [label="Crude lead bullion with silver and gold" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tapped at 950-1050 C, the bullion is about 95-98% lead with 1-3% silver, a little gold, and copper, arsenic, antimony and bismuth. Silver at 1-3% is not a trace - it is often the difference between a viable mine and a marginal one, and the Parkes process that recovers it is the reason lead smelters "];
  "metal.lead.copper-drossing" [label="Hot-dross the copper out of the lead bullion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Before the Parkes zinc drossing, the bullion is hot-drossed for copper. The blast-furnace bullion comes out at 950-1050 C holding copper, which is insoluble in lead at that temperature and forms a separate copper matte that floats on top; the refinery skims it off, cools it, and recovers the copper "];
  "metal.lead.dross-smelting" [label="Retorting the Parkes dross to recover the zinc" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="After the silver has been skimmed off as dross, the dross itself has to be processed, and it is done in a cast-iron retort: the dross is mixed with charcoal and a little limestone, charged into a retort at 800-950 C, and the zinc distils out - it boils at 907 C - into a condenser where it is collect"];
  "metal.lead.dross-retort" [label="The dross retort and its condenser" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A cast-iron or steel tube, 1.5-3 m long, charged at one end and heated in a coke-fired setting, with a condenser of iron pipes at the cool end into which the zinc vapour condenses and runs out as a crude metal. The retort has to be skimmed of oxide and it is a batch process, so a refinery ran a bank"];
  "metal.lead.parkes" [label="Parkes process: zinc drossing and desilverisation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The genius step, invented in 1850 and still standard in 1982. The molten bullion is stirred with a few tenths of a percent of zinc, which has a much stronger chemical affinity for silver than lead does, so a zinc-silver intermetallic (AgZn2, Ag2Zn5) migrates to the zinc 'dross' floating on top. The "];
  "metal.lead.vacuum-degassing" [label="Vacuum-degass and dross the lead bullion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The modern version of both processes in one machine: a 6-10 m tall cylindrical steel vacuum vessel with an internal fractionating column. Molten lead is sprayed into the vacuum; the volatiles - hydrogen, oxygen, zinc, and the remaining silver on its way out - distil out and are fractionated, so the "];
  "metal.lead.betts" [label="Betts process: electrolytic refining" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The rival to the Parkes process, and the answer for the last fraction of a gram of bismuth. Anodes of impure lead are dissolved in a caustic soda solution containing sodium plumbite (Na2PbO3), and pure lead plates onto the cathode. The chemistry is unusual - the anode dissolves as plumbite and the d"];
  "metal.silver.amalgamation" [label="Mercury amalgamation of small silver operations" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Before cyanide, silver was recovered by amalgamating the crushed ore with mercury: 4Au + 8Hg -\> 4AuHg4 and 4Ag + 4Hg -\> 4Ag2Hg, then squeezing the amalgam in a heated press and retorting the sponge at 500-700 C to drive the mercury off and recover the metal. The process left 10-100 grams of mercury "];
  "metal.mercury-source" [label="Mercury: cinnabar from Idrija and Almaden" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Amalgamation consumes mercury and does not give it back, so a silver mine that amalgamated needed a mercury supply. Cinnabar, HgS, the vermilion pigment mineral, came from Idrija in Slovenia - the largest mercury mine in the world for 500 years - and from Almaden in Spain, and from the New Idria and"];
  "metal.cinnabar.bed" [label="The cinnabar vein" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a scarlet vein of cinnabar in brecciated limestone, sometimes 10 m wide, worked by open pit and by fire-setting underground since Roman times. It is the only mercury ore that has ever mattered commercially, and it is a beautiful and rather grim example of a metal that exists in one mineral"];
  "metal.silver.mine-mexican" [label="The Zacatecas-Potosi silver vein mines" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Mines like the Fresnillo, the Zacatecas and the San Luis Potosi: an underground operation on steeply dipping epithermal quartz veins carrying native silver, argentite and the antimony sulphides, worked in the Spanish colonial era by water-driven mills, mercury amalgamation and patio amalgamation, an"];
  "metal.gold" [label="Gold: plating, bonding wire, contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A C64 contains perhaps 0.2-1 g of gold. It is the flash on the edge-connector fingers, the plating on the IC leads, the ball or wedge bond from the die pad to the package, and the contacts in the 'jewel' switch mechanisms. Gold is the only metal that does not tarnish and does not form an insulating "];
  "metal.gold.ingot" [label="Refined gold anode, 99.99%, and the plated flash" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The gold that reaches the electronics industry is 99.99% minimum, as a rolled sheet or an anode, and it is used in three ways. Flash plating for connector fingers and IC leads: the gold is dissolved in a cyanide gold bath - typically a gold cyanide, potassium cyanide, gold hydroxide bath at 0.5-2 g/"];
  "metal.gold.wire-bonding" [label="Gold wire bonding of the die" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Each die has to be electrically connected to the package. The 1982 method was wire bonding: a 25 um gold or aluminium wire is threaded through a ceramic or carbide capillary, the tip is melted by a discharge or by a heated work holder, and the first weld is made to the die's aluminium pad. The wire "];
  "metal.gold.contact-jewel" [label="Gold-plated contacts: the edge connector and the switches" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two very different gold contacts in the C64. The 62-way edge connector at the rear has gold-flashed fingers - a 35 um copper-alloy finger with 0.1-0.5 um of gold and a nickel underplate, inserted in the board by a wipe in the card guides. The keyboard and the rocker switches use either gold-plated c"];
  "metal.gold.cyanide-synthesis" [label="Sodium cyanide from natural gas" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cyanide is an industrial chemical, not a mined material, and its feedstock is the same natural gas that makes hydrogen cyanide for acrylonitrile. The Andrussow process is the cheap one: natural gas plus ammonia plus oxygen over a platinum-rhodium catalyst at 900-1000 C gives hydrogen cyanide, which "];
  "metal.gold.cyanide-leach" [label="Cyanide leaching of gold ore at pH 10-11" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The reaction is 4Au + 8NaCN + O2 + 2H2O -\> 4Na[Au(CN)2] + 4NaOH, and it needs three things: cyanide, lime to hold the pH at 10-11 so the cyanide does not protonate into deadly hydrogen cyanide gas, and dissolved oxygen. The pulp - ground ore plus lime, at 25-50% solids by mass - is agitated in an ae"];
  "metal.gold.miller" [label="Miller chlorination and the Aubure process" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The refinery end. Gold doré - typically 90-95% gold with 5-10% silver - is fluxed with borax and litharge and blown with chlorine gas in an MRL (Mineral Resources Limited) chlorinator, heated by a propane torch, for 1-3 hours. The chlorine dissolves the gold as AuCl3 and the silver as AgCl, and the "];
  "metal.gold.dore-melting" [label="Melt the dore bars" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Before the gold can be chlorinated it has to be melted, and the doré - whatever mixture of gold and silver the refinery is holding - is melted with a little borax and litharge in a clay or graphite crucible to 1000-1200 C and poured into a dore ingot weighing 20-50 kg. The litharge is what makes the"];
  "metal.gold.electrorefining" [label="Electrorefine the gold to 99.99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Doré is cast into anodes and dissolved in an aqueous solution of gold chloride in hydrochloric acid at 50-60 C. The electrolyte is the Wohlwill process electrolyte, with a little silver nitrate added to make the silver chloride stay solid and drop out. Gold plates onto a pure gold or platinum ca"];
  "metal.gold.cip" [label="Carbon-in-pulp and activated carbon adsorption" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Gold is present in the pregnant solution as the cyanide complex at only about 1 mg/L, far too dilute to precipitate or to recover directly. The industry fix is adsorption onto activated carbon: the slurry after leaching goes through a bank of 4-8 tall columns of 1.5-3 mm coconut-shell carbon, which "];
  "metal.activated-carbon" [label="Activated carbon from coconut shell" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The adsorbent is a botanical product, not a mineral: coconut or palm-kernel shell, or coal, carbonised at 500-900 C and steam-activated at 800-1000 C until the pore volume is about half the material's bulk volume. The pore structure is what holds the gold cyanide. So the carbon in the carbon-in-pulp"];
  "metal.activated-carbon.coal" [label="Hard coal and petroleum coke as activated carbon feed" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="By 1982 the industry was moving off coconut shell, which had become scarce and expensive as the plastics and activated-carbon markets grew, onto hard coal and - as the process matured - onto delayed petroleum coke, which has the right isotropic pore structure and essentially no ash. The activation i"];
  "metal.steam-activation" [label="Activate the carbon with steam at 800-1000 C" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The step that makes charcoal into activated carbon. A bed of coke or coal is held at 800-1000 C while steam and carbon dioxide are passed over it, and the gas burns the carbon away, opening a pore structure with a surface area of 700-1200 m2/g. The rate of the burn is the whole process: too fast and"];
  "metal.gold.pachuca" [label="Pachuca tank and the Dorr agitator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 1982 standard leach vessel was either a Pachuca tank - a tall cylindrical tank with a cone at the bottom and a compressed-air rising pipe that boils the pulp like a carbonated drink - or a Dorr agitator, a vertical tank with a rotating central shaft and radial arms and baffles. A typical circuit"];
  "metal.gold.ore" [label="Low-grade gold ore (0.5-5 g/t)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Gold at a few parts per million is not visible and not dense in a hand specimen - it is in the ore as discrete grains, often only microns across, or in tellurides. Most of it is in quartz veins, often with sulfides. The 1982 world had two completely different industries: the deep underground Witwate"];
  "metal.gold.wits-mine" [label="Witwatersrand underground gold mining" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other end of the industry. A 15 cm to 3 m band of gold-bearing conglomerate called 'the reef' lies at 1000-6000 m below the Witwatersrand, and from 1886 to 1982 the industry sank shafts and drove long decline haulages and ventilation and cooling galleries into it. Ventilation was the limiting fa"];
  "metal.gold.reef" [label="Witwatersrand gold reef conglomerate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a 2.9 billion year old quartzite with rounded pebbles of quartz and jasper in a fine matrix - a fossil beach. The gold is in the matrix, as discrete grains a few microns across, and the deepest part of the deposit has the highest grade because it is the oldest and the most distal. It is on"];
  "metal.gold.uraninite" [label="The uraninite in the Witwatersrand reef" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The gold reef is also the world's largest gold-uranium conglomerate, and the same reef that was being mined for gold was being mined for uranium by a separate set of shafts from the 1950s onwards. The uraninite is present as discrete grains on the carbon seams, and it recovers in the gold plant's gr"];
  "metal.gold.heap-leach" [label="Heap leaching with cyanide, 10 m pads" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Run-of-mine ore, after a cyanide spray test, is crushed to a few centimetres and stacked in 100,000-tonne heaps on an impermeable pad. It is flooded with 0.02-0.05% NaCN solution, allowed to percolate for 30-60 days, and then drained and sprayed again. The pregnant solution is sent through a carbon "];
  "metal.chromium" [label="Chromium: stainless, ferrochrome, hard chrome" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Chromium does three separate jobs: it makes stainless steel stainless, it is the alloying element in the NiCr film resistors on the board, and as hexavalent chromium it is the hard chrome plating on switch contacts, the keyboard frame hinges and the RF shielding. The plating chemistry is the one wit"];
  "metal.chromate-passivation" [label="Chromate conversion coating" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A nitric acid or chromic acid bath - most often a sodium dichromate bath in 1982 - converts a zinc, tin or cadmium surface to a chromium oxide-hydroxide film a few hundred nanometres thick. Yellow is dichromate, olive is chromic acid, and the black 'iridium' used on some zinc die castings is a diffe"];
  "metal.chromium.metal" [label="Electrolytic chromium metal from chrome alum" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Pure chromium metal - for the chromium plating chemistry and for chromium sputtering targets - is made the opposite way to ferrochrome: hydrometallurgically. Chromite is roasted with soda ash to a water-soluble sodium chromate, the chromate is leached, the hexavalent chromium is reduced to trivalent"];
  "metal.chromium.metal.cell" [label="Chromium electrowinning cell and the diaphragm" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Chromium is electrowon from a trivalent chromium sulphate solution at 5-8 V with a lead or lead-alloy cathode, in cells separated by a diaphragm - traditionally asbestos cloth, later a synthetic fibre - because the anodic chlorine would otherwise oxidise the trivalent chromium back and the two would"];
  "metal.chromium.cell.diaphragm" [label="The diaphragm and the asbestos inheritance" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cell needs the anodic chlorine kept away from the trivalent chromium in the catholyte, and the membrane that does that was, until the 1980s, asbestos cloth - the same asbestos as the chlor-alkali diaphragm cells. So the chromium and chlor-alkali industries of the early twentieth century share a "];
  "metal.hex-chrome-plating" [label="Hexavalent chromium electroplating bath" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The classic chromiumic acid bath: CrO3 at 50-250 g/L in dilute sulfuric acid at 100-250 g/L, run at 45-60 C, 3-7 V, 15-60 A/dm2, with a lead-lead dioxide anode. A special steel anode is used and the bath is extremely hard to waste-treat: the chromium has to get from Cr(VI) down to Cr(III) before it "];
  "metal.chromium.aod" [label="Argon-oxygen decarburisation and the stainless vessel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The step that makes stainless steel possible. A 300-450 tonne ladle is fitted with a lance that injects a mixture of argon and oxygen under the steel surface. The argon stirs the melt and dilutes the carbon, the oxygen burns carbon to carbon monoxide, and because the gas is argon rather than air the"];
  "metal.chromium.aod.lance" [label="The AOD lance and its tuyere block" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The lance is a 10-25 cm carbon-molybdenum or refractory tube lowered into the metal on a mast, and it carries both the argon and the oxygen. Around it sits a refractory tuyere block in the ladle lining with a single small hole through which the gas is injected into the melt, so the gas is introduced"];
  "metal.refractory-ladle" [label="The refractory ladle lining" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The AOD vessel is lined with magnesia or with magnesia-carbon, and the slag at pH 5 or higher eats a basic lining - which is why a stainless ladle lining is more expensive and more carefully maintained than a mild steel one. The lining is a consumable of its own, replaced every few hundred heats. So"];
  "metal.chromium.stainless-use" [label="Where the chromium ends up in a 1982 C64" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="AISI 304 in the audio jack shell, the shield cans and any stainless fastener or bracket; 430 where a magnetic ferritic grade was wanted. Chromium at 18% is above the roughly 11% needed for the surface to passivate, which is the whole trick: chromium forms a self-healing chromium oxide film about 2 n"];
  "metal.ferrochrome" [label="High-carbon ferrochrome, 60-70% Cr" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Chromite ore and coke are charged into a submerged arc furnace at 1400-1600 C with a chromite self-fluxing slag, and the result is an alloy of 60-70% chromium, 1-3% silicon and 5-8% carbon. It consumes about 500-700 kWh per tonne of ferrochrome - a fifth of aluminium's energy per tonne but on a much"];
  "metal.ferrochrome.slag" [label="The self-fluxing chromite slag" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A ferrochrome furnace is self-fluxing, which means the ore's own silica is balanced by the ore's own magnesia and there is nothing to add - the chromite is 16% MgO and 20% SiO2 and the ratio of the two is already about right for a liquid slag at 1500 C. That is why the smelter does not need lime, an"];
  "metal.chromite" [label="Chromite ore (FeCr2O4), 38-46% Cr2O3" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A hard, black, opaque spinel - FeO plus chromium oxide in a cubic lattice - that occurs not as veins but as layer after layer in the lower part of the Bushveld Complex and the Transvaal Group. It is unusually resistant to both chemical attack and heat, which is why chromite is also found in beach pl"];
  "metal.chromite.mine" [label="Layer-seam chromite open pit, 200 m deep" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Bushveld chromite mines are among the deepest and most geometrically awkward open pits in the world. A 1-6 m chromitite layer is covered by 100-200 m of layered norite, and the pit must be advanced strip by strip along the seam, so the benches are narrow and the truck travels a long way for ever"];
  "metal.chromite.bench" [label="Following a seam: the narrow-bench problem" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A normal open pit advances in benches 15 m high and 100-200 m wide, with haul roads on every bench. A chromite seam is 1-6 m thick, so the pit has to advance along the seam instead, and the benches are 100-200 m high with a 5-10 m working width and a haul road cut into the wall. The consequence is a"];
  "metal.chromite.layered" [label="Layered norite of the Bushveld Complex" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a 2 km thick layered mafic intrusion with a spectacularly regular magmatic stratification - a metre-scale layering of plagioclase and pyroxene laid down by a magma chamber over about a million years - with the chromite concentrating into a single thin seam at one horizon. It is the single "];
  "metal.chromite.merensky" [label="The Merensky Reef: the same layer as the chrome" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Just below the chromitite seams of the Bushveld Complex lies a 0.4-1 m layer of pyroxenite carrying 3-8 g/t of platinum-group metals - the Merensky Reef - and it is the reason the chromite is mined as it is. The waste from a chromite pit is a platinum ore, so the two industries are financially inter"];
  "metal.manganese" [label="Manganese: the steel deoxidiser" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Manganese has no visible place in a C64, but without it the steel the case and the shielding are made from would have inclusions and blowholes in it. Almost all manganese ore is used to make ferromanganese, which is added to the steelmaking furnace as a deoxidiser and desulphuriser and as an alloyin"];
  "metal.manganese.ladle-charging" [label="Charge the ferromanganese into the steel ladle" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A ladle of steel is a bad place to drop a lump of ferromanganese, because the lump floats, the reaction is violent and the steel is left with unreacted alloy inclusions. So the FeMn is added in sized lumps and, increasingly in the 1980s, in cored wire - a steel tube filled with powdered ferromangane"];
  "metal.ferromanganese" [label="High-carbon ferromanganese, Mn 65-80%, C 6-8%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Made in a submerged arc furnace, 25-100 MVA, three electrodes, at 1400-1600 C with the electrode tips submerged in the slag. The charge is sintered manganese ore, coke, limestone and often a good deal of iron ore, because the aim is an alloy of about 70% manganese at 6-8% carbon, and manganese is ex"];
  "metal.manganese.smelter-electricity" [label="Why a ferroalloy smelter is built beside its power station" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 100 MVA submerged arc furnace draws about 80-90 MW continuously and cannot be stopped without destroying the furnace. So it needs a long-term supply contract and, in most cases, its own generating plant or a dedicated hydro or coal share. In the 1980s several ferroalloy industries - Norway's ferro"];
  "metal.manganese.furnace-lining" [label="The submerged arc furnace and its refractory" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A ferromanganese furnace is 25-100 MVA with three electrodes 1.5-2.0 m in diameter, and the difference from a silicon furnace is the slag: the ferromanganese process makes a large volume of a liquid, low-basicity slag, so the furnace is a slag vessel with an electrode in it. The shell is steel with "];
  "metal.graphite.electrode" [label="Graphite and carbon electrodes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The EAF electrodes, the submerged arc furnace electrodes of the ferrosilicon, ferrochrome and ferromanganese industries, the aluminium pot anodes, the silicon furnace electrodes and the large carbon blocks of a blast furnace hearth all consume the same two feedstocks: calcined petroleum or needle co"];
  "metal.manganese.deoxidiser" [label="Deoxidation and desulphurisation in the steel ladle" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where the alloy is actually used. During tapping, ferromanganese is added to the steel. Manganese has a higher oxygen affinity than iron, so it combines with the dissolved oxygen to form MnO inclusions - which is the point: the oxygen is now safely tied up in an oxide the steelmaker can float out in"];
  "metal.manganese.inclusion-removal" [label="Removing the inclusions: degassing and filtration" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A modern low-sulphur steel has few MnS inclusions but a lot of fine Al2O3, and that is a different problem: fine alumina does not float out, so it has to be filtered. A 1980s plate mill that wanted clean, formable sheet for deep drawing or for tinplating ran a ladle through a vacuum degasser to flus"];
  "metal.manganese.melt-deoxidation" [label="Aluminium and silicon deoxidation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The last step in the ladle is the one that actually decides the steel's quality. Manganese ties up the bulk of the dissolved oxygen as MnO inclusions, and then a calculated addition of aluminium - 0.5-2 kg per tonne - ties up the rest as Al2O3. Those inclusions have to go, and in a well-run plant th"];
  "metal.manganese.sintering" [label="Sintering the manganese ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Manganese ore is very fine and very friable, so it cannot be blown into a submerged arc furnace as a powder; it is sintered first, on a travelling grate exactly like the lead sinter, to make a 30-50 mm porous cake. The sinter belt at a manganese works is a zinc-works sinter plant or a lead-works sin"];
  "metal.manganese.ore" [label="Pyrolusite and manganiferous ore, 25-50% Mn" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Manganese ore is almost never a clean manganese mineral. The Groote Eylandt and South African supergene ores are a hard, black, sooty mass of MnO2 cryptocrystalline material - wad and psilomelane - interbedded with iron formation. Grade is 25-50% Mn, and low-grade ore needs beneficiation: the South "];
  "metal.manganese.nodule" [label="Abyssal manganese nodule dredge" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The other, entirely different source: polymetallic nodules lying in thousands of square kilometres of the Clarion-Clipperton Zone and the Peru Basin at 4,000-6,000 m depth, containing 30% Mn, 2% Ni, 1% Cu and 25% Fe. Collecting them means a 10,000-tonne ship dragging a pipe across the seabed for 20,"];
  "metal.collector" [label="The hydraulic collector head" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The machine the whole idea rests on: a manifold of suction nozzles, 10-30 m across, towed along the seabed at 1-3 knots, that lifts the nodules into a riser pipe and up to a ship 4,000-6,000 m above. The 1970s trials worked at the point where the ocean is deepest to learn whether nodules survive bei"];
  "metal.manganese.nodule-tech" [label="Why the 1970s nodule trials were abandoned" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="By 1982 seabed nodule mining was a real programme with real money behind it, and the money is the interesting part. The incentive was not manganese - the grades were assumed recoverable but nobody had proved it at scale. The incentive was nickel and cobalt, because the strategic metal studies of the"];
  "metal.manganese.nodule-rock" [label="Manganese oxide concretions on pelagic clay" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: millimetre-to-centimetre potato-shaped concretions of Mn-Fe oxide lying unattached in a soft abyssal red clay. The metal has precipitated out of seawater around a nucleus, slowly, over millions of years. It is the most extraordinary leaf in this tree: the manganese in a C64's steel is, in "];
  "metal.abyssal-clay" [label="The abyssal red clay beneath the nodules" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The true bottom of the manganese branch: a soft, red, pelagic clay that covers the abyssal plains at 4,000-6,000 m and that took the better part of 100 million years to accumulate, one centimetre at a time. The nodules sit on top of it. So the deepest leaf in this tree is a handful of dark potatoes "];
  "metal.manganese.laterite-mine" [label="Supergene manganese mining in the Kalahari" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Transvaal and the northern Cape were the world's largest producers in 1982 by a wide margin, and the operation was surface mining on a 5-20 m ore layer over a 100 m thick band of black manganese-bearing sandstone. Drag hoists, belt conveyors, and a scrubber-and-jig beneficiation plant, then a si"];
  "metal.manganese.washing-plant" [label="Scrubber, sluice and jig beneficiation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Groote Eylandt and the South African operations do not smelt the raw lump: they wash it. The ore is dumped on a heap, a high-pressure water monitor scrubs off the fines and the clay, and the coarse goes over a sluice and a jig where the density difference between the manganese minerals (4.5-5.1) and"];
  "metal.manganese.oxide-phase" [label="The oxide and carbonate phases of the ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the manganese branch's rock: the manganese in a supergene ore is not one mineral but four. Pyrolusite MnO2 is the black, soft, fibrous one; psilomelane is a barium-bearing cryptocrystalline mixture; braunite Mn2O3 is the hard brown one; and rhodochrosite MnCO3 is the pink carbonate in th"];
  "metal.nickel" [label="Nickel: connector plating, alloys, stainless" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Nickel is small in mass and enormous in function. In a C64 it is the plating on the connector shells and the gold-plated edge connector fingers' underlayer, the constantan and nichrome of the thin-film resistors on the board, the Invar of the composite-video circuit's chroma delay, and the bulk of A"];
  "metal.nickel.ingot" [label="Electrolytic or cathode nickel, 99.9%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Nickel metal arrives at the user as a cathode or a briquette: 99.8% for ferronickel-grade stainless, 99.9% for stainless and for plating. For a plating line the metal arrives as matte or as a soluble anode, but in 1982 the majority of nickel plating anodes were still the insoluble carbon steel anode"];
  "metal.nickel.electroplating" [label="Nickel electroplating and the gold underplate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The single most important metallurgical fact about nickel in a 1982 C64: gold plating does not sit on copper. It sits on a nickel underplate of 1-3 um, and the reason is electrochemistry - gold will not plate directly onto copper because the copper dissolves faster than the gold deposits, and a gold"];
  "metal.nickel.sherritt-gordon" [label="Sherritt-Gordon ammonia leach and hydrogen reduction" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The process that unlocked the laterites in the 1970s and 1980s. The slurry is leached at atmospheric pressure with concentrated ammonia and oxygen at 50-70 C: nickel and copper form soluble ammine complexes while the iron precipitates as hematite. The pregnant liquor is heated in a pressure autoclav"];
  "metal.ferronickel" [label="Ferronickel, the laterite iron-nickel alloy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The dominant way nickel actually became metal in 1982. Dried laterite and quartzite are fed with coke into a large submerged arc furnace, 30-60 MW, three electrodes, running at 1350-1500 C. The laterite's iron is already present as hematite, so the whole charge reduces together and the product is no"];
  "metal.nickel.mond" [label="Mond carbonyl refining (nickel from 99.9 to 99.999)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Ludwig Mond's 1890s process, still used in the 1980s for the last nines. Nickel carbonyl Ni(CO)4 is formed at 50-60 C in a saturator, distilled off as a colourless liquid boiling at 43 C, and decomposed in a decomposer column at 200-230 C onto 99.99% nickel pellets. The trick is that the carbonyl of"];
  "metal.nickel.mond.safety" [label="Why nickel carbonyl is the deadliest room in metals" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Nickel tetracarbonyl is a colourless, volatile liquid that boils at 43 C and is lethal by inhalation at concentrations far below anything that can be smelled. A Mond refinery therefore runs on a rule that no one enters a carbonyl area without a respirator, that the vapour is continuously monitored a"];
  "metal.nickel.electrowinning" [label="Nickel electrowinning from a pure sulphide circuit" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where the ore is sulphide rather than laterite, Sherritt's original ammonia-pressure process dissolves the concentrate in ammoniacal ammonium sulphate at 160-190 F (70-90 C) with oxygen, and then electrowins the nickel directly out of the pregnant liquor onto titanium cathodes in diaphragm cells at "];
  "metal.nickel.sulphide-concentrate" [label="Nickel sulphide concentrate (Sudbury)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Sudbury basin of Ontario was the world's nickel capital and it is a genuinely strange deposit: an impact structure full of shock-melted, metal-rich breccia at the base of a 1.8 billion year old impact crater, with pentlandite (Fe,Ni)9S8 and millerite NiS. The INCO operation mined both undergroun"];
  "metal.nickel.impact-melt" [label="Impact melt sheet at the base of the Sudbury crater" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a foot-thick to 20 m layer of rock melted and shock-altered by the impact, in which the nickel and copper sulphides were disseminated as droplets. It is a metamorphic and melt rock, not an igneous intrusion, and it is a band about 60 km long and 20 km wide across northern Ontario. The mini"];
  "metal.nickel.autoclave" [label="The ammonia-hydrogen autoclave" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 10-15 m long, 3-5 m diameter, titanium or 316L-lined pressure vessel in a 6-10 stage cascade. In each stage the liquor is contacted with hydrogen and stirred, and the nickel comes out onto the walls and the cooling surfaces. The whole chemistry depends on the fact that ammonia complexes nickel and"];
  "metal.nickel.laterite" [label="Nickel laterite (garnierite ore)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A soft, earthy, red-brown clay-and-laterite ore, really a weathering product of ultramafic rock: olivine and pyroxene in a serpentinised peridotite are altered from scratch, and nickel substitutes for magnesium in the clay lattice, giving garnierite. There is no nickel mineral to concentrate; the wh"];
  "metal.nickel.drying" [label="Dry the laterite before smelting" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A laterite arrives at 30-45% moisture and the submerged arc furnace cannot take it wet, because the water costs more steam than the reaction releases. So the dewatered ore is dried in a rotary dryer or - in the Cuban and Philippine operations of the 1980s - in a suspension dryer with the hot flue ga"];
  "metal.nickel.laterite-mine" [label="Bucket-wheel excavator laterite mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The soft, wet, deeply weathered ore is the ideal bucket-wheel material. A walking or rail-mounted bucket-wheel excavator with a 20-40 m boom cuts the overburden and the ore bench itself, dropping it onto a belt conveyor, and the ore goes to a long washing and screening plant rather than to a crusher"];
  "metal.nickel.ore-washing" [label="The laterite washing and screening plant" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The laterite arrives from a bucket-wheel excavator as a slurry, and it goes through a log washer, a scrubber, a screen tower and a series of hydrocyclones, and the -0.5 mm fraction is rejected outright as low-grade clay. The coarse fraction is dewatered on a screen and stacked in a concrete bin. In "];
  "metal.nickel.limonite-garnierite" [label="Limonite and garnierite: the two nickel minerals" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the nickel branch, and it is a mineral solution rather than a mineral. There is no nickel mineral in a laterite. Nickel substitutes for magnesium in the smectite and kaolinite clays - the mineral is garnierite, which is really a mixture - and it substitutes for iron in the goethite of th"];
  "metal.nickel.peridotite" [label="Serpentinised peridotite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: an ultramafic igneous rock, mostly olivine, now largely converted to serpentine, that has been chemically weathered for tens of millions of years in a warm wet climate. The nickel is in the clay and in the limonite, not in a crystal. Cuba, New Caledonia and the Philippines all have large s"];
  "metal.zinc" [label="Zinc: die-cast alloy, plating, brass, varistor" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc is the fourth non-ferrous metal and the most recycled one. In a 1982 C64 it appears as a die-cast zinc alloy for the case-mounted parts and the key plungers, as a plating on steel for EMI shields and chassis fasteners, as brass (copper-zinc) for the bus-bar and connector bodies, and as zinc oxi"];
  "metal.zinc.electrowinning" [label="Electrowinning of zinc on aluminium cathodes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc is one of the few metals won by electrolysis out of an aqueous solution at industrial scale. The reason is that zinc is more negative than hydrogen and would just deposit hydrogen from water if it were not for the high hydrogen overvoltage on aluminium; each cathode is an aluminium plate of 1-2"];
  "metal.zinc.electrowinning-cell" [label="The single-deck electrolysis cell" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 1980s zinc tankhouse cell was a plain open-top concrete or rubber-lined steel box, 3-6 m long, 1.5-2.5 m wide, 2-3 m deep, with an aluminium cathode every 15-25 mm and a lead anode between them. The electrolyte is fed in at one end and pulled out at the other; the plates go in, run for 24-72 hou"];
  "metal.zinc.stripping" [label="Strip the cathodes and melt the SHG ingot" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="At the end of the cycle each cathode is lifted out, tipped against a steel plate that shears the zinc slab off, and the slabs are charged to a 100-500 kg induction furnace. The furnace has to be held at 450-470 C and under a cover flux because zinc oxidises readily and every gram of oxide is a loss."];
  "metal.zinc.ingot" [label="Special high grade zinc ingot, 99.995%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Twenty-five kilogram ingots, or the 500 kg minimum-content ingot that the bigger plants preferred by 1982. Special high grade at 99.995% was the grade specified by the galvanising industry and by die-casters, because both are very sensitive to lead, cadmium and iron. Two thirds of world zinc goes in"];
  "metal.zinc-oxide-varistor" [label="Zinc oxide varistor: the mains spike clamp" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64's power supply faces the mains directly. A zinc oxide varistor disc - ZnO powder pressed and sintered between two fired electrodes - sits across the input, and its resistance is astronomically high until the mains edge is exceeded, at which point it conducts and clamps the surge. ZnO is esse"];
  "metal.zinc-oxide-powder" [label="Zinc oxide powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc oxide for ceramics was, in 1982, made by burning high-purity zinc in air, by calcining high-purity zinc carbonate, or - for the highest grades - by the vapour-phase oxidation of high-purity zinc metal, which gives spherical sub-micron particles with a very tight size distribution. A varistor po"];
  "metal.zinc.brass" [label="Brass (Cu-Zn)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc's largest engineering use is as the alloying element in brass and bronze. Brass is 60-95% copper with zinc, and the connector bodies, bus bars, shield cans and some switch frames in a C64 are brass, often brass plated with tin or nickel. Brass is melted and cast from cathode copper and zinc ing"];
  "metal.zinc.electroplate" [label="Electrolytic zinc plating of steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="For small plated parts - chassis fasteners, brackets, EMI shields - zinc is electroplated rather than dipped. An alkaline cyanide bath, a non-cyanide acid chloride bath, or in the barrel a simple sulphate. The deposit is sacrificial: even if the coating is scratched, the zinc corrodes preferentially"];
  "metal.zinc.die-cast" [label="Zamak die-cast alloy and the pressure die-caster" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc's most interesting product for this tree. A zinc alloy with 4% aluminium and a little copper - Zamak 3 is Zn 96 / Al 4 / Cu 0.5, Zamak 5 with 5% aluminium for better die-castability - is injected into a steel die at 380-430 C under 20-150 MPa by a cold-chamber machine with 200-800 tonnes of cla"];
  "metal.zinc.ingot-furnace" [label="Zinc alloy melting furnace" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The die-caster does not melt its own alloy from primary metal; it buys 10-25 kg alloy ingots of a certified composition and melts them in a thermostatically controlled holding furnace - typically a 100-500 kg crucible furnace with an immersed resistance heater and a lead bath to keep the alloy away "];
  "metal.zinc.galvanising" [label="Hot-dip galvanising of steel strip" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Steel sheet, strip or fabric is pickled in hydrochloric acid, fluxed in an ammonium chloride-zinc chloride bath, and dipped in a kettle of molten zinc at 450-470 C. The zinc wets the steel, forms an iron-zinc intermetallic, and the kettle surface is skimmed of dross by a wiper roll. Every galvanisin"];
  "metal.zinc.dross-recovery" [label="Recover the zinc from the galvanising dross" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The iron-rich dross and the bottom ash skinned off a galvanising kettle are picked out, and the metal is recovered in a specialised horizontal crucible furnace, where the dross is heated with a flux and the iron drops out as Fe-Zn hard zinc. The remaining zinc is pumped out. A galvanising line there"];
  "metal.zinc.anode-mb" [label="Lead-silver anode in the tankhouse" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The insoluble anode is a lead alloy - 99% Pb with 0.5-1% Ag, cast as 10-40 mm thick plates - which works because when it does corrode slowly, the lead dioxide layer that forms is a perfectly good anode surface. Silver is added because it slows the corrosion. So the zinc tankhouse is structurally a l"];
  "metal.zinc.leach-purify" [label="Acid leach and purification of the zinc sulphate solution" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The dead-roast sinter is leached in spent electrolyte diluted with water, at pH about 4.5 and 40-60 C, in a series of leaching tanks. Then the solution must be purified, because almost every impurity that reaches the cell either consumes current or plates out on the cathode as a short circuit. Iron "];
  "metal.zinc.iron-removal" [label="Iron removal by the jarosite and goethite routes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The purification chemistry in one process. The leach liquor's pH is raised with limestone and the iron is oxidised with air in a bank of aeration tanks at 90-100 C. Whether the precipitate is jarosite (the older route, at pH 5.2 with sodium sulphate added, at 90-100 C over 10-20 hours) or goethite ("];
  "metal.zinc.jarosite-residue" [label="Jarosite residue and the zinc it costs" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The iron precipitation step that makes the zinc plant's chemistry work is also its biggest single loss. Oxidising the iron out at pH 5.2 throws down jarosite or goethite, and both of them carry zinc in their lattice - typically 4-10% Zn. At 1-3 tonnes of residue per tonne of zinc made, and 5-10% of "];
  "metal.zinc.dust" [label="Zinc dust for cementation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The purification step uses zinc metal itself, atomised from a small kettle, as the reagent that throws out copper, cadmium and cobalt. The cemented metals - a black sludge heavy with copper and cadmium - is filtered off and shipped to a cadmium refinery, which is where the world's cadmium came from "];
  "metal.cadmium-residue" [label="Copper-cadmium residue" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 1980s cadmium plant roasted the residue in a retort at 600-800 C: the cadmium volatilised off (it boils at 767 C) and was condensed in a condenser, giving 99.9% cadmium metal, while the copper stayed behind. This is the only major industrial source of cadmium, and it is a zinc by-product. It matte"];
  "metal.zinc.roasting" [label="Roasting of sphalerite to zinc oxide" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The concentrate is roasted in a fluidised bed or on a sinter strand, 850-1000 C, with the result 2ZnS + 3O2 -\> 2ZnO + 2SO2. The 1980s plant that grew out of this is the roaster-sinter plant, in which the hot ZnO and Fe2O3 sinter together into a porous 'dead roast sinter' that is a much better feed f"];
  "metal.zinc.fume-catch" [label="Catch the cadmium and lead in the roaster gas" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The roaster gas carries a fine zinc oxide fume and, in the vapour phase, the cadmium and the lead. Economically the cadmium is the point: the fume goes to a baghouse, and the cadmium is recovered in a separate cadmium plant. The lead in the same gas is a nuisance and an occupational hazard, and in 1"];
  "metal.zinc.roaster" [label="Fluidised-bed roaster" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Concentrate is fed into a bed of 1-3 m of the roast itself on a distributor of tuyeres, held at 850-950 C by the fluidising air, and the bed overflows continuously into a hot cyclone and then a sintering conveyor where the last 100-150 C of roast and the sintering happen together. The whole thing wa"];
  "metal.sphalerite" [label="Sphalerite (ZnS) concentrate, 50-60% Zn" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sphalerite is one of the few ores that is not visually recognisable: it is a black to honey-brown cubic crystal whose colour depends on the iron substituting in the lattice, so the industry grades it in grades of iron - a 4% iron concentrate looks black and a 12% iron concentrate looks ruby red-brow"];
  "metal.sphalerite.mine" [label="Underground zinc-lead mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Red Dog and Sullivan type deposit: a massive sulphide body in a sedimentary basin, mined underground from a ramp or a shaft, with the ore hoisted in a friction hoist or a skip, and the mill right at the portal. Broken Hill's Broken Hill Proprietary was the other archetype, an open pit above a ve"];
  "metal.sphalerite.host" [label="The host basin and its caprock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The final leaf of the zinc chain is not a rock but a structure: a sedimentary basin with a metal-bearing horizon in it. The Red Dog deposit is a Devonian shale-hosted lens in the Buckland River basin on the Tukarakka Peninsula; Broken Hill is a metamorphosed, deformed massive sulphide in a sequence "];
  "metal.sphalerite.mill" [label="Zinc concentrator: differential flotation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ore is crushed to 10-25 mm, ground to 50-100 um in a ball or autogenous mill, and floated in stages. The classic circuit is differential: copper and lead sulphides are floated first with a stronger collector and a higher pH, and then the sphalerite is floated from the tailings with a weaker coll"];
  "metal.sphalerite.dms" [label="Dense-media separation of galena from sphalerite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Galena at 7.6 and sphalerite at 4.1 are separable on a shaking table or, much faster, in a dense-medium separator using a ferrosilicon or magnetite suspension at a cut density of about 3.0-3.5. The lead concentrate and the zinc concentrate are thereby split before flotation, which lets the flotation"];
  "metal.dms.fe-silicon" [label="Ferrosilicon powder for dense-media separation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The liquid in a dense-medium separator is ferrosilicon - an alloy of iron and silicon made in exactly the submerged arc furnace described in the electrical-steel and metallurgical-silicon branches of this file. So a 1982 concentrator that separated galena from sphalerite by density was running a slu"];
  "metal.sphalerite.massive-sulphide" [label="Massive sulphide in a sedimentary basin" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a lens-shaped body of fine-grained sulphide - sphalerite, galena, pyrite, with chalcopyrite and arsenopyrite - conformable with the bedding of a marine shale or carbonate, and sitting at a specific stratigraphic horizon. The Red Dog deposit is exactly this: a Devonian shale-hosted lens in "];
  "metal.solder" [label="63/37 Sn-Pb solder: wire, bar, paste" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The alloy that makes electronics manufacturable. 63% tin, 37% lead is a true eutectic, so it melts and freezes at one temperature, 183 C, with no mushy interval - which means a joint goes from solid to liquid in milliseconds and the reflow time window can be measured in seconds. That property is why"];
  "metal.solder.waste" [label="The solder factory's own waste streams" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A solder pot, a dross heap, a flux residue stream, a wash-water drain and a baghouse for the flux fumes. The dross goes to a reclaimer or to a hazardous-waste disposal contract; the baghouse dust is lead oxide and rosin and is genuinely hazardous; and the wash water goes to the drain with the lead i"];
  "metal.solder.waste-baghouse" [label="The flux fume baghouse and its dust" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The wave-solder hood has an extraction duct and a baghouse, because the fume is an aerosol of lead oxide and of rosin-acid degradation products. The dust is 30-60% lead oxide and it is collected as a hazardous waste. A big 1982 line threw away tens of kilograms a week. The control measure was local "];
  "metal.solder.cleaning" [label="Cleaning the flux residue off the board" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A rosin-fluxed board is covered in a sticky, slightly acidic film of flux decomposition products that will corrode the copper and will not accept a conformal coating. In 1982 it was removed in an aqueous machine - a conveyor with spray bars and drying ovens - often followed by a trichloroethane or p"];
  "metal.solder.waste-water" [label="The solder wash-water waste stream" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A C64 assembly line's cleaning water carried dissolved lead, tin, flux residues and a lot of suspended solids. In 1982 the standard practice was dilute and discharge: a settling tank and a pH adjustment, then to the drain. The lead was almost all in the sludge, so the sludge went to a hazardous-wast"];
  "metal.solder.waste-leachate" [label="Lead in landfill leachate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Landfill leachate is where the lead problem stops being a factory problem. Dross and baghouse dust that could not be recycled went to a hazardous-waste landfill, and a landfill that takes metal-bearing waste generates leachate in which lead concentrations are routinely orders of magnitude above the "];
  "metal.solder.pot" [label="The wave-solder pot and its dross" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 5-20 kg pot of 63/37 at 250-280 C with a pump and a wave-forming nozzle, plus a rotating skimmer and an oil skimmer. The surface of a working solder pot accumulates a metre of black oxide dross - tin oxide, lead oxide and flux residue - and it has to be skimmed off every shift and every night, and"];
  "metal.solder.dross-oxide" [label="The dross oxide film on the solder pot" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="At 250-280 C the surface of molten Sn-Pb oxidises continuously - tin oxide forms preferentially because it has the lower oxidation potential - so the pot grows a skin of SnO2 with lead oxide and flux in it. The skimmer takes it off, but the formation is unavoidable, which is why wave-solder lines ha"];
  "metal.solder.lead-hazard" [label="The leaded-solder problem in the 1980s" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Lead in solder was the first workplace poison to arrive with electronics. It was already deeply regulated for the smelter workers - the whole lead industry is under air-monitoring and blood-lead surveillance - and by the early 1980s occupational-health attention had turned to the solder bench: solde"];
  "metal.solder.silver-solder-paste" [label="Silver-bearing solder and silver paste" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A distinct and important thing in the 1980s electronics industry, which is easy to confuse with the lead-tin eutectic: high-temperature silver solder - 62Ag/38Cu, 50Ag/50Cd, 70Ag/30Sn - melts at 600-750 C and is brazed, not soldered. Silver-tin solder melts at 217-220 C and is used where lead-free b"];
  "metal.solder.paste" [label="Solder paste: powder in flux" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Solder paste is a mixture of atomised alloy powder at 60-90% by weight in a flux vehicle, and in 1982 it was the technology for surface-mount parts, which Commodore did not really use on the mainboard but which every other electronics maker in the world was. The powder particle size is controlled by"];
  "metal.solder.wave-solder" [label="Wave soldering with the alloy pot" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The board assembly method for a 1982 C64. The bottom of the board is swept across a standing wave of molten 63/37 at 250-280 C, the excess drains through a hot-air knife, and the whole board lives in a hood full of the vapour of flux decomposition products. A few kilograms of solder in the pot are h"];
  "metal.solder.wave-solder.knife" [label="The hot-air knife and the solder wave" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A pump throws molten solder up a nozzle to form a standing wave 3-8 mm high, and the board is dragged across it on a conveyor with fingers. As the board leaves, a 'hot-air knife' - a duct blowing 200-300 C air across the solder line - blows the excess solder off the pads and off the solder side of t"];
  "metal.solder.preheater" [label="Preheater, fluxer and the thermal profile" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Before the wave, the board goes through a preheater - infrared lamps, a hot plate or hot air - to 100-150 C, and through a fluxer that lays a foam or sprayed layer of flux on the solder side. The reason for preheating is thermal expansion: a FR-4 board with a big hole pattern expands by a different "];
  "metal.solder.board-warp" [label="Board warp, thermal stress and the epoxy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Why preheat exists at all. A single-sided 1.6 mm FR-4 board with a large hole pattern expands and contracts with a coefficient of about 14-18 x 10^-6 per K, and it is not isotropic - the copper layer and the epoxy cloth have different coefficients, and the weave gives the board different stiffness i"];
  "metal.solder.joint-microscopy" [label="Intermetallics and lead leaching in the solder joint" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A good 1982 through-hole joint is a shallow concave meniscus filling the pad hole about 70-90%, with a fillet at the lead. The metallurgical reason it works is the intermetallic: copper and tin react to form Cu6Sn5 and Cu3Sn, and the joint is a composite of unreacted solder and intermetallic compoun"];
  "metal.solder.wire" [label="63/37 solder wire with a rosin core" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The wire that went through a C64 assembly line: a solid 63/37 core inside a flux core inside a tin-lead sheath, made by the cored-wire method and drawn on the same kind of multiwire machine the copper industry uses, in a smaller and more delicate setup. This is a deliberate convergence node: the sol"];
  "metal.solder.alloy-making" [label="Casting the 63/37 bar" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The alloy is made in a handful of ways, all of them embarrassingly simple. The best is the cored wire method: rods of high-purity tin are cast inside a steel tube pre-filled with molten lead at exactly 37%, so the tube's own wall meters the lead; the tube and the core are then rolled and drawn out t"];
  "metal.solder.cored-wire" [label="The cored-wire manufacturing method" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The standard way to make electronics solder wire, and the reason the alloy composition is so consistent. A steel tube with a wall thickness calculated for 37% lead by volume is filled with molten high-purity tin - actually with the exact alloy, but the wall does the metering - and the tube is cold-d"];
  "metal.solder.flux-core-fill" [label="Fill the flux core with activated rosin" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The flux core is a paste, not a powder: rosin dissolved in a solvent - turpentine, or in the 1980s increasingly a mild-rosin paste - with the zinc chloride activator, made viscous enough to hold a column. It is injected into the tube with a hydraulic filler in a batch, and the tube's ends are crimpe"];
  "metal.solder.antimony" [label="Antimony addition to stiffen the alloy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Pure Sn-Pb solder creeps. A 1982 instrument or a through-hole joint under a hot component would push itself out of the joint over a few years, and the classic failure was a capacitor that walked out of its pads. Two tenths of a percent of antimony, as an Sb-Sn hardener or as a lead-antimony master a"];
  "metal.lead" [label="Lead: the base metal of 63/37 solder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Lead is the soft, low-melting, cheap half of the solder alloy, and it is the reason soldering works at all: pure tin wets copper badly and has no strength, and pure lead does not wet anything. Lead also has its own huge pre-electronics industrial life - storage batteries, cable sheath, pigment - and"];
  "metal.lead.sintering" [label="Roasting/sintering of galena to PbO" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The concentrate is roasted in a sinter plant: a travelling grate on which a 1-2 m bed of concentrate is ignited and slowly drawn through with the gas being pulled after the fire. The sulphide oxidises to lead(II) oxide: 2PbS + 3O2 -\> 2PbO + 2SO2. The point of sintering rather than fluidised-bed roas"];
  "metal.lead.self-fluxing" [label="Self-fluxing sinter mix" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sinter is not simply roasted concentrate. The mix is designed so the sintered cake is its own flux: the silica that comes with the concentrate is balanced with limestone so the blast furnace slag has a basicity near 1.5-2.0. Anything between about 1.0 and 2.0 works; outside that range the slag eithe"];
  "metal.lead.sinter-plant" [label="Dwight-Lloyd sinter strand" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A continuous chain of cast-iron pallets on an endless track about 1.2-1.5 m wide, with the ignition hood at the discharge end. Feed is a mix of concentrate, sinter returns, flux and coke spread to exactly 1-2 m - there is nothing clever about it and it works. The strand advances 1-2 m/min, the fire "];
  "metal.galena" [label="Galena (PbS) concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Galena is cubic, bright grey when fresh and dull grey when oxidised, extremely heavy at 7.6 g/cm3, and it is one of the few common ores that a prospector could identify by eye. The great mining districts in 1982 were the Tri-State district of Missouri, Oklahoma and Kansas (which supplied most of the"];
  "metal.galena.mine" [label="Underground lead-zinc mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Tri-State district's galena bodies sit as veins and bedding replacements in Mississippian limestone only a few metres thick, and they were worked by room-and-pillar mining - essentially taking the limestone out and leaving the lead in place, because the ore grade at 3-6% Pb did not pay for selec"];
  "metal.galena.sorting" [label="Hand picking and gravity sorting" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Tri-State district's ore was a 3-6% lead limestone that could not be selectively mined and could not be concentrated, so the technique was brute force: the rock was shot down, the lead-bearing rock was picked out of the face by hand by a face worker, and then the cars were run over a sorting tab"];
  "metal.galena.sorting-table" [label="The sorting table" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A sloping table with a light on it and a raised lip, worked by two or three people, one on each side. Galena is bright and heavy and galena-bearing limestone is dark and light, so the visual contrast is real, and the sorter's job is to lift out the dark lumps at perhaps 2-4 tonnes per shift. It is t"];
  "metal.galena.rock" [label="Galenite replacement in carbonate rock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: galena crystals and veins in a fossiliferous limestone - literally the remains of a Carboniferous sea floor. The Tri-State district is one of the great fossil-mineral coincidences of industrial history: a lead-bearing limestone under a farm field."];
  "metal.tin" [label="Tin: solder alloy, tin plate, contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tin is the metal that made electronics solderable. On a 1982 C64 board there are roughly 3,500 through-hole joints and most of them are joined with a lead-tin alloy, so there is a couple of hundred grams of tin per machine. Tin also does two other jobs in the machine: it is the coating on tin-plated"];
  "metal.tin.refining" [label="Refine the crude tin to 99.9%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Crude tin at 99.5-99.8% still carries iron, copper and arsenic, and the difference between 99.5% and 99.9% matters enormously for solder because iron is a solder poison and raises the liquidus. Two routes. Liquation, the old one, melts the crude tin at about 400 C: tin melts at 232 C, so the iron an"];
  "metal.tin.plating" [label="Tin plating of steel and of copper alloys" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tinplate - a 1.1-11.2 g/m2 tin coating on 0.15-0.3 mm of steel - is made by electrolytic tinning of a continuous steel strip on a horizontal passivating line: the strip is degreased, pickled in acid, tinned in a sulphate or fluoroborate bath, rinsed, and then either passivated in chromic acid (food "];
  "metal.tin.contact-plating" [label="Tin plating of copper-alloy contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Not tinplate on steel, but tin on brass or phosphor bronze - the plating on the contacts of a C64 switch or a keyboard connector. The 1980s chemistry was an acid stannate or an alkaline stannate bath: SnSO4 with sulfuric acid, or sodium stannate with caustic soda and a puller such as sodium gluconat"];
  "metal.tin.ingot" [label="Refined tin ingot or tin powder, 99.9-99.97%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Shipped as a 25-50 kg or 250 kg ingot at 99.9-99.97%, or - for the electronics industry - atomised into a fine powder. This is the deliberate convergence point of the tin smelting branch and the solder branch: the solder manufacturer needs the ingot, and the solder-paste manufacturer needs the powde"];
  "metal.tin.atomisation" [label="Air atomisation of tin to powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Molten tin at about 1150 C is poured into a launder and a high-pressure air jet at the bottom of a vertical stack sprays it into a fog of droplets that solidify on the way down. It is cheap, it works for metals that do not oxidise much, and it gives a bimodal powder. Under nitrogen it gives the fine"];
  "metal.tin.fluid-bed-reactor" [label="Fluid-bed reduction of fine cassiterite (post-1982 route)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Worth noting as the direction the industry went in rather than as 1982 practice: the fine concentrates that a gravity circuit now produces reduce easily in a fluidised bed at 850-1000 C, which is a temperature no reverberatory furnace can hold at its hearth. This process is why the modern tin indust"];
  "metal.tin.smelting" [label="Tin smelting: reduction of SnO2 with carbon" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cassiterite is one of the easiest oxide ores to reduce: SnO2 + C -\> Sn + CO2 goes at only 1200-1400 C, far below copper or iron, because tin's oxides are unstable. The Malaysian and Indonesian smelters in 1982 were reverberatory or electric furnaces on 5-20 t capacity, fired with coke, charged with "];
  "metal.tin.slag" [label="The tin smelter's slag and its tin loss" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A tin smelter loses 1-3% of its tin into the slag, and the slag is a silicate of iron and silica with tin locked up in it. Recovering that tin is a separate business: the slag is crushed and re-smelted, or in Malaysia and Brazil it was re-smelted in a small furnace to recover the tin. A tin smelter "];
  "metal.tin.charge-prep" [label="Dry, sieve and mix the smelter charge" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cassiterite concentrates are roasted if they carry fluorine or arsenic, dried to remove the 8-12% moisture, and sieved - because a reverberatory furnace with fine charge in it just makes dust. The charge is then mixed with the limestone flux and the coke, in the ratio that gives 15-20% flux and 15-2"];
  "metal.tin.bolivian-roast" [label="Roasting of fluorine-bearing Bolivian concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Bolivian concentrates carry fluorine, which would carry through into the tin as a stubborn impurity. So they are roasted at 600-800 C before smelting to drive off the fluorine as HF and the arsenic as arsenic oxide, and the hot gas is scrubbed with lime to make calcium fluoride before release. That "];
  "metal.fluorspar" [label="Fluorspar (CaF2) ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Calcium fluoride rock, the only commercial source of fluorine. A 1982 hydrofluoric acid plant buys 97-98% acid made by treating acid-grade fluorspar (97%+ CaF2) with sulphuric acid in a kiln, and the acid then goes into the aluminium fluoride and cryolite that the Hall-Heroult bath needs and into th"];
  "metal.fluorspar.mine" [label="Fluorite open-pit and underground mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Fluorite fills the cavities and fracture zones of limestone, so it is mined either as a replacement body in a working limestone quarry or as a vein. Mexican fluorite was underground at Naica - where it grows the famous 12 m transparent crystals in a cave - and in open pits in Chihuahua. The South Af"];
  "metal.fluorspar.vein" [label="Fluorite vein in limestone" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a purple, green or white vein of CaF2 a few centimetres to a few metres thick in a fractured carbonate host, in a limestone hill."];
  "metal.cassiterite" [label="Cassiterite concentrate (SnO2)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cassiterite is a very heavy, very hard, brown-to-black mineral - the specific gravity of 7 makes gravity separation unusually effective on it, which is why tin mining has always had a placer tradition. The concentrates bought by an smelter in 1982 came from four very different operations: alluvial a"];
  "metal.cassiterite.alluvial" [label="Alluvial cassiterite gravel: dredging and panning" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tin gravel is a beach or stream deposit: pebbles and clay with a few grams of cassiterite per tonne. The classical recovery is the sluice and the jig, where a stream of water over an inclined riffled box stratifies by density and the heavy mineral stays behind. The Kinta Valley in Perak, Malaysia, w"];
  "metal.cassiterite.gravel" [label="Stanniferous alluvium and laterite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: unconsolidated stony clay and gravel of a valley floor or a coastal plain, with cassiterite grains scattered through it and concentrated at the base of gravel layers where the wave or the stream action is right. In Malaysia it is a young alluvial fan over limestone; in Bolivia it is not al"];
  "metal.cassiterite.lode" [label="Cassiterite lode in the Bolivian and Cornwall tin belt" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="For contrast: the tin lodes of the Cordillera Real in Bolivia and of the Cambrian granite cupolas in Cornwall are hydrothermal cassiterite in quartz veins and greisens, and they have to be blasted and milled. Bolivia's Oruro and La Paz districts still supply a fraction of world tin from ore averagin"];
  "metal.cassiterite.greisen" [label="Greisen and quartz vein" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf for the hard-rock route: a pneumatolytic alteration halo of topaz-bearing greisen and milky quartz with cassiterite disseminated through it, around a granite cupola. It is a rock, it is in a hillside, and it is drilled."];
  "metal.cassiterite.topaz" [label="Topaz and the greisen alteration" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf, geologically: a pneumatolytic alteration halo around the granite cupola in which the feldspars are replaced by topaz (Al2SiO4F2) and quartz, and in the interstices sit the crystals of cassiterite, wolframite and native bismuth. The fluorine in the topaz is the whole reason the alteration h"];
  "metal.copper" [label="Copper: track, winding wire, foil, contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The most-used metal after iron, and in a C64 it is everywhere the electricity goes and everywhere the heat has to leave. The PCB tracks are 35 um copper foil on 1.6 mm FR-4; the switch-mode transformer is wound with 0.2-0.4 mm enamelled magnet wire; the rectifiers and the regulator leadframes are co"];
  "metal.copper.foil" [label="Copper foil: 35 um PCB and 6-18 um capacitor foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two completely different foils with the same name, and this is a deliberate convergence node. The PCB foil is 35 um copper (70 um on very old boards) electrolytically deposited or rolled; the C64's board is single-sided, so it is one sheet of foil laminated to 1.6 mm FR-4. The capacitor foil is elec"];
  "metal.copper.foil.ra" [label="Rolled annealed copper foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The traditional foil: heavy copper strip rolled on a Sendimir mill to 35-105 um and slit. It has a rolled, fibrous grain that is anisotropic, so it is easy to form and easy to drill, and it was the foil of record for anything that needs to be bent rather than etched. By 1982 electronic-grade rolled "];
  "metal.copper.foil.ed-drum" [label="Electro-deposited drum foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A rotating drum cathode, 1-2 m diameter and 1-1.5 m wide, dips into an acid copper sulphate bath and turns; copper plates onto it at 20-100 A/dm2. A scraper peels the foil off at the top of the drum as a continuous 6-18 um web, which is then slit and wound. The deposit is not bulk metal: it is a col"];
  "metal.copper.foil.bonding" [label="Nodular treatment for PCB foil adhesion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Bare copper foil will not bond to epoxy; it delaminates. So the foil is treated in a warm sulfuric acid bath with a silicofluoride or a silane - historically sodium silicofluoride, later silanes - which grows a forest of microscopic oxide nodules on the surface. The resin flows into the forest and l"];
  "metal.copper.wire" [label="Enamelled magnet wire for transformer windings" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The switch-mode transformer in a C64's power supply - and any small mains-frequency transformer - is a coil of enamelled copper wire. The wire starts as 6-8 mm rod, is patented (heated to 200-350 C so the work-hardening from drawing anneals out), then drawn through a series of diamond dies to 0.15-0"];
  "metal.copper.enamelling" [label="Enamelling and cure of magnet wire" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The drawn wire is passed through a die and coated with liquid polyurethane enamel in a continuous length of 10-20 coats, each flash-dried, then cured in a vertical oven at 150-220 C for up to an hour. The result is a wire whose insulation survives a 155 C winding temperature and whose dielectric str"];
  "metal.copper.enamelling.furnace" [label="The enamelling line and the cure oven" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A magnet-wire enamelling line is a 20-40 m long machine: a pay-off, a bunch of dies applying 10-20 coats with flash-off between them, a vertical cure oven 8-15 m tall, a capstan, a take-up. The cure oven is gas or electric at 150-220 C with the wire passing through it in a helix, and it is the cure "];
  "metal.copper.enamel-resin" [label="Polyurethane enamel from a di-isocyanate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The wire enamel is a thermosetting urethane, made from a diol and a di-isocyanate such as toluene di-isocyanate, with a blocked-isocyanate cure so the coating is stable in the coating bath and only reacts in the cure oven. Its advantage over phenolic (vinyl formal, the pre-war standard) and polyeste"];
  "metal.copper.multiwire-drawing" [label="Multiwire drawing through diamond dies" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Since the 1960s the industry stopped drawing one wire at a time and started drawing 7-19 wires simultaneously through a cone of dies, so the die size needed for a 0.2 mm wire is much larger and lasts much longer. The dies are natural diamond - or, as synthetic diamond tooling matured through the 198"];
  "metal.copper.patenting" [label="Patenting: annealing the rod before drawing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Copper work-hardens fast: a few percent of reduction raises the tensile strength enormously and the wire simply snaps in the die. So the rod is heated - in steam, in air, or in a fluidised bed - to about 200-350 C, which recrystallises the elongated drawing structure back to equiaxed grains, and the"];
  "metal.copper.wire-lubricant" [label="Drawing lubricant: copper sulfonate or oil emulsion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The die must be lubricated or the copper galls and the die wears out. In the 1980s the standard was a water-based emulsion or a copper-sulfonate-and-fatty-acid concentrate, applied in a die box or as a once-through bath. Lubricant chemistry is also the friction and galling story in the die, which is"];
  "metal.copper.tankhouse" [label="Electrolytic refining of copper to 99.99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The refinery's tankhouse is a building a couple of hundred metres long containing rows of concrete or rubber-lined steel cells. Each cell holds a 150-250 g/L copper, 200-250 g/L free sulfuric acid solution at 55-65 C, with one or two anodes and one cathode per cell. Current at 20,000-40,000 A per ce"];
  "metal.copper.tankhouse.starters" [label="Starter sheets, seeding and the tankhouse floor" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Everything in a tankhouse depends on nucleation. The cathodes start on stainless steel or titanium starter sheets, or on copper seed plates that have been roughened. If too many nuclei start, the deposit is dull and soft and hard to strip; if too few, it is bright and hard and needs a big shear forc"];
  "metal.anode-slime" [label="Anode slime: the real reason to smelt copper" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The sludge on the tankhouse cell floor, 5-20 kg per tonne of copper refined, containing gold, silver, platinum, palladium, selenium and tellurium. It is dried and shipped to precious-metals refineries, where the gold is separated by cupellation, the selenium by roasting to SeO2 and volatilising, and"];
  "metal.anode-slime.selenide" [label="Selenium and tellurium from the anode slime" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two of the minor metals that pay for a copper smelter. The slime is roasted in a fluidised bed and the selenium volatilises as SeO2, caught in a hot cyclone or an electrostatic precipitator; the tellurium comes out of the roasted residue by leaching and electrowinning. Both are a few per cent of the"];
  "metal.anode-slime.cupellation" [label="Cupellation of the gold-silver dross" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The slime is smelted with lead and flux into a litharge, then the litharge is blown with air in a cupellation furnace. The lead and everything else oxidises and is absorbed by a bone-ash or magnesite cupel, and what is left on the cupel is gold and silver together. This is the oldest refinery proces"];
  "metal.cupellation.cupel" [label="Bone ash cupel from animal bone" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Traditional cupels are made of calcined bone - tricalcium phosphate - ground and pressed into a shallow cup with a porous, low-density surface that absorbs the molten litharge. Bone ash is the reason cupellation is called what it is, and by the 1982 industry the major refineries had largely moved to"];
  "metal.palladium" [label="Platinum-group metals from the anode slime" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The anode slime also carries platinum, palladium, ruthenium, iridium, rhodium and osmium, at grams per tonne of copper rather than kilograms, so the volumes are tiny and the values are astronomical. In 1982 most of the world's refined palladium came from the nickel-copper ores of the Sudbury and Nor"];
  "metal.copper.cathode" [label="Electrolytic copper cathode, 99.99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The end product of the mine-to-metal chain and the shape in which essentially all world copper trades. A flat plate grown on a stainless steel or copper starter sheet, with a characteristic slightly rough surface because too many nucleation sites make a soft, dull deposit. Cathode mass is 45-125 kg "];
  "metal.copper.anode.casting" [label="Anode casting and the anode scrap stream" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="About 10-20% of the anode never makes it into a cell - it is broken in handling, or the bars are too thin, or the busbar is corroded away - and it goes back to be remelted and recast. Every tankhouse therefore has a stub and an anode scrap yard and a melt, and the plant's anode book is a mass balanc"];
  "metal.copper.ingot-casting" [label="Copper ingot and rod casting" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cathode is melted in an induction furnace or a reverberatory with a controlled sulphur addition, then cast - either continuously into wire rod by the Properzi upcast method or into 250-450 kg ingots for further processing. The 1980s standard route to rod was continuous casting; the alternative, "];
  "metal.copper.upcasting" [label="Upcast wire rod line" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="In the Properzi upcaster the melt runs out of a horizontal holding furnace into a vertical casting trough behind a steel die that is being drawn upward by a rotating carousel; the trough is water-cooled and the copper solidifies against the moving die face, giving a controlled solidification structu"];
  "metal.copper.anode" [label="Blister copper anode, 99.9%+" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Blister is poured into a horizontal straight-mould casting machine - a ladle of molten copper that pours into a row of cast-iron moulds on a moving chain, giving a rectangular anode of 200-500 kg, roughly 700-1000 mm long, 400-800 mm wide and 30-50 mm thick, with a hanging loop cast on top. In 1982 "];
  "metal.copper.converting" [label="Peirce-Smith converter to blister copper" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The matte from the flash furnace is charged into a horizontal cylindrical steel converter with refractory lining and is blown with air through tuyeres along the side. The iron in the matte oxidises preferentially and goes into a slag that is skimmed off the top every few hours; once the iron is gone"];
  "metal.copper.converter" [label="The Peirce-Smith converter and its tuyeres" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The converter is a cylindrical steel shell, 3.5-4.8 m inside diameter and 8-10 m long, on trunnions so it can be tilted, lined with magnesite brick, with 20-40 tuyeres along one side at the trunnion line. Air at 30-50% oxygen is blown in at 5-10 bar through water-cooled tuyeres that must be cleared "];
  "metal.copper.fire-refining" [label="Fire-refine the blister copper" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Blister copper still carries arsenic, antimony and bismuth, and every one of them is a nightmare for the electrolytic tankhouse because they float to the cathode and destroy the deposit. So the blister is ladle-refined: an oxidising slag of sodium carbonate with the impurities skims off, and then th"];
  "metal.copper.smelting" [label="Flash smelting of concentrate to copper matte" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Concentrate, silica flux and limestone are dropped into a vertical reverberatory shaft where a hot gas blast, 40-60% oxygen-enriched, burns the concentrate as it falls. Three reactions happen simultaneously: the sulphides oxidise to copper oxide and iron oxide, the oxides react with each other to fo"];
  "metal.copper.flash-furnace" [label="The flash furnace, its tuyeres and the concentrate burn" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The furnace is a 20-25 m tall, 5-7 m diameter vertical shaft with a refractory lining, fed by a few hundred tonnes an hour of dried concentrate through a hopper, and blown at the bottom by 30-60% oxygen-enriched air through tuyeres. The concentrate burns as it falls - it is the fuel as well as the c"];
  "metal.sulphuric-acid" [label="Sulphuric acid, 93-99%, from smelter SO2" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The most-produced inorganic chemical in the world, and about half to three fifths of it was made not from burning sulphur but from the offgas of the copper, zinc and lead smelters in this file. The plants are the same: gas cleaning, drying, 10-14% SO2, 400-450 C over a vanadium pentoxide catalyst, a"];
  "metal.phosphate-ore" [label="Apatite ore for phosphoric acid" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Florida pebble phosphate - a marine sedimentary phosphorite of fossil bones and excreta - is the classic source, and it is the end consumer of most of the sulfuric acid made in the copper and zinc branches of this file. It is mined by dragline or bucket-wheel stripping of 5-15 m of overburden ov"];
  "metal.phosphate.rock" [label="Marine phosphorite bed" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a 1-10 m bed of unconsolidated, often glauconitic phosphatic sand and clay in a marine or coastal plain sequence, sitting directly on limestone. It is dug, washed in a slurry pipeline, and floated or gravity-separated."];
  "metal.sulphuric-acid.uses" [label="Where the smelter acid goes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Roughly half of world sulfuric acid production is made from smelter offgas. In 1982 the biggest single use was fertiliser: phosphoric acid from Florida apatite, then superphosphate. The second was metal refining - the copper tankhouse electrolyte, the zinc plant leach circuit and the iron and steel "];
  "metal.silica-flux" [label="Silica flux from a sand quarry" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The smelter adds sand or quartzite so that the iron in the concentrate can be thrown off as a slag instead of entering the matte. The quartz is often a cheap nearby dune sand - which is exactly the same material the glass industry buys, and the reason a copper smelter in the 1980s had a sand dryer a"];
  "metal.copper.concentrate" [label="Copper flotation concentrate (~25-30% Cu)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A damp, fine, almost black powder shipped by rail or truck to the smelter. What the smelter really wants is not the copper but the sulphur, because that is what makes the smelter a sulfuric acid plant. A 25% Cu concentrate is roughly 50% sulphur and 10% iron, and the smelter's job is to burn out the"];
  "metal.copper.concentrate-handling" [label="Thicken, filter and ship the flotation concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The flotation concentrate at 25-30% Cu arrives as a pulp at 10-20% solids and has to be dewatered to 8-12% moisture to be shipped, because shipping water is shipping money. A 30,000 t/day concentrator produces 1,000-3,000 t of concentrate a day, and it is thickened in a 30-50 m diameter tank, filter"];
  "metal.copper.ore" [label="Porphyry copper ore (0.4-1% Cu)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A porphyritic intrusion - a granite-textured quartz monzonite that has been split by its own crystallisation and had its cracks filled with hot copper-bearing fluids. The copper minerals are chalcopyrite CuFeS2, bornite and chalcocite Cu2S, covellite and enargite Cu3AsS4. The ore body is graded by p"];
  "metal.copper.flotation" [label="Froth flotation of chalcopyrite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The slurry is conditioned with lime to a pH of 8-9.5 and with a xanthate collector, then aerated in a bank of 20-50 cubic metre rectangular cells. Air bubbles pick up the particles of chalcopyrite that the collector has made water-repellent and float them into a froth; the gangue stays in the water."];
  "metal.pine-oil-frother" [label="Pine oil and MIBC frothers" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Frothers are oily surfactants that stabilise the air-water interface. Until the 1970s the standard was pine oil distilled from pine stumps and mill residues - a forestry residue, not a mineral. Pine oil was being displaced by methyl isobutyl carbinol, a petrochemical, precisely because the natural s"];
  "metal.caustic-lime-flotation" [label="Lime and soda ash as pH regulators" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Flotation needs the ore surfaces clean of slime and the water at a controlled alkaline pH, or the xanthate decomposes to xanthic acid and carbon disulphide and evaporates. Lime from a limestone kiln is the cheapest and most common regulator; soda ash where lime is unavailable; and in the deep concen"];
  "metal.xanthate" [label="Ethyl xanthate collector (SIPC)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Xanthate is the workhorse flotation reagent, and it is a petrochemical in disguise: carbon disulphide (from natural gas or from coke-oven gas and methane) reacts with ethanol and caustic soda. Potassium and sodium ethyl xanthates were the standard 1980s collectors for sulphide copper, with a frother"];
  "metal.cs2-synthesis" [label="Carbon disulphide (CS2)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Carbon disulphide, the feedstock for every xanthate collector and for the rayon and cellophane industries, was made in 1982 almost entirely from natural gas or from methane recovered from coke-oven gas: the gas and a fraction of hydrocarbon oil are passed over a silica or alumina gel at 600-700 C, a"];
  "metal.copper.grinding" [label="Crushing and grinding to 100-150 um" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ore arrives at 1 m. It goes through a gyratory crusher, then a semiautogenous mill (which uses the ore itself as the grinding medium, so no steel balls to pay for at that stage), then one or two ball mills in a closed circuit with a spiral classifier and then a cyclone. Autogenous grinding came "];
  "metal.copper.steel-grinding-media" [label="Forged steel grinding balls" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Even in an autogenous circuit the ball mills need some steel, and the balls themselves are a ferroalloy product: rail steel or scrap forged and quenched. Hardness matters more than anything - a 1982 ball mill ran 100-150 mm balls in the harder grind, and the wear rate was the plant's second-biggest "];
  "metal.copper.mine" [label="Open-pit copper mine" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="An enormous terraced pit. Ore is drilled and blasted on 15 m benches, loaded into haul trucks by 3-6 cubic metre shovels or loaded onto a conveyor, and hauled in 100-150 tonne trucks out of the pit on ramps. The waste:ore ratio at a mature porphyry pit is often 2-5:1, which is why the waste dumps ar"];
  "metal.copper.haulage" [label="The haul road, the ramp and the truck cycle" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The economics of an open pit are a haulage problem. A 130-tonne truck on a 2 km round trip from the loading point to the crusher, at about 25 km/h loaded and 40 km/h empty, takes 3-5 minutes per cycle including spotting and loading, so a shovel with a 4-second loading cycle needs about 6-8 trucks to"];
  "metal.copper.dust-suppression" [label="Suppress the pit dust on the haul road" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 100,000 t/day pit with unsuppressed haul roads produces a very large tonnage of airborne dust, and by the 1970s that had become a legal problem: the Clean Air Act of 1970 and the 1974 federal mine-safety inspection regime both applied to metal mines, and the pit had to water its roads, restrict th"];
  "metal.copper.block-cave" [label="Block cave drawpoints and ore pass" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The block cave is the deepest and most sophisticated piece of 1980s mining engineering anywhere near a C64: a slot is cut along the base of the ore block, the slot is blasted, the overlying ore fractures and subsides into the void, and a finger stoper trains stop releasing ore once the cave front ha"];
  "metal.copper.intrusion" [label="Tertiary porphyry intrusion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a granodiorite or quartz monzonite stock, once liquid and now frozen, with the copper-bearing sulphide veins filling fractures and disseminated grains through it. Under a 100 m of leached, iron-stained capping lies the transition ore and then the primary sulphides, so the pit's first 50-15"];
  "metal.aluminum" [label="Primary aluminium: ingot, foil, sheet, extrusion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where the C64's aluminium comes from. Bauxite is dug, digested in caustic soda to alumina, and electrolysed in molten cryolite to 99.7% metal - about 13,000-17,000 kWh per tonne, the largest single industrial electricity draw anywhere in this tree. The ingot is then DC-cast to a rolling ingot and ro"];
  "metal.aluminum.extrusion" [label="Aluminium extrusion: the case and the heat sinks" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A hot billet is forced through a steel die by a ram at 420-500 C, at 20-100 MN of pressure, at several metres a second, and the profile that comes out is straight and, if the die is good, dimensionally within a fraction of a millimetre. The Commodore case is a complicated extrusion: the cooling fins"];
  "metal.aluminum.extrusion.die" [label="Steel extrusion die and its forging" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The die is a disc of hot-work tool steel with a cavity machined into it, and it has to be made before it can be used: drilled, rough-machined, heat-treated, then finish-machined and nitrided. A case profile takes a lot of steel and a lot of hours, which is why the extrusion industry is full of small"];
  "metal.aluminum.extrusion.press" [label="Hydraulic extrusion press, 500-2500 t" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The industry standard was the 2300-2500 t class press with a 200-500 mm container, driven by a hydraulic intensifier that gave a hard step change in speed at the start of the stroke. A heated soak furnace beside the press holds the billets, a shear cuts them to length, and the runout bench stretches"];
  "metal.aluminum.soak-furnace" [label="The soak furnace and the billet's temperature history" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Billet quality is set before the press ever sees it. The billets are heated in a gas- or oil-fired pit furnace to 420-500 C and held for 3-24 hours so that the temperature is uniform through the section, because a billet with a hot core and a cold skin extrudes with a surface tear and a soft interio"];
  "metal.aluminum.sheet" [label="Aluminium sheet: the case and the shields" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two uses in a C64: the heat-sink and shield material, and the case itself. The Commodore breadbin case is an aluminium extrusion with a stove-enamel finish, and the internal shields and the modulator compartment are plain sheet. Cold-rolled 1050A or 3003 sheet at 0.3-1.5 mm goes into drawing and sta"];
  "metal.aluminum.blending" [label="Alloying to 3003, 6063 or A13" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The rolling mill is not a refinery. It melts primary ingot, remelted can scrap and dross recovery in a reverberatory or an induction furnace, adds the alloying elements as lumps of master alloy, degasses with a rotating argon lance to strip hydrogen, and skims the dross. Manganese for 3003, magnesiu"];
  "metal.aluminum.foil" [label="Aluminium foil, 6-25 um, for capacitors" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64's power supply is full of electrolytic capacitors, and every one of them is an aluminium foil sandwich: a thin cathode foil wound with a thicker anode foil and a paper separator soaked in electrolyte. Making 6 um foil is a genuinely hard rolling problem - at that gauge the metal is thinner t"];
  "metal.aluminum.anode-foil" [label="Etched aluminium anode foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The trick that made a decent electrolytic fit into an 8 mm diameter can. Plain anode foil etched in a hot acid bath grows an aluminium oxide/hydroxide layer over essentially the whole surface - up to about a hundred times the geometric area, and therefore a hundred times the capacitance per unit of "];
  "metal.aluminum.cold-rolling" [label="Cold rolling on a Sendimir high-roll mill" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 2-6 mm hot strip goes to a multi-high cold mill - a Sendimir 20-high or 24-high arrangement where a small work roll is backed by many intermediate rolls, giving the enormous rolling force needed at a small gap without a mill body the size of a building. The work rolls are hardened tool steel, 10"];
  "metal.aluminum.rolling-oil" [label="Aluminium rolling oil and the degreaser" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cold rolling of aluminium would tear without a lubricant: a water-in-oil emulsion or a neat mineral oil with fatty-acid esters, applied by nozzles at order 0.1-1 g/m2. The oil is what lets the gauge get down without the surface tearing, and it has to be removed afterwards - a few grams of oil left o"];
  "metal.aluminum.rolling-oil.tallow" [label="Fatty-acid esters from tallow and palm fat" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ester lubricants are made from animal or vegetable fats - tallow, palm, rapeseed - hydrolysed to fatty acids and re-esterified, on a paraffinic mineral base or a synthetic ester. This is the one place in the aluminium chain where biomass chemistry is genuinely load-bearing: an aluminium rolling "];
  "metal.aluminum.hot-rolling" [label="Hot rolling to 2-6 mm" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ingot is preheated to 300-400 C for wide slab or 450-550 C for narrower, thicker slab, and a reversing hot mill takes it down to 2-6 mm in one or two passes. Aluminium's rolling window is famously narrow - too cold and it tears at the edges, too hot and the ingot surface oxidises and scales - so"];
  "metal.aluminum.mill-scale" [label="Mill scale (iron-bearing aluminium oxide)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Hot rolling oxidises the surface and the scale is knocked off by the descaling rolls. The chips that fall are a mix of alumina and iron oxides pulled in from the ingot surface, and they are the same material the ferroalloy and cement industries fought over - and, ground and magnetically separated, t"];
  "metal.aluminum.dc-casting" [label="DC casting to a rolling ingot" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Molten aluminium at 700-750 C is poured into a steel mould that is being withdrawn by a hydraulic jack at a controlled few millimetres per second, so the metal freezes progressively behind a solidification front instead of all at once. The front is water-cooled and the emerging surface is squirted w"];
  "metal.aluminum.homogenising" [label="Homogenising soak" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A long hold in a gas- or electrically-heated pit furnace, typically 12-24 h at 450-600 C, whose only job is to let the alloying elements diffuse evenly. Without it a slab rolled on one edge behaves differently from the same slab rolled on the other and the foil tears. It also rounds the dendritic st"];
  "metal.aluminum.ingot" [label="Primary aluminium ingot, 99.7%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The metal itself, and the deliberate convergence point of the electrolysis branch and the rolling branch. Tapped from the pot every 24-48 hours, brought up to about 720 C, filtered to remove the entrained alumina and metal droplets, alloyed if the customer wants it, and cast. In 1982 the standard pr"];
  "metal.hall-heroult" [label="Hall-Heroult electrolysis of alumina in cryolite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cell is a steel shell the size of a bathtub lined with carbon, containing 300-500 t of molten cryolite at 950-970 C. Alumina dissolves in the melt and is reduced at a carbon cathode; current passes through the anodes, where the oxygen from the alumina burns the carbon away and makes carbon dioxi"];
  "metal.hall-heroult.dross" [label="Aluminium dross and the skimmings" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two or three tonnes of dross and sludge per pot per year: oxide, carbon, and aluminium that never reached the cathode. Because aluminium dross burns spectacularly it must be handled under cover, and the smelter had a dross furnace to burn out the aluminium and get it back. The recovered metal is dir"];
  "metal.hall-heroult.alumina-dredge" [label="Alumina dredges and the alumina crust" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Alumina fed into the pot floats and solidifies into a chilled crust on the bath surface, which is far more stable than loose powder. Machines called dredges or skimmers break holes in this crust and feed alumina down them at a controlled rate; the classic 1980 design was the dual-feed with pneumatic"];
  "metal.hall-heroult.pot-lining" [label="Carbon pot lining and the cathodic block" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The steel shell is lined first with refractory brick, then with a monolithic carbon lining about 400-600 mm thick made of calcined coke, graphite and hot pitch, rammed in place. A block lining of graphitised carbon blocks is better and was common in newer cells. The lining is deliberately cathodic: "];
  "metal.cryolite" [label="Cryolite (Na3AlF6), the electrolyte solvent" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The white, insoluble, water-repellent mineral that makes aluminium electrolysis possible at all. Pure alumina melts at 2072 C; a bath of cryolite with excess aluminium fluoride and some calcium fluoride is liquid at 950-970 C, and the alumina dissolves in it at 6-10%. The other crucial property is t"];
  "metal.aluminium-fluoride" [label="Aluminium fluoride and the bath" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The excess AlF3 in the bath is not a deliberate additive so much as the consequence of the water and the fluorides in the alumina; smelters add it back to hold the bath at the composition that gives the best current efficiency, which is somewhere between 8 and 14% excess AlF3. Too little and the bat"];
  "metal.cryolite.synthesis" [label="Synthetic cryolite from hydrofluoric acid" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Because natural cryolite was one mine in Greenland, the industry made its own. Sodium carbonate or caustic soda reacts with hydrofluoric acid and aluminium hydroxide at 60-90 C: 6HF + 3Na2CO3 + Al(OH)3 -\> 2Na3AlF6 + 3CO2 + 3H2O. So the electrolyte solvent of the aluminium pot is manufactured from fl"];
  "metal.alumina" [label="Smelter-grade alumina, 99.5% Al2O3" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A white, sandy, hygroscopic powder delivered to the smelter in railcars or 1-2 t bags. The contract is brutal: 99.5% Al2O3 minimum, loss on ignition below about 1%, total iron below 0.02%, because every gram of iron that reaches the cell ends up in the metal and the ingot stops being 99.7%. In 1982 "];
  "metal.bayer" [label="Bayer process" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 110-year-old caustic-leach purification that every tonne of aluminium still goes through. Five steps: crush and wash, digest in caustic at pressure, clarify and throw away the red mud, cool and precipitate pure aluminium hydroxide, calcine to alumina. It works because the alumina in bauxite is amp"];
  "metal.bayer.calcination" [label="Calcine the trihydrate to smelter-grade alumina" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The hydrate goes into a rotary kiln or, more often by 1982, a fluidised-bed calciner, and at 1000-1100 C it gives off its water of crystallisation and recrystallises from gamma through theta to alpha alumina. Alpha alumina is the hard, insoluble, spark-resistant form that will dissolve in cryolite. "];
  "metal.bayer.calciner" [label="Fluidised-bed calciner" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Hot gas from a multi-flash evaporator is blown through a bed of alumina hydrate on a distributor of alloy nozzles, holding the bed fluid at 1000-1100 C by balancing gas throughput against feed rate. The bed fluidises like a boiling liquid and every grain sees the same temperature, which is exactly w"];
  "metal.bayer.precipitation" [label="Precipitate aluminium hydroxide from the cooled liquor" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The clear caustic aluminate solution is cooled from 100-110 C to 60-80 C and seeded with a few grams per litre of finely ground aluminium hydroxide already in the plant. The seed grows into the supersaturated solution as a hexagonal-plate alumina trihydrate over 24-48 hours, and the mud is filtered "];
  "metal.bayer.filter" [label="Rotary vacuum filtration and counter-current washing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The hydrate is filtered on a rotating vacuum drum with cloth- or paper-covered segments, then washed on a multi-stage counter-current filter. By the time the wash water reaches the pregnant liquor the caustic is essentially recovered, so the caustic circuit closes to better than 95%. This single pie"];
  "metal.bayer.clarification" [label="Clarify the liquor and dam the red mud" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The digested slurry is diluted with process water, cooled, and settled in a bank of gravity settlers with mechanical rakes; the red mud bottoms out and is pumped to the mud dam. A large plant produces 100-150 tonnes of red mud per tonne of alumina, which is why every alumina refinery has a mud dam e"];
  "metal.red-mud" [label="Red mud (bauxite residue)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A caustic, red, kaolin-rich sludge, typically 2.5-6 mol/L free NaOH, containing essentially all the iron, titanium and silica from the ore. A large refinery produces 100-150 tonnes of it per tonne of alumina. In 1982 the industry standard for handling it was to build a dam and pump it in; the search"];
  "metal.bayer.digestion" [label="Caustic digestion in autoclaves" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Bauxite slurry and 150-300 g/L free caustic (as Na2O) are charged into stirred autoclaves and held at temperature for 30-90 minutes. The alumina dissolves as NaAl(OH)4; the iron oxide stays as a red slurry. Older low-temperature plants used a bank of horizontal autoclaves with indirect steam heating"];
  "metal.bayer.desilication" [label="Reactive silica and the lime reaction" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The killer impurity in bauxite is reactive silica - kaolinite clay and opal - which dissolves in the caustic and then reacts with aluminate to form a non-redissolving kaolinite-nepheline hydrate that permanently traps alumina. Two defences. In low-temperature digestion the clay is left undissolved b"];
  "metal.bauxite" [label="Bauxite (hydrated alumina ore)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A soft, red-brown, pisolitic or clayey laterite, 45-60% Al2O3, still essentially the ore it was before anybody wanted it. Ore below about 180 C of gibbsite digest easily at low temperature and is called low-temperature bauxite; ore with diaspore and boehmite needs 240-280 C and is called high-temper"];
  "metal.bauxite.mine" [label="Open-cast bauxite mine with dragline" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Strip mining on a 10-20 m bench height, with a bucket-wheel excavator or a walking dragline stripping 10-50 m of overburden and then a bench of bauxite perhaps only 3-6 m thick. The dragline is the point: it digs the bauxite, swings and dumps it onto a ramp, and strips the next strip of cover, all i"];
  "metal.bauxite.beneficiation" [label="Bauxite crushing, washing and drying" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Bayer plant does not want a 300 mm boulder and it certainly does not want the clay. The run-of-mine ore is crushed in a gyratory or a jaw crusher to 25-50 mm, and then - in the wet-grinding route that became standard for high-temperature bauxite - it is ground in a ball mill in a rod or a wet sc"];
  "metal.bauxite.laterite" [label="Lateritic bauxite profile" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a 3-12 m pisolitic blanket of iron-stained bauxite nodules sitting on a Cretaceous or Tertiary lateritic surface above a kaolinitic clay. Guinea's deposits are the classic tropical pisolite; the Australian Weipa and Gove ores are flat-lying terrace deposits on Cretaceous sediments. Either "];
  "metal.bauxite.kaolinite" [label="The kaolinite and gibbsite beneath the bauxite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf of the aluminium branch, and the reason the alumina process exists in the form it does. The economic bauxite is a 3-12 m blanket of iron-stained nodules sitting directly on kaolinite clay, and the kaolinite is the reactive silica that has to be neutralised by lime or washed out. Below that "];
  "metal.bauxite.creek-wash" [label="Wash the bauxite in a creek sluice" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The last leaf of the aluminium branch. In the Weipa and Gove deposits on the Cretaceous laterite surface, bauxite is dug in the wet season and the clay is washed out of it in a creek: a 200 m long, 30 m wide sluice excavated along the edge of the terrace, into which the bauxite is tipped and a strea"];
  "metal.laterite-terrace" [label="The Cretaceous laterite surface" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf, geologically: a flat, deeply weathered surface on Cretaceous sandstone where a tropical lateritic profile has developed over tens of millions of years, with a bauxite horizon at the top, kaolinite below it, and the saprolite and fresh rock beneath. The flatness is the point - it is why the"];
  "metal.potassium" [label="Potassium chloride (KCl) for tantalum reduction" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Not a C64 component - potassium never appears in the machine - but it is a mandatory reagent in the tantalum branch, because the metal is won as a fluoride salt and reduced with metal, not from an oxide. Potassium also divides tantalum from niobium in the fluoride chemistry. It is included so the ta"];
  "metal.potassium.flux" [label="Potassium chloride as the tantalum flux" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The only reason potassium is in this tree. Potassium chloride is added to the tantalum fluoride solution to precipitate K2TaF7, and potassium fluoride is used as a flux in some silicon and aluminium processes. Both are bought from the potash industry as a commodity, in tonnes. So a handful of grams "];
  "metal.sylvinite" [label="Sylvinite (KCl + NaCl)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ore of the Devonian Prairie Evaporite in Saskatchewan, mined underground at 800-1100 m by potash shafts where the ground is artificially frozen so the salt stays put, or by solution mining in Ontario. Run of mine is about 8-14% K2O; it is beneficiated by flotation or hot leaching and sold as mur"];
  "metal.sylvinite.solution-mining" [label="Solution mining of potash" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where the potash seam is deeply buried and thin - as at Thames, Ontario - the shaft is not worth sinking. Instead two horizontal wells are drilled into the seam, one to inject hot water and one to draw the resulting brine, and the potash is recovered from the brine by evaporation. Two wells can prod"];
  "metal.solution-mining.well" [label="The injection and recovery well pair" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Solution mining is two wells and a plant. A cemented well is drilled horizontally into the potash seam and a casing is set with a perforated liner; hot water at 80-90 C is injected at a pressure of a few hundred psi, the seam is about 1-3 m thick and dissolves over a 30-200 m horizontal section, and"];
  "metal.sylvinite.beneficiation" [label="Potash flotation and hot leaching" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Run-of-mine sylvinite is about 12% K2O with a lot of NaCl and clay. Beneficiation is by flotation of the sylvinite in a saturated KCl-brine solution (because sylvinite is barely soluble while halite is much more so, so the brine selectively wets the sylvinite), or by hot leaching at 85-100 C which d"];
  "metal.potash-product" [label="Muriate of potash and its tailings" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The product is 60-95% KCl, bagged or bulk, and the waste is a slurry of NaCl and clay that goes into a tailings pond impoundment. Two points of interest for this tree: the potassium chloride that ends up in a potassium fluoride bath for the tantalum reduction is bought from this industry, and the ta"];
  "metal.potash.stockyard" [label="Move the potash out by unit train to the port" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Saskatchewan potash moved to world markets by unit train - a dedicated train of a hundred or more hopper wagons - to Vancouver, Prince Albert or Minneapolis, and then by ship from Vancouver or by barge and lake freighter to Chicago. So the potassium in a 1982 tantalum capacitor travelled down a pota"];
  "metal.sylvinite.freezing" [label="Freeze the ground around the potash shaft" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The Saskatchewan potash ore is 800-1100 m down in a bed that is 30-40 C and gassy, and it is a weak, water-bearing shale-like rock that would flow into the shaft. The solution since 1967 has been to freeze it: brine is circul through freeze pipes in the shaft and the decline for two to three years b"];
  "metal.potash.evaporite" [label="Potash evaporite bed" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf: a buried evaporite sea - sylvinite interbedded with halite, carnallite and mudstone, formed by the same evaporation of a restricted sea that made the salt used in chlor-alkali. The Colville and Hatchet lakes area of Saskatchewan sits over one of the largest."];
  "metal.sodium" [label="Sodium compounds: caustic soda and soda ash" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sodium is not in the machine as a metal, but the C64 could not exist without it: caustic soda dissolves bauxite to make alumina, and the same chlor-alkali industry that makes the caustic soda also makes the chlorine and hydrogen that become the hydrochloric acid for the silicon plant. Behind both si"];
  "metal.caustic-soda" [label="Caustic soda (NaOH), 50% solution" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two entirely different consumers in this tree. The Bayer process digestion liquor, where a caustic grade tanker holds roughly 300-400 t of 50% solution and a refinery's caustic and steam costs dominate its operating cost. And the chlor-alkali industry, where the same product comes out of the same ce"];
  "metal.chlor-alkali" [label="Chlor-alkali electrolytic cell" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="2NaCl + 2H2O -\> 2NaOH + H2 + Cl2. In 1982 almost all of it was a diaphragm cell or a mercury cell. The diaphragm cell - an asbestos-cloth cathode separator - gave low-purity caustic loaded with salt and was steadily displaced through the late 1970s and 1980s by the membrane cell, which delivers 50% "];
  "metal.chlor-alkali.dechlorination" [label="Dechlorination and the brine circuit" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The brine leaving a cell still has hypochlorite in it, because chlorine dissolves in the caustic. It is treated with sodium sulphite and then acidified, the brine is re-saturated with salt and re-purified on a soda ash and barium carbonate cycle, and it goes back to the cell. So a chlor-alkali plant"];
  "metal.chlor-alkali.digram-cell" [label="The diaphragm cell and the asbestos problem" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 1980s chlor-alkali cell was a rectangular steel box, 2-3 m long, with an anode compartment of brine and a cathode compartment of water separated by an asbestos cloth diaphragm deposited on a wire gauze. The diaphragm was cheap, durable and effective, and it was the reason the world's chlor-alkal"];
  "metal.hydrogen" [label="Electrolysis hydrogen from the chlor-alkali cathode" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The wet hydrogen off the cathode goes to acid synthesis, and in a silicon plant it also goes to the Siemens reactor as the carrier that carries trichlorosilane to the rod and strips the chlorine off. The purification train is a caustic scrubber, then a drying tower, and the carbon monoxide has to be"];
  "metal.chlorine" [label="Chlorine gas (Cl2)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A pale yellow-green gas denser than air, made in the chlor-alkali plant and piped or tanker-shipped to the hydrochloric acid synthesis unit. Around 1982 much of it went to solvent makers and to water treatment as chlorinated organics; only a fraction ever reached a silicon plant, and silicon-grade c"];
  "metal.silica" [label="High-purity silicon feedstock (9N polysilicon)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The refined element every chip in the machine is cut from. A 1982 Commodore 64 mainboard carried a handful of silicon dies and one 2 MHz quartz crystal, both traceable to this chain: quartzite or silica reduced to 98-99% metallurgical silicon, chlorinated to trichlorosilane, distilled to 9-nines, de"];
  "metal.silica.quartz-crystal" [label="2.048 MHz quartz clock crystal" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64's timing reference: an AT-cut quartz plate about 7.6 x 2.5 mm brazed between metal caps, resonating at 2.048 MHz. Quartz is the only material with a usable temperature-stable mechanical resonance that cheap. The plate itself is usually not natural rock - it is hydrothermally grown synthetic "];
  "metal.silica.quartz-crystal.synthetic" [label="Grow the clock crystal hydrothermally" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A seed of vein quartz hangs in aqueous sodium hydroxide in a steel autoclave at 380-450 C and 100-200 bar, and quartz grows on the seed at 1-3 mm a day until a synthetic boule of a few kilograms is complete. The advantage over mined Brazilian rock is that the growth conditions are repeatable, so the"];
  "metal.quartz" [label="Natural vein quartz" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Vein and pegmatite quartz, of which Brazil's Minas Gerais was the world source for the resonator industry. In 1982 it had two jobs: the seed for hydrothermal growth of synthetic quartz, and, in small volume, high-stability resonators for frequency standards where natural material genuinely beat synt"];
  "metal.silica.czochralski" [label="Czochralski monocrystalline ingot" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where the polysilicon chain ends and the semiconductor business begins. A 2 kg charge is melted at 1414 C in a silica crucible over a seed crystal, and a slow pull at 0.5-2 mm/min with rotation grows a 75-150 mm boule under high-purity argon. Every defect the 6510 die carried - dislocations, oxygen "];
  "metal.silica.field-oxide" [label="Thermally grown silicon dioxide (SiO2)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The gate oxide, the field oxide and the photoresist mask oxide of a 1982 NMOS die are not bought - they are grown out of the silicon itself. A wafer goes into a 1000-1150 C steam or dry-oxygen furnace for an hour and a half and roughly a thousand angstroms of SiO2 comes off the wafer, consuming abou"];
  "metal.silica.czochralski.crucible" [label="Fused quartz growth crucible" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The crucible the melt sits in is made from the same silicon chain one step further down, from high-purity quartz fused in vacuum or wet hydrogen to knock out the alkali that would poison a p-type wafer. It is expensive, fragile, and replaced every few hundred pulls."];
  "metal.silica.polysilicon" [label="Polysilicon ingot and rod, 9N" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Shiny grey metalloid deposited as a polycrystalline boule on electrically heated silicon seed rods and then sawn into chunks. The chunks are the cathode-grade feedstock that the crystal puller charges. In 1982 the world outside Japan consumed polysilicon as an expensive specialty chemical, not as a "];
  "metal.silica.tcs" [label="Trichlorosilane (HSiCl3), 11N" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Colourless fuming liquid, the money molecule of the whole silicon industry. Made by chlorinating impure silicon with hydrogen chloride in a fluidised bed, then purified to 11 nines purely by fractional distillation, because every one of the metal impurities that matter happens to be a chloride with "];
  "metal.silica.tcs.fluid-bed-chlorination" [label="Fluid-bed chlorination of silicon at ~300 C" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Metallurgical silicon is fluidised with nitrogen and metered HCl gas at 290-320 C. Silicon converts to trichlorosilane with hydrogen off; a large fraction of the feed instead goes to silicon tetrachloride, because the oxygen in the oxide layer and the trace metals push the stoichiometry. The bed is "];
  "metal.hydrochloric-acid" [label="Hydrochloric acid (32-37%)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The hydrochloric acid that chlorinates metallurgical silicon. Industrial acid is made by burning hydrogen in chlorine, both products of the chlor-alkali electrolysis of brine, so the silicon plant's feedstock chain runs back through a 1970s chlor-alkali plant and, behind that, a salt mine or a solar"];
  "metal.hydrochloric-acid.synthesis" [label="HCl synthesis and absorption" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Chlorine and hydrogen are fed to a steel burner with a plug of graphite or a water film that quenches the flame - a free gas-phase hydrogen-chlorine reaction is not something you leave unattended. The hot HCl gas is then absorbed in a water scrubber to make the azeotrope, and the acid is pumped out "];
  "metal.silica.tcs.sicl4-recycle" [label="Silicon tetrachloride by-product and recycling" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Roughly half to two thirds of the silicon fed to a fluidised-bed chlorinator leaves as SiCl4 instead of HSiCl3, because oxygen in the oxide layer and the trace metals conspire. SiCl4 boils at 57.6 C and separates easily; it was largely dumped in the 1970s and later re-admitted as a chlorinating agen"];
  "metal.silica.mgsi" [label="Metallurgical silicon, 98-99%" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The commodity-grade output of a submerged arc furnace: a grey-black brittle lump material about the density of bone. In 1982 a Japanese or Taiwanese silicon plant bought it as if it were ore. The specification that matters is not the silicon but the boron - anything above roughly 20 ppm boron cannot"];
  "metal.silica.submerged-arc-furnace" [label="Submerged-arc furnace reduction at 1800-2000 C" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A three-phase submerged arc furnace drops quartz, coal and woodchips down between graphite electrodes into a molten carbide bath at 1800-2000 C, and carbothermic reduction strips the oxygen off silica. Chemical silicon came to dominate this furnace by the late 1970s because the copper-clad electrode"];
  "metal.silica.reducer" [label="Carbothermal reductant: coke and woodchips" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The silicon furnace burns roughly one tonne of high-ash-reduction carbon per three tonnes of silicon, and the recipe is a tight compromise: too much ash and you get slag, too much volatile and the furnace fumes. Producers blend low-ash petroleum coke or anthracite with a proportion of woodchips, bec"];
  "metal.silica.petcoke" [label="Calcined petroleum coke for silicon" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Delayed-coker green coke calcined in a rotary or shaft kiln to 2200-2800 C and quenched. The silicon grade is chosen for low ash, low vanadium and low sulphur, which is a narrow slice of the coke market - needle coke and sponge coke are the mainstream products and both carry more vanadium than the s"];
  "metal.silica.tcs.distillation" [label="Fractional distillation of HSiCl3" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tall, stainless, pressure-rated columns that separate trichlorosilane from its neighbours purely on volatility. The separation is the entire purification strategy: nothing clever happens chemically, the same material is simply re-fractionated until the boron is gone."];
  "metal.silica.polysilicon.deposition" [label="Siemens deposition on UHF-heated rods" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Trichlorosilane and hydrogen flow over silicon rods held just below their melting point by radio-frequency induction, and silicon atoms drop out as polycrystalline growth. Rod temperature is the whole ballgame: too cold and you get powder, too hot and you get liquid droplets that fall into the bed. "];
  "metal.silica.polysilicon.flaking" [label="Crushing, flaking and acid etching of the rod" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The deposited rod is sawn with a slurry-impregnated circular saw, then the pieces are fractured and leached in hot hydrochloric-nitric acid to strip near-surface contamination from the seed end. What the puller wants is small pieces with clean fracture faces, because every surface is a chance to rec"];
  "metal.silica.polysilicon.doping" [label="Doped vs undoped polysilicon" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two grades were produced side by side in the same plant. UNdoped polysilicon went to the crystal puller for Czochralski ingots. Heavily doped material - p-type with boron or phosphorus, n-type with phosphorus or arsenic - went to subcollector plugs, to doped polysilicon films in later processes, and"];
  "c64.joystick" [label="Joystick controller" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The self-centring 4-way joystick Commodore bundled with the C64 and sold with most of its software: a moulded gate plate held neutral by four springs, four miniature microswitches underneath it, a steel shaft with a ball top, a plastic knob, and a coiled cable to one of the DE-9 control ports. Five "];
  "c64.joystick.cable" [label="Coiled cable and DE-9 plug" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A coiled cable of seven or nine conductors leaving the chassis bottom, terminating in a DE-9 male plug that looks mechanically identical to the socket it plugs into but has male pins. The plug's contacts are gold-flashed brass, made the same way and by the same stamping and plating route as the sock"];
  "c64.joystick.cable.plug" [label="DE-9 male plug contacts" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Stamped brass pins, gold-flashed over nickel, moulded into a thermoplastic D-shell that keys to the socket's D-shape. Because the D-shell is polarised, the plug physically cannot be inserted backwards - the same mechanical trick as the moulded keyway on a D-sub generally."];
  "c64.joystick.cable.plug.conductor" [label="Stranded conductor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Fine-stranded copper, tinned, for flexibility. Seven or nine of them, depending on the joystick model - the C64 reads five signals plus the common and the fire button."];
  "c64.joystick.cable.plug.conductor.tinning" [label="Tinning of the conductor" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The stranded conductor is tinned so the crimp barrel grips cleanly and the joint is solderable. Tin from cassiterite, in a peripheral cable a few centimetres from the machine."];
  "c64.joystick.cable.plug.cable-assembly" [label="Cable assembly and strain relief" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The conductors are crimped into the DE-9 pins and the cable is clamped by a moulded strain relief at the shell, so bending the cable does not work the pin out of the crimp. Strain relief is the single cheapest thing that decides whether a peripheral cable survives five years of use."];
  "c64.joystick.knob" [label="ABS knob and top button" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The moulded handle on the top of the shaft. On a 2-button unit it is a plain grip knob; on a 3-button unit a fire switch is moulded into the shaft and a third button into the knob's top, sprung against the shaft and actuated by a rod inside it. Black ABS, textured on the grip faces."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs" [label="ABS (acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse thermoplastic of consumer electronics housings in 1982: injection mouldable to a hard gloss surface, tough enough for a hand-held knob, and cheap. The same material as the keycaps, and largely the same hydrocarbon feedstock."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.fire-button" [label="Fire button on shaft and knob" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="On 3-button sticks a fire switch is moulded into the shaft itself and a third button into the knob top, actuated by a rod inside the shaft. Each is a miniature microswitch of the same family as the direction switches."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.moulding" [label="Injection moulding of the knob" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The knob is a two-shot or single-shot ABS moulding, often with a moulded-in texture or a soft overmould. It has to be comfortable and it has to stay on the shaft for decades."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.moulding.tool" [label="Knob mould tool" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A hardened steel cavity tool, polished so the knob has a gloss finish. Thousands of knobs per tool life; the polish is the difference between a shiny knob and a matt one."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.filler" [label="Pigments and fillers in the ABS knob" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The knob is black because of a pigment - usually a carbon black or an iron oxide - dispersed in the ABS before moulding, and it is stiffened with glass or mineral filler. Where a lighter colour was wanted the whitener was rutile titanium dioxide from ilmenite, which is the same titanium chain as the"];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.filler.pigment-dispersion" [label="Pigment dispersion in the ABS" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The colourant is dispersed into the polymer in a compounding extruder before the moulding, not added at the press. A black joystick knob therefore contains a few per cent of carbon black and an antioxidant package."];
  "c64.joystick.knob.abs.filler.pigment-dispersion.tio2" [label="Titanium dioxide whitener" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Where a lighter colour was used, the whitener was rutile TiO2 from ilmenite - the same titanium chain as the barium titanate powder."];
  "c64.joystick.shaft" [label="Steel shaft and ball top" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A cross-section steel shaft pressed into the gate, rising through the chassis with a moulded ball or ball-and-paddle top at the user end. The ball is the most-comforted part of the design and is also the part that wears smooth or cracks in old machines."];
  "c64.joystick.shaft.steel" [label="Cold-drawn steel bar" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Mild or low-alloy steel, cold-drawn to a square section and cut to length. The gate's press fit is what locates the shaft; the steel is unplated except where it passes through the chassis, where corrosion is a genuine failure mode on a machine stored in a damp shed. The steel begins as sintered hema"];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches" [label="Four miniature microswitches" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Four sealed miniature switches under the gate, one per direction. Each is a plastic case with a plunger and a fixed leaf plus a sprung leaf, gold-plated so the contact resistance stays low through millions of actuations. The plunger is depressed by a rib on the gate as it slides."];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.plastic-case" [label="Switch case polymer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small moulded case in a filled thermoset or a glass-filled nylon, chosen so the case survives being dropped off a desk with the stick still in hand - which, for a joystick sold with a computer, was a frequent event."];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.plastic-case.solder-lugs" [label="Through-hole mounting and solder lugs" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The switches are pushed into holes in the joystick's printed board and their pins soldered from underneath. Positioning them on that board is what sets how far the gate has to travel before a direction registers - the mechanical timing of the whole joystick."];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.plastic-case.nylon" [label="Glass-filled nylon case" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A glass-filled nylon case gives a microswitch enough stiffness to keep its contact force with a thin wall, and enough toughness to survive being dropped. It is the standard material for that class of switch through the 1980s."];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.plastic-case.nylon.nylon-6-6" [label="Nylon-6-6 resin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The base polymer of the glass-filled nylon case, made from hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid. Caprolactam is the route to nylon 6, a different grade. A single C64 contains nylon in its switch housings, its joystick bushings and its microswitch cases."];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.leaves" [label="Sprung leaf contact" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Inside each switch a formed leaf is bowed against a fixed contact; pressing the plunger straightens the leaf onto it. The contacts are gold-plated over nickel because a joystick switch is a low-current, high-cycle signal contact where a stable millivolt-level resistance matters, and because the leaf"];
  "c64.joystick.microswitches.leaves.gold-plating" [label="Gold plating of the microswitch leaf" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The contact is gold-plated over nickel so its resistance stays at the millivolt level through millions of cycles. Gold leaf contacts in this class of switch were a real reliability advantage over the silver and nickel contacts used in cheaper equivalents."];
  "c64.joystick.gate" [label="4-way direction gate" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A cross-shaped sliding gate that physically interlocks so only one of the four directions can be closed at a time - the same gating trick as a keyboard's 3-key rollover prevention. The gate rides on four ribs and is held centred by four springs, so it is self-centring and has no detented 'gate 2' ha"];
  "c64.joystick.gate.friction" [label="Bushing and friction washer" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The shaft is supported by a plastic bushing or a nylon washer in the chassis, and a felt or fibre friction pad under the gate sets the feel. Friction pads compress and shed debris with age, which is the second classic cause of a stiff or drifting stick."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.friction.felt-pad" [label="Friction pad" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small felt or fibre pad under the gate that sets the lateral friction. Felt pads need almost no oil, dry-run cleanly, and shed a black dust that eventually gums the gate if the stick is never cleaned."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.friction.felt-pad.wool" [label="Wool felt" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Pressed wool felt sets the lateral friction of the gate. Unlike an oiled pad it needs no lubricant, which matters because an oiled pad in a 1982 joystick attracts dust and eventually gums."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.friction.nylon-bushing" [label="Nylon bushing in the chassis" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A moulded nylon bushing in the ABS chassis that the steel shaft runs in. Nylon is used because it is self-lubricating and will not gall the shaft; a metal bushing would seize onto the shaft as it rimes."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.friction.nylon-bushing.resin" [label="Lubricated nylon 6,6 bushing resin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A lubricated or unfilled nylon grade, chosen for low friction against steel. The base polymer is nylon 6,6 from hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid; caprolactam is the route to nylon 6, a different grade. The joystick contains nylon in this bushing, in its microswitch cases and in its gate."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.spring" [label="Centring springs" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Four small compression or torsion springs push the gate back to neutral from every direction. They are the part that wears out: once the springs weaken, the stick stays pushed, which is the most common repair on a vintage joystick and the reason 'joystick centering' appears in every retro repair gui"];
  "c64.joystick.gate.spring.replacement" [label="Why springs are the failure point" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Gate springs carry the whole centring duty and are cycled by every direction press. Once one is weakened the stick sits off-centre and the machine reads a direction the user is not pressing - the most common single joystick fault of the era."];
  "c64.joystick.gate.spring.wire" [label="Centring spring wire" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Drawn music-wire or stainless spring wire, coiled and closed. Centring springs are the cheapest consumable in the whole joystick and the one that is always replaced rather than repaired."];
  "c64.extras" [label="Finishing and bundled extras" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Three branches that hang off the machine rather than being part of it: how legends and colours were printed onto moulded plastic, how one keyboard key becomes a bit in the matrix, and the joystick that shipped in the box in some markets. None of them is needed to trace the machine's own supply line "];
  "c64.keyboard-switches" [label="Keyboard matrix switches and domes" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="How one keystroke on a C64 becomes a 1 in memory: 63 rubber domes on a rigid printed circuit board, scanned by CIA #1 as an 8 x 8 matrix. The domes are carbon-loaded rubber pills that short two plated pads when a keycap's plunger drives them closed, and the scanned result is simply a pair of row and"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.ribbon" [label="Flat ribbon cable to the mainboard" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A flat multi-conductor ribbon leaves the keyboard board, loops through the case, and terminates at the pin header on the front-left edge of the mainboard. The ribbon is crimped into an insulation-displacement connector on the keyboard end in later designs, and soldered directly to the header on the "];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.ribbon.film" [label="PET/PVC flat flexible cable" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Parallel copper conductors laminated between polyester film layers with a PVC jacket. The jacket and the film are all hydrocarbon polymers, so this cable is chemically the same family as the switch housings and the case."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.ribbon.film.pvc" [label="PVC jacket" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ribbon's outer jacket is extruded PVC, the cheapest flexible insulation available. Vinyl chloride is made by oxychlorinating ethylene with chlorine, and the chlorine comes from salt - rock salt mined or solution-mined - while the ethylene comes from a steam cracker. So the ribbon cable ends at b"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.shift-lock" [label="SHIFT LOCK and RESTORE, the two off-matrix keys" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Two of the 66 keys are not in the scanned 8x8 matrix. SHIFT LOCK is wired in parallel with LSHIFT on the same wire, so the firmware cannot tell the two apart and pressing SHIFT LOCK simply holds the shift bit down. RESTORE is on a completely different wire, straight to the 6510 NMI pin, so pressing "];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.shift-lock.latching-dome" [label="Latching dome for SHIFT LOCK" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A dome that latches: pressing it collapses it and it stays collapsed, requiring a second, firmer press (or a press on a neighbouring key) to release. Mechanically it is a deformation of the rubber dome itself rather than a separate switch, which is why no space in the matrix is spent on it. The latc"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap" [label="Keycap and plunger mechanism" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A double-shot moulded keycap on a cross-shaped plastic stem. The stem sits in a plunger barrel and carries either a coil spring back up (the common later C64 keycap) or, on the earliest VIC-20-derived keyboards, nothing at all - those rely purely on the rubber dome for their return and feel markedly"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.spring" [label="Coil return spring" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small stainless or music-wire spring inside the plunger barrel returning the key to its rest position and setting the initial (pre-dome) force. Without it the user bottoms out on the dome itself, which is why the earliest keyboards feel hard and inconsistent."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.spring.coil-winder" [label="Spring coiling machine" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A cam-controlled coiler winds a coil spring from wire, sets its free length and closes the ends, tens of thousands per hour. Every keycap plunger spring on the keyboard was made on a machine of this type."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.spring.coil-winder.steel" [label="Tool steel cam and dies" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The coiler tool is hardened tool steel, and the coiler is a Camweld or similar automatic machine. Ferrous tooling, so iron ore again."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.spring.music-wire" [label="Music wire (patented steel)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A high-carbon steel wire, patented and drawn to a fine diameter, with a very high tensile strength - which is what lets a millimetre-thick coil inside a keycap return thousands of times without taking a set."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.double-shot" [label="Double-shot moulding of the top legends" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The legends on the TOP of the keycap - letters, numbers and symbols - are moulded into the plastic, not printed on it. The cap is injection-moulded twice in one tool cycle: first in the dark brown-grey base colour, then a second shot of a contrasting lighter colour is injected into a slightly larger"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.double-shot.legend-colour" [label="Second-shot colour" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The outer shot is pigmented with an organic or inorganic colourant at high loading. The light legends on the dark brown-grey caps - and, on some sets, the orange or grey function-key caps - are all achievable with pigmented ABS; the moulding must be run at high enough pressure that the two shots fus"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.double-shot.legend-colour.pigment.iron-oxide" [label="Iron oxide pigments" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Synthetic iron oxides, made by oxidising iron scrap or precipitating from an iron solution, were the standard browns, reds and blacks of the period and are still used. They begin at iron ore and end up in the keycap, which makes the keycap a surprisingly direct consumer of the iron chain."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.double-shot.legend-colour.pigment" [label="Colourant in the second shot" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The second-shot colour is a pigment dispersed in the ABS matrix at high loading. Organic pigments give brighter colours and cost more; inorganic iron oxides give the earthier browns, reds and blacks. The moulder buys a masterbatch rather than raw pigment, so the colour arrives as a compounded materi"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.abs" [label="ABS copolymer (three monomers)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene: a styrene-acrylonitrile matrix rubberised with polybutadiene. It is tough, dimensionally stable, chemically scratch-resistant and - crucially for legends - it takes paint and plating well. Almost every C64 keycap is ABS or ABS/PC."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.abs.acrylonitrile" [label="Sohlo ammoxidation of propylene" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The acrylonitrile monomer is made by the Sohlo process: propylene, ammonia and oxygen are passed over a bismuth-antimony or bismuth-molybdenum catalyst to give acrylonitrile selectively. Propylene itself comes from steam cracking of naphtha, so the keycap of a C64 is roughly one and a half hydrocarb"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.abs.acrylonitrile.bismuth-catalyst" [label="Bismuth-antimony catalyst" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The ammoxidation catalyst is a bismuth molybdate or bismuth-antimony promoted silica. Bismuth came from bismuthinite, a comparatively rare ore worked chiefly in Bolivia and China, and it was a real bottleneck for acrylonitrile capacity in the 1980s."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.abs.butadiene" [label="Polybutadiene rubber phase" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The elastic phase that makes ABS impact-resistant, made by polymerising butadiene derived from refinery butadiene, which itself comes from steam cracking propylene. This is the hydrocarbon tree: propylene to butadiene to the rubber in the keycap's grip surface."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes" [label="Carbon-loaded rubber domes" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Each key position has a small moulded rubber pill, domed upwards, whose underside carries a conductive carbon-loaded contact that bridges two pads when the dome is crushed. Because carbon is loaded into the rubber as a filler rather than plated on, the dome is its own contact - it wears slightly wit"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.moulding" [label="Compression moulding of the dome sheet" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The domes are moulded as a continuous sheet on a heated platen press: uncured compound is placed in a multi-cavity tool, pressed, cured, and the sheet released as one piece with every key's dome attached. Domes therefore arrive as a single printed-circuit-sized rubber sheet that has to be aligned to"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.moulding.tool" [label="Steel dome tool" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A machined or spark-eroded steel tool with one hemispherical cavity per key. It has to be replaced as the dome geometry is re-specified, and its surface finish is what the dome's mating face is polished against."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.moulding.tool.spark-erosion" [label="Spark-erosion machining of the dome cavity" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A fine copper electrode is brought within micrometres of the workpiece in a dielectric bath and pulses of a few microseconds melt a microscopic crater per pulse. Spark erosion is how a one-micron hemispherical cavity gets cut in hardened tool steel without any mechanical stress on the surface."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.moulding.tool.spark-erosion.graphite-electrode" [label="Graphite tool electrode" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Graphite is the standard spark-erosion electrode: it is conductive, machinable to a fine tip, and wears slowly by re-graphitising. Machined graphite is made from petroleum coke calcined at ~3000 C."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.moulding.platen-press" [label="Heated platen press" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A hydraulic flat-bed press with heated platens holds a multi-cavity dome tool against the compound long enough to cure the elastomer. Small presses of this type did the whole dome sheet for a keyboard in one shot."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.composition" [label="Carbon-filled elastomer compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Carbon black is compounded into the elastomer at a few tens of per cent by weight. The particles form a percolation network so the dome conducts along its own bulk, and the higher the loading the lower the resistance - but too much loading makes the dome brittle. This is the same black powder that g"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.composition.carbon-black" [label="Carbon black filler (petroleum-derived)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The same furnace-black feedstock used in the resistors, compounded into the elastomer. The dome's conductivity and its wear rate are both set by the grade and loading of this powder. A high-structure furnace black gives a stronger percolation network at lower loading, so a dome can be made conductiv"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.composition.elastomer" [label="Silicone or neoprene elastomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The dome body. Silicone (polydimethylsiloxane) was favoured for its high compression set and wide temperature range; neoprene (polychloroprene) for oil and abrasion resistance. Both are thermoset elastomers, vulcanised in the mould rather than injection-moulded as thermoplastics, which is why they a"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.domes.composition.elastomer.silicone" [label="Silicone dome compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Silicone rubber is built on a silicon-oxygen backbone and is filled with reinforcing silica. It stays elastic over a wide temperature range and does not take up much oil, which is why a silicone dome does not swell and seize."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix" [label="8 x 8 keyboard matrix" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Eight column lines snake around the board and eight row lines snake around the other way, each pair of crossing traces forming one key. Pressing a key connects its column to its row; the firmware walks the matrix by writing a pattern to CIA #1's port A and reading port B. Because one row line can to"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix.pcb-glass-epoxy" [label="Glass-epoxy laminate (FR-4)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Woven glass-fibre cloth impregnated with epoxy and pressed into a sheet. The standard printed-circuit laminate since the 1960s, replacing the older phenolic paper board. The keyboard board is a trivial two-layer FR-4 part, but it goes through the same copper foil lamination and hole-drilling lines a"];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.pcb-resin" [label="Epoxy resin of the laminate" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The epoxy that binds the glass cloth is derived from bisphenol A and epichlorohydrin, both of which are petrochemical. A two-layer keyboard PCB is thus a woven silica sheet glued with a fossil-derived resin and clad in copper."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix.pcb" [label="Rigid keyboard PCB with plated pads" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A glass-epoxy laminate board, drilled and plated through in the usual way, carrying the matrix traces as copper and terminating each key position in a pair of plated pads. On the breadbin keyboard the pads are plated metal; on the earlier VIC-20-derived boards they were bare copper, and bare copper "];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix.pcb.pad-plating" [label="Plating of the key pads" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The exposed pads are plated - gold over nickel on later keyboards - so the carbon contact of the rubber dome has a low-resistance, non-oxidising surface to bridge onto. Where the plating is absent or has worn through, the bare copper oxidises and the key needs cleaning."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix.pcb.pad-plating.gold" [label="Gold flash on the key pads" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A thin gold flash over nickel on the key pads. Gold does not oxidise, so the dome keeps making contact for decades; where the flash is thin or gone, the key stops working until cleaned."];
  "c64.keyboard-switches.matrix.pcb.copper-foil" [label="Electrodeposited copper foil" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The matrix traces are copper, deposited on the laminate by an electroplating line using acid copper sulphate solution and an insoluble anode. The same copper foil process as the mainboard, at a much thinner gauge."];
  "c64.decor" [label="Decorating the case and the keycaps" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Getting colour and type onto moulded plastic in 1982 meant printing it, not moulding colour into it. Commodore pad printed the sixty-six keycap legends in two colours and printed the rainbow Commodore badge on the bezel with the same two-colour tampo press; the shiny bars of the badge used ink loade"];
  "c64.decor.prepress" [label="Prepress: artwork to film and plate" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Every legend and every logo started as artwork, composed mechanically or photographically in 1982, then copied onto black-and-white film by a process camera; the film was used to expose a plate, either a photopolymer or an electrotyped copper one. Text was set in hot metal or phototype, and the Comm"];
  "c64.decor.prepress.film" [label="Ortho photographic film (negative)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The black-and-white film that carries the image to the plate. It is a cellulose or polyester film coated with a silver-halide emulsion; exposed in a process camera or a contact frame, then developed and fixed in a chemical line."];
  "c64.decor.prepress.silver-halide" [label="Silver halide emulsion" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Silver bromide or silver iodide crystals suspended in gelatin. The silver comes from silver ore, and most photographic silver of the era was recovered as a byproduct of lead and copper refining rather than mined for itself."];
  "c64.decor.prepress.film-base" [label="Film base (cellulose triacetate or polyester)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A flexible transparent film on which the emulsion is cast. Cellulose triacetate was the standard photographic film base, made from acetic anhydride and cellulose; polyester (PET) film base became common later for its dimensional stability."];
  "c64.decor.prepress.cellulose" [label="Cellulose (for triacetate)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Dissolved from wood pulp and regenerated into fibre. The film route is a small, high-purity use of cellulose compared with paper, but it is the same feedstock: a tree."];
  "c64.decor.prepress.acetic-acid" [label="Acetic acid and acetic anhydride" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by catalytic oxidation of ethanol, or by the carbonylation of methanol. Methanol comes from synthesis gas, which comes from natural-gas reforming."];
  "c64.decor.metallised" [label="Metallised and pearlescent ink" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The shiny bars in the Commodore rainbow badge are metallised ink: pigment-grade aluminium flake suspended in a binder and printed, then optionally burnished between heated polished rollers so the flakes lie flat and mirror rather than scatter. The same effect could be had from a hot foil, pressed fr"];
  "c64.decor.metallised.binders" [label="Metallised ink binder" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A drying ink binder that holds the flakes flat and lets them reflect. Formulated so the flake lies parallel to the surface; this is what produces the flat metallic look rather than a sparkly one."];
  "c64.decor.metallised.flake" [label="Atomised aluminium flake" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Molten aluminium atomised with air into 5-15 um flakes, then washed, sieved by size and coated with an oxidation-inhibiting lacquer so they do not go grey. The same atomisation principle as the aluminium in the EMI shield, at a different scale: the flake is pigment, the foil is sheet."];
  "c64.decor.screen-printing" [label="Screen printing" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Silk screening through a mesh, used on Commodore parts where the surface is flat enough: printed panels, cartridge labels and some case markings. It cannot print the grain-textured bezel reliably, which is exactly why pad printing won on the case and on the keycaps."];
  "c64.decor.screen-print.ink" [label="Screen-printing ink (one-part epoxy)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A one-component epoxy or polyester ink with a latent hardener, screen-printed through the stencil and then heat-cured. Epoxy ink is unusually durable on plastics and is what screen-printed Commodore product panels and keycap overlays used."];
  "c64.decor.screen-print.squeegee" [label="Squeegee blade" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A rubber or neoprene blade in a wooden or aluminium holder that forces ink through the stencil and wipes the surface. The durometer of the blade is the operator's variable: hard for fine detail, soft for heavy coverage."];
  "c64.decor.screen-print.neoprene" [label="Neoprene squeegee rubber" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polychloroprene, chosen because it resists the solvents in screen ink and wears well. It is the same chloroprene rubber used for cable jackets and gaskets, again pointing back to the C4 cracker cut."];
  "c64.decor.screen-print.emulsion" [label="Screen emulsion (PVA or diazo)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The photosensitive coating that becomes the stencil. Diazo emulsions, standard in 1982, are washed out with water after exposure; PVA emulsion requires a separate exposure through the base and is more solvent-resistant. The stencil is exposed through a film positive and then washed."];
  "c64.decor.screen-print.mesh" [label="Screen mesh (nylon monofilament or stainless wire)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A woven mesh stretched and glued to an aluminium frame. Nylon is used where solvent or epoxy inks would attack a wire screen; stainless wire is used where long life under abrasive inks matters. Both are woven from a drawn filament or wire on a loom."];
  "c64.decor.pad-printing" [label="Pad printing the legends and badge" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse decorating process on this machine. A soft silicone rubber pad is pressed onto a flat metal cliche carrying the image in wet ink, picks the ink up on its raised relief, then deforms against the curved or textured plastic part and transfers the image. One pad, one colour, one station; t"];
  "c64.decor.uv-curing" [label="UV curing of ink" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A mercury or metal-halide lamp floods the printed part in under a second and the photo-initiator in the ink polymerises it in place. It was the big printing-technology change of the late 1970s and early 1980s: instant cure, no solvent, no oven, and a harder, more abrasion-resistant print."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.ink" [label="Pad-printing ink (solvent or UV)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A free-flowing, low-viscosity ink that will transfer off a soft pad and then dry on a plastic part. In 1982 most pad inks were solvent-based (glycol ether / xylene / ester solvent with an alkyd or acrylic resin and a pigment); UV-curing pad inks existed and were gaining ground, and were faster but n"];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.ink-solvent" [label="Ink solvent (glycol ether / ester blend)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The volatile carrier that gives pad ink its low viscosity. Common 1980s pad-ink solvents were propylene glycol ethers, dibutyl phthalate or xylene blends. It flashes off the pad quickly enough to give a sharp print and slowly enough that the impression can be placed."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.ink-pigment" [label="Legend and logo pigments" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The legend white is almost always titanium dioxide in an alkyd binder; the rainbow logo uses a set of saturated organic pigments - phthalocyanine blue, quinacridone magenta, azo yellow - and sometimes a metallised flake for the bright bars."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.ink-resin" [label="Ink binder resin (alkyd or acrylic)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The film-forming part of the ink: an alkyd built from drying-oil fatty acids and a polyacid (the same chemistry as the printing ink vehicle on the box), or an acrylic copolymer. It is chosen for adhesion to ABS, which is notoriously hard to print on without a surface treatment."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.cliche" [label="Cliche (printing plate)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The flat copper or nickel electrotype that carries the image, sunk into a plate table and flooded with ink. The traditional route photographs the artwork onto a sensitised copper plate, electroplates it up to about 1 mm and then grinds and polishes the surface back so that only the image stands prou"];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.electroplating" [label="Copper electrotyping the plate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The sensitised copper plate is electroplated in an acid copper bath to build a thick deposit on the image, then the surface is ground and polished back so only the image stands proud. It is an old, slow process, but it gives an image hard enough to print millions of impressions."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.pad" [label="Silicone rubber printing pad" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A soft, solvent-resistant, low-surface-energy pad, almost always silicone rubber, cut or cast with the relief to pick up and transfer the image. It is the consumable that has to be replaced every few thousand impressions, and pad hardness relative to the detail size is the operator's main variable."];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.pad-compound" [label="Two-part silicone pad compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A polydimethylsiloxane casting system cured at room temperature with a tin catalyst, or an addition-cure platinum system. Pads are cast in a mould against the plate image so that the relief matches the cliche exactly; the chemistry is the same PDMS as the conductive rubber in the keyboard switch and"];
  "c64.decor.pad-print.machine" [label="Two-colour pad printing press" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small cast frame with a plate table, an ink cup or doctor blade, a silicone pad on a shuttle head, and part fixtures on a rotating or indexing table. Each colour station is one table position with its own pad."];
  "peripheral" [label="Television, cassette drive and connectors" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A C64 shipped with no screen. It drove a television set through an RF modulator, saved to a cassette deck through the 1541 drive, and took input from a joystick. Three separate supply chains meet here: the television, the cassette drive, and the connectors that carry the signal between them."];
  "peripheral.plug" [label="The connectors: aerial socket, serial link, user port, cassette port" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Three connectors that made a C64 usable rather than just powered: the aerial socket the RF modulator hijacked, the nine-pin link to the 1541, and the 34-conductor ribbon on the back that opened the machine up. This branch is deliberately short. The mains plug and the C64's own power inlet belong to "];
  "peripheral.plug.cassette-port" [label="The six-pin cassette port" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The connector that carries both the audio to a tape recorder and the power to a 1541. It is a 6-pin connector, carrying read, write, sense, motor, 5 V and the 9-12 V motor rail. Commodore's own cable turned it into a DIN-style socket that plugged into a consumer cassette recorder, so a C64 could sav"];
  "peripheral.plug.userport" [label="The 34-conductor user port ribbon" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="On the back of the C64, a 34-conductor edge connector on a card, which brought out the 6510's eight data lines, twelve address lines, the read and write strobes, the IRQ and NMI lines, the serial and parallel ports, and the 5 V and ground. It was the machine's expansion door, and it was how a disk i"];
  "peripheral.plug.userport.connectors" [label="Card-edge and D-sub connectors" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The connectors were gold-plated or nickel-plated brass and copper contacts in a thermoplastic (usually glass-filled nylon or a phenolic) insulator, crimped or soldered to the ribbon. A D-sub has a metal shell (often tinned steel or nickel-plated brass) that both shields the connection and provides t"];
  "peripheral.plug.userport.pvc" [label="PVC jacket and polyester film from crude oil" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ribbon's jacket is PVC (from ethylene and salt-derived chlorine) and its insulation between conductors is polyester film (from the same PET chain as the cassette tape base). Both are grandchildren of crude oil, and both are the same two chemistries already traced elsewhere in this tree."];
  "peripheral.plug.serial-link" [label="Nine-pin serial link to the drive" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The 1541 was not daisy-chained through the cassette port; it plugged into the C64's second serial port through a nine-pin high-density D-subminiature cable. That separate port is how the computer knew a disk drive was present at all: there was no other way for the 1541 to announce itself except by t"];
  "peripheral.plug.rf" [label="The aerial socket, hijacked" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The 75 ohm coaxial socket on the back of the television. The C64's modulator lead was a short 75 ohm coax with an F-type plug that went into it in parallel with the set's aerial. Because the modulator was a weak source, the lead had to be a good 75 ohm match and the set's aerial input had to be isol"];
  "peripheral.cassette" [label="Datasette and cassette" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A C64 with no drive could not save. Commodore's answer was a 1530 or 1541 disk-shaped drive that took ordinary compact cassettes and wrote them with a magnetic head at 9,600 baud, plus a spool of tape that was mostly polyester film and mostly iron oxide. Saving a 1541 program to tape was a two minut"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape" [label="The cassette tape itself" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A compact cassette is a 3.81 mm wide strip of polyester film 12 microns thick, coated on one side with about one to two microns of acicular iron oxide pigment in a binder, and wound onto hubs inside a plastic shell. The C64 wrote 9,600 baud onto it, which at 4.76 cm/s works out to a flux transition "];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.slitting" [label="Slitting to 1/8 inch" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The coating line made a web a metre or more wide; it was then slit into 3.81 mm strips with rotary knives, tens of kilovolts of static keeping the thin film from clinging to the rollers. The slit tape was wound onto master reels, then onto cassette reels by a winding line that also cut it to length "];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.pad" [label="Pressure pad and felt" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A small pad of felt or synthetic non-woven on a stainless steel leaf spring, mounted in a plastic carrier inside the shell behind the head window. Its job was to press the tape gently against the head so the head could read and write reliably, and to press the tape against the shell's ribs to select"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.pad.materials" [label="Felt and the stainless spring" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The pad was wool felt or a bonded polyester fibre web about a millimetre thick, glued to a stainless steel leaf spring in a plastic carrier. Wool felt is carded wool fibre matted and pressed; the spring was a 300-series stainless, so the iron came from the blast furnace and the chromium from chromit"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.shell" [label="Cassette shell and hubs" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The compact cassette case: two thin polystyrene or ABS shells, each with a hub, so that the tape is enclosed in a rigid 100 x 64 x 12 mm box with five windows on one edge for the head, capstan and the pressure pad, two hub windows, and the write-protect notches. The hubs were glass-filled nylon or m"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.shell.hub" [label="Hub, glass-filled nylon" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The reel hub runs on the shell's spindle and drives the tape through a wind process. It had to be dimensionally stable and dimensionally tight so the tape wound at a given radius came off at the right tension. It was glass-filled nylon or ABS, with the six drive slots on the inside face for the wind"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.shell.abs" [label="Shell material: polystyrene or ABS" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A cassette shell is thin and needs to hold a dimension, so it was moulded from general-purpose polystyrene (cheap, brittle, easy to mould) or from ABS for a stiffer shell. Audio tapes were conventionally clear or smoke polystyrene or ABS; computer and C64 tapes were frequently the same shells, since"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.coating" [label="Coating line: doctor blade, orientation field, cure ovens" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A web of polyester film, a metre or more wide, unwinds and passes under a coating station where the magnetic dispersion is laid down in one or two passes by a doctor blade or a gravure roll, a few microns thick. It passes between magnets that align the acicular particles along the length of the film"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.coating.backing" [label="Backing coat: carbon black for the leader and anti-smear" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="On the back of the magnetic coating was a sub-micron layer of extra pigment and carbon black. It did two things: it gave the visible leader tape (the clear or white strip at the start of a cassette) its colour and its opacity so a deck could find it, and it suppressed print-through, where a signal o"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.dispersion" [label="The dispersion: pigment, binder, dispersant" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The pigment was not coated as a powder. It was dispersed in a binder so that the needles separate and stay separated, and then magnetically oriented on the film. A typical binder was a cellulose lacquer, an acrylic or polyurethane, dissolved in a solvent such as toluene, xylene or methyl ethyl keton"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.oxide" [label="Acicular iron oxide pigment" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The magnetic pigment is acicular gamma-iron(III) oxide, gamma-Fe2O3: needle-shaped crystals about 0.3 microns long by 0.08 microns across by 0.01 thick, an aspect ratio of about 3 to 5. It is precipitated from an iron(II) salt by oxidising with air while a surfactant is present, and the surfactant i"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.oxide.chromium" [label="Chromium dioxide (Type II pigment)" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="For high-bias Type II tape, chromium dioxide (CrO2): acicular, 20-50 nm, higher coercivity and higher signal-to-noise than ferrite. It is made by oxidising chromic chloride in the presence of a surfactant under pressure and heat, and was patented and controlled tightly by its Japanese inventors. Chr"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.oxide.surfactant" [label="Crystal-habit modifier, fatty acid or sulphonate" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Crystal habit is controlled by what is adsorbed on the growing face. The pigment industry used a fatty acid or an alkylbenzene sulphonate, or in the classic process a hydrolysed protein or a polyol, adsorbed on the iron oxide as it precipitated so that growth was fastest along one axis. Without it t"];
  "peripheral.cassette.tape.oxide.iron" [label="Iron from ore or scrap, leached to a ferrous salt" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tape pigment iron did not need to be virgin: it was often made from ferrous scrap, mill scale or ore, dissolved in hydrochloric or sulphuric acid to ferrous chloride or ferrous sulphate, then precipitated and oxidised. The ore route, when it was used, was the same hematite or magnetite that goes to "];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck" [label="The 1541 disk drive" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A disk-shaped grey box with a slot on top, containing a stepper motor, a capstan flywheel, a read/write head on a load arm, and a small circuit board with a 6502 CPU, a 6522 interface chip and a mask-programmed logic array. It reads and writes the same standard compact cassette as a music cassette d"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.link" [label="The nine-pin serial link to the C64" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The 1541 connected to the C64 by a nine-pin high-density D-subminiature cable carrying the serial bus, which is how the drive knew there was a computer and the computer knew there was a drive. This is the peripheral-plug branch's territory and is cross-referenced there rather than duplicated."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.led" [label="Activity LED" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small red LED on the front of the drive, lit while the motor was running or the head was active. It was a gallium arsenide or gallium phosphide die in a 2.5 mm epoxy lens, running at about 2-10 mA from the 5 V rail through a resistor. The LED itself was 1980s technology; the indicator lamps on ear"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.led.gallium" [label="Gallium arsenide from gallium in the Bayer liquor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A red or amber LED of the period was gallium phosphide or gallium arsenide phosphide on a gallium arsenide substrate. Gallium is not mined on its own: it is recovered as a minor by-product from the Bayer liquor when bauxite is digested in caustic soda, because gallium is amphoteric and dissolves wit"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.case" [label="The 1541 plastic case" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A grey ABS clamshell, disc-shaped, about 40 cm across and 9 cm deep, moulded in two halves with the lid carrying the Commodore logo badge and the slot for the cassette. The material is the same ABS as a television cabinet, from the same styrene-butadiene-acrylonitrile chemistry."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.psu" [label="Power supply: 5 V and 12 V from the C64" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The 1541 has no mains input. It gets 5 V and about 12 V DC from the C64 through the six-pin cassette port, and regulates them locally: the 12 V feeds the motor and the head bias, the 5 V feeds the logic, and a linear regulator or a discrete dropper makes the 5 V from a higher rail on some models. Th"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.psu.chain" [label="The regulator and its electrolytics" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The 1541 regulated the 9-12 V rail down to 5 V with a small linear regulator or a discrete zener-transmitter, and used a handful of aluminium electrolytics to ripple-filter it. Both are the same parts as the television reservoir: etched four-nines aluminium foil in a wound foil anode, with a fibre s"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.power.reservoir" [label="Aluminium electrolytic reservoir, 200 microfarad 160 V" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The reservoir is a wound aluminium foil anode with an oxide film formed on it, an aluminium cathode can, and a paper or synthetic-fibre separator soaked in electrolyte (an ammonium borate or an aluminium chloride solution in glycol). Formed at a few hundred volts, it hums and it leaks, and in a hot "];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.board" [label="The 1541 circuit board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A single FR-4 board with the CPU, the PIA, the PLA, a handful of logic gates, the head preamplifier, the stepper driver transistors, and the power regulators. It was a through-hole board in 1982, socketed for the CPU and the PIA so they could be replaced, and hand- or wave-soldered."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.head" [label="Read/write head, gap about 1 micrometre" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A miniature magnetic recording head, essentially the same object as the one in a cassette deck but smaller and more carefully shielded. A ferrite or permalloy core with a laser-cut gap of about a micrometre, a figure-eight winding of lacquered copper to cancel hum from the drive's own motor, a mumet"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.head.winding" [label="Lacquered copper wire, figure-eight" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The winding was a fine enamelled (lacquered) copper wire, typically 40 AWG, a few hundred to a couple of thousand turns, wound so that it sits astride the gap with two halves that see the drive motor's stray field in opposite directions. The hum-cancelling figure-eight was the reason a 1541 did not "];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.head.core" [label="Permalloy or ferrite core" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The gap has to be made with both faces flat and parallel to a micron or so across a couple of millimetres, which ferrite does well by lapping and permalloy does well by etching. A high-permeability nickel-iron alloy, permalloy, gives the inductance needed for the playback winding; the mu-metal shiel"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.transport" [label="Tape transport: capstan, pinch roller, worm reduction" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The cassette slid in and the tape was pressed against a capstan on the drive by a rubber pinch roller, at 4.76 cm/s. The capstan was on a flywheel turned by the stepper motor through a worm reduction, which also let the motor's back-driving put the capstan in the right phase. The same motor rotation"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.transport.worm" [label="Worm and worm-wheel reduction" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The stepper's rotation was reduced by a worm on the motor shaft running in a nylon or brass wheel on the capstan shaft. A worm gear is quiet and gives a high ratio in a small space and is self-locking, which is exactly what is wanted: when the power was off the capstan could not back-drive. The whee"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.transport.pinch-roller" [label="Pinch roller and rubber" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small roller of black nitrile or butyl rubber on a moulded plastic or nylon hub, on a bent spring steel arm. The rubber had to grip the capstan and the tape without slipping and without depositing rubber onto the tape, and a dried-out pinch roller was the single most common cause of a 1541 failing"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.stepper" [label="Hybrid stepping motor" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A hybrid stepping motor: a permanent-magnet rotor with fine teeth running between two toothed stator poles, with coils on the poles. Two phases driven in sequence step the rotor a fraction of a degree at a time. This is how the 1541 knew where the tape was, and the motor's own inertia is why a 1541 "];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.stepper.housing" [label="End bells, bushings and the mounting bracket" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The motor is held between two stamped steel end bells with bronze or sintered bronze bushings for the shafts (or plastic bushings on a cheap drive), and mounted on a steel bracket riveted or screwed into the deck. The whole assembly sat on a die-cast or folded steel chassis plate."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.stepper.housing.materials" [label="End bell steel and sintered bronze bushings" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The stamped steel end bells came off the same low-carbon sheet as the shadow mask and the speaker basket. The bushings were sintered bronze, a copper-tin alloy made by pressing bronze powder and sintering it in a reducing atmosphere so it had a connected pore structure that held oil. Copper and tin "];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.stepper.rotor" [label="Sintered ferrite permanent magnet rotor" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The rotor is a ferrite or Alnico magnet with fine teeth machined or ground onto its circumference, magnetised radially or axially. A ferrite magnet is barium or strontium ferrite, the same material as a speaker magnet; a stepper rotor is a small ring of it."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.stepper.stator" [label="Toothed stator laminations of silicon steel" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The stator is a stack of thin silicon steel laminations with fine teeth, each pole carrying a bobbin of magnet wire. The laminations are punched from the same 0.35 mm silicon steel as the television's mains transformer, but the teeth are punched to a pitch of well under a millimetre, which is the re"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.logic" [label="6502 CPU, 6522 PIA and M50174 PLA" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The 1541's electronics are almost identical in content to the C64's, which is not a coincidence: Commodore reused its own chips. A 6502 runs the operating system and the serial protocol; a 6522 versatile interface adapter talks to the tape, drives the stepper and reads the serial bus to the C64; and"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.logic.gold-wire" [label="Gold bonding wire from the anode slimes" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The 6502 was a 40-pin DIP with gold or tinned copper wires ball-bonded or wedge-bonded from the die pads to the lead frame. Gold came almost entirely from the anode slimes of copper electrolytic refining, and a modest 40-pin DIP of the period had a few milligrams of it."];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.logic.silicon" [label="The silicon this logic is made from" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6502, the 6522 and the PLA were all made on 1980-era processes: a 3-5 micron NMOS or bipolar line, or a custom bipolar array. The wafer was the same trichlorosilane-to-polysilicon-to-epitaxy chain already traced under the television's video IC, and the die was diced, wire-bonded and epoxy-moulde"];
  "peripheral.cassette.deck.logic.silicon.mould-compound" [label="Epoxy mould compound, silica-filled" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The black or off-white plastic that a chip is set in. It is an epoxy novolac resin filled to about 70% with ground fused silica, with a cure promoter and a mould release. The silica filler is what stops the encapsulant from shrinking and cracking the die during cure, and it comes from the same kind "];
  "peripheral.tv" [label="Domestic colour television set, 13 inch NTSC" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A 1982-84 US domestic set: 12.5 or 13 inch diagonal, 525-line NTSC, 60 Hz field rate, all-signal-in-a-can. It was the only display a C64 owner was likely to have, and the C64 looked terrible on it: no separate chrominance bandwidth, a modulated sound carrier that carried the computer's audio, and a "];
  "peripheral.tv.mains" [label="Mains lead, suppression filter and fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A 1982 set had a two or three core mains lead, an entrapment and bypass capacitor for radio interference, and one or two fuses. The mains filter is the reason a television did not buzz on an AM radio, and the reason the plastic of a 1980s set smelled faintly of phenolic. The fuse was a small glass o"];
  "peripheral.tv.mains.fuse" [label="Mains fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A 3-4 amp buss fuse in the mains lead holder on sets with a detachable cord, or an internal fuse on the line. The fuse element was a low-melting alloy or a silver wire in a glass cartridge."];
  "peripheral.tv.mains.fuse.material" [label="Glass cartridge and silver fuse wire" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A small glass or ceramic cartridge containing a link of silver or of a fusible alloy such as a lead-bismuth-tin eutectic, in a sand or chalk packing that quenched the arc when the link melted. The glass was soda-lime, drawn as a tube and sealed with an end cap."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns.silver" [label="Silver activator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The blue ZnS is activated by silver at a fraction of a percent, supplied as silver nitrate. Silver itself came from argentite and from the anode slimes that accumulate in electrolytic copper refining, which is where most of the world's silver of the era was actually recovered."];
  "peripheral.tv.mains.filter" [label="Mains suppression: X capacitor and common-mode choke" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A high-voltage capacitor across line and neutral (the X capacitor) plus a ferrite-beaded choke in series, to keep the set's switching noise out of the radio. In a 1982 set this was often a paper or film capacitor at 0.01 microfarad rated for mains transients; the modern purple or orange epoxy-drop X"];
  "peripheral.tv.mains.filter.metallised-film" [label="Metallised polypropylene mains capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A polypropylene or polystyrene film, vacuum-metallised with a thin aluminium layer on both faces, wound and potted in epoxy. It was the capacitor of choice across the line because a self-healing metallisation would recover from a mains transient rather than failing short. Polystyrene was also used f"];
  "peripheral.tv.mains.cord" [label="Mains cord, PVC jacket over stranded copper" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A flexible cord of 16 or 18 AWG stranded copper inside a PVC or rubber jacket, moulded on to the plug and to the set's inlet. The set's inlet on a 1982 television was not an IEC C13 appliance coupler (that became common on computers and later sets); most were a hard-wired rubber or vinyl cord straig"];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial" [label="Aerial input and antenna lead" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The 75 ohm coaxial aerial socket at the back, the twin-lead alternative for indoor aerials, and the telescopic whip on a portable. The C64 was plugged into that socket, in parallel with whatever aerial the set had. The socket was either a threaded F-type, or a cheaper bayonet or push-on type in Euro"];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.whip" [label="Telescopic whip antenna" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="On a portable set, a telescopic whip: a chrome-plated steel rod in a plastic base, with a ball joint for the angle. It was a whip antenna for VHF, and on UHF-only portables it was a loop inside the cabinet."];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.whip.plating" [label="Chrome-plate the whip rod" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A telescopic rod plated in hard chrome over nickel, in several decorative layers so it could be extended and retracted thousands of times without wearing through. The chrome came from chromite ore, the same ore that made the chromium dioxide in a Type II cassette tape."];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.coax" [label="Coaxial or twin-lead antenna lead" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="RG-59 or RG-6 coax: a copper or tinned copper braid, a polyethylene or PTFE dielectric, and a solid copper centre conductor. RG-59 is 52 ohm and was what an indoor or a bench lead of the era was. The alternative was 300 ohm flat twin-lead, which matched indoor dipoles and did not need coax. The poly"];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.coax.polyethylene" [label="Polyethylene from ethylene" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polyethylene is polymerised from ethylene, which comes from a steam cracker on naphtha. Where the chlorination route was used (for PVC or for the chloromethane that goes into the foam blowing), the chlorine came from salt electrolysis. Both the polymer and the chlorine are therefore descendants of c"];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.f-connector" [label="F-type coaxial connector" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A threaded, quarter-turn connector for 75 ohm coax, usually brass with a nickel or cadmium plate and a dielectric insert of PTFE or polyethylene. Brass is copper-zinc, so this little part pulls from both the copper and the zinc industry; the zinc can also be the zinc plated steel shell."];
  "peripheral.tv.aerial.f-connector.brass" [label="Brass from copper and zinc" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Brass is melted from cathode copper and zinc ingot, roughly 60/40 for free-cutting brass, and cast or extruded into fittings. The zinc came from sphalerite and the copper from chalcopyrite, both already in this branch."];
  "peripheral.tv.controls" [label="Front controls: pots, switches, dial" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Brightness, contrast, colour, tint, volume and the channel selector, plus a fine-tuning and a V-hold behind the cabinet. The pots are carbon track or cermet; the switches are an AC line switch and often a rotary band switch or push-buttons for the a/v functions."];
  "peripheral.tv.controls.vhf-dial" [label="VHF dial and channel drum" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The channel numbers were on a printed drum or card behind a window, or on a flat dial plate, driven by the cord from the knob. The UHF channels 14-83 needed a drum or a fan-shaped card because there were 70 of them, which is why UHF dials of the period were a distinctive piece of mechanical design."];
  "peripheral.tv.controls.switch" [label="AC line switch and interlock" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A rotary or push-pull mains switch on the front panel, often interlocked so that a set with a back cover off could not be switched on (the 'interlock' on a portable). The switch contacts were silver-plated brass, and the whole switch was a snap-action mechanism in a phenolic body."];
  "peripheral.tv.controls.switch.silver" [label="Silver-plated switch contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Mains switch and rotary tuner contacts were silver-plated brass or copper, because silver has the highest conductivity of any metal and does not oxidise. Silver plating was applied from a cyanide or an acid bath, and the silver itself came overwhelmingly from the anode slimes of copper refining."];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet" [label="Cabinet and front bezel" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The outside of the set: a moulded plastics shell wrapped around the chassis and the tube, with a wood-grain print or a real veneer. A 1982 13 inch set was typically an injection-moulded ABS or ABS/polycarbonate cabinet, textured, with the front bezel carrying the speaker grille, the control knobs an"];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet.badge" [label="Brand badge and control knobs" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The logo badge was an embossed or plated plastic medallion, and the knobs were moulded ABS or phenolic on a zinc or aluminium die-cast core with a moulded pointer. On a cheap set the badge was printed; on a good one it was a plated plastic insert."];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet.screws" [label="Case screws and brackets" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The chassis was fastened into the cabinet with self-tapping screws into plastic bosses, and the back was held with a handful of machine screws into zinc-plated or black-oxide steel. The brackets that carried the tube and the board were zinc-plated sheet steel."];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet.screws.zinc-plating" [label="Electroplate zinc onto the case screws" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Self-tapping screws into plastic bosses and a handful of machine screws into zinc-plated or black-oxide steel. Zinc plating is the cheapest corrosion protection for a small steel part and it came off the same zinc chain as everything else in this branch."];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet.wood" [label="Wood veneer or woodgrain laminate" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A console or a large set of the early 1980s was often a real cabinet: a wood frame veneered with walnut, mahogany or teak, sometimes over particle board. The cheaper route was a printed polyester or melamine laminate, several sheets laminated and printed with a photographic woodgrain. A 13 inch port"];
  "peripheral.tv.cabinet.moulding" [label="Injection mould the cabinet" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="ABS pellets were dried, melted at 230-260 degrees C, injected into a nickel-chromium plated steel mould at 1000-2000 bar, held under pressure to pack, cooled on a water circuit and ejected. The cabinet was a single moulding with the speaker grille, the bezel ledges, the screw bosses and the airflow "];
  "peripheral.tv.speaker" [label="Loudspeaker, 8 ohm, 3-4 inch" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="An 8 ohm speaker of about 3.5-4 inch diameter on a small set: a paper pulp cone, a pressed steel or plastic basket, a copper voice coil on a former, and a ferrite magnet. It was screwed to a baffle in the cabinet with the back magnet projecting into whatever space was left, which is why small speake"];
  "peripheral.tv.speaker.voice-coil" [label="Copper voice coil on a former" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Two layers of enamelled copper wire, about 40-60 turns, wound on a paper or aluminium former and centred in the magnet gap. It is a minute amount of copper, on the order of a gram, and it sits in a gap of under a millimetre."];
  "peripheral.tv.speaker.magnet" [label="Ferrite magnet" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A ring or cup magnet of barium or strontium ferrite, sintered and magnetised across the gap. Barium ferrite needed barium carbonate, which is why barite mining also fed speakers. Ferrite, not Alnico, was the standard by 1982 because it was cheap and had a higher coercivity."];
  "peripheral.tv.speaker.basket" [label="Pressed steel basket" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cone is glued to a stamped steel basket that holds the magnet and the voice coil in register. It came off the same cold-reduced low-carbon sheet as the shadow mask and the chassis, with the same iron ore behind it."];
  "peripheral.tv.speaker.cone" [label="Paper pulp cone" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The cone is a paper pulp: refined wood fibre pressed wet into a shaped blank and dried, giving a light, stiff, cheap diaphragm. It is essentially the same material as a paper bag, which is why it creases if you poke it. A small set used a paper cone; a later or better one a woven or moulded polyprop"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis" [label="The chassis: video, sync, line output, audio" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The electronics of the set. By 1982 this was a single-sided or double-sided printed circuit board carrying a mix of discrete transistors, a handful of silicon integrated circuits (the video amplifier, the chroma, the vertical, the audio), a rotary switch bank and about forty electrolytic and film ca"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.power" [label="Power supply: mains transformer and reservoir" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Most 1982 sets were linear: a hot-chassis or a cold-chassis series/parallel mains transformer stepping 117 V down to about 110 V AC, a diode bridge rectifier, and a big electrolytic reservoir at 120 V feeding the line output. The main electrolytic was the largest, hottest component on the board and "];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.power.bridge" [label="Mains rectifier bridge" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Four silicon diodes in a moulded bridge package, rated at a few amps and 100-200 V. Cheap sets rectified with two diodes and a centre-tapped transformer instead. The bridge was usually on the chassis edge with the heatsink tab or in free air."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.power.transformer" [label="Mains transformer, E-I silicon steel" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A laminated core of thin silicon steel (about 0.35 mm) stamped into E and I shapes and stacked, with copper windings on bobbins, potted in epoxy or wrapped in tape and varnished. The laminations were needed because mains hum would otherwise magnetostrict the core; the 1982 design was exactly the des"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.audio" [label="Audio: 4.5 MHz intercarrier to a speaker" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The sound carrier is taken off the same IF strip at 54.75 MHz, recovered at 4.5 MHz above the video, limited and FM-demodulated, then amplified. The 4.5 MHz intercarrier was chosen so the sound did not interfere with the video and to let the sound survive a picture cut-off (the 'intercarrier' idea)."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.audio.volume-pot" [label="Volume potentiometer, carbon track" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A rotary carbon track pot, typically 10 kohm or 100 kohm logarithmic (audio taper), spliced into the audio line. The track is a carbon film printed or sprayed on a phenolic or ceramic substrate; the wiper is a sprung brass or copper blade. This is the same technology as the volume control in a pocke"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.audio.amp" [label="Audio output amplifier" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="An integrated amplifier (a few watts, a single 8-14 pin IC) or a discrete predriver plus two complementary power transistors pushing 5-20 W peak into 8 ohms from a 115 V rail. The output stage ran in class B or class AB, and the bias and the idle current were the main design problem."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.audio.amp.power-device" [label="The output power transistor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The device that actually pushed the current into the speaker: an NPN/PNP complementary pair on a thick die in a TO-3 or plastic power package, bolted to the chassis as its own heatsink. From 1990s multisilicon dice replaced the single-crystal die, but a 1982 set had one transistor per output channel"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video.ic.die" [label="Silicon wafer, P-type" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer this die is cut from is a slice of 99.99% silicon: p-type boron-doped substrate with n-epitaxy grown on top for the bipolar transistors, then oxidised, patterned and metallised. A 4-micron process of 1982 ran on a 100 or 125 mm wafer, with 150 mm arriving in 1983. The difference between th"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.audio.traps" [label="Intercarrier sound trap and SAW filter" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The 4.5 MHz intercarrier has to be lifted out of the video baseband without disturbing the 3.58 MHz colour subcarrier, so a trap or SAW filter at 4.5 MHz ran between the video detector and the audio detector. A ceramic or SAW trap on 4.5 MHz was a very visible part in a 1982 set."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.vertical" [label="Vertical output stage, 60 Hz" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The vertical sweep at 60 Hz. The oscillator ran at twice the line rate and a count-down circuit divided by two, a design that keeps the vertical locked to the horizontal. The output stage amplified a small ramp and drove the vertical yoke, with a small electrolytic capacitor across the coil to corre"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.vertical.ic" [label="Vertical IC" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A single IC contained the oscillator, the count-down divider, the ramp generator and the driver, needing only two or three external transistors. It was one of the first large consumer uses of a mixed-analogue IC that made a television much easier to build, and it is the reason a 1982 TV was a single"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.line-out" [label="Horizontal line output stage, 15,734 Hz" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The heart of the set's power and geometry. The line oscillator runs at 15,734 Hz; the driver and the output transistor switch the flyback primary, so the collector current rises linearly during scan and then collapses in the flyback retrace. The flyback uses that collapse to develop a high-voltage p"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.line-out.hv-caps" [label="High-voltage film capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A metallised polypropylene or polystyrene film capacitor rated at kilovolts. The film is stretched polypropylene, metallised with a thin aluminium or zinc layer, wound, and the whole thing impregnated and potted. Zinc metallising was cheap and equivalent to aluminium for small values; aluminium beca"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.line-out.hvt" [label="EHT rectifier and capacitor" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="After the flyback, a single silicon rectifier at 25 kV. On larger sets a doubler or tripler with plastic film capacitors at 2-5 kV each was used. The current was tiny (1-2 mA) so the capacitors were small in value but enormous in voltage rating, and their failure mode was spectacular and occasionall"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.line-out.choke" [label="Line choke or choke-and-flyback combination" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Some sets had a separate choke, some combined the choke with the flyback into one transformer. The choke carries the DC component of the line current and presents a high impedance to the 15.7 kHz pulse. It was an iron-powder or ferrite part with a thousand volts across it and was a common failure."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.line-out.oscillator" [label="Line oscillator and AFC" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A free-running LC oscillator locked to the incoming video by a sync separator and an automatic frequency control loop. When the set was fed a C64 signal it was free-running off the internal oscillator and being pulled by the modulator's sync; it could not lock to a broadcast, so it did not drift. Mo"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video" [label="Video chain: IF, detector, video amp, colour" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 45 MHz IF is amplified, limited, then detected: the video signal rides on the picture carrier at 45.75 MHz, the colour subcarrier sits 3.58 MHz below it as a sideband, and the sound carrier sits 9 MHz above at 54.75 MHz. The synchronous detector recovers the baseband video; a PLL multiplies the "];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video.agc" [label="Automatic gain control" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The AGC samples the DC level off the video detector and feeds a reverse bias back to the first two IF stages, holding the picture bright whether the aerial is good or the C64 modulator is a milliwatt away from nothing. This was the one part of a TV that genuinely cared about the C64 signal, because "];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video.transistors" [label="Discrete IF, driver and video transistors" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Most transistors in a 1982 set were discrete: a high-gain IF transistor, the video driver, the video output transistors, the horizontal oscillator and driver, the vertical output, the audio output, and the horizontal output. Each is a tiny die, 10-40 transistors, wire-bonded to a plastic or metal ca"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video.transistors.die" [label="The transistor die and its package" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small-signal NPN transistor is a few hundred square microns of silicon, aluminium metallised, mounted on a header and sealed in epoxy or metal. The die itself is a slice of the same 99.99% wafer as the video IC, cut with a diamond-impregnated wheel and diced on a saw."];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.video.ic" [label="Silicon integrated circuit, 4-micron NMOS" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The video, chroma and audio functions in a 1982 set were often one or two chips of 40 to 80 pins, made on a 4-5 micron NMOS or bipolar process. The die is a slice of 99.99% silicon, oxidised, photolithographically patterned through eight to ten mask steps, and packaged in epoxy or ceramic. Purity, o"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.pcb" [label="FR-4 epoxy-glass printed circuit board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Glass-reinforced epoxy laminate with a copper foil laminated to each side and printed photolithographically. The glass fibre is the reason FR-4 has a low, controlled thermal expansion; the epoxy binds it. It is drilled, plated through with copper, mask-printed and silkscreened. After etching, the wh"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.pcb.plating" [label="HASL: hot-tin-dipped tin-lead solder on the pads" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The board was dipped or dragged through a molten bath of 60/40 tin-lead, which left a solder film on the pads and bridged the copper under the mask. Immersion tin plating or gold-flash were also used, but a television of 1982 was overwhelmingly hot-tin-dipped. Tin from cassiterite, lead from galena,"];
  "peripheral.tv.chassis.pcb.glass" [label="E-glass fibre from silica sand" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The reinforcement is a textile of drawn glass filament: silica sand is melted in a bushing at 1400 degrees C, drawn to a fibre a few microns across, and woven into cloth. It is the same silica as the tank window glass, run through a different furnace."];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner" [label="RF tuner, VHF and UHF" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The front end. In 1982 the US TV band was VHF channels 2-13 (55-220 MHz) and UHF 14-83 (470-890 MHz), and most sets had either a combined tuner or two side-by-side ones behind a single dial. A high-quality set used a rotary switch with a chain of interlocking contacts; a cheap one used a varactor di"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.dial-cord" [label="Dial cord and pulley gear train" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The channel knob turned a pulley that wound a braided cord onto another pulley geared to the tuner rotor. It was a real mechanical system that stretched, slipped and broke, and it is the reason every vintage-TV owner has a drawer of spare dial cords. The cord was a braided polyester or nylon sheath "];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.can" [label="Die-cast zinc or stamped steel RF can" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The tuner and front end were a sealed metal can soldered to the main board. On a cheap set it was a stamped zinc-plated steel can; on a good one a die-cast zinc or aluminium can with a machined seam. Zinc plating comes from zinc electroplating baths, so it is the same zinc industry as the galvanised"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.can.zinc" [label="Electroplate zinc onto the RF can" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The stamped steel RF can was plated in a zinc sulphate or zinc chloride bath with an aluminium or steel anode, then often given a chromate passivation. Zinc plating is cheap corrosion protection and it is the same zinc industry as the CRT phosphor and the die-cast tuner body."];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.front-end" [label="RF amplifier and mixer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A low-noise bipolar or MOSFET amplifier at the antenna frequency, then a mixer down to 45 MHz. In 1982 the RF device was often a silicon planar MOSFET (dual-gate, for the UHF) or a wideband bipolar. The whole RF section lived in a die-cast or stamped zinc-plated steel can to keep it away from the sw"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.front-end.saw" [label="SAW filter at 45 MHz" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="By the early 1980s a SAW (surface acoustic wave) filter had largely replaced the LC or ceramic filter in the IF path. Two interdigital transducers on a piezoelectric crystal launch and pick up an acoustic wave along the surface; the finger pattern is designed to pass the wanted band and reject the a"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.front-end.saw.lithium" [label="Lithium niobate wafer" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The acoustic medium. Spodumene (LiAlSi2O6) ore is beneficiated and converted to lithium carbonate by sulphuric acid roasting; the carbonate is reacted with niobium pentoxide from pyrochlore (columbite-tantalite) tantalum residue in a lithium hydroxide or carbonate flux at 1000-1200 degrees C to grow"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.front-end.saw.lithium.spodumene" [label="Spodumene pegmatite ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A lithium aluminium silicate, 6.0-7.0% Li2O, that occurs in granitic pegmatites and is mined in enormous tonnages because a lithium battery plant in 1982 needed thousands of tonnes a year. Roasting with sulphuric acid turns it into lithium sulphate, and the impurity that everyone fights is iron."];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.uhf" [label="UHF tuner with varactor diode" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="At 500-800 MHz a lumped LC circuit is hopeless, so the UHF tuner used a coaxial cavity or a stripline distributed resonator made of folded sheet metal, with a silicon varactor diode as the tuning element. Reverse-biasing the diode changed its junction capacitance from tens to a few picofarads, which"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.uhf.cavity" [label="Cavity resonator, brass or nickel-plated steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The UHF resonant circuit is a piece of metal: folded sheet or machined brass forming a half-wavelength line, with the shunt capacitance of the varactor at the open end and a tap for the transistor. This is one of the few places in a television where the RF circuit is metal rather than a PCB, because"];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.uhf.varactor" [label="Varactor diode, silicon planar junction" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A reverse-biased silicon pn junction used as a voltage-variable capacitor. At zero bias the depletion layer is thin and the capacitance is at its maximum; raising the reverse voltage widens the depletion layer and drops the capacitance. The diode is a small pill or a leaded axial part soldered into "];
  "peripheral.tv.tuner.vhf" [label="VHF gangs: RF amplifier and mixer coils" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Two tuned circuits on ferrite cores: an RF amplifier and a mixer, both switched through a gang so they track. The coils are a few turns of enamelled copper on ferrite slug cores; the switch has a rotor with printed or wound inductors that tap through the band, and an AFC or motor to pull it into loc"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt" [label="The picture tube (CRT)" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A vacuum envelope of two pieces of glass welded together: a flat-ish panel carrying the phosphor screen inside its face, and a flanged funnel tapering to a cylindrical neck that carries the electron gun. The panel-to-funnel joint is sealed with a lead-oxide frit fired in a belt kiln. Inside the tube"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.implosion" [label="Implosion protection and the safety faceplate" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A colour TV tube holds about 0.5-1.5 tonnes per square metre of glass at a quarter atmosphere, and shatters into shards that will cut. Three defences: a laminate or thicker-than-necessary faceplate whose edges are the thickest part, a mask retaining ring that holds the faceplate to the frame rather "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.vacuum.rear-al" [label="Aluminium coating on the funnel wall" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A separate aluminium mirror, vapour-deposited on the inside of the funnel behind the gun, intended to bounce stray light back towards the phosphor and raise light output. It is distinct from the phosphor back layer, which is where the black level comes from. Modern practice replaced it with a black "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.vacuum" [label="Evacuation, getter flash and exhaust tip" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The tube is evacuated on a manifold to better than 1e-6 mbar with a backing line of mechanical and diffusion pumps, tested with a spark-gap or ion gauge. Then the exhaust tip, a small tubulation drawn on the end of the funnel with a tantalum or aluminium-alloy cap welded over it, is opened with a gl"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.vacuum.barium" [label="Barium getter foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The getter is a thin foil strip of barium, titanium and palladium powder bonded with a glass frit in a nickel tube. When RF-heated it explodes in a burst of vacuum, sprays barium across the neck, and the barium burns and sticks to the glass as a mirror film. That film is the biggest vapour getter in"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback" [label="EHT / line output transformer (flyback)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The flyback does three jobs at once: it steps the 120 V B+ up to 20-25 kV for the anode, it develops the high flyback pulse that the line output transistor uses to make its collector current fall fast, and it carries auxiliary windings that supply the 12-25 V the gun heater, the tuner motor, the osc"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.anode-lead" [label="Anode terminal: the lead burned through the funnel wall" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The EHT gets into the tube through a metal button, usually a nickel or Kovar cup, sealed into the funnel wall with a glass frit and then fired. Above that terminal sits a silicone-rubber-covered anode cap that had to survive being touched with a screwdriver in a live set. A rubber anode cap failing "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.anode-lead.seal" [label="The glass-to-metal seal materials" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The anode button was a nickel or Kovar cup frit-sealed into the funnel wall, and the external cap was moulded silicone rubber. The seal frit was the same lead borosilicate as the panel seal, which is why the button had to be fired at the same 430-460 degrees C in the same continuous kiln as the tube"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.grids.kovar" [label="Kovar, the Fe-Ni-Co lead-in glass match" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 29% Ni, 17% Co, 53% Fe alloy whose expansion matches a lead borosilicate glass. It is how the anode lead and the stem get sealed into the glass funnel without cracking the tube wall, and it is the same idea as the frit seal but metal instead of glass."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.frit" [label="Lead borosilicate sealing frit" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The seal that makes a television tube possible, and a substance rather than an object: a glass with a recipe, printed onto the parts that have to become one. Lead oxide, silica, boric oxide and alkali are mixed with a water-soluble or organic vehicle into a paste and screen-printed through a 60-100 "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.frit.pbo" [label="Lead oxide (litharge), PbO" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The lead in the frit started as galena, PbS, roasted in air to a PbO litharge cake, then smelted. Nothing about this is exotic: it is the same 1900s Parkesine process that made lead-acid battery plates, refined to the point where the oxide is pure white and free of the bismuth and antimony that woul"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.frit.pbo.roast-smelt" [label="Roast, sinter and smelt the lead concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The whole lead chain in one furnace hall. Concentrate is roasted in a fluid-bed roaster to litharge, PbO, with the SO2 sent to a contact plant for sulphuric acid; the litharge is mixed with sinter, coke and limestone and charged to a blast furnace that produces crude lead at about 1250 degrees C and"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.hv-rectifier" [label="Silicon EHT rectifier diode" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A single high-voltage silicon diode, or a small stack of them, rated 3-10 kV reverse and 1-5 mA forward, potted in silicone rubber or epoxy so the 25 kV does not arc. The cathode end of the HV winding goes to the rectifier and the anode to the tube's anode terminal. A set with a failing EHT rectifie"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.hv-rectifier.silicon" [label="High-purity silicon and polysilicon" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The EHT rectifier needed silicon of 200-1000 ohm cm with very low oxygen, because the junction had to hold 25 kV without leakage or breakdown. High-purity silicon is made by reducing trichlorosilane or silane, in the trichlorosilane route, from metallurgical silicon, which itself is made in a submer"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.hv-rectifier.silicon.quartz" [label="Quartz lump for silicon reduction" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Metal-grade quartz, not glass sand. The furnace needs the silicon dioxide to be chemically clean enough that the reduction goes to 98-99% silicon in one pass. Quarried rock is crushed and purified by acid leaching to quartz, then reduced with coke in a submerged arc furnace where the arc itself is t"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.flyback.core" [label="Ferrite E-I or C core" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="An open-frame core of MnZn ferrite or powdered iron with a gapped section. The gap is deliberate: the flyback has to store a lot of field energy and be reset every line. The core is wound on a bobbin, then potted in epoxy so the 15 kHz and 25 kV windings cannot move."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke" [label="Deflection yoke and deflection coils" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A bell-shaped ferrite or powdered-iron core clamped on the tube neck carrying two orthogonal saddle windings. The horizontal winding is driven by the line output stage and sits in series with the flyback, so the scan current is the same current that steps up to 25 kV. The vertical winding is in the "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke.geometry" [label="Conicity and geometry correction magnets" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A 13 inch set with a 90 degree deflection had visible pincushion and barrel distortion. Correction was partly mechanical: a pair of small magnets on the neck that tilted the field to change the deflection centre, a static C-core for pincushion, and inside the flyback a 'geometry' winding that shapes"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke.geometry.magnets" [label="Alnico and samarium-cobalt correction magnets" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The small magnets that set the deflection centre and the geometry of a 1982 set were usually Alnico, an iron-aluminium-nickel alloy, or a small ferrite. Samarium-cobalt was the high-performance alternative but was too expensive and too new for a domestic set; it became standard in the small deflecti"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke.winding" [label="Enamelled copper winding, single harness" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Copper wire, 0.2-0.4 mm, enamel-insulated and wound onto a bobbin with the horizontal coils outside and the vertical coils inside, then immersed in epoxy or polyurethane varnish to lock the winding rigidly. The hum-cancelling trick on the read head of a cassette deck has an ancestor here: the horizo"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke.winding.bobbin" [label="Glass-filled nylon bobbin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Yoke bobbins and coil formers were glass-filled nylon (PA66-GF30) because the winding runs hot and phenolic would crack. Nylon 6,6 comes from adipic acid plus hexamethylenediamine, both from petroleum."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.yoke.core" [label="MnZn ferrite or powdered iron core" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The yoke has to carry a 15 kHz sawtooth of several amps and not heat up, so the core is a high-permeability ferrite or a bonded powdered iron (ferrite dust in a phenolic or rubber binder), pressed and sintered. Ferrite is MnO with ZnO added to raise the saturation flux; powdered iron (Ferralium-type"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun" [label="Electron gun, three guns" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Three parallel electron guns in one pinch, one per phosphor, each with a hot cathode, a control grid, a screen grid, a focus grid and an anode. In a 13 inch set of 1982 they were usually separate but adjacent, converging at the screen; the convergence magnets on the neck bent them into line and the "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.focus-coil" [label="Static focus coil on the neck" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="On the cheaper sets the three guns were focused by one small coil slipped over the neck outside the vacuum envelope, adjustable from the back of the set. Sets without static focus simply ran the focus grid at a fixed voltage and adjusted focus only for the corners with a convergence adjustment in th"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.blackening" [label="Blacken the anode with a sooty flame" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The anode block and the inside of the funnel near the neck are flame-blacked with a sooty acetylene flame so that any stray electrons are absorbed rather than bouncing around the tube. Carbon black, the same pigment as in the cabinet and in a car's tyres, does the job here."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.grids" [label="Focus and screen grids, nickel sheet" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The grid stack is a set of thin nickel foils laser-welded or spot-welded into a unit: G1 the Wehnelt and video modulator, G2 the screen grid at a few hundred volts, G3 the focus grid at 5-20% of the anode voltage, and the anode block itself in graphite or aluminium with a ceramic or mica spacer stac"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.heater" [label="Tungsten heater filament" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A hair-fine tungsten wire folded into a flat zig-zag under the cathode cup and buried in its oxides. Typical wire 0.04-0.08 mm diameter, 60-150 mm long, running at 6.3 V. It is the only hot tungsten in the gun and by far the largest source of tube failure."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.heater.wolframite" [label="Drawn tungsten wire" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Tungsten does not exist as a pure ore anywhere. Wolframite (Fe,Mn)WO4 and scheelite CaWO4 are decomposed with caustic soda or sodium carbonate to sodium tungstate, then to APT (ammonium paratungstate), then to tungsten trioxide reduced with hydrogen at 800-1000 degrees C to a fine powder, which is d"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.heater.wolframite.mine" [label="Tungsten mine and concentrator" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Wolframite is dense and refractory, so the ore is mined, crushed and gravity-concentrated or floated to a 60-70% WO3 concentrate. Because the ore is often a by-product of a tin or molybdenum mine, tungsten supply followed the fortunes of tin mining, and the price spiked hard whenever the tin market "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.cathode" [label="Oxide-coated cathode, Ba-Sr-Ca on nickel-chromium" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A cup-shaped nickel-chromium alloy base, sprayed with barium, strontium and calcium carbonates fired to oxides, with a caesium or platinum-tantalum dispenser. The oxides are a thermionic emitter: work function about 2 eV against 4.5 eV for tungsten, which is why a cathode runs at 750-850 degrees C w"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.gun.cathode.nio-cr" [label="Nickel-chromium cathode base metal" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cup that carries the emitting coating: a nickel-chromium alloy, about 82% Ni with 18% Cr, spun or deep drawn to a shallow cup. The alloy is chosen because it does not react with the alkaline-earth oxides fired onto it and does not outgas badly in a vacuum that has to hold for the life of the tub"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.mask" [label="Shadow mask and aperture grille" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Without something to stop every beam landing on every phosphor, a CRT would be a monochrome tube with three faint colour washes. The shadow mask is a curved steel foil perforated with about 25,000 slots; the electron beam is steered so each of the three guns aims through the correct hole. The altern"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.mask.grille" [label="Aperture grille (Trinitron-type)" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The alternative to a foil: two combs of vertical wires under tension, typically nickel-chrome plated steel wire, at a 0.5-0.6 mm pitch. Because the mask is vertical wires only, the beam can strike them from any horizontal position, which gives the Trinitron its famous vertically sharp picture and le"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.mask.etch" [label="Photoetch of the slots" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The flat sheet is cleaned, coated with photoresist, exposed through a master plate that defines every slot, developed, and etched. Ferric chloride is the etchant of the period for steel. After etching the sheet is washed, stress-relieved by heating, then pressed into a spherical section on a matched"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.mask.steel" [label="Cold-reduced low-carbon sheet steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Low-carbon sheet with very little residual stress, because a mask that warped would break the geometry. It comes off the hot strip mill, is cold-reduced to 0.1-0.2 mm in a reversing mill, annealed in a hydrogen or nitrogen atmosphere, and slit."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.mask.steel.bf" [label="Smelt the iron and blow it in a basic oxygen furnace" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sintered iron ore, coke and limestone are charged to a blast furnace at 1500-1600 degrees C. Hot metal comes out at about 4% carbon, is blown in a BOF to 0.05-0.1%, and is continuously cast into slabs that go to the hot strip mill."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor" [label="P22 phosphor screen, blue/green/red" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The inside face of the panel carries a printed tri-colour phosphor: blue zinc sulphide doped with silver, green zinc sulphide doped with gold, and a red phosphor around 610 nm, arranged as a dot triad maybe 0.6-0.8 mm pitch behind the shadow mask. The whole layer is only about 25-40 microns thick we"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns" [label="Phosphor-grade zinc sulphide" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Zinc sulphide is the host crystal for all three P22 phosphors. It came from sphalerite, the zinc-iron sulphide ore, roasted in air to ZnO with the sulphur captured as SO2 for sulphuric acid. The ZnO was then converted back to high-purity ZnS, either by reacting with H2S or by reducing with hydrogen "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns.europium" [label="Europium oxide" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The 1980s red was the least settled of the three. CaS:Eu and Eu-doped ZnS were both in service; CaS:Eu has the better red chromaticity but is hygroscopic and needs protection from moisture. The europium came from bastnasite, a light rare-earth carbonate, cracked with acid and solvent-extracted to 99"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns.copper" [label="Copper activator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Copper, usually added as copper oxide, deepens the green and creates the donor level that the blue's co-activator needs. It came from copper concentrate, smelted and electrorefined to 99.99% copper and converted to CuO."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns.gold" [label="Gold activator" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The green ZnS:Au needs a discrete gold level about 0.1% down. The gold came from cyanide leaching of refractory ore and from the anode slimes of copper refining, refined by the Wohlwill process to 99.99% and dissolved in aqua regia."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.zns.so2" [label="Convert sulphur dioxide to sulphuric acid" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The roaster's off-gas is cleaned, converted to SO3 on a vanadium pentoxide catalyst at about 450 degrees C and absorbed in 98% acid in a double-contact plant. It is the reason a 1982 zinc smelter had an acid plant as large as the roaster."];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.aluminium" [label="Vacuum-deposited aluminium backing layer" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The single most important trick in a CRT, and a layer rather than an operation: a 0.1-0.5 micron aluminium film, 0.1-0.5 microns thick and better than 99.99% pure, evaporated onto the inside of the printed phosphor. Without it, ambient light reflecting off the phosphor coating would lift the black l"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.print" [label="Print and fire the internal phosphor screen" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Three slurries are laid down in turn: red, then green, then blue, each through a fine nylon mesh onto the inside of the panel, each registered to the last to a few tens of microns. The mesh, not a photo-emulsion, defines the dots; a photographic stencil fixes the screen area and the black border. Ea"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.phosphor.print.mill" [label="Three-roll mill" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The phosphor slurry is refined on a three-roll (three-mill) gap mill: the rollers set a gap of a few microns and shear the agglomerates down so that the particle size distribution is tight. Screen printing needs a narrow size distribution or the dots print with a rough edge and the resolution falls "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel" [label="Faceplate panel and funnel, borosilicate glass" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The tube is made of two glass parts that must expand at the same rate or the tube shatters at the seal. The funnel is a leaded or unlead borosilicate; the panel is a flatter, alkali-free glass with a much lower thermal expansion coefficient. Their junction is a strip of lead borosilicate frit, print"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.panel-glass" [label="Panel glass, alkali-free" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The panel is not the same glass as the funnel. It is alkali-free borosilicate with an expansion coefficient about a third lower than the funnel, so that when the panel is pressed against a mould during cooling it develops exactly the curvature the shadow mask geometry needs and stays there. Alkali i"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.panel-glass.batch" [label="Alkali-free batch: silica, alumina, boron" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Where the funnel batch leaned on lead and alkali for its expansion match, the panel batch deliberately excluded sodium and potassium and got its properties from silica, alumina and boric oxide plus a small amount of barium to suppress solarisation under the phosphor firing. It is the same silica san"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.seal-kiln" [label="Continuous belt kiln" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A continuous refractory belt kiln, with a jig that aligns the printed panel rim to the funnel flange as the two travel through the firing zone where the frit melts and fuses. The seal is judged visually, by how far the heated frit bulges past its own edge. The tube is then helium leak tested on a se"];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.funnel-glass" [label="Funnel glass batch, melted at 1400 degrees C" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="Funnel glass is a borosilicate with low alkali, formulated so that its thermal expansion matches the panel. The batch is silica sand, boric acid, soda ash, limestone and a little red lead or litharge as a fining agent, melted for tens of hours in a regenerative gas-fired tank furnace and then drawn "];
  "peripheral.tv.crt.panel.funnel-glass.borax" [label="Boric acid" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Borates lower the glass's expansion coefficient and its melting point, which is exactly what a funnel that must survive a 25 kV implosion needs. Most borax for the western hemisphere came from borate ores in southern California and from Turkey."];
  "peripheral.why-rf" [label="Why a television set was mandatory" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The VIC-II chip outputs a 1 MHz-ish analogue composite signal on its own pin: luma, blanking and a colour subcarrier, but no RF. In 1982 there was no agreed analogue video standard between a computer and a monitor, no DE-15 or VGA, and no DVI. Commodore's answer was a small RF modulator, a printed b"];
  "peripheral.why-rf.modern-comparison" [label="Later video connectors: the 1702 DIN and the C128 RGBI" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Commodore's own 1702 monitor took a composite luma-and-blank input over a 5-pin DIN, and in 1985-86 Commodore adopted the DE-9 connector used by the C128's RGBI monitors, which carried separate red, green and blue analogue signals on pins 5, 6, 7 with no modulation at all. There was still no standar"];
  "peripheral.why-rf.tv-loses-75-ohm" [label="Signal-level margin between a modulator and a transmitter" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A broadcast TV station transmitted tens of kilowatts; a C64 modulator put out about a milliwatt. The set had to be tuned by hand, and reception quality was a function of the modulator's output impedance matching the aerial input. Anyone who ever moved the TV or unplugged the aerial lead knows how ma"];
  "c64.packaging" [label="Retail packaging: printed box, foam, polybag, manual" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A breadbin C64 shipped in a corrugated board box printed lithographically with Commodore's rainbow graphics, with the machine cradled in two moulded polystyrene end blocks and wrapped in a polyethylene bag, plus the user's manual and a warranty card. Boxes went to a corrugated master carton on a tim"];
  "c64.packaging.packaging-line" [label="Pack-out and case packing" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="At the end of the case-moulding and assembly plant, machines are bagged, placed in the foam blocks, dropped into the printed box with the manual and the warranty card, and the box is closed, labelled and case-packed. This was semi-automatic and was the labour-heavy step that determined where a home "];
  "c64.packaging.master-carton" [label="Shipping master carton and skid" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Retail boxes were packed into corrugated master cartons, several to a carton, and banded on a timber skid for palletised shipping from the Hong Kong or Taiwan assembly plant to a regional warehouse. Skids - not euro-pallets - were the norm for export shipping of this period."];
  "c64.packaging.carton-board" [label="Master-carton board" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Heavier double-wall corrugated, usually a recycled medium and recycled liners, with the shipping marks stencilled or flexo-printed rather than litho-printed."];
  "c64.packaging.strapping" [label="Strapping (steel or polypropylene)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Steel band with a stamped seal, or the polypropylene strapping that had largely displaced it by the 1980s because it does not rust, does not cut the carton and is lighter to ship back as waste."];
  "c64.packaging.skids" [label="Timber skid" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A wooden skid: three or more bearers with a top deck, or a bare skid with the cartons loaded straight onto it. Skids are made from rough-sawn softwood offcuts, which is why they are the cheapest way to make a heavy load forkable."];
  "c64.packaging.timber" [label="Rough-sawn softwood" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Kiln-dried or green softwood, band-sawn to bearers and deckboards. The sawmill waste that cannot be pulped into paper - knots, ends, slabs - is exactly what a skid is made of, which is a neat closure of the wood chain."];
  "c64.packaging.warranty-card" [label="Warranty and registration card" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A small printed card with the warranty terms and a serial-number registration slip. It is the same litho process as the manual on a heavier board stock, and it is the only part of the package with a legal function."];
  "c64.packaging.manual" [label="User's manual (fold-in-fold booklet)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The Commodore 64 user's manual, part 320974 in the service-manual parts list, is a cheap but well-made fold-in-fold booklet: a single sheet of coated wood-pulp paper printed in colour on both sides, folded twice with a 16-page section glued into the middle crease. The result opens flat and costs alm"];
  "c64.packaging.manual.printing" [label="Colour offset printing of the manual" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Sheet-fed offset litho in four colours (CMYK), plus spot colours for the Commodore rainbow and the red 'RESTORE' key highlights. The manual's diagrams and BASIC listings were set in hot-metal or phototypeset, then printed from plates."];
  "c64.packaging.manual.paper" [label="Coated printing paper" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Wood-pulp paper filled and coated with clay (kaolin) and a binder, then calendered, so the ink sits on the surface and the colours are bright and sharp. It is the same stock as a supermarket leaflet."];
  "c64.packaging.polybag" [label="Polyethylene sleeve over the machine" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A plain polyethylene bag over the boxed machine, to stop the moulded case and the printed manual from rubbing and to keep dust out during transit. It is a low-cost LDPE sleeve, blown as a tube on the same film-blowing line used for all commodity packaging film."];
  "c64.packaging.film-blowing" [label="Film blowing and bag making" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="LDPE pellets are blown as a thin tube through a die and a bubble, cooled in an air ring, collapsed and wound; the tube is then cut and heat-sealed into a bag. Film blowing is high-volume and very cheap - the bag is cents, not dollars."];
  "c64.packaging.foam-caps" [label="Moulded polystyrene end blocks" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Two white expanded-polystyrene blocks that cradle the ends of the machine so the case cannot be crushed in the box and cannot slide. They are moulded to the machine's outline in a steam-heated block mould, then cut down to size."];
  "c64.packaging.eps" [label="Expanded polystyrene (EPS)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Small beads of polystyrene, each already blown up with a chlorinated blowing agent, heated and expanded again in a mould so the beads fuse into a solid block shaped to the part. The polymer is the same styrene feedstock as the ABS and the SAN; only the physical form is different. In 1982 the blowing"];
  "c64.packaging.eps-moulding" [label="Block moulding in a steam-heated mould" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Beads are blown into a heated aluminium mould and the steam expands them to fill it; the mould is then cooled and the block ejected. A block mould makes a slab that is later cut on a band saw or hot wire into the end blocks. The mould is a heat exchanger - the steam lines run through it."];
  "c64.packaging.eps-expansion" [label="Bead expansion (bead expander)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Beads are fed into a steam-heated expander where they expand, are held at temperature to sinter them into a cake, and are then cooled and dried in a silo. The expander is the machine that sets the final foam density."];
  "c64.packaging.eps-beads" [label="EPS beads (expanded in a bead expander)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Styrene is suspension-polymerised in water to small beads, then a chlorinated blowing agent is dissolved into them under pressure; on heating the agent flashes and expands each bead from ~0.5-1 mm to 2-6 mm, and the beads are chilled and sieved by size."];
  "c64.packaging.chlorinated-blowing-agent" [label="Chlorinated blowing agent (CFC-11 / dichloromethane)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Trichlorofluoromethane, or methylene chloride in the European trade. Both are excellent blowing agents: low boiling point, no flammability risk in the bead, and chemically inert enough to survive the polymerisation. They are also chlorinated, which is the whole problem - they destroy stratospheric o"];
  "c64.packaging.retail-box" [label="Retail corrugated box" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A regular slotted container or a tuck-end box in single-wall corrugated board, printed on a sheet-fed or web offset press in up to four or six colours plus the rainbow gradient, then creased, cut, folded and glued. It is a commodity: the board is a mill product and the printing is a printshop produc"];
  "c64.packaging.parts-tray" [label="Moulded parts tray" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Inside the box, below or beside the machine, the power cord, the A/V lead and the paperwork were either tied together in the foam or held in a separate moulded tray. Where a tray was used it was expanded polystyrene, or on later runs a folded pulp tray."];
  "c64.packaging.box-printing" [label="Box printing (offset litho) and the rainbow graphics" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The printed sheet is run on an offset litho press - sheet-fed for short runs, web-fed for long - in a small number of process colours, with the Commodore rainbow reproduced as a multi-stop gradient from duotone overprints at varying screen densities or, on later short runs, a spray unit. A thin aque"];
  "c64.packaging.offset-litho" [label="Offset lithographic printing" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The image is on a flat plate, wetted with dampening solution so the image areas reject ink, and printed by a rubber blanket onto paper. Litho gives fine detail and consistent colour on uncoated board, at the cost of a plate-making step per colour."];
  "c64.packaging.litho-press" [label="Offset press (sheet-fed or web)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A printing press: plate cylinders, blanket cylinders, inking units, and either a sheet feed with a delivery stacker or a web unwind and rewind with a folder. For a corrugated box liner, the press is the capital equipment a converter owns."];
  "c64.packaging.litho-ink" [label="Offset printing ink" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A pigment dispersed in a drying-oil-based vehicle (linseed/soy alkyd) with resin, wax and a drier. Water-based offset inks became the norm for corrugated in the 1990s; oil-based inks were standard in 1982."];
  "c64.packaging.ink-pigment" [label="Ink pigments (organic and inorganic)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="In 1982 most packaging colourants were still inorganic: iron oxides, chrome yellow, ultramarine. The bright synthetic organic pigments (phthalocyanines, azo yellows) were used for the saturated rainbow, and the titanium dioxide white for the light areas."];
  "c64.packaging.soy-oil" [label="Soy or linseed oil (ink vehicle base)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A vegetable oil pressed from a crop. It oxidises (dries) on the printed sheet, which is what 'drying ink' means. In 1982 soybean oil was becoming the preferred ink vehicle because of the 1970s linseed-oil shortages."];
  "c64.packaging.ink-resin" [label="Alkyd ink vehicle" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Built from vegetable oil (linseed or soybean) fatty acids condensed with a polyol and a phthalic or maleic anhydride, then crosslinked by oxidative polymerisation on the press. So the ink vehicle on the box traces back to a phthalic anhydride plant, the same chemistry as the pigment in the case."];
  "c64.packaging.litho-plate" [label="Lithographic printing plate" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A thin aluminium or copper plate, grained and treated, with a photopolymer or metal-image layer carrying the separations. The classic long-run offset plate is an aluminium sheet electro-grained and anodised; short runs use photopolymer plates directly."];
  "c64.packaging.box-board" [label="Corrugated board (liner + medium)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Two kraft liners glued to a fluted medium, corrugated between two rotating fluted rolls with heat and starch glue and then glued to the liners. The flute is a beam, which is the whole reason a pizza box folds and a used sheet does not, and it is why a C64 box is B or C flute single wall rather than "];
  "c64.packaging.starch-adhesive" [label="Starch corrugating adhesive" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The glue holding the flutes and the liners is a corn- or tapioca-starch dextrin, cooked on the corrugator and applied hot. It is the standard adhesive in corrugated board and the reason the board is not a fire hazard in the way solvent glues would be."];
  "c64.packaging.corn-starch" [label="Corn (maize) starch" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Starch is wet-milled from maize: the kernels are steeped in warm water to soften the germ, ground, and the starch is separated by gravity or centrifugally and dried. Every corrugated box is glued with a plant, not a petrochemical."];
  "c64.packaging.maize" [label="Maize crop" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A cereal grain grown in enormous tonnages in the US corn belt and in Europe, China and Brazil; the harvested cobs are shelled and the kernels go to wet milling. This is the agricultural end of the adhesive chain."];
  "c64.packaging.corrugated-medium" [label="Fluted medium (recycled)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The wavy centre of the board is usually made from 100% recycled paper, because the flute does not need virgin-fibre strength. Old corrugated boxes are collected, pulped (the recycled / OCF route), and run onto a corrugating machine."];
  "c64.packaging.wastepaper" [label="Recovered paper (OCC / mixed waste)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Collected corrugated and box board, baled, repulped in a hydrapulper and screened. The deinking stage is needed for printed waste; unprinted OCC is repulped as it is. The fibre in a C64 box's fluted medium is therefore almost certainly several previous boxes."];
  "c64.packaging.deinking" [label="Repulping and deinking" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Bales are dropped into a hydrapulper, torn up into fibre, screened, and deinked with a flotation cell and surfactant to remove printing inks. A high proportion of the world's corrugated medium fibre is recovered in exactly this way."];
  "c64.packaging.kraft-liner" [label="Kraft liner (topside and underside)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Unbleached paper made from long-fibre softwood pulp, typically 120-200 gsm. Its brown colour is the kraft process itself: the pulp is brown before it reaches the paper machine, because the kraft cook keeps the lignin."];
  "c64.packaging.kraft-pulp" [label="Kraft pulp" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Wood chips are steamed, cooked in a kraft digester with sodium hydroxide and sodium sulfide, washed, and bleached only lightly. The brown colour is retained kraft lignin, which is why kraft paper is brown and recycling it is easy."];
  "c64.packaging.kraft-process" [label="Kraft grade versus bleached" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Unbleached kraft keeps its brown colour and is the cheapest strong liner; the brown is the kraft process itself, because the pulp is brown before it reaches the paper machine. A bleached or semi-bleached top liner costs more and is used where the print needs a white background. Commodore's boxes are"];
  "c64.packaging.digestion" [label="Kraft cooking (digestion)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Chips are cooked in a spherical or continuous digester with white liquor (sodium hydroxide + sodium sulfide + sodium carbonate) at 160-180 C. The alkali dissolves the lignin; the sulfide keeps the dissolved lignin in solution so the fibres do not darken."];
  "c64.packaging.wood-chips" [label="Wood chips from sawmill waste" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Roundwood is debarked and chipped into 20-30 mm chips in a disc chipper, ready for the digester. The chips are the whole reason the paper mill sits next to a forest: you cannot make paper without wood fibre."];
  "c64.packaging.forest-stand" [label="Standing softwood in a managed plantation" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Managed conifer plantations - spruce, pine or southern pine - felled on a rotation and replanted, where the fibre chain for a box begins. A box is wood, and the wood is a tree that was grown, cut and chipped."];
  "c64.packaging.log" [label="Roundwood logs" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Trunk sections felled and delimbed, usually conifer in northern Europe and North America. Softwood is preferred for kraft because its long fibres give paper strength; hardwoods go into hardwood kraft or into mechanical pulp."];
  "c64.cables" [label="Supplied cables: RF lead, A/V lead, mains cord" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Three cables shipped with or sold alongside a breadbin C64, plus the optional TV switch box. The RF cable carries the modulator's VHF output from the machine's phono socket to a television's aerial input, so it is a 75-ohm coaxial. The A/V lead carries luma, chroma, composite and audio to a monitor "];
  "c64.cables.switch-box" [label="TV RF switch box" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Commodore part 904778-01, an external box that let the C64's RF output share a television aerial socket with a real antenna, switching between them. It is an accessory rather than a cable, but it is the same moulded-plastic-and-coax family and shares the cable supply chain."];
  "c64.cables.power-cord" [label="Mains power cord" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A detachable mains lead for the external power supply: two or three PVC-insulated copper conductors, twisted together on a planetary twister so the cord resists unwinding, then jacketed in a second extrusion over the twisted bundle. Each conductor is passed through a die and coated with rigid or sem"];
  "c64.cables.power-cord.mains-plug" [label="Moulded mains plug" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A plug with a thermoplastic cord body wrapped around the cable entry to take the bending strain, and two or three brass prongs riveted or crimped in. The cord body is the part that actually fails: the plug is unbolted from the prongs and slides off the cable."];
  "c64.cables.power-cord.cord-body" [label="Cord body / strain-relief grip" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Moulded in PVC or polypropylene and over-moulded onto the jacket. It is deliberately flexible so the cable can swivel, which is also why a moulded cord body eventually tears at its root after years of being yanked out of a wall socket by the wire."];
  "c64.cables.power-cord.conductors" [label="Flexible stranded copper conductors" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Fine-strand flexible copper, made by bunching many small wires in a roving machine and twisting them. Flexible stranding is what lets a mains lead bend without breaking the strands, and class 2 or 5 stranding at 0.5-0.75 mm2 is what a 2.5-10 A cord of this period used."];
  "c64.cables.av-cable" [label="A/V cable to a 1701/1702 monitor" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A monitor lead carrying luma, chroma, composite video, audio in and out, and +5V/ground, in an 8-pin DIN connector at each end. It was often bundled with the monitor rather than sold with the computer, which is why many C64 owners improvised with a phono lead for composite only."];
  "c64.cables.av-cable.wires" [label="Cable conductors and insulation" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Stranded tinned copper conductors with PVC insulation, colour-coded to the DIN pinout, with the audio lines twisted or shielded to keep the 1 MHz clock out of the speaker. The wire gauge is not recorded in the sources available here."];
  "c64.cables.av-cable.din" [label="DIN connector and cable" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A moulded DIN plug with a metal shell and a screw-on or clip-on backshell that clamps the cable and provides strain relief. The shell is nickel-plated steel or brass, and the interior is filled with a phenolic or thermoplastic insert holding the female contacts."];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable" [label="RF cable (phono plug to TV plug)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A 75-ohm coaxial lead. Its job is to get a 1 MHz video signal, sitting on a VHF carrier, into a television's analogue aerial input without picking up half the room on the way. Impedance matching and a decent screen are what keep the picture from ghosting."];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.strain-relief" [label="Strain-relief boot" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A moulded rubber or PVC boot at each cable end where it enters the plug body, taking the bending load so the conductor does not fatigue at the termination. Cheap to add, and the difference between a lead that survives ten years and one that breaks at the root."];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.plug" [label="Moulded phono plug and TV plug" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Both ends are moulded: a phono (RCA) plug with a nickel-plated centre pin in a moulded barrel at the C64 end, and a television coaxial plug at the other. The pin is electroplated rather than bare so it survives being shoved into and out of the television a thousand times; nickel is the classic choic"];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.coax" [label="75-ohm coaxial cable" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The cable itself: a centre conductor, a dielectric, a braided screen and an outer PVC jacket, assembled in that order. The braid is what does the shielding; the jacket is only there to survive being dragged under a TV cabinet for twenty years. A consumer lead of this type was often thin-wall 75-ohm "];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.polymer-extrusion" [label="Cable extrusion line" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A vertical or horizontal line where conductor is drawn through a die, dielectric tube is extruded or foamed around it, braid is laid on from a rotating carrier, and the jacket is extruded and cooled in a trough. Cable extrusion is one of the few polymer processes that is genuinely continuous and hig"];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.braid" [label="Braided screen (tinned copper)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A flat bundle of tinned copper wires woven over the dielectric by a braiding machine, usually with a second pass to push coverage from ~70% toward 90%. Tinning reduces oxidation and makes the braid easier to terminate."];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.jacket" [label="Coaxial cable PVC jacket" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A lead-compounded PVC jacket, flexible grade with a plasticiser so the cable stays bendable. It has nothing to do with the signal; its job is abrasion and handling. The plasticiser is what makes the lead supple instead of stiff and brittle, and the phthalate ester it is made from comes off the same "];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.plasticiser" [label="Plasticiser in flexible PVC" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Cable PVC is not rigid: it is compounded with a plasticiser such as dioctyl phthalate (DOP) or trimellitates, dropped into an extruder at 5-10% and mixed before extrusion. The plasticiser is what makes the lead supple instead of stiff and brittle."];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.dielectric" [label="Dielectric (polyethylene or PTFE)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The insulating foam between conductor and braid. Polyethylene is the volume material for consumer coax, often gas-injected or foam-injected to lower the dielectric constant and hold 75 ohm with a bigger centre conductor. PTFE (Teflon) is the low-loss, low-noise alternative used where a very clean si"];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.ldpe" [label="Low-density polyethylene (the volume dielectric)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The bulk of consumer coax dielectric is LDPE made by high-pressure or low-pressure polymerisation of ethylene. LDPE is branched and low-density, which is exactly what you want here: the branching lowers the dielectric constant so the coaxial geometry lands on 75 ohm with a reasonable centre-conducto"];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.centre-conductor" [label="Centre conductor (copper, or copper-clad steel)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The signal-carrying core. Consumer coax uses solid bare copper or, to save money, copper-clad steel. At RF frequencies a copper-clad-steel core is perfectly adequate over a metre of cable, and the copper wire rod for it comes from the same electrolytic refinery as the winding wire and the contact me"];
  "c64.cables.rf-cable.copper-wire" [label="Copper wire rod and drawing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Copper cathodes are melted, cast into wire rod, then drawn through a series of dies to the finished diameter. A cable plant buys rod and draws it in-house; drawing anneals and work-hardens the copper in the same step."];
  "c64.mains" [label="C64 mains side: cord, plug, switch, fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Everything between the wall socket and the transformer primary: the three-conductor line cord and its moulded plug, the rocker switch, the line fuse, the X-rated suppression capacitor and the protective-earth bonding. The brick's case is entirely plastic, so the earth conductor never bonds to an enc"];
  "c64.mains.approvals" [label="Safety approvals and standards" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The brick is certified as a component rather than as an appliance. In the United States that is UL 478, Power Supply Units, with the file number moulded into the case - 13J5 with 'FOR USE WITH COMMODORE COMPUTER' on the 3-83 build. In Canada it is CSA, in Singapore the NLS scheme, and in West German"];
  "c64.mains.approvals.normal-tests" [label="Routine production tests" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Beyond the type tests, every unit gets a dielectric withstand test between the primary and the secondaries, an earth-continuity test, and a functional test with a load on both outputs. The dielectric withstand is the test that actually keeps people alive, because the failure it detects - a foil shor"];
  "c64.mains.approvals.normal-tests.aql" [label="Lot sampling and the critical-component control plan" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Listing does not mean every unit is tested at every test. Instead the manufacturer submits a control plan: the dielectric withstand and earth continuity are 100% tests because they are the safety-critical ones, while the routine functional and leakage-current checks are sampled at an agreed quality "];
  "c64.mains.approvals.safety-file" [label="The safety file" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Listing is not just a test. The applicant submits a design file: schematic, printed-board artwork, bill of materials, critical-components list, the transformer's winding instruction and insulation system drawing, the enclosure material specifications, and the test report. The follow-up inspection ch"];
  "c64.mains.earthing" [label="Protective earth bonding" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The brick is entirely moulded ABS with no metal chassis, so there is nothing external to bond earth to. The earth conductor runs from the plug, into the epoxy-filled case, and terminates on the aluminium or copper screen buried between the transformer's primary and secondary windings. The earthing p"];
  "c64.mains.line-capacitor" [label="X-rated line filter capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small polypropylene film capacitor connected across line and neutral to shunt radio-frequency noise that the switching action of the rectifier diodes and the regulator would otherwise inject back onto the supply. It is rated X - that is, for use between line and neutral - which in 1980s practice m"];
  "c64.mains.line-capacitor.metallised-film.winding" [label="Film winding and vacuum metallisation" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The film web is wound on a rotating vacuum-chamber drum while a wire feed head traverses it, laying down an aluminium layer 20-50 nm thick by resistive evaporation. At the same time a nucleation solution is dosed in to promote columnar crystal growth, so the metal grains follow the drum direction an"];
  "c64.mains.line-capacitor.metallised-film.winding.roll-film" [label="Cast film production" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Polypropylene is extruded through a flat die onto a chilled cast roll, then stretched and heat-set so it is strong in both directions, then slit into webs 20-60 um wide. A plain film is wound on the same type of machine as the metallised film, so the two steps are usually separate plants."];
  "c64.mains.line-capacitor.metallised-film" [label="Metallised polypropylene film capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A 6-12 um polypropylene film is metallised with vapour-deposited aluminium to form the electrodes, then wound with a polypropylene or paper separator. Because the metallisation is only 20-50 nm thick it self-heals when a dielectric breakdown occurs: the arc evaporates the local metal, the arc is ext"];
  "c64.mains.line-capacitor.metallised-film.polypropylene" [label="Polypropylene film" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by polymerising propylene from a steam cracker in a slurry or gas-phase process; the resulting isotactic polypropylene is extruded into a thin film, biaxially oriented for tear strength, and wound onto a core. It is the normal dielectric for a suppression capacitor because it does not soften or"];
  "c64.mains.fuses" [label="Primary fuse and thermal fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two serial protections on the mains side. The primary fuse is a small cartridge type rated at roughly 120 mA (115 V) or 200 mA (230 V), sized at about 125% of the running input current so the transformer draws its inrush without nuisance failure. The second, documented in the 251053-02, is a thermal"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.thermal-fuse" [label="Thermal fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A one-shot temperature fuse: a fusible alloy link, often a fine wire of a low-melting alloy such as a bismuth-lead-tin-indium eutectic, bonded to the transformer or mounted in thermal contact with it. At its trip temperature - typically 100-130 C - the link melts and opens the circuit permanently. I"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.thermal-fuse.alloy" [label="Low-melting fusible alloy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="An eutectic or near-eutectic mixture chosen so that it melts sharply at one temperature. A common mains fuse alloy is 50% tin, 50% lead melting at 180 C; a common thermal-fuse alloy is a bismuth-lead-tin-indium low-melting eutectic below 100 C. The alloy is cast or extruded into a strip or wire in a"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.thermal-fuse.alloy.bismuth" [label="Bismuth and its ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Bismuth is recovered commercially as a by-product of lead and copper refining, from anode slimes and from the residues of the Wohlwill process, and as a minor component of some tin and tungsten ores. Native bismuth and bismuthinite occur too, but refinery by-product is the industrial source."];
  "c64.mains.fuses.ceramic-wirewound" [label="Ceramic wirewound fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A slow-acting fuse for the high inrush of the reservoir capacitor: a length of resistance wire wound on a ceramic bobbin, shunted by a strip of fusible alloy that runs beside it. The strip carries the current normally; the winding heats up when the fuse is overloaded and melts the strip, so the fuse"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.glass-cartridge" [label="Glass cartridge fuse" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A fuse element of pure tin or a tin alloy, historically a lead-antimony or tin-lead alloy, held between two metal end caps inside a glass tube. The glass is there so a failure is visible: the element melts, an arc forms, the tube fills with metal vapour and the arc is quenched as the pressure collap"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.glass-cartridge.tube-glass" [label="Soda-lime tube glass" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The fuse tube is a drawn borosilicate or soda-lime glass tube, chosen because it is dimensionally stable, easy to seal and does not outgas. Glass tubing is made by melting the batch and drawing the softened gather between rollers, so the wall thickness and diameter stay within a few microns along th"];
  "c64.mains.fuses.glass-cartridge.tube-glass.batch" [label="Glass batch and silica sand" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The batch is a few hundred kilograms of silica sand, soda ash, limestone and cullet melted in a regenerative furnace at 1400-1500 C. The silica sand, dug from high-silica deposits, is the leaf of the glass chain."];
  "c64.mains.fuses.glass-cartridge.tube-glass.batch.regenerator" [label="Regenerative furnace and cowper" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A glass furnace cannot simply burn gas, because glassmelting needs far more heat than combustion can deliver at that temperature. The answer is regeneration: a Cowper regenerator beside the furnace stores the heat of the exhaust in a refractory mass, then blows the air through it on the next firing."];
  "c64.mains.fuses.glass-cartridge.tube-glass.batch.regenerator.refractory" [label="Refractory lining and checker plates" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The furnace and regenerator are lined with refractory brick, and the regenerator's internal mass is a stack of checker plates of silica or chrome-magnesite. Refractory brick is made by pressing or hand-moulding a graded mineral aggregate with a clay binder and firing it in a tunnel or car kiln; the "];
  "c64.mains.switch" [label="Rocker switch" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A mains switch in the line conductor, mounted in the case wall so the rocker is the only part exposed. It is a snap-action mechanism: a spring-loaded plunger forces a moving contact against a fixed one with a definite wiping motion as it passes through centre, which both makes the circuit and scrape"];
  "c64.mains.switch.actuator.filler" [label="Glass-reinforced switch body" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Glass fibre is added to the switch body at 20-30% by weight and the compound is compression moulded. The glass reduces creepage and raises the distortion temperature, which is what keeps the moulding from softening near a hot transformer."];
  "c64.mains.switch.actuator" [label="Rocker actuator and case" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The external rocker is moulded in a glass-filled thermoset or a self-extinguishing thermoplastic and clips over a phenolic or nylon body carrying the fixed contacts. The case must be rated for the insulation class and have the flammability rating appropriate to an appliance, and the actuator must no"];
  "c64.mains.switch.contacts" [label="Silver-alloy contacts and arc erosion" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Silver has the highest electrical and thermal conductivity of any metal and, unlike copper, its oxide is still conductive, so a silver contact keeps working even when it blackens. Contacts are riveted or welded to brass carriers so the spring force is set by the carrier rather than by the contact ma"];
  "c64.mains.switch.contacts.copper-silver" [label="Copper and silver ores" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The moving and fixed blades are brass or phosphor bronze (copper with tin), and the contact faces are silver. Copper comes from sulphide concentrate and silver from argentiferous lead ores or from cyanide leaching of low-grade residues."];
  "c64.mains.switch.contacts.copper-silver.silver-refining" [label="Silver refining from lead residues" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Argentiferous lead concentrates are roasted and smelted, and the silver reports to the lead bullion, from which it is recovered by Parkes' process: the bullion is cooled so copper crystallises out and is skimmed, then zinc is added, which scavenges the silver and gold into a zinc crust that is skimm"];
  "c64.mains.switch.contacts.copper-silver.silver-refining.cupellation" [label="Cupellation" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="An ancient refining step that survives into the 1980s for precious metals: the metal is melted with a lead oxide flux and blown with air, which oxidises the lead and the base metals to litharge that floats off, while the silver and gold stay as metal. Agricola described it in the 16th century and it"];
  "c64.psu" [label="Commodore 64 external 'brick' power supply" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A sealed black ABS box roughly 150 x 100 x 60 mm that sits under the C64 and converts wall mains into 5 V DC for the logic plus unfiltered ~9 V AC for the cassette motor, the RF modulator, the VIC-II's CAN +5 V rail, the SID's +12 V doubler and the 50/60 Hz time-of-day clock. It is a linear supply: "];
  "c64.psu.assembly" [label="Brick assembly and final test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The order of operations is visible in the potted builds: the transformer is soldered to by the diodes and fuses at its own pins, the small board is populated, the LED and connector are fitted, the assembly is dropped into the case, epoxy is poured, the halves are screwed together, and the finished u"];
  "c64.psu.assembly.approval" [label="UL 478 / IEC 65 listing" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The brick is certified as a component, not as a consumer appliance: UL 478 'Power Supply Units' in the United States, the equivalent IEC/EN 41003 and IEC 65 (BS 415 in the UK, VDE in West Germany) in Europe, plus CSA in Canada and Singapore's NLS scheme. Getting on the list required submitting the t"];
  "c64.psu.assembly.approval.isolation-creepage" [label="Isolation, creepage and clearance" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The physical rules that make the layout safe: a primary-to-secondary creepage of about 6.4 mm and clearance of 3.2 mm for a 250 V basic insulation, the earthed foil between the windings, and the bobbin and terminal block designed so that a wire cannot fall across the isolation barrier. Every one of "];
  "c64.psu.assembly.hipot" [label="Hi-pot dielectric withstand test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="1-1.5 kV AC is applied between the primary and the joined secondaries for about one second, with the leakage current monitored and the test set tripping on either a flashover or an excess leakage current. It finds a pinhole in the enamel, a missing layer of insulation paper, or a solder splash bridg"];
  "c64.psu.nameplate" [label="Rating label and moulded markings" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The safety-critical data: input volts and frequency, VA input, 5 V DC watts, 9 V AC VA, the approval marks and the warning text. On the 3-83 build the text is moulded into the case; on the 13-83 it is printed on the underside, duplicated on a top label and given a date on a metallised sticker. Every"];
  "c64.psu.nameplate.metallised-label" [label="Metallised polyester label" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A 25 um metallised polyester film printed with one or two hit spot colours and applied with an acrylic adhesive. Metallised polyester is used because it is tear-resistant, solvent-resistant and survives the polyester solvents in the potting epoxy, unlike paper."];
  "c64.psu.nameplate.metallised-label.bopp" [label="Metallised BOPP film" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Biaxially oriented polypropylene film, stretched in both directions so the molecules line up to give high tensile strength and tear resistance in any direction, then vacuum-metallised with aluminium. The printing inks are solvent-based dyes and pigment pastes that are dried and overlaminated."];
  "c64.psu.nameplate.metallised-label.bopp.orientation" [label="Biaxial orientation" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A cast polypropylene or cast polyethylene film is stretched in the machine direction, cooled, then stretched again in the transverse direction and heat-set. The two stretching steps line the polymer chains up in both directions, so the film has high tensile strength and tear resistance whatever the "];
  "c64.psu.power-led" [label="Power indicator LED" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Not present on every brick. Where fitted it is a 3 mm red LED in a small nylon bezel, fed from the 5 V rail through a 330 Ohm to 1 kOhm resistor so that the constant ~7 mA is well inside the LED's safe operating current, and the resistor - not the LED - is what limits the current when the rail is at"];
  "c64.psu.connector" [label="6-pin DIN output connector" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A moulded round DIN socket on the case face carrying the two 5 V conductors, the two 9 V AC conductors and, in the six-pin version, the second 9 V AC leg. Later builds reduced it to four pins. The C64's mainboard has the mating plug, and the two ends of the 5 V pair are deliberately larger-diameter "];
  "c64.psu.connector.nylon-shell" [label="Connector nylon shell and brass pins" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The shell is moulded from a glass-filled nylon (PA66-GF) with a chamfered keyway that defines the pin polarisation; the pins are brass, often nickel-plated for wear, and are a press-fit or riveted into the shell. The 5 V pins are larger so that the plug is keyed by power, not by numbering."];
  "c64.psu.case" [label="Black ABS enclosure" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A two-half injection-moulded case in black ABS, ~150 x 100 x 60 mm, with a tongue-and-groove seam, ribbing on the sides for stiffness, four case screws and one larger screw holding the cable clamp. On the potted builds the seam is a line of epoxy. Some units have the rating text moulded into the pla"];
  "c64.psu.case.screws" [label="Case screws" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cross-recessed pan-head self-tapping screws into moulded bosses in the plastic, and in a mains appliance they must be short enough that they cannot reach the primary wiring - a specific check in the safety audit."];
  "c64.psu.potting" [label="Epoxy potting pour" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="From mid-1983 Commodore filled the case with a two-part epoxy so the whole assembly became one solid block: transformer, board, fuses, leads, the lot, in a minty-green or cool-grey mass. It made the unit tamper-proof and un-serviceable, and it turned out to be the primary cause of failure because th"];
  "c64.psu.board" [label="Small regulator PCB, 'PNE-5'" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A single-sided glass-epoxy board about 40 x 30 mm carrying the regulator, the two electrolytics, the tantalum and two resistors, with the identification 'PNE-5' etched into the copper on the earlier build and no marking at all on the later one. It is held in place only by the epoxy that floods the w"];
  "c64.psu.aux-caps" [label="Tantalum and small electrolytic capacitors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Secondary filtering: a 0.22 uF 35 V tantalum and a 47 uF 10 V electrolytic on the 5 V output for stability and for the load transients the VIC-II produces when it banks memory. The tantalum is polar and fails short, so it is the more dramatic failure of the pair."];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap" [label="4700 uF 16 V aluminium electrolytic" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The single reservoir capacitor on the rectifier output. It is the part that has killed the most C64s: as the electrolyte dries out the ripple current is no longer smoothed, the series regulator loses headroom and starts pumping 100/120 Hz ripple onto the 5 V rail, and eventually the cap leaks curren"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.winding" [label="Winding and impregnating the cell" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The anode foil (etched) and the cathode (bare aluminium foil, or the can itself in later types) are separated by the separator and wound on a mandrel into a compact roll, the leads are brought out, and the roll is impregnated under vacuum at 1-10 mbar with the electrolyte so the pores fill completel"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.separator" [label="Cell separator" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A porous dielectric sheet between the anode and the cathode foil, keeping them apart without shorting. Low-voltage parts in 1980-82 used cellulose (paper) separators, which is the direct cause of the decades-later failure: paper is hygroscopic and absorbs electrolyte, and the carbonisation of that p"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.separator.cellulose" [label="Cellulose paper separator" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin kraft paper sheet made on a Fourdrinier or twin-wire machine from long-fibre softwood pulp cooked at 160-180 C in sodium hydroxide; the lignin is washed out into a black liquor that is burned for process heat, roughly 1.2-1.5 t per tonne of pulp, and the pulp is bleached with chlorine or hypo"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.separator.polypropylene" [label="Polypropylene separator" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A microporous isotactic polypropylene film, 12-25 um thick and about 35-45% porous, made by extruding a film and stretching it or by solvent extraction so that the electrolyte fills the voids while the sheet still keeps the electrodes apart. It replaced paper because it absorbs no electrolyte and, w"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.electrolyte" [label="Electrolytic electrolyte" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="An aqueous solution of an ammonium or amine salt - ammonium pentaborate was the classic low-voltage recipe - that forms the counter-electrode ion layer against the oxide. In an aluminium electrolytic the cathode reaction deposits a hydrated aluminium oxide or tin layer from the metal ion, and the le"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.alu-foil" [label="Aluminium anode foil" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The anode is a 12-20 um aluminium foil that has been chemically etched in a hot chlorate or permanganate bath to roughen the surface into a high-area sponge. Because capacitance is proportional to real surface area, the etch multiplies the effective area by 30-100x and is the entire reason a 4700 uF"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.alu-foil.formation" [label="Electrolytic formation of the anode oxide" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The wound wet cell is energised from a DC source at a voltage ramped up to the rated working voltage. At the anode the aluminium oxidises to a dense amorphous alumina film only a few nanometres thick - the dielectric. Raising the voltage thickens the film; raising the temperature thickens it faster "];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.alu-foil.etching" [label="Chemical etching of the anode foil" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The foil is threaded continuously through an etch tank at 70-80 C. Sodium chlorate with hydrochloric acid, or potassium permanganate, dissolves aluminium faster where the surface is already rough, so the etch runs away into a deep microscopic pore structure rather than eating away uniformly. The etc"];
  "c64.psu.bulk-cap.alu-foil.etching.permanganate" [label="Potassium permanganate etchant" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="An alkaline potassium permanganate bath oxidises the aluminium surface; the loose MnO2 sludge is removed by brushing or a nitric acid dip. The alternative chlorate chemistry was being phased out for safety and environmental reasons through the 1980s because chlorate/dioxide is toxic."];
  "c64.psu.heatsink" [label="Folded aluminium heatsink" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A thin aluminium plate folded into a U so that one leg clamps under the case and the other presents an area against the regulator. There are no fins: the design relies on the natural convection of a brick sitting under a desk. It is the reason the case runs at 60-70 C and the epoxy never fully cools"];
  "c64.psu.heatsink.forming" [label="Sheet folding and tapping" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Strip or sheet is blanked, then folded on a press brake to a U; the mounting hole is punched or drilled and tapped with a thread-forming tap. Aluminium's low modulus means the fold must be given a generous inside radius or the sheet tears, and the tapped hole must be at least 1.5 diameters from the "];
  "c64.psu.regulator" [label="5 V linear voltage regulator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The pass element that turns roughly 8-10 V of pulsating DC into 5.00 V. Three different implementations appear in C64 bricks inside two years, and the choice of which one determines how much heat has to be removed. At full load the drop across the pass device is 3-5 V at 1.5 A, so 4.5-7.5 W has to g"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.mounting" [label="Bolting the regulator to the heatsink" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The regulator tab or TO-3 can is bolted to the folded aluminium plate with a thin layer of thermal compound - in 1982 typically a zinc-oxide paste in a silicone or hydrocarbon base, or sometimes simply a piece of thin copper foil. The thermal interface matters more than the compound brand: dry metal"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete" [label="Discrete 2N3055 / 2N3792 series-pass regulator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The earliest 'repairable' silver-label brick used a discrete design: an NPN power transistor in a TO-3 can bolted to a heatsink as the series pass, a smaller driver transistor, a zener or low-current regulator as the reference, wirewound resistors in the emitter and base for current limiting, and a "];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.thermal-shutdown" [label="Thermal shutdown" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A temperature-sensitive element in contact with the heatsink - a thermistor or a bimetallic thermal switch - that shorts or removes the driver bias when the sink passes roughly 90-110 C, so the supply folds back rather than destroying itself. Commodore's bricks more often relied on a thermal fuse in"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.thermal-shutdown.thermistor" [label="NTC thermistor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A bead or disc of semiconducting metal oxide whose resistance falls steeply with temperature (10 kOhm at 25 C, ~1 kOhm at 100 C for the standard 10K curve). Glued or bolted to the heatsink and shunting the driver bias, it gives a soft, non-resetting protection that must cool before it re-conducts."];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.emitter-ballast" [label="Emitter ballast resistors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="One to three wirewound resistors in series with the pass transistor's emitter. Their purpose is to develop a signal proportional to output current for current limiting and to force a standing current through the pass transistor so its hFE and therefore the loop gain do not collapse at light load. Wi"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.emitter-ballast.manganin-wire" [label="Manganin wire" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A copper-manganese-nickel alloy (typically 86% Cu, 12% Mn, 2% Ni) whose resistivity barely changes with temperature. A spiral-wound wirewound resistor is made by winding manganin or constantan wire onto a ceramic or plastic former and baking it under high tension so the winding stress-relaxes and st"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.reference" [label="Voltage reference" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="In a 1982 discrete supply the reference is either a 5.1-6.2 V zener diode biased from a constant-current source, or a low-current three-terminal regulator such as a TL431 or a small 7805 in a TO-92 used purely as a reference. The zener route needs a stable bias current; the temperature coefficient o"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.driver" [label="Driver / error-amplifier transistor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small-signal NPN whose job is to supply the ~0.7 V of base drive plus the feedback signal the pass transistor needs. In a 1982 discrete supply this is typically a TO-92 part with hFE around 100-400, or one half of a matched pair. Its collector dissipation is small but its junction temperature is c"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.pass-npn" [label="2N3055 / 2N3792 power NPN" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A triple-diffused planar NPN power transistor in a TO-3 can: a bolted metal header with an isolation disc, the die brazed or soldered to the header, one or two emitter bonds, and a hermetic can with a glass-sealed pin feedthrough. The 2N3055 is grossly oversized for a 1.5 A load (15 A, 60 V, 115 W d"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.pass-npn.wire-bond" [label="Emitter wire bonds" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Two to four 100-200 um aluminium wires are wedge-bonded from the emitter metallisation to the emitter lead to carry the full emitter current; the collector current flows through the die's back contact straight into the header. Ultrasonic wedge bonding at 50-250 g per wire was the universal method - "];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.pass-npn.die-attach" [label="Die attach and hermetic TO-3 sealing" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The die is brazed to the header in a hydrogen atmosphere at 700-800 C with an active-metal braze, or soldered with a 50Pb-50Sn or AuSn preform at 220-300 C; the header is then nickel-plated. The can is crimped or cold-welded onto the header with an annular glass or ceramic feedthrough insulator; mic"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-discrete.pass-npn.triple-diffusion" [label="Triple-diffused planar process" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Three sequential dopings - collector-base, base-emitter, and a deep collector-emitter - through a photolithographically masked oxide. The heavy collector-emitter diffusion carries the load current while the shallow, tightly controlled base-emitter diffusion sets the gain and the breakdown voltage. T"];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-si3052p" [label="Sanken SI-3052P integrated regulator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The regulator in the later 902503-02, which many hobbyists misidentify as a transistor because it is moulded in a TO-3P-looking package. It is a 5 V / 2 A linear regulator with an internal error amplifier and a foldback current limit that kicks in at about 2.4 A. The datasheet explicitly warns that "];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-si3052p.stability-cap" [label="Output stability capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small electrolytic or tantalum directly across the regulator's input and output pins. The datasheet requires it for loop stability; a 2.2 uF 16 V tantalum appears on some 902503-02 builds and the 3-83 version has the holes for it but does not populate it, which suggests it was a late cost change."];
  "c64.psu.regulator.variant-7805" [label="7805-class three-pin regulator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The simplest form: a monolithic regulator containing the reference, error amplifier, current limit and the pass transistor, in a TO-220 or TO-3P-like plastic body with a tab bolted to the heatsink. In the 251053-02 brick it is a three-legged part with rectangular legs, screwed straight into the fold"];
  "c64.psu.rectifier" [label="Rectifier diodes, 400GI / 1N5400" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two or four stud-less axial rectifier diodes convert the 18 V centre-tapped secondary into pulsating DC. The 902503-02 uses two General Electric 400GI silicon rectifiers in a full-wave centre-tapped configuration, which saves the two extra dice of a bridge; other builds use four 1N5400s or a moulded"];
  "c64.psu.rectifier.diode" [label="Axial rectifier diode" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A single silicon junction in an axial package with a formed lead, cathode band marked with a stripe. Inside: a die a few hundred microns across with a heavy anode metallisation, a glass passivation, and epoxy or a voidless glass body filled with silicone stress-relief gel to survive the power cyclin"];
  "c64.psu.rectifier.bridge-alternative" [label="Moulded bridge rectifier alternative" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Some builds and most replacement supplies use a packaged bridge: four dice mounted on a common ceramic or moulded body with the terminals formed into the four corners. Commodore's own choice of two discrete diodes over a bridge saved two dice and one package, at the cost of a centre tap on the trans"];
  "c64.psu.rectifier.diode.die" [label="Silicon rectifier die" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 100-150 mm wafer ground to a die typically 2 x 2 x 0.3 mm, with a diffused N-type cathode and metallised anode. The same Czochralski silicon as the VIC-II and the 6510; the detailed wafer process belongs to the silicon branch of this tree."];
  "c64.psu.rectifier.diode.packaging" [label="Die attach, wire bond and axial encapsulation" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The die is die-attached to the anode lead with silver-loaded glass or a soft solder, one or two aluminium wires are wedge-bonded from the cathode metallisation to the cathode lead, then the whole assembly is transfer-moulded or cast in an epoxy body filled with silicone gel to relieve the thermal cy"];
  "c64.psu.transformer" [label="Mains transformer, 40-46 VA" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The whole of the supply's energy conversion. One primary on 117 V mains, a centre-tapped ~18 V secondary feeding the 5 V regulator, and a separate ~9 V winding feeding straight out to the C64. The two secondaries are galvanically isolated from each other as well as from the primary - that isolation "];
  "c64.psu.transformer.pinboard" [label="Transformer pin terminal board" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A row of brass pins on a small phenolic or nylon board carrying the primary, the interwinding earth, and all the secondary taps. The rectifier diodes and fuses in the 1983-84 bricks are soldered straight onto these pins, which is why the schematic 'terminals' that appear in a teardown are the transf"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.pinboard.phenolic" [label="Phenolic laminate board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Paper or cotton-linen web impregnated with phenol-formaldehyde resin under heat and pressure. Cheap, dimensionally stable and self-extinguishing, which is why it was the default material for transformer terminal boards and fuseholders."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding" [label="Enamelled copper winding" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The primary, the 18 V centre-tapped secondary and the 9 V output secondary are wound in layers on a moulded bobbin, with a few hundred turns of 0.25-0.4 mm enamelled copper wire. Layer insulation between each pass and interleaved padding between the primary and the secondaries carry the 1000-1500 V "];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.impregnation" [label="Polyester varnish impregnation and oven cure" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The finished assembly is vacuum impregnated with a thermosetting polyester or epoxy varnish so the winding becomes a solid block and cannot move at 50 Hz. Viscous impregnating varnishes were standard by the early 1980s; the alternative is dip-and-bake in an epoxy or a polyurethane."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.impregnation.varnish" [label="Impregnating varnish" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A low-viscosity, high-solids unsaturated polyester resin dissolved in styrene, with an organic peroxide cure catalyst. It cures by crosslinking rather than by solvent loss, so it shrinks very little and fills completely."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.process" [label="Layer winding on a coil winder" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A single-spindle or two-spindle coil winder turns the bobbin while traversing the wire, and the operator lays interleaved insulation between each layer with a slow-moving 'paper feeder'. Bobbin layers are counter-wound to flatten the coil, and the leads are brought out through the bobbin flange and "];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.process.lead-termination" [label="Lead termination and crimped flag terminals" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The winding leads are soldered or, in the 1983-84 bricks, soldered directly to the transformer's own pins; the line cord and interwinding conductors terminate in tin-plated brass crimp flags. Under the epoxy pour the crimps are the weak link - they can anneal and let go - and the PVC of the line cor"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.screen" [label="Interwinding earth screen" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The protective-earth conductor is bonded to an aluminium or copper foil laid between the primary and the secondary windings - or sometimes to the transformer frame bolt. It does not leave the transformer: the earthing path is closed inside the potted block and never reaches the plastic case or the c"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation" [label="Layer and interwinding insulation" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Crepe paper or glass-cloth tape between winding layers, a wide kraft or composite tape wrap between the primary and the secondaries, and in the potted builds strips of polyimide (Kapton) film around the whole winding. The 1983-84 bricks visibly used masking tape on the core yoke edges and something "];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.kapton" [label="Polyimide (Kapton) film" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A 12-25 um amber polyimide film used as the barrier between the coil and the epoxy pour and, in later builds, as the layer insulation. Its value is that it does not soften, dissolve or outgas in a hot epoxy cure."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.kapton.pyromellitic-dianhydride" [label="Pyromellitic dianhydride monomer" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The diacid anhydride half of Kapton is made by air oxidation of pseudocumene to trimellitic acid and then dehydrating it - a petroleum-aromatics chain. The diamine half is 4,4'-oxydianiline or p-phenylenediamine, both aromatics intermediates."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.glass-cloth" [label="Glass-cloth insulation tape" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Woven E-glass yarn cloth, ~0.1 mm thick, silane-sized for adhesion, wound in the last layer before the outer tape. Glass has no moisture absorption and much higher dielectric strength than paper, which is why the safety-approved design puts it nearest the primary."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.crepe-paper" [label="Crepe paper insulation" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A lightly crinkled, unbleached kraft paper that stretches to follow the winding contour without bunching. Made on a Fourdrinier machine from a dilute suspension of long-fibre softwood pulp."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.crepe-paper.pulp" [label="Mechanical wood pulp" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Softwood (spruce/fir) is debarked, chipped, steamed and refined in a disc or refiner mill; the long fibres are ideal for electrical insulation paper because they give high dielectric strength and mechanical strength in the thin sheet."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.insulation.crepe-paper.pulp.softwood" [label="Softwood pulp" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The species matters as much as the process: long-fibre softwood pulp from spruce, fir or pine gives the dielectric strength and tear resistance that electrical insulation paper needs, while hardwood pulp from birch or aspen gives opacity and printability and is used in the thinner papers. Softwood i"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.bobbin" [label="Moulded nylon bobbin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A spool-shaped coil former in glass-filled or unfilled nylon 6 or nylon 6,6, with cheek flanges that locate it on the core leg and a continuous slot that keeps the layer insulation in place. Bobbins were (and still are) a Miles-Platts product line, which fits the 'MP' mark found on this transformer."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.bobbin.moulding" [label="Injection moulding the bobbin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Nylon is hygroscopic so the pellets are dried at 80 C for 4-8 h before moulding, then injection moulded at 240-280 C into a multi-cavity tool with hot runners and a mould temperature of 60-90 C. The mould cavity is polished because a flash of loose fibre on the winding slot would be a turn-coat defe"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.wire" [label="Enamelled magnet wire" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Bare copper rod drawn down to 0.25-0.4 mm through a series of tungsten-carbide or diamond dies, then coated in two or more passes of a thermosetting enamel - typically polyester-imide (Grade 2 / THEIC) - and baked to cure it. Each coat adds roughly 5-10 um, so a double-grade wire has 25-40% more cop"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.wire.enamel" [label="Polyester-imide enamel coating" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="The wire runs continuously through a die-casting oven where a 25-30% solids solution of the modified polyester-imide resin in cresol/xylene is applied, then through a 350-450 C curing oven, and through the same pair again for a second coat. The thermal cure crosslinks the resin to a hard film that r"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.wire.enamel.resin" [label="Polyester-imide resin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse magnet-wire enamel of the 1980s. The base is a polycondensation of dimethyl terephthalate (or trimellitic anhydride) with polyols; the imide is introduced with a polyamide of trimellitic anhydride and a diamine such as ethylenediamine, which is what raises the class to H (180 C)."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.winding.wire.drawing" [label="Wire drawing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 8 mm copper cathode rod or coil is pickled in sulphuric acid, washed, annealed in a continuous bright-annealing furnace at 500-700 C, then pulled through progressively finer dies with a phosphated soap lubricant. Ten or more die passes take 8 mm to 0.3 mm, and the die temperature is water-cooled t"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core" [label="E-I laminated silicon-steel core" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A stack of punched E and I laminations with interleaved joints, taped along the yoke edges with a single wrap of masking or Kapton tape. For a 40 VA unit at 60 Hz the stack is maybe 25-35 mm tall in 0.35-0.5 mm plate, and it is clamped hard enough that the laminations must be isolated from each othe"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.ferrite-alternative" [label="MnZn ferrite E-core alternative" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Later Commodore bricks and the C64C/Plus4 supplies switched to a pressed manganese-zinc ferrite core. Ferrite has much higher resistivity than silicon steel so it can be used as a solid moulded shape with no lamination joints, giving lower core loss at 60 Hz and a much lighter assembly - at several "];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.stack" [label="Core stack assembly and clamping" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="E and I plates are stacked in alternating layers with the joints staggered so no section of the stack has an unbroken air gap, then clamped end to end by angle plates and bolts or by rivets through the yoke. The joints are the dominant core loss, so the assembler has to keep the interleaving sequenc"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.stack.clamp-hardware" [label="Clamp bolts and angle plates" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Mild steel angle plates bolted across the yokes, or two long tie bolts through the core legs, with the axial clamp force chosen so the interlaminar pressure is roughly 3-10 kg/mm2 - enough to stop the stack buzzing at 50 Hz, not so much that the punched edges crack."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.lamination" [label="Punched E and I lamination" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="One E and one I punched from a strip of grain-oriented electrical steel by a high-speed progression press, roughly 200-600 strokes per minute. The die is a hardened tool-steel stamping die with a compound profile; the strip is fed on a roll and the interlamination burr left by the punch is small eno"];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.lamination.punch" [label="High-speed stamping press" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="A vertical crank or hydraulic press with the compound E+I die in the lower ram and a blanking punch above. In a 1982-era plant the strip ran on a powered roll feed with a pilot pin for registration; the same press also cut the mounting-hole side features and the interleaving key."];
  "c64.psu.transformer.core.lamination.punch.die" [label="Hardened tool-steel stamping die" fillcolor="#ff9e6422" color="#ff9e64" tooltip="Tungsten-chromium or high-chromium tool steel, hardened and ground, with the compound E and I outline in the same 25-40 mm stroke. Dies lasted millions of hits before the cutting edge rolled and the burr grew past the electrical-steel tolerance."];
  "c64.mainboard" [label="Mainboard ASSY 250407" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A single two-layer FR-4 board carrying the whole computer: 1.6 mm of laminate with 35 um of copper on each face, about 1200 unplated component holes, and not one plated-through hole or via anywhere on it. Eight 4164 64 Kbit DRAMs give the 64 KB of main RAM and one 2114L static chip holds 1 KB of scr"];
  "mb.photo" [label="Phototooling: how C64 copper artwork became an etched board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The chain that turns a board database into a resist image. The 250407 artwork is number 251137. It was drawn on a design workstation, reduced to aperture-table plot files, plotted onto photographic film at a jobbing house, developed, checked on a test coupon, and used as the mask that prints the boa"];
  "mb.photo.panel" [label="Panelising the order" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The board is one part out of a panel of identical boards nested in a sheet large enough for the process equipment. The panel gets tooling holes for registration and for the drill, and the boards are held together by small perforated mouse-bite tabs that are broken out by hand or by a router after th"];
  "mb.photo.mask" [label="Printing the artwork onto the resist" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The artwork film is laid against the exposed dry-film resist on the board and exposed in the vacuum frame. Where the film is clear, the resist hardens and resists the etch; where it is opaque, the resist stays soft and the copper beneath is etched away. This is the moment the phototooling chain fina"];
  "mb.photo.rephoto" [label="Rephotography, the master, and the coupon" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The plotted working film is rephotographed onto a higher-quality film to get a sharper, dimensionally stable master. Before a whole panel is committed, a test coupon is exposed and etched with every line width the panel uses, and the widths are measured under a measuring microscope. This is how a sh"];
  "mb.photo.rephoto.master" [label="The artwork master" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The film treated as the authoritative copy of the design, stored flat and handled by the edges. It is the board's master record, and losing it means paying for the panel again."];
  "mb.photo.develop" [label="Developing and fixing the film" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The exposed film is developed, fixed, washed and dried before it can serve as a mask. The chemistry is ordinary silver-halide photographic processing, run as a short line of tanks."];
  "mb.photo.develop.fixer" [label="Fixer (ammonium thiosulfate)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The unexposed silver halide is dissolved away and the image made light-stable, so the film can be handled, stored and re-exposed without latent-image changes."];
  "mb.photo.develop.developer" [label="Photographic developer (hydroquinone)" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The film emulsion is silver halide; hydroquinone reduces the exposed grains to metallic silver, giving the opaque image that will block the resist. The reducing agent is itself an aromatic chemical made from benzene, so the mask chemistry is a petroleum chain like almost everything else in this bran"];
  "mb.photo.plotter" [label="Photoplotting the artwork film" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The plot files drive a photoplotter that draws the copper artwork onto a transparent film. In 1983 these machines lived at the fabricator, not the design office, and were the company's most valuable single asset."];
  "mb.photo.plotter.laser" [label="Laser plotting, arriving later" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="An argon-ion or, later, a diode laser writes the film or the photoresist directly. Sharper than corona discharge and faster, but in 1983 it was still being adopted and the electrostatic plotter was not yet history. This is the last piece of 1980s phototooling equipment before direct imaging replaced"];
  "mb.photo.plotter.pen" [label="Pen plotter" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A carriage carrying a light-sensitive pen draws the artwork directly on film or mylar. Simple and reliable, but slow, with real line-width variation, so it was being displaced by the electrostatic plotter through the early 1980s. Still used for check plots and for prototype single-layer boards well "];
  "mb.photo.plotter.film" [label="Artwork film stock" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A high-contrast, dimensionally stable photographic film that the plotter exposes and the shop develops into a finished mask. It must hold its geometry over a store door and be opaque where there is copper. The base is a dimensionally stable polyester, the same polymer family as the dry-film resist c"];
  "mb.photo.plotter.electrostatic" [label="Electrostatic plotter" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The dominant plotting technology of the era. A fine wire or shaped electrode is stepped across the film under computer control and a corona discharge exposes the emulsion wherever copper is wanted. Flatbed machines laid a film flat and exposed quickly, which is why they became the standard for two-s"];
  "mb.photo.cam" [label="Data reduction and CAM" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The database is converted into per-layer plot files. This is where a net becomes copper, a component pin becomes a pad, and the drill hits are separated out; autorouters handled most of the bulk in 1983 and a human cleaned up the critical nets, which is why the copper on a 1983 board looks the way i"];
  "mb.photo.cam.apertures" [label="Aperture table" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Every distinct pad shape and track width is assigned an aperture in a table the plotter reads, so a 1.0 mm round pad and a 0.3 mm track are drawn as repeated simple shapes rather than as free curves. The Gerber format was invented at this time to carry exactly this table, which is why it is still ab"];
  "mb.photo.design" [label="The board database on the design system" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="In 1983 the layout lived on the CAD vendor's own workstation, not in a modern repository. Traces, pads, components and drill data were edited on the machine and periodically written out as plot files on nine-track tape and then on 8-inch floppy disks. Copying it to the jobbing house was a physical a"];
  "mb.photo.design.backannotation" [label="Backannotation from a drawn overlay" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The designer taped mylar over the plotted printout, drew routing changes with a special pen, and passed the overlay through a digitizer that read the new lines and apertures back into the database. Some of the most awkward-looking copper on a 1983 board is the residue of this workflow: a routing dec"];
  "mb.photo.design.workstation" [label="PCB CAD workstation" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A dedicated 16-bit microcomputer or small minicomputer with its own storage and a high-resolution monochrome display, often with a pen plotter and a digitizer on the desk. The digitizer mattered as much as the plotter: designers drew and revised artwork on mylar overlays and digitised the changes ba"];
  "mb.photo.design.workstation.storage" [label="Plot-file storage media" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Plot files and library data were written to nine-track tape, then to 8-inch floppy disks. The magnetic oxide on a thin polymer backing is the physical carrier the jobbing house receives. This is the handover point between design and fabrication."];
  "mb.photo.design.workstation.display" [label="Monochrome CRT display" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A high-resolution green or white phosphor CRT on which the layout was examined trace by trace. This is a designer's tooling CRT, not the machine's own video output."];
  "mb.photo.design.workstation.display.glass" [label="CRT envelope glass" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The bulb is soda-lime glass formed on a glass lathe from the same kind of batch as the reinforcing cloth: silica sand plus limestone plus alkali. A design workstation's screen is a small consumer item, but it is the terminal through which every copper trace of the board was examined."];
  "mb.test" [label="Factory test of the C64 mainboard and machine" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="How a populated C64 board was proved working before it left the factory. Electrical test on the bare board, then a functional test that boots the machine from a diagnostic ROM in the cartridge port and checks it against a monitor and a test cradle, then burn-in, then final test and packing. The prac"];
  "mb.test.final" [label="Final test and packing" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A last pass over the complete machine in its case: keyboard, control ports, serial port and a final power-on. Surviving machines are packed with the cord and the manual for shipment, in corrugated board with a printed outer. The packaging branch has its own industrial chemistry under c64.packaging; "];
  "mb.test.final.ports" [label="Control and serial port check" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The two control ports and the serial port are exercised with known-good peripherals or loopback plugs. These are the connectors most often left untested on a line that was under pressure, and it is where the documented field failures of this era cluster."];
  "mb.test.burnin" [label="Burn-in" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Finished machines are powered up under load, often at elevated temperature, for an extended period. This was originally to weed out infant-mortality failures in the custom chips, which is exactly where the C64's problems were, and it is why there had to be a diagnostic ROM that could run unattended."];
  "mb.test.burnin.oven" [label="Burn-in oven or rack" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A room or cabinet holding powered machines on racks, warmed to speed up failure. The heat comes from nichrome resistance elements, an alloy of nickel and chromium that resists oxidation at red heat, so the oven is a consumer of both metals in small but real quantity. It is also one of the largest el"];
  "mb.test.burnin.oven.rack" [label="Burn-in rack" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A steel frame carrying a shelf of machines, ventilated so the heat is actually reaching them. The rack had to carry a full load of powered machines for hours at temperature, so the frame is structural steel rather than sheet-metal convenience."];
  "mb.test.video" [label="CRT display test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The machine's own composite video output is fed to a test monitor and the inspector reads the picture. Nothing instruments this: the pass criterion is that the picture is correct, which means the human being in the loop is doing the measuring. The engineering team said outright that testing colour q"];
  "mb.test.video.monitor" [label="Test monitor" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A small composite monochrome monitor, or a colour television, used as the reference picture. Each is a cathode-ray tube: drawn glass envelope, phosphor coating, deflection yoke and a high-voltage supply."];
  "mb.test.functional" [label="Functional test with a test ROM" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The populated board is mounted in a test cradle with a power supply and a monitor, and the machine proves itself by running a diagnostic program from the cartridge port. This exercises the real boot path - CPU, ROM, RAM, character ROM, VIC, SID and the I/O chips - all in the configuration they will "];
  "mb.test.functional.keyboard" [label="Keyboard and ribbon test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Because the keyboard connects to the mainboard through a soldered ribbon on this design, the functional test has to cover that joint: every key is checked, which is a simultaneous test of the connector, the ribbon, the keyboard matrix and the CPU's scanning routine."];
  "mb.test.functional.cradle" [label="Test cradle" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A jig that holds the board on locating pins, presents a regulated 5 V and 12 V, routes the video and audio outputs to a monitor and speaker, and presents the cartridge and edge connectors. The C64's 5 V and 12 V came from its external supply, so the factory had to prove both the board and that suppl"];
  "mb.test.functional.cradle.psu" [label="Test power supply" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A regulated supply with a current limit, so a shorted board does not destroy it. This is a bench instrument, not the machine's own power brick."];
  "mb.test.functional.rom" [label="Diagnostic and dead-test ROM" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A small EPROM in a cartridge shell which, on power-up, prints a screenful of named tests and runs each one, reporting RAM, ROM and video faults by pattern rather than by instrument. The operator's job is to look at the picture and read the verdict. These test ROMs were so successful that Commodore l"];
  "mb.test.ict" [label="In-circuit electrical test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A bare populated board is checked electrically before it is ever plugged into a machine. The point is to catch a missing component, a shorted pad or a broken trace while it is still trivially fixable. The board is pressed down onto a bed-of-nails fixture whose probes touch every node of interest, so"];
  "mb.test.ict.tester" [label="The tester itself" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A rack of switching electronics driving the fixture, with a go or no-go readout and a reject bin. It is ordinary 1980s digital test equipment: a small computer, a relay matrix and a display, all built from the same logic families the C64 itself uses."];
  "mb.test.ict.presence" [label="Component-presence and seating check" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Checking that the right part is in the right place is a distinct and important test on a board with thirty sockets and a hundred passive parts. A missing, reversed or half-seated socketed IC is the single most common fault on any 1980s logic board. Verifiers that push each socket to confirm its pins"];
  "mb.test.ict.measure" [label="Continuity, resistance and short detection" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The tester looks for opens in the artwork and for shorts between nets that should be isolated, and on some fixtures measures absolute values. On a simple two-layer board with no vias, continuity checking is very nearly the whole job."];
  "mb.assembly" [label="Populating, cleaning and finishing the C64 mainboard" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="What happens between the bare board and the tested machine. Components are inserted, a few hand-fitted wires and jumpers are added, the board is soldered, cleaned, shielded and screwed into the case. On this board the whole operation is through-hole and hand-and-machine insertion: there is no surfac"];
  "mb.assembly.volume" [label="Volume and yield reality of the line" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Commodore sold on the order of two million C64s a year at the 1983-84 peak, on a production cost that started near 130 dollars and fell as volume rose. Every board here was one of millions per year through the same line, which is the context that explains every process decision in this branch: a two"];
  "mb.assembly.repair" [label="Board-level rework and repair" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Bad joints are reworked on the line: the joint is reflowed with an iron, the part is replaced, and a full set of consumable tips is kept to do it. Commodore's own engineering position was famously to ship rather than to hold back, which shows up here as rework being a normal, high-volume operation r"];
  "mb.assembly.shield" [label="EMI shield bonding" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The metal shield around the video and audio circuitry is bonded to the board's ground so that the enclosure is actually part of the ground return. Without a real bond the shield picks up the very signals it is there to contain. On this board the bond is a combination: a small tab on the shield solde"];
  "mb.assembly.shield.tab" [label="The VIC-II heat-sink tab" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A metal tab welded inside the shield lid pressed against the top of the video chip package, so the shield doubled as a heat sink for the chip that dissipated about 1.5 W. This was a deliberate design fix for a real heat problem, not a later addition, and it is a neat example of the enclosure, the gr"];
  "mb.assembly.conformal" [label="No conformal coating on this board" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A deliberate absence, recorded here so it does not read as an omission. The C64 mainboard was not conformally coated. Conformal coating existed in 1983 for military and automotive work and was increasingly common in commercial electronics, but not for a home computer in a dry indoor case."];
  "mb.assembly.reflow" [label="No surface-mount parts and no reflow on this board" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A deliberate absence, recorded here so it does not read as an omission. It is worth being explicit that the C64 mainboard has no surface-mount parts and no reflow step: everything is through-hole, everything is either waved or hand-soldered. Reflow ovens were already known in 1983, but they were use"];
  "mb.assembly.jumpers" [label="Wire jumpers and configuration links" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Because the board has no vias, the few places where a connection must cross between the two copper layers with no component to do it are bridged with a short tinned copper wire, hand-fitted into two holes and hand-soldered after wave soldering. These are the E1 and E2 links on the 250407 layout, and"];
  "mb.assembly.jumpers.wire" [label="Tinned single-core jumper wire" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A short length of fine tinned copper wire, cut and formed to the two holes, fitted and soldered by hand. It is the mechanical ancestor of the jumper link on every later board."];
  "mb.assembly.sockets" [label="DIP sockets as the C64 serviceability choice" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="This is the single most underappreciated decision on the board. The C64 sockets its integrated circuits rather than soldering them directly, which means any chip can be swapped in the field - and it means the number of joints roughly doubles, because each socket has two contacts per pin. A home comp"];
  "mb.assembly.insertion" [label="Component insertion" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Every part goes into the board from the top. Because there are no vias and no bottom-side components, the whole machine is one-sided: one operator can see every joint from the solder side and there is no second-side insertion operation at all. Axial parts are cut to length and formed to a right angl"];
  "mb.assembly.insertion.fixture" [label="Insertion fixture and board handling" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The board is held in a jig with locating pins while components go in, and the same family of jig carries the board through the wave and into test. Every fixture on the line solves the same problem: the board has to land in exactly one position, every time."];
  "mb.assembly.insertion.engine" [label="Insertion engine and its cycle time" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A machine with a magazine per part type picks a part, leads it to the right hole and pushes it in, typically in 0.3-0.6 s per component. On a board of about 90 parts that is roughly half a minute of machine time, which is why insertion engines paid for themselves on volume this high. The bedplate is"];
  "mb.assembly.insertion.engine.magazines" [label="Part magazines and vibratory feed" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Radial magazines shake components down a track to a pick head. Cut, formed axial leads are the hardest part to feed; resistors and capacitors with wide leads feed easily."];
  "mb.assembly.insertion.layout" [label="Component spacing dictated by the insertion machine" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Component spacing on this board was dictated by the insertion machine rather than by the circuit designer, which is exactly the argument Commodore had with its Japanese plant and lost. The same board could not be hand-inserted efficiently and machine-inserted efficiently at once."];
  "mb.solder" [label="Soldering the C64 mainboard" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Every joint on the C64 mainboard is made with 63/37 tin-lead alloy activated by rosin flux: about 600 in one pass through a wave-soldering machine, the rest by hand afterwards on the connectors, fuse clips, big electrolytics and wire jumpers. This branch follows the machines, the settings and the be"];
  "mb.solder.selective" [label="Connectors, fuse clips and heavy joints" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The parts that cannot simply be taken through the wave in the usual way: the D-sub and DIN connectors, the fuse clips, the wire jumpers and the large electrolytics. The board-mount connectors on this board are right-angle types with solder tails projecting out of the back of the shell, so they were "];
  "mb.solder.hand" [label="Hand soldering on the line" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Soldering iron, solder wire and pliers on a bench under a smoke extraction arm. Used for the connectors, the fuse clips, the wire jumpers, the big electrolytics and every joint the inspector rejected. A small minority of joints, but where the line rejects come from too. A small hand-held solder pot "];
  "mb.solder.hand.extraction" [label="Fume extraction" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="An arm or hood over the bench draws off the flux smoke. The smoke is resin vapour, not tin vapour, but it is unpleasant and it is the reason every solder bench in 1983 had a hood over it."];
  "mb.solder.hand.iron" [label="Soldering iron and tip" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A nichrome or cartridge heater in a barrel with a replaceable copper tip. The tip has to be kept tinned; a dry tip oxidises and then stops transferring heat at all, which is the real reason beginners conclude solder will not melt on a joint."];
  "mb.solder.reliability" [label="Copper-tin intermetallic at the pad" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Solder does not merely sit on a pad, it reacts with it. Within seconds of contact, tin and copper fuse at the surface into Cu6Sn5, and a Cu3Sn layer follows as the joint ages. That intermetallic is the actual metallurgical bond holding the joint, and because its growth is limited by diffusion throug"];
  "mb.solder.profile" [label="The wave-soldering profile" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The machine is set by five numbers together: pot temperature, conveyor speed, preheat temperature, preheat time and flux concentration. Get them wrong in a specific way and you get a specific, diagnosable defect. A joint cools from 250 deg C to 183 deg C in about a second; if the board and the lead "];
  "mb.solder.profile.defects" [label="Joint defects and what causes them" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The diagnostic vocabulary a line supervisor used in 1983, and it has barely changed since. Dull grainy joints mean a dirty pad or too little preheat. Dry joints and unfilled holes mean a hole too tight for the lead or a wave too slow. Solder balls and spatter mean moisture in the board. Bridging bet"];
  "mb.solder.profile.thermalmass" [label="Heavy leads set the slowest joint" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Why the big electrolytics are among the joints most often hand-touched after the wave, and why the instruction to go back and check them existed at all."];
  "mb.solder.profile.thermal" [label="Why preheat sets joint quality" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The thermal-shock argument for the preheat tunnel, in one place. Starting the joint at 120 deg C lets the alloy flow and wet before it freezes; starting cold does not."];
  "mb.solder.wave" [label="Wave-soldering machine" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A conveyorised machine that drags the underside of the board through a standing wave of molten solder. Components are all inserted from the top so they point the same way, and the wave only ever touches leads and pads. It is why a 1983 line could put hundreds of sound joints on a board for the price"];
  "mb.solder.wave.cooling" [label="Cooling zone" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="After the wave, boards sit in moving hot air before leaving the machine. Hot air rather than cold, because chilling a freshly soldered joint around a large thermal-mass lead is how you get a cracked solder fillet."];
  "mb.solder.wave.nitrogen" [label="Nitrogen blanket over the pot" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Many machines piped nitrogen over the solder surface to keep the oxide skin thin and stop dross forming. It also stops the skin from insulating the pot from the wave, which makes the wave itself erratic. Cheaper machines ran open to air and simply lived with the dross."];
  "mb.solder.wave.conveyor" [label="Conveyor chain and fixtures" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A stainless or phenolic chain carries boards through the machine, held by their edge in fixtures spaced along the chain. Chain pitch and fixture design, not the pump, set the real throughput. The fixtures were often the board's own edge, which is one reason board edge quality was a production concer"];
  "mb.solder.wave.foam" [label="Flux foamer" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Just before the wave, a bank of porous stone immersed in rosin flux bubbles air through it and lays a thin, even, pre-activated film on the board's underside. Foaming gives uniform coverage a spray or brush never does, and it is where the smell of boiling pine on a board line comes from."];
  "mb.solder.wave.foam.stone" [label="Sintered porous stone" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A sintered polyethylene or polyurethane stone whose pore size is chosen to bubble air finely through the flux. It clogs with crystallised flux and has to be soaked out on a schedule."];
  "mb.solder.wave.preheat" [label="Preheat tunnel" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="An oven before the wave that brings the whole board to roughly 120 deg C so the thermal shock of hitting 250 deg C solder is spread out. It also drives off moisture, which would otherwise spit solder out of the holes as the board hits the wave."];
  "mb.solder.wave.preheat.air" [label="Recirculating hot-air preheat" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Preheating is by moving air or radiant tubes, with the zone held below the solder melting point so nothing melts early. Too little preheat gives thermally shocked joints; too much dries the resin and lifts pads off the laminate. A board full of large electrolytics takes a long time to come through, "];
  "mb.solder.wave.frame" [label="Welded machine frame and shell" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The pot, tunnel and conveyor sit in a welded mild-steel frame that also carries the fume hood and the flux plumbing. The mass of the frame is what keeps the wave flat while the pot runs. The steel is arc- or induction-furnace steel from the same iron ore and coke as every other machine tool on this "];
  "mb.crystal" [label="Master clock crystal and oscillator" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Y1, a hermetically sealed HC-49/U quartz crystal, plus the four glue-logic chips that turn it into the machine's entire timing reference: a colour subcarrier crystal oscillator, a PLL that quadruples it, a divider chain, and a phase/frequency detector that locks the dot clock to the colour clock. Ev"];
  "mb.crystal.oscillator" [label="Oscillator and PLL circuitry" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The crystal is only the frequency reference. Turning it into the machine's clocks takes a 74LS629 dual VCO crystal oscillator, a Motorola MC4044 phase/frequency detector, a 74LS193 divider, and a 74LS74 flip-flop, with R27 or a trimmer capacitor setting the exact pull."];
  "mb.crystal.oscillator.pll" [label="PLL: MC4044 into the 74LS629 VCO" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The 74LS629's second oscillator is a voltage-controlled oscillator. The MC4044 phase/frequency detector compares a divided colour clock against the VIC-II's phase-0 clock and returns a DC control voltage on pin 8, which the 74LS629's frequency-control pin steers. That is the whole of the C64's PLL: "];
  "mb.crystal.oscillator.pll.pal-variant" [label="PAL crystal, 17.734475 MHz" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The PAL machine uses a 17.734475 MHz crystal (four times the 4.433619 MHz PAL subcarrier) in the same circuit, and the 74LS193 divider is switched to divide by nine instead of seven. Later C64B/C boards moved to a 16 MHz crystal and a dedicated clock-generator IC, dropping the discrete VCO."];
  "mb.crystal.oscillator.pll.trim-range" [label="Why a trim is required at all" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The NTSC colour subcarrier is specified to about 5 parts in 10^7, and an untrimmed crystal and oscillator would wander far more than that over temperature and supply. The trim element (R27 on breadbin boards, a capacitive trim later) lets the whole PLL be pulled onto the exact frequency at the facto"];
  "mb.crystal.oscillator.pierce" [label="Pierce oscillator and load capacitors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The crystal acts as a negative-resistance element in a Pierce oscillator with a small load capacitor at its CL and the drive level set by a series resistor. Its frequency is set by the crystal's CL, the load capacitors and the transistor's input capacitance all in series: at 14 MHz with a few tens o"];
  "mb.crystal.y1" [label="Y1 quartz crystal, HC-49/U" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A plano-convex quartz plate, 0.07 mm thick, with electrodes fired on its two faces, held on supports inside a small metal can filled with argon and sealed by a crimped, resistance-welded cap. The quartz is the oscillator's frequency-setting element: the whole machine's timing accuracy is the accurac"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case" [label="HC-49/U case: crimp, weld and argon fill" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The plate sits on a pair of wire supports in a crimped and welded nickel can, filled with argon or krypton at a few kPa. The can is drawn from nickel-plated steel or brass, and its small welded lid is what makes the unit hermetic - the reason a 1982 crystal does not drift as the humidity of an attic"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.support-wire" [label="Spring-wire supports" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Two short wires spot-welded to the plate's major faces at the nodal points. Because quartz is piezoelectric, mechanically stressing it shifts its resonant frequency, so the supports must sit where the strain of the vibrating mode is smallest - the whole reason the plate is bevelled and domed."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.support-wire.wire" [label="Gold or nickel support wire" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Fine wire, 0.1-0.2 mm, spot-welded to the plate electrodes. Gold is preferred where maximum Q is needed; nickel is the cost-driven choice for the volume 1982 HC-49."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.argon-fill" [label="Argon or krypton fill gas" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The can is filled with a chemically inert gas before welding. Argon is preferred because it does not react with the electrode and cannot outgas; krypton was used where a slightly more hermetic atmosphere was wanted. The gas also damps acoustic vibration in the plate."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.nickel-can" [label="Nickel can stock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="HC-49/U cans were drawn from nickel or nickel-plated strip steel, or from brass with a nickel finish. Nickel is used because it matches the thermal expansion of the quartz and the electrode leads closely enough that the mounting stress does not drift the frequency."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.nickel-can.weld" [label="Welded lid and crimped collar" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The lid is spot-welded or resistance-welded to the collar in an atmosphere of dry hydrogen or forming gas, which keeps the internal oxide clean and lets the fill gas stay inert. Hermeticity is what makes a forty-year-old crystal hold its frequency."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.nickel-can.weld.gas" [label="Forming gas for welding" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A mixture of hydrogen and nitrogen used as a cover during the lid weld. Hydrogen also diffuses any internal oxide onto the getters, which is why a well-made can can have a getter mirror at the bottom after opening."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.case.nickel-can.stock" [label="Drawn can stock" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The HC-49/U can is deep-drawn from thin nickel-plated strip in a progressive tool, then the crimped collar is rolled and the lid welded on. Both operations happen before the plate goes in - the can is made empty and loaded later."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.cutting" [label="Cutting the plate and vacuum baking" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The plated blank is cut with a diamond wheel into individual resonator plates, then the plates are vacuum-baked at around 400 C to drive off adsorbed moisture and contaminants. That vacuum bake is what removes the piezoelectric pyroelectric drift and the 'dope' effect - the stress-induced frequency "];
  "mb.crystal.y1.cutting.cleanroom" [label="Cleaning between steps" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Every step from lapping onward is done washed and clean: a fingerprint on a 0.07 mm plate changes the frequency. Crystal preparation is one of the few wet, cold, dirty-looking processes in electronics."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.cutting.cleanroom.deionised-water" [label="Deionised rinse water" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Every crystal process rinse uses deionised water, because a single dissolved ion on a resonating plate changes the frequency measurably. City water is nowhere good enough."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.cutting.dicing-saw" [label="Diamond dicing saw" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin resin-bonded diamond wheel, ~0.03-0.05 mm thick, cutting the plated blank into resonators. The wheel has to be extremely thin and extremely flat to avoid chipping a 0.07 mm plate."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.electrodes" [label="Metal electrodes on the blank faces" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Chromium is evaporated or sputtered onto both faces as an adhesion layer, then gold or silver is deposited over it as the conductive electrode, through a shadow mask that defines the electrode area. Chromium-gold is the classic 1980s crystal electrode stack: Cr to bond, Au to conduct."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.electrodes.gold-film" [label="Gold electrode film" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Gold evaporated through a shadow mask to give the electrode its final geometry and conductivity. The mask pattern fixes the electrode area, which together with the plate thickness is what sets the crystal capacitance."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.electrodes.gold-film.mask" [label="Evaporation shadow mask" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin metal mask placed above the rotating blank during evaporation so the electrode pattern is defined. The mask is made from a drawn stainless or nickel strip, chemically etched to the electrode shape."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.electrodes.chromium" [label="Chromium adhesion layer" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Chromium is evaporated as the adhesion layer because bare quartz will not hold gold by van der Waals forces alone. Chromium wets both the quartz surface and the gold film, and it is itself a hard, chemically inert metal."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.supports" [label="Mounting and soldering of the crystal" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The four HC-49 leads are formed to a right angle and soldered onto the board in a wave-solder pass. The crystal's case sits at the board's natural mechanical frequency well below the circuit's operating frequency, which is deliberate: an HC-49 body has several overtone resonances that would otherwis"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.grinding" [label="Grinding the lens shape" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The face is bevelled and the outline ground so the finished blank is a lens or plano-convex dome rather than a flat slab. The convexity is deliberate: it makes the crystal's resonant frequency less sensitive to surface damage and to where the mounting points press on it."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.grinding.diamond-wheel" [label="Diamond grinding wheel" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A resin-bonded diamond wheel, used wet, that bevels the plate edge and cuts the lens curvature. In 1982 diamond abrasive was largely synthetic, made from graphite under a shock wave; natural diamond was reserved for dressing the wheels."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.blank-preparation" [label="Sawing, lapping and X-ray orienting the blank" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Rough crystal is sawn into blanks, then the cut face is lapped to about 0.15 mm and X-ray diffracted to find its crystallographic orientation. Orientation is everything: only an AT-cut at the right angle gives the flat temperature curve a frequency-stable oscillator needs."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.blank-preparation.lapping" [label="Lapping and double-lap polishing" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The cut blank is lapped on rotating cast-iron plates with progressively finer abrasives (aluminium oxide, then colloidal silica) down to about 0.15 mm, then double-lapped against a second plate to remove bow and damage left by the saw. Crystal blanks for a 1982 oscillator were lapped on plates run b"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.blank-preparation.lapping.colloidal-silica" [label="Final lapping abrasive" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The last lapping stage uses a colloidal silica slurry, which polishes rather than cuts. Colloidal silica is precipitated from a dilute sodium silicate solution, itself made from silica sand and sodium carbonate."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.blank-preparation.xray-orienting" [label="X-ray orientation and AT-cut angle selection" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A synthetic rough is indexed by X-ray diffraction so its Z axis and X axis are known, then sawn at a chosen angle to the X axis (the 'AT-cut'). The mid-1980s standard for general-purpose oscillators was about 35 degrees; this angle is chosen because it lands the resonance on a flat, temperature-inse"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.natural-quartz.synthetic" [label="Hydrothermal synthetic quartz" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Because natural quartz is limited and variable, industry moved to growing it: an autoclave is charged with silica (often crushed natural quartz) in a hot sodium-hydroxide solution and heated at around 400 C and 150 MPa. Quartz grows on seed crystals hung in the solution, growing the rough in days ra"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.natural-quartz.synthetic.silica-feed" [label="Quartz sand / crushed quartz feed" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The silica feedstock for the autoclave is cleaned quartz sand or lumps of milky quartz. Natural quartz sand is a common beach and dune sand: essentially SiO2, left behind as quartz survives weathering while everything else dissolves and washes away."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.natural-quartz.synthetic.autoclave" [label="Steel autoclave with seed crystals" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A filled, sealed pressure vessel - usually Hastelloy or 17-4PH steel liner - held at high temperature and pressure so an alkaline solution dissolves silica from the feed and re-deposits it on thin zoned seed plates hung in the vessel. Temperature gradients create the supersaturation that drives grow"];
  "mb.crystal.y1.natural-quartz.synthetic.autoclave.caustic" [label="Alkali solvent" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The growth solution is hot concentrated sodium or potassium hydroxide, which dissolves silica at the elevated temperature while quartz re-deposits on the seeds. The alkali is made by chlor-alkali electrolysis of brine, so the crystal ultimately touches rock salt as well as quartz sand."];
  "mb.crystal.y1.natural-quartz" [label="Natural rock crystal" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Gem-quality clear quartz for crystal units came historically from the rock-crystal veins of Minas Gerais in Brazil, prized for natural faces that need no lapping and for the very low defect density that makes a low-phase-noise oscillator possible. By the early 1980s this was being displaced by hydro"];
  "mb.passives" [label="Mainboard passive components" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The roughly 250-odd resistors, capacitors, trimmers and networks soldered to a Commodore 64 ASSY 250407 motherboard. No ICs, no connectors: only the two-terminal and adjustable parts that set the bias, timing and filtering of everything else on the board."];
  "mb.passives.caps" [label="Capacitors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The mainboard's decoupling, timing, filtering and coupling capacitors. Overwhelmingly ceramic (0.1 uF bypass plus small timing values), with a smaller tail of aluminium electrolytics for the low-frequency supplies and film polyester for the SID output coupling."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking" [label="Capacitor wrap, sleeving and marking" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Not a separate component but the packaging shared across the capacitor families: dipped epoxy for ceramic disc and monolithic parts, printed PVC or polyester sleeving for film caps, cardboard or aluminium sleeves for electrolytics, and printed value/brand markings on all of them."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking.pvc-sleeve" [label="Printed PVC sleeve" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Film capacitors arrived in a printed plastic sleeve printed with the value, the rating and the maker. Sleeve polymer is the same PVC family as the case and the cable jacket."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking.ink" [label="Capacitor value marking ink" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Values were printed on the sleeve or dipped coat in an oil-based or epoxy ink on an offset or screen coater. The two-digit and three-digit codes (104 for 100 nF, 104K5V) only became an industry standard in the 1970s."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking.ink.pigment" [label="Ink pigment" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The value marking pigment is carbon black for dark parts and azo or phthalocyanine colour for bright ones, in a resin vehicle that will not be attacked by the board cleaning solvents."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking.epoxy-dip" [label="E dipped conformal coat" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Ceramic and monolithic parts were dip-coated in epoxy or PVC after firing to seal the porous body and to carry the printed marking. A dipped part looks identical to a moulded one, which is why it is hard to tell from a photograph whether a given C64 ceramic is disc or monolithic."];
  "mb.passives.caps.wrap-and-marking.epoxy-dip.tank" [label="Dip-coating line" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Parts on a wire rack are dipped in an epoxy or PVC solution and withdrawn so the coating tapers at both ends, leaving a characteristic tapered shoulder at the lead exit. A second dip gives the second shoulder."];
  "mb.passives.caps.trim" [label="Trimmer and variable capacitors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Small adjustable capacitors used to compensate or trim a circuit. On C64-family boards a trimmer capacitor sits in the crystal oscillator's load network, pulling the frequency exactly onto the NTSC or PAL colour subcarrier; later Commodore boards replaced the earlier adjustable resistor with this ca"];
  "mb.passives.caps.trim.air-gap" [label="Air-gap / film trimmer capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Two sets of interdigitated metal plates with an adjustable overlap, separated by air or a thin film. Turning the screw changes the overlap and therefore the capacitance continuously, letting the factory trim the oscillator to the exact subcarrier frequency. The dielectric is air, which is why these "];
  "mb.passives.caps.trim.air-gap.ceramic-plate" [label="Stator and rotor plates" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The trimmer's rotor is a small disc of alumina ceramic, silver-metallised on one face through a spiral or interdigitated pattern. Turning the screw rotates the disc against a fixed plate, so the overlapping metallised area - and hence the capacitance - changes continuously."];
  "mb.passives.caps.trim.air-gap.ceramic-plate.alumina" [label="Alumina rotor plate material" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The rotor disc is alumina, polished and metallised with a spiral or interdigitated silver pattern. Alumina is used because it is dimensionally stable, machinable to a fine finish and a good insulator at the trimmer's working voltage."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica" [label="Mica capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A capacitor built from thin sheets of natural muscovite mica with vacuum-deposited aluminium or silver counter-electrodes. Superb temperature stability and high dielectric strength, too expensive and too fragile for general use, so mica was reserved for precision and high-voltage niches in equipment"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.metallisation" [label="Vacuum-deposited counter-electrodes" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Aluminium or silver is evaporated or sputtered through a shadow-mask onto the mica sheets; sheets are stacked with a thin metallised layer between each, bonded under heat and pressure, and the stack is diced into individual capacitors."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.metallisation.aluminium-evaporation" [label="Aluminium electrode evaporation" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Aluminium is evaporated rather than sputtered because it is cheap and vapourises at a manageable temperature; it is then left unoxidised under vacuum. Silver electrodes were used where the highest capacitance per unit area was needed."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.muscovite" [label="Muscovite mica, heat-split into sheets" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Natural muscovite is mined in large crystal books from pegmatites and delaminated by heating and cementing against a hot plate, so it splits along its perfect basal cleavage into micrometre-thick, optically flat sheets. The crystal's naturally layered atomic structure gives a very uniform, very thin"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.muscovite.heat-splitting" [label="Heat-and-cement splitting and ruby splitting" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A mica book is mounted on a hot plate; a hot blade of mica is pressed onto it with a cement stick, which drives a wedge along the perfect basal cleavage and peels off a sheet a few microns thick. Repeated, it yields hundreds of optically flat sheets from one crystal, and it is the labour-intensive s"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.muscovite.heat-splitting.knife" [label="Hot knife and cement stick" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A heated mica blade driven by a cement stick along the cleavage plane. The blade has to be hot enough to part the sheets without charring the cement, and both blade and stick are specialist consumables of the splitting trade."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mica.muscovite.pegmatite" [label="Pegmatite host rock" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Mica books grow in the giant granitic pegmatites of the Precambrian shield - Minas Gerais in Brazil, Rajasthan in India, Madagascar, and in the United States the Appalachian and Spruce Pine districts of North Carolina. It is mined in situ and split, not manufactured. The books are followed undergrou"];
  "mb.passives.caps.film" [label="Film capacitors (polyester / polystyrene)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Film capacitors use a thin metallised plastic film as the dielectric. Non-polar, stable, and tolerant of voltage and temperature excursions. The C64 uses polyester (Melinex/PET) for coupling and decoupling in the analogue paths; polystyrene appears in the precision timing networks of the same era's "];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polystyrene" [label="Polystyrene film capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A polystyrene dielectric: exceptionally stable, very low dielectric absorption and low loss, historically the timing capacitor of choice for precision RC oscillators before polystyrene's poor mechanical behaviour pushed it into disfavour. Used in analogue equipment of the era, but not a significant "];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polystyrene.metallisation" [label="Vacuum metallisation of PS film" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The same vacuum-deposited aluminium electrode as on the polyester film, but on a more delicate substrate: polystyrene outgasses and embrittles under vacuum heat, so the film has to be baked and the deposition kept cool."];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polystyrene.styrene-polymerisation" [label="Styrene polymerisation to polystyrene" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Free-radical or ionic polymerisation of styrene, in bulk suspension or emulsion, gives the amorphous thermoplastic. Unmodified polystyrene is brittle and has poor moisture resistance, which is precisely why metalised polystyrene was restricted to sealed precision parts and lost out to polypropylene."];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polyester-metallisation" [label="Vacuum-metallised polyester film" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="PET pellets are extruded into a cast film, then stretched fast in two perpendicular directions to several times their original length, orienting the polymer chains. That gives a very thin, very uniform, high-tensile dielectric; then a sub-micron metal layer is vacuum-deposited on one or both faces a"];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polyester-metallisation.pet-polymerisation" [label="PET polymerisation" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The film base is polyethylene terephthalate, made by condensing bis(2-hydroxyethyl) terephthalate - itself from ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid - in a melt polymeriser at ~250-290 C under vacuum. The terephthalic acid came from paraxylene, which came from the aromatics fraction of a steam crac"];
  "mb.passives.caps.film.polyester-metallisation.pet-polymerisation.paraxylene" [label="Para-xylene" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Paraxylene sits in the aromatics complex of a large refinery, separated from a mixed xylene fraction by fractional distillation and adsorption. It is the pivot molecule of the entire polyester industry: 1982 saw PET moving from bottle-grade into film, fibre and capacitor dielectric at once."];
  "mb.passives.caps.ceramic-disc" [label="Ceramic disc (monolithic disc) capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A single fired barium-titanate disc with silver electrodes fired onto its two faces. Enormous capacitance for its size and very cheap, but microphonically noisy, temperature-drifty and physically large, with no self-cancelling inductance. On C64 boards of this era the small 0.1 uF parts were already"];
  "mb.passives.caps.ceramic-disc.press-sinter" [label="Pressing, sintering and silver-firing" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="BaTiO3 powder is pressed into a disc, sintered at 1200-1300 C, then silver electrode paste is screen-printed onto each face and fired at 700-850 C so the silver is glass-bonded to the ceramic. Only two electrodes exist, so the part cannot cancel its own inductance - the structural reason a disc is u"];
  "mb.passives.caps.ceramic-disc.press-sinter.body" [label="Disc body material" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The ceramic body of a high-K disc is a modified barium titanate with a glass phase, on an alumina or steatite base. The dielectric-strength figure of about 2.5 kV per micron of effective thickness is a design guideline for the ceramic, not for the finished part."];
  "mb.passives.caps.ceramic-disc.press-sinter.body.base-ceramic" [label="Steatite or alumina base" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Low-firing ceramic substrates, steatite (magnesium silicate) or alumina, onto which the high-K layer is applied. Steatite is cheap and fires low; alumina is tougher and better at heat."];
  "mb.passives.caps.ceramic-disc.press-sinter.blade" [label="Disc press and sinter" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A mechanical or hydraulic press blanks the disc to a controlled thickness; the sinter then densifies it and fires on the silver electrodes."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum" [label="Tantalum capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A polarised capacitor built around sintered tantalum powder with an anodic oxide dielectric and a solid electrolyte. Low leakage and very good stability for its size; in the early 1980s tantalum was the default decoupling choice on high-performance professional digital boards."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.ore-to-powder" [label="Columbite ore to capacitor-grade powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Columbite-tantalite ore is digested in hydrofluoric acid in an autoclave at 100-200 C to dissolve the tantalum as a fluorotantalate, the solution is treated with potassium chloride or potassium hydroxide to precipitate K2Ta2O7, and that intermediate is converted to a metallic or oxide powder by sodi"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.ore-to-powder.columbite-ore" [label="Columbite-tantalite ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Columbite-tantalite is a solid solution of columbite and tantalite, mined both as placer gravels and as lode pegmatites. Concentrating it needs gravity, magnetic and electrostatic separation followed by an acid-leach circuit, because only a few per cent of the rock is Ta2O5; the concentrate may reac"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.supply-crisis.recycling" [label="Capacitor-grade recycling loop" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Because tantalum metal is expensive, tantalum-bearing scrap - rejected capacitors, anode dust, trimmed pellet scrap - was recovered and returned to the powder supply. This is why a 1983-84 shortage hit prices so hard: recycled powder could not be substituted for virgin powder in a dielectric that ha"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.supply-crisis" [label="Early-1980s tantalum supply squeeze" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Capacitor tantalum depends on columbite-tantalite ore, essentially all of it from the Kivu region of Zaire and neighbouring Rwanda and Burundi. A collapse in the electronics market in 1982-83 drained the supply chain's stocks, then demand spiked again in 1984 on the PC and automotive booms. The USGS"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.packaging" [label="Tantalum lead, braze and epoxy seal" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The anode lead is drawn tantalum wire, spot-welded to the sintered pellet, then the assembly is sealed by an epoxy dip. On wet-slug types the hermetic case is glass or a welded metal can with a Teflon ring seal. The part is then marked with its value, rating and the manufacturer."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.packaging.seal" [label="Epoxy dip seal" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Solid tantalum parts were sealed by dipping in epoxy and curing, which protects the brittle MnO2/dielectric stack against handling and moisture. Wet-slug parts instead used a welded metal can with a glass or Teflon seal."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.packaging.seal.bisphenol-a" [label="Epoxy resin and bisphenol A" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The standard epoxy of the period: bisphenol A condensed with epichlorohydrin, cured with an amine. Both halves are petrochemical, and bisphenol A was the dominant resin for adhesive, coating, printed-board and capacitor sealants alike."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.dielectric" [label="Anodic Ta2O5 formation and MnO2 solid electrolyte" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The sintered anode is anodised to grow the Ta2O5 dielectric, then coated with manganese dioxide by pyrolytically decomposing manganese chloride vapour on the surface - the classic 1980s solid tantalum part. A sintered silver-paste counter-electrode existed as an alternative construction. Today's con"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.dielectric.forming-voltage.cathode-body" [label="Counter-electrode body" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Below the manganese dioxide sits the original tantalum anode body and its second termination. The layer stack is metal, dielectric, counter-electrode, then a conductive path to the case - all inside an epoxy dip."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.dielectric.manganese-chloride" [label="Manganese chloride for pyrolysis" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="MnCl2 is vapour-decomposed on the hot tantalum surface in a furnace at several hundred degrees, leaving a conformal MnO2 layer that acts as the solid counter-electrode. The chloride is shipped as a hydrate and is hygroscopic, so it had to be kept dry."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.anode" [label="Sintered tantalum anode" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Capacitor-grade tantalum powder is pressed around a tantalum wire anode lead and sintered at 1200-1400 C under vacuum, producing a porous body with an enormous internal surface area. The Ta2O5 dielectric then grows on that surface, which is why a gram of powder stores far more capacitance than a gra"];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.anode.press" [label="Anode pressing and sintering" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Powder is blended with a binder and pressed around the wire, then debound and sintered in vacuum or flowing hydrogen at 1200-1400 C. The pressed pellet is deliberately porous - roughly half its volume is void - because that void is where the dielectric forms."];
  "mb.passives.caps.tantalum.anode.wire" [label="Drawn tantalum wire" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The anode lead is fine drawn tantalum wire, spot-welded to the sintered pellet, drawn by the same machinery as refractory metals for furnace leads. Whether the anode body is built from reduced metallic powder or from sintered oxide powder, the specification is the same: extremely high purity, sub-mi"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic" [label="Aluminium electrolytic capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The polarised bulk capacitors: about fifteen 10 uF/25 V radial parts decoupling the SID and video analogue rails, plus larger axial 100-2200 uF parts on the rectified supply rails. Value comes from a nanometres-thick anodic oxide on etched aluminium foil soaked in electrolyte - cheap, large capacita"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.aging" [label="Ageing and burn-in" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Finished parts are held at an elevated temperature under DC bias so the oxide re-thickens and the leakage current settles: typically 1-2x the rated voltage at 85-125 C for hours to days. A 1980s capacitor therefore arrives already partly formed, which is why an electrolytic recovered from a two-year"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.aging.test" [label="Capacitance and leakage test" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Parts are tested for capacitance, dissipation factor and leakage at rated temperature. Rejection rates in a commodity 10 uF 25 V part were small but non-zero, and every rejected part was a loss of scrap aluminium. A bridge or LCR meter at rated frequency and temperature gives capacitance and dissipa"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.aging.chamber" [label="Burn-in chamber" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A temperature-controlled rack of trays in which finished parts are held at temperature and bias. A good capacitor plant in 1982 might burn in for hours or days, which is why vintage electrolytics so often arrive already formed."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.assembly" [label="Crimping, sleeving and end-fill bung" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Aluminium or tinned-copper alloy terminal tabs are crimped onto the anode and cathode edges of the wound pack. The pack is then sleeved - PVC heat-shrink or a printed cardboard outer - and sealed at the end with a rubber or PVC bung that retains the electrolyte against its expansion over temperature"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.assembly.tab" [label="Aluminium terminal tab" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A soft aluminium or aluminium-alloy tab, crimped onto the foil edge. Pure aluminium is used because it is the only metal that will not form a galvanic or resistive joint with the foil it must touch."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.assembly.bung" [label="End-fill bung" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A rubber or PVC disc that fills the end of the sleeve and retains the electrolyte against thermal expansion and the internal pressure generated during burn-in. It is the visible rubber disc at the base of a radial electrolytic."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.assembly.bung.silicone" [label="Silicone rubber bung" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Heat-cured silicone rubber, chosen because it stays soft and chemically inert in contact with the acid electrolyte for decades. Silicone is a siloxane polymer built on a quartz-derived silicon-oxygen backbone."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.winding" [label="Winding on a mandrel and impregnation" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Anode foil, separator and cathode foil are wound together on a rotating mandrel into a compact roll. Electrolyte is then vacuum- or pressure-impregnated into the wound roll, filling every pore and pushing out air, which is what finally gives the part its capacitance."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.winding.mandrel" [label="Winding mandrel" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A polished steel mandrel, often Teflon-coated, on which the foil sandwich is wound. Mandrel diameter and surface finish are set by the smallest foil thickness so the finished pack can be released without creasing."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.separator" [label="Separator (paper or polyolefin)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A porous, electrolyte-wicking separator keeping the foils apart while letting current through. Bleached kraft paper was still the standard for most 1980s aluminium electrolytics; low-porosity oriented polypropylene or polyethylene sleeves were the premium, longer-life alternative."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.separator.kraft-paper" [label="Bleached kraft paper separator" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A very thin, very porous cellulose tissue wound between the foils. It must be wetted completely by the electrolyte but must not contain ions that would raise the leakage current, so it is washed and bleached before use. Kraft paper separators are why the electrolyte is held in place at all."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.electrolyte" [label="Working electrolyte" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The conducting liquid that soaks the separator and carries current between the oxide and the cathode: boric or phosphoric acid dissolved in ethylene glycol, dimethylformamide or dimethyl sulfoxide. The solvent must wet the porous separator without dissolving or attacking the oxide, and the acid conc"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.electrolyte.glycol-ethers" [label="Glycol-ether solvent from petrochemistry" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Ethylene glycol and the glycol ethers used as high-voltage capacitor solvents were derived from ethylene oxide, itself made by oxidising ethylene from a steam cracker. They replaced the glycol-ester electrolytes of the 1950s because they dissolve the anode oxide without attacking it."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.electrolyte.glycol-ethers.ethylene-oxide" [label="Ethylene glycol ethers from ethylene oxide" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Ethylene oxide is made by direct oxidation of ethylene over a silver or chromite catalyst, then reacts with an alcohol to give the glycol ether. The solvent chemistry of an aluminium electrolytic is therefore set two or three steps downstream of a steam cracker."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.electrolyte.boric-acid" [label="Boric acid in the electrolyte" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Boric acid sets the oxide chemistry of the anode and, with phosphoric acid, is the reason an aluminium electrolytic has a usable voltage range at all. Commercially it was made from borax, which is mined from evaporite deposits."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.electrolyte.boric-acid.tincalconite" [label="Borax (tincalconite) evaporite" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Sodium tetraborate decahydrate occurs in the playas and evaporite deposits of closed desert basins, where boron-bearing volcanic hot springs have fed an evaporating lake for millions of years. It is mined, not synthesised, in the borate districts."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.cathode-foil" [label="Cathode foil" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Aluminium foil, usually a cheaper alloy and often unetched, serving as the return electrode. It carries no dielectric of its own; its job is simply to collect the return current through the electrolyte."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.cathode-foil.foil-roll" [label="Cathode foil rolling" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="High-purity aluminium rolled to a few tens of microns, often lightly etched, slit into ribbons for the winding. It uses the same aluminium as the anode but a looser specification, because no dielectric quality depends on it."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.anode-foil" [label="High-purity anode foil, rolled and etched" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="99.5 %+ aluminium foil, chemically etched in a hot hydrochloric/nitric acid or chlorate bath to grow microscopic pits on the surface. Effective cathode area rises by a factor of fifty to a hundred, which is how a hundred microfarads fits into a 5 mm can."];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.anode-foil.scrap-remelt" [label="Anode foil from re-melted foil-mill scrap" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Rolled foil offcuts, edge trim and rejected capacitor-grade foil were re-smelted in a holding furnace and re-rolled into fresh anode foil rather than going back to the general scrap stream. The other half of the loop is primary metal: alumina from bauxite, smelted in a Hall-Heroult cell by dissolvin"];
  "mb.passives.caps.alu-electrolytic.anode-foil.forming" [label="Anodic formation (the real dielectric)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The etched foil is made the anode in a phosphoric or boric acid bath at 90-200 V DC, growing a barrier-type aluminium oxide layer tens of nanometres thick. The rated working voltage of the finished capacitor is just the voltage the oxide can hold, so higher ratings need a thicker oxide and hence les"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc" [label="Monolithic ceramic capacitor (MLCC)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The 0.1 uF bypass and small timing capacitors. Dozens of layers of barium-titanate ceramic with screen-printed metal electrodes buried between them, then fired and terminated. Monolithic construction gives huge capacitance in a tiny body because the electrode area is the whole face of every layer."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.terminations" [label="End terminations (nickel + solderable outer)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="After firing, the two exposed end faces are terminated with a conducting band that collects current from the hundreds of buried electrodes. In 1982 this was a fired-on or sputtered nickel layer with a solderable silver or tin outer layer, or a simple dipped solder band. The C64 boards were wave-sold"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.belt-kiln" [label="Firing in a continuous belt kiln" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The blanks go through a continuous belt or pusher kiln under a controlled low oxygen partial pressure, so the ceramic sinters dense and hermetic while the nickel internal electrodes are not oxidised away. Density matters enormously: a porous body lets moisture attack the electrode stack and destroys"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.belt-kiln.elements" [label="Kiln heating elements" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Silicon-carbide or nichrome elements in a continuous belt kiln held at ~1300 C for hours. Long soaks at high temperature are what drive the ceramic to theoretical density."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.dicing" [label="Dicing into blanks" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The laminated bar is cut with a resin-bonded diamond saw into hundreds or thousands of identical blanks, each one capacitor body. One sintered bar yields thousands of capacitors and one furnace run yields many bars, so the economics only work at that scale - which is why MLCC production had consolid"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.dicing.blade" [label="Resin-bonded diamond blade" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin, flat, run-of-the-mill diamond wheel; the same abrasive technology as the crystal grinder but applied to a sintered ceramic bar."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.stack-laminate" [label="Stacking, lamination and debinding" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The printed green sheets are stacked offset so every sheet's electrode lands alternately on the two opposed faces, then compressed in a hydraulic laminator and the organic binder burned out slowly. The result is a monolithic bar of hundreds of ceramic layers with the electrodes sealed inside."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.stack-laminate.laminator" [label="Hydraulic laminator" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A heated hydraulic press that stacks and consolidates hundreds of sheets at once, aligned by registration pins. This is the step that turns a pile of loose printed film into one solid bar."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.print-electrodes" [label="Screen-printing internal electrodes" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A metal paste is printed onto the green sheets to define the buried internal electrodes. In 1982 the commodity MLCC used a base-metal nickel paste fired in a low-pO2 atmosphere, because silver would diffuse down the grain boundaries of the BaTiO3 and wreck its insulation resistance. The older, more "];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.print-electrodes.nickel-paste" [label="Base-metal nickel paste" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The internal electrode paste: finely divided nickel in a glass frit and an organic vehicle, screen-printed onto the green sheet and co-fired with the ceramic. Nickel was chosen over silver for cost and over palladium for cost, and it is what let a 0.1 uF 50 V bypass capacitor be sold for a fraction "];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.tape-cast" [label="Tape casting to green sheets" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The slip is doctor-bladed onto a moving polyester carrier film to a controlled dry thickness. This single step sets the eventual capacitance density, because capacitance is dielectric thickness divided by the number of electrode layers stacked."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.tape-cast.doctor-blade" [label="Doctor blade" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A precisely-ground steel or ceramic blade with a controlled edge angle, set to micrometres over the belt. The single most important tool in MLCC making: it sets layer thickness and therefore capacitance density."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.tape-cast.belt" [label="Moving carrier belt" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The green sheet is carried on a continuous polyester belt and dried in a short oven as the doctor blade lays it down, so the green thickness is set by blade clearance against a moving web rather than by a single-shot cast."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.slurry" [label="Tape-casting slurry (powder + frit + binder)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The BaTiO3 powder is blended with a glass frit - an aid to sintering and to dielectric strength at the electrode interface - and an organic binder into a homogeneous, flowable slip. In 1982 this was an acrylic or PVA binder in water, or a cellulose ester in an organic solvent; fully water-based PVA "];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.slurry.glass-frit" [label="Glass frit in the slurry" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A few per cent of low-melting glass, added so that on sintering it wets the barium titanate grains, closes the porosity and gives the internal electrode a hermetic interface. Without it the MLCC is not gas-tight and its insulation resistance collapses."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder" [label="Barium titanate dielectric powder" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The heart of the MLCC: sub-micron BaTiO3 crystallites, the only powder that has a very high dielectric constant AND survives the high-temperature processing. Chemistry is shifted from pure BaTiO3 by dopants to move the Curie point below room temperature, giving a Class II (X7R/Z5U) part with high ca"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder.doping" [label="Donor / acceptor doping" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Trivalent donor dopants (La2O3, Dy2O3, Y2O3) or pentavalent acceptors (Nb2O5, Ta2O5, Sb2O5) are added during calcination. Donors leave the ceramic 'depleted' so the grains sit uniformly in the paraelectric phase at room temperature, which gives the stable, moderate-loss X7R curve. Acceptor dopants r"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder.doping.dopant-oxides" [label="Rare-earth dopant oxides" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Lanthanum, dysprosium and yttrium oxides, ground and sieved to sub-micron, are the donor dopants that set the temperature coefficient of an X7R dielectric. They are rare-earth minerals mined and separated in small tonnages, and their price was a visible line item in MLCC costing."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder.doping.dopant-oxides.bastnaesite" [label="Rare-earth concentrates (bastnaesite, monazite)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Rare-earth minerals are worked chiefly from carbonatite and monazite/bastnaesite concentrates, with the world supply dominated by one mining complex and its associated separation plants. The oxides are then produced by acid leach and solvent extraction."];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder.synthesis" [label="Solid-state BaTiO3 synthesis" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Barium carbonate and titanium dioxide are ball-milled with the dopants in water to a homogeneous slurry, then calcined in a kiln at about 1050-1200 C. The carbonate decomposes and the perovskite BaTiO3 crystallises. The mass is reground and wet-milled with a dispersant down to the target particle si"];
  "mb.passives.caps.mlcc.powder.synthesis.titanium-dioxide" [label="Titanium dioxide (TiO2) feed" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Ceramic-grade TiO2, made by chlorinating ilmenite or rutile to TiCl4 and re-oxidising, or by sulphuric acid digestion. Low-iron pigment-grade material is used so the fired ceramic has good insulation resistance."];
  "mb.passives.resistors" [label="Resistors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Fixed resistors on the 250407: about seventy 1/4 W axial parts in values from 100 ohm to 1 megohm, plus four thick-film SIP resistor networks and one 500 ohm trimmer. Almost all were 5 percent carbon film; the handful of 1 percent metal film parts sat in the VIC-II colour and timing network."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers" [label="Trim and adjustable resistors" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The single adjustable part on the 250407 is R27, a 500 ohm cermet trimmer potentiometer that biases U31's crystal oscillator so the 14.31818 MHz colour subcarrier lands exactly on NTSC's subcarrier frequency. It is the reason a C64 needs no colour adjustment in normal use but will show colour shimme"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers.adjuster" [label="Adjuster screw and slider" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A knurled brass or stainless screw drives a moulded slider that rides the wiper along the track. Backing-off epoxy locks the setting once the board is aligned."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers.adjuster.lock" [label="Epoxy lock" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="After alignment a drop of epoxy is flowed over the adjuster so the setting cannot drift in service. On a machine with no calibration facility this trimmer is effectively a one-time factory adjustment for forty years. It is a deliberate, destructive lock: once the trim is set on the factory line that"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers.adjuster.screw" [label="Adjuster screw" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A brass or stainless screw, knurled or slotted, driving the slider. Brass is the usual choice because it machines cleanly at small diameters and does not gall against the moulded slider."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers.cermet-element" [label="Cermet resistive element and track" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thick-film RuO2 resistive track deposited on the top face of an alumina substrate, with a metal-foil wiper that slides along it. Because the element is a printed film rather than a carbon blob, the tracking is smooth and the part does not develop a scratchy potentiometer feel the way a carbon comp"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.trimmers.cermet-element.wiper" [label="Wiper alloy" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A nickel-alloy or precious-metal spring contact rubbing on the RuO2 film. Because the cermet element is hard, the wiper is deliberately the sacrificial element and is made of a soft, wear-resistant alloy."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks" [label="Thick-film cermet SIP resistor networks" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The four resistor arrays on the 250407: two 8-way isolated 33 ohm packs (series terminators on the DRAM data bus), one 8-way 3k3 bussed pack and one 10-way 3k3 bussed pack. Each is one alumina substrate with several ruthenium-oxide resistive tracks screen-printed and fired together."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.encapsulation" [label="Epoxy potting and marking" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The trimmed network is coated in a fire-resistant epoxy or phenolic varnish that also carries the colour code - here a small white dot or body colour denoting the resistance. Glass-filled epoxy was the standard SIP encapsulant."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.encapsulation.lead-frame" [label="Lead frame" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The array's leads are formed from a copper-alloy lead frame, plated in tin or solder, so the package can be inserted on a 2.54 mm pitch and wave-soldered."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.encapsulation.glass-epoxy" [label="Glass-filled epoxy coat" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The whole array is coated in an epoxy loaded with fine glass or silica, which both seals the tracks and gives the package its mechanical rigidity. The colour body is the colourant in that coat."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.trim" [label="Laser trimming of the network" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="An excimer or Nd:YAG laser ablates slots in each printed resistor until a closed-loop bridge calls out the right value. Bulk trimming can be 'slug' or 'v-cut' (pushing a wedge of resistive material aside) rather than ablating material away."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.trim.yag" [label="Nd:YAG laser crystal" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Neodymium:yttrium-aluminium-garnet was the standard solid-state laser for resistor trimming: a garnet crystal doped with neodymium, pumped optically and emitting at 1.06 microns. The yttrium-aluminium-garnet host is grown from alumina and yttrium oxide."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.trim.laser" [label="Laser trimming system" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A pulsed laser head over a moving substrate with an in-line ohmmeter, cutting and re-measuring thousands of times per second. Excimer lasers were used for fine-resolution cuts on thick films."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.screen-print" [label="Screen printing through a photoemulsion" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A stainless-steel mesh stretched over a frame, coated in photosensitive emulsion, contact-exposed through a film or direct-imaged artwork, developed, and then the paste squeegeed through the open areas onto the substrate. Line width is the resistance knob. Thixotropy is essential to the paste: it mu"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.screen-print.mesh" [label="Stainless steel mesh" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The printing screen is a woven stainless-steel or nylon mesh stretched over a frame and photo-imaged. Stainless was standard because it must survive repeated abrasion by the abrasive paste in the paste."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.ruthenium-paste" [label="RuO2 cermet resistive paste" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The resistive phase is sub-micron ruthenium dioxide powder blended with a lead-borosilicate glass frit and an organic vehicle (butyl acetate / terpineol) into a thixotropic paste. Its sheet resistance is a known function of powder loading, so a given RuO2 fraction sets a given ohms-per-square."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.ruthenium-paste.platinum-ore" [label="Platinum-group ore bearing ruthenium" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Ruthenium occurs almost only as a minor by-product in platinum and nickel ores (Merensky Reef, Sudbury, Norilsk) rather than in a ruthenium ore of its own. It reaches the resistor paste as ammonium ruthenium chloride after a complex refining sequence."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.ruthenium-paste.platinum-ore.reef" [label="Merensky Reef and nickel-copper sulphide ores" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Ruthenium and the other platinum-group metals have no ore of their own. They occur at a few parts per million in the Merensky Reef of the Bushveld Complex and in the Sudbury and Norilsk nickel-copper sulphide ores, so the resistor industry bought ruthenium as a refinery by-product at platinum-group "];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.ruthenium-paste.ruo2" [label="Ruthenium dioxide powder" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Anhydrous RuO2 is the only resistor phase that survives to 900 C without oxidising away; the surrounding glass is what actually carries the current. Its scarcity set the price of every cermet resistor made in the 1980s."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate" [label="Alumina substrate with printed elements" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A 0.6 mm thick 96 percent alumina bar, pre-fired and metallised. Conductive paste is printed through a photo-plated stainless screen onto the top face only, then the whole bar is fired to fuse the glass frit and mature the resistor."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate.sintering" [label="Tunnel-kiln firing of the printed bar" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A continuous belt or pusher kiln takes the printed substrate through a controlled temperature ramp so the resistors are 'aged' in the firing. Thick-film resistors are typically driven 50 percent above their rating for 15-25 minutes to burn in the grain boundaries and stabilise the value."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate.sintering.refractory" [label="Refractory kiln furniture" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Alumina setters and shelves carrying the fired substrates through the kiln, themselves made from tabular alumina. Furnace furniture consumption is a real cost line in ceramics, and it comes from the same alumina as the substrate."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate.sintering.kiln" [label="Continuous kiln" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A belt or pusher kiln with profiled hot zones, carrying the printed bar through drying, burnout, peak and controlled cooling. The cooling rate matters as much as the peak, because too fast a cool micro-cracks the glass."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate.metallisation" [label="Thick-film conductor metallisation" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A base-metal paste of finely divided silver or copper in a glass frit, screen-printed and fired at ~850 C to form the end lands, jumpers and common bus the resistive tracks connect to. Silver-palladium pastes were common in this era; copper pastes needed a nitrogen atmosphere to avoid oxidising."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.cermet-networks.substrate.metallisation.silver-powder" [label="Atomised silver powder" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The conductor paste is fine silver flake or powder, not a bulk metal: it has to print as a paste and then fuse into a continuous conductor at 850 C without melting. Domestically mined silver was almost entirely gone by 1982; the metal was bought on the London spot market."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial" [label="Discrete axial resistor families" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Three manufacturing routes to a 1/4 W axial resistor coexisted in 1982. The 250407 used overwhelmingly carbon film; carbon composition was still common in cheaper consumer goods; metal film was reserved for precision."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-foil" [label="Metal-foil precision resistor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="An ultra-precise resistor built from rolled, etched metal foil - nickel, a nickel-chromium-iron alloy, or copper - clamped between a rigid steel/ceramic case and a polished die. Changing the roll geometry stretches the foil, so the resistance can be set with a screw to one part in a million."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-foil.case-tension" [label="Case, die and tension screw" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The foil roll is sandwiched between a hardened case and a polished die with a slight bow. Turning the adjustment screw changes the roll's circumference, stretching the foil and raising the resistance by a controlled fraction of a part per million."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-foil.foil-roll" [label="Etched metal foil roll" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A strip of pure or alloy foil is rolled to a very tight radius on a mandrel and acid-etched to remove most of it, leaving fine islands of metal in series across the roll. The number of islands, not the sheet resistivity, sets the resistance."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-foil.foil-roll.foil-metal" [label="Foil metal" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Pure nickel or a nickel-iron-chromium alloy, chosen for a low temperature coefficient of resistivity and for its mechanical stability under tension."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-foil.foil-roll.etch" [label="Selective acid etch" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The rolled foil is immersed in a ferric-nitrate or nitric-acid etch that removes most of it, leaving a fine grid of metal islands. Resistance is then set by the number of islands in the path, which is why foil resistors have their characteristic very high, very stable resistance values."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound" [label="Wirewound power resistor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Resistance wire wound on a ceramic bobbin and vitreously enamelled. Used for the few watts-and-above duties - notably the 7805 regulator's dropping resistor and the video level network. Accurate, stable, but large and inductive."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.alloy" [label="Resistance alloy wire (NiCr / FeCr / CuNi)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Nichrome has the highest resistivity of the common resistance alloys and so minimises the wire length needed; constantan (CuNi) is used where a lower temperature coefficient matters more."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.enamel" [label="Vitreous enamel coating" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The wound wire is coated in a glassy enamel and baked, which is why wirewounds are glassy-bodied. Continuous operation above about 200 C needs cement or silicone instead."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.enamel.glass" [label="Vitreous enamel" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A glassy coating on the wound wire, applied as a slurry and baked. The enamel is what makes a wirewound part look like a glass-bodied component and is what lets it run hot without the wire oxidising."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.bobbin" [label="Ceramic bobbin" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A grooved steatite or alumina former on which a length of enamelled resistance alloy wire is wound. Steatite (magnesium silicate) was the classic low-cost bobbin material."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.bobbin.steatite" [label="Steatite bobbin material" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Steatite, a compacted and fired magnesium silicate, was the classic low-cost bobbin and ceramic insulator material: it machines green, fires to a hard white body and is cheap."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.wirewound.bobbin.steatite.talc" [label="Talc and soapstone" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Steatite is a mixture of talc with clay. Talc is mined from metamorphosed dolomitic rock and is one of the few minerals still worked in a form close to its natural state."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film" [label="Metal film resistor (1 % precision)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A NiCr or FeCr film vacuum-deposited onto an alumina substrate, then laser-trimmed to a tight tolerance. Where the VIC-II colour burst and the composite video level had to be set to a few parts in a thousand, a 1 percent metal film part was used instead of a 5 percent carbon film."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.termination" [label="Termination cap and lead" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A crimped steel or aluminium cap grips the lead and makes contact with the film, then the whole assembly is plated in nickel. Metal film parts are always nickel-plated leads, never bare tin."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.termination.cap" [label="Termination cap material" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A nickel-plated steel or aluminium cap, crimped onto the lead and forming the low-resistance joint to the film. Metal film parts are always nickel-plated leads: the plating has to be uniform for the part to be consistent."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.laser-trim" [label="Laser scribing of the spiral" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A pulsed ruby or Nd:YAG laser ablates a thin groove across the film, lengthening the resistive path. A closed-loop measuring bridge runs thousands of cuts per second until the value converges. This - not a mechanical wheel - is what buys the 0.1-1 percent tolerance."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.vacuum-deposition" [label="Vacuum deposition bell" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Parts are racked in a bell jar under vacuum; NiCr is evaporated at high current and condenses as a sub-micron film over the resistive area only, defined by an evaporated aluminium mask. Vacuum deposited - not sputtered - was standard for axial resistors in 1982."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.nichrome" [label="NiCr resistance alloy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="80/20 nickel-chromium, or 75/25 iron-chromium, evaporated or sputtered from a heated wire or boat in a vacuum bell. NiCr is chosen for its bulk resistivity (~1.1 uohm-m) and, critically, for having a near-zero temperature coefficient of resistivity."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.substrate" [label="Flat ceramic substrate (not a tube)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Metal film parts use a flat alumina bar or a flat ceramic tube rather than a tube-with-a-skin, because the uniform flat substrate is what makes laser scribing produce a stable, precise spiral."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.metal-film.substrate.ceramic" [label="Metal-film substrate material" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Metal film resistors need a very flat and very uniform substrate because the film resistance is set by the length of the deposited track, not by the substrate geometry. Alumina ceramic bar or a flat ceramic tube is used for that flatness."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition" [label="Carbon composition resistor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A rod of carbon and clay, compression-moulded into a small cylinder with metal end caps. Very cheap and very noisy, with a wide temperature coefficient and drift. Still used in 1982 in low-cost consumer goods and audio gear, but on the C64 itself it was already being displaced by carbon film."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition.end-cap" [label="Swaged metal end caps and wire leads" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Soft tin or brass caps are pressed over the moulded slug, then a tinned copper wire lead is swaged inside the cap, giving the familiar bulbous ends of a composition resistor."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition.moulding" [label="Compression moulding" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The mix is pressed in a matched-metal die at tens of tonnes to fill a slug shaped like a small pill, then baked. End caps of tin or brass are pressed on after cure."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition.moulding.die" [label="Matched metal die" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A matched-metal compression die with a cavity shaped like the finished slug. The cavity is chrome plated to survive the abrasive mix and the pressure."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition.powder" [label="Composition mix (graphite + clay + binder)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A stiffer, higher-carbon paste than the film slurry. Because it is bulk rather than surface-conductive, the resistance is set by the geometry of the moulded rod itself, not by a pattern on its skin."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-composition.powder.diluent" [label="Diluent clay in the composition mix" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A much higher clay fraction than in the film slurry, because a composition resistor has to be a weak conductor in bulk. Kaolin was the traditional diluent, the same mineral as in the film mix."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film" [label="Carbon film resistor (1/4 W axial)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The bulk of the board's resistors. A thin, discontinuous film of carbon and clay laid on a hollow alumina ceramic tube; carbon loading sets the resistance. Cheap, 5 percent, and tolerant of being bent during manual insertion."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.colour-code" [label="Offset-printed colour-code bands" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The value and tolerance rings are printed on the epoxy body by an offset (rotogravure / dry-offset) coater using inks fired onto the surface. Three bands give significant figures, one multiplier, one tolerance. A 5 percent part gets a gold band; 1 percent gets brown."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.colour-code.ink" [label="Band ink" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Offset printing inks cured onto the epoxy body. The ink is a pigment in an oil and resin vehicle fired at a few hundred degrees so it will not rub off or solvent-attack during board cleaning."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.package" [label="Moulded epoxy body" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The trimmed resistor is transfer-moulded in epoxy, or coated in a phenolic shell, sealing the film from humidity. Moulding compound is a phenolic or epoxy resin filled with silica flour."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.package.moulding" [label="Epoxy moulding of the resistor body" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The resistor is placed in a transfer tool and encapsulated in epoxy resin cured at ~150-175 C for several minutes. Epoxy is chosen because it shrinks little and does not stress the film on cooling."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.trim" [label="Value trimming (abrasive wheel)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A diamond wheel cuts a narrow slot through the film from the end of the tube, lengthening the resistive path a few micrometres at a time until the measured resistance hits the middle of the tolerance band. This is why carbon film resistors are called 'film' and why their tolerance is fixed after fir"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.trim.wheel" [label="Abrasive cut-off wheel" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin abrasive wheel, run at tens of thousands of revolutions per minute, cuts a slot through the film. Each pass lengthens the resistive path slightly; a few passes reach value."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.trim.wheel.abrasive" [label="Aluminium oxide abrasive" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The standard grinding abrasive of the era, made by calcining alumina. Synthetic diamond was used only where the cut had to be finer."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.termination" [label="Axial lead termination (electroplate)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A lead is pushed down each end of the tube through the carbon film, and the assembly is barrel-plated in an electroless or electrolytic nickel bath so the film and lead make a low-resistance, solderable joint. In 1982 this was almost always nickel, sometimes with a thin silver flash."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.termination.lead-wire" [label="Tinned copper lead wire" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The axial lead is drawn copper wire, cut to length, tinned in a molten tin or tin-lead pot so the resistor dips cleanly in the wave-solder bath. Tinning is what stops the bare copper wire from oxidising between the barrel plating step and the board."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.termination.nickel-plating" [label="Electrolytic nickel plating bath" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The lead and film are plated in a sulfamate or a Watts-type nickel bath. A sulfonamide-derived depolarising agent suppresses hydrogen evolution, so the deposit is ductile and the lead does not become brittle - which matters because a brittle lead snaps inside the resistor."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.termination.electroplating" [label="Electroplating line (sulfamate / alkaline nickel)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Racks of resistors are plated in a barrel or belt line, then rinsed and dried. Plate thickness is the mechanical anchor that stops the lead being pulled out."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.film-deposit" [label="Film deposition on the tube" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The carbon-clay slurry is sprayed or dipped onto the rotating tube in a controlled spiral so the film lands only over the intended resistance length. Patterning the film length, not its thickness, is the primary way the value is dialled in."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.substrate" [label="Ceramic tube substrate" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A thin-wall extruded alumina tube, 0.5 mm outside diameter, whose wall carries the film. Hollow construction halves the ceramic mass per resistor, and the tube's inner bore is what the trimmer's abrasive cut reaches."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.substrate.extrusion" [label="Tube extrusion and firing" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Plasticised alumina is extruded through a die to a tube, cut to length on a flying saw, and fired in a tunnel kiln. Density is controlled so the film sees a reproducible surface."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder" [label="Resistor slurry (carbon + clay + binder)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Carbon black mixed with china clay as a diluent and a phenolic binder in alcohol to a printable paste. Clay fraction is the coarse knob on resistance; binder keeps the film from flaking off the tube."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder.phenolic-resin" [label="Phenolic binder resin in the slurry" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Phenol-formaldehyde, dissolved in alcohol, holds carbon and clay to the tube and survives the 260 C cure. Cured phenolic also serves as the resistor's opaque body coating, which protects the film from humidity."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder.kaolin" [label="Kaolin (china clay) diluent" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Hydrated aluminium silicate, crushed and classified to a fine particle size. It is inert and non-conducting, so raising its share raises the sheet resistance of the dried film."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder.carbon-black" [label="Carbon black (channel/process black)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Furnace black made by feeding heavy residual oil (or natural gas) into a hot air-cooled furnace so that it impinges and soots on water-cooled steel. Furnace black from petroleum feedstock was the standard conductive phase in resistors by the 1980s."];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder.carbon-black.residuum" [label="Petroleum residuum (heavy oil feed)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The heaviest fraction of an atmospheric or vacuum refinery distillation: a viscous, bituminous, aromatic-rich residue. It was the classical feedstock for furnace black, but from the 1950s onwards natural gas was the cheaper and cleaner alternative, and a carbon-black plant of 1982 could run on eithe"];
  "mb.passives.resistors.discrete-axial.carbon-film.powder.carbon-black.furnace" [label="Carbon black furnace (soot reactor)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A refractory-lined combustion chamber with a ~1500 C flame and water-cooled steel scraper blades. Feed oil is sprayed across the flame; the resulting soot is scraped off as a mat and pelletised."];
  "mb.rf-modulator" [label="Commodore RF modulator module (VHF/UHF TV)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The box on the C64 mainboard that turns the VIC-II's separate luma and colour outputs plus the SID's audio into one RF carrier for a television's antenna socket. Commodore published it as schematic 251025 and 251696: a handful of Japanese small-signal transistors, two miniature RF transformers, one "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.quality.composite-chain" [label="Two composite paths in one module" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Because the module has to keep working as a monitor passthrough, it carries two paths: the luma and colour signals are buffered straight through to CN5 so a monitor still gets an unmodified signal, while a separate summed composite is what drives the RF section. This is why S-Video from a breadbin i"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.quality.composite-chain.ntsc-vs-pal" [label="NTSC and PAL variants" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The earlier 5-pin mainboard mixed sync, luminance and colour into composite on the board itself and fed that to the modulator, whereas the 8-pin boards pass luma and colour separately so the modulator can also provide S-Video. That difference is why the very first modulators are simpler than the lat"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.assembly" [label="Modulator manufacture, alignment and test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A 1980s RF module was assembled by hand through a small number of components, soldered, then aligned: the channel trimmer is set against a television signal generator or a spectrum analyser, the intercarrier selector link is fitted for the market, the level network is checked on a video monitor, and"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.quality" [label="Why the RF picture was poor" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The modulator is not the only reason the C64's television picture was soft and cross-talk-ridden, but it removes the last chance to recover quality: the VIC-II's own composite output already mixes luminance and chroma imperfectly, the television's 6 MHz vision channel is a narrow band-pass, the soun"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.variants" [label="Modulator part numbers and revisions" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Commodore changed the modulator repeatedly and warned in the service manual that most modulators are different and the parts are not interchangeable between mainboards. Documented identifiers include 250469 (the short-board C64C), 326130-01, 51A1-10 Rev 2, and Japanese Mitsumi-built modules MD6-UE36"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.variants.toad" [label="The 'toad' module nickname" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Early Commodore modulators are sometimes described by hobbyists as a toad or a blob: the epoxy-covered module sits on the mainboard with its lead frame splayed out, and from the top it does look like an animal. The nickname is not a Commodore part name - it exists only in the collector community."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.variants.mitsumi" [label="Mitsumi-built modules" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Mitsumi Electric was a major Japanese supplier of consumer-electronics subassemblies and shipped RF modulator modules to Commodore. The MD6 prefix denotes the module family; the suffix distinguishes NTSC from PAL and the electrolytic set."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.variants.mitsumi.contrast" [label="The 1984 C64C video path" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The later C64 boards do not simply carry a better modulator - they changed where the signal is encoded. A dedicated video encoder IC generates the composite and separate luma/chroma outputs from the VIC-II's signals, so the video is encoded once, to a defined standard, outside the modulator; the RF "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.variants.mitsumi.contrast.encoder-ic" [label="Video encoder integrated circuit" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A mask-programmed device that takes the VIC-II's luminance and chrominance plus the synchronisation inputs and generates composite video and separate luma and chrominance outputs internally, using on-chip resistor-string DACs for the levels. Building the encoder alongside the VIC-II was what made th"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module" [label="Illustrative hybrid module construction (not Commodore)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="ILLUSTRATIVE ONLY - this is not how any Commodore RF modulator was built. Both Commodore drawings that can be read (schematic 251025 and 251696) show a discrete through-hole board with Japanese small-signal transistors, two miniature RF transformers, a trimmer and about thirty resistors and capacito"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.alignment" [label="Substrate alignment and RF screening" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="After assembly the module is screened: an RF sweep is injected at the output terminal and the response curve is checked against a mask. A damaged substrate, a lifted chip termination or a cracked lead frame shows up as a notch in the response, which is why a hybrid modulator's yield is dominated by "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.chip-passives" [label="Chip inductor and chip capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="In a hybrid RF module the high-value capacitors and the low-value inductors are not screen-printed but chip-bonded: a fired ceramic chip capacitor with metallised terminations is reflowed onto the substrate, and an inductor is a tiny spiral of metallised polyimide on a ceramic chip. The polyimide is"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.chip-passives.laser-trim" [label="Laser trimming of the film resistors" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A laser is pulsed at a deposited resistor while a bridge measures its value, cutting a slot in the film a few microns wide to raise the resistance by a controlled amount. Each pulse changes the value by a known increment and the bridge closes the loop, so a resistor can be trimmed to 0.1% or better "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.assembly" [label="Hybrid module assembly and transfer moulding" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The die or chip components are bonded to the substrate, the lead frame is attached, and the whole assembly is transfer moulded in epoxy: a silicone-rubber mould loaded with epoxy powder is vibrated to pack it around the part, the encapsulant is pressed in, and it is cured at 150-175 C. This is the s"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.assembly.lead-bond" [label="Attaching the substrate to the lead frame" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The substrate is aligned to the lead frame to within a few microns, given a thin coat of conductive epoxy - silver-filled epoxy is the usual choice because it cures at only 150-200 C - or a screen-printed solder paste is printed and reflowed, then the assembly is baked so the die attaches without st"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.assembly.lead-bond.epoxy" [label="Silver-filled conductive epoxy" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Epoxy resin loaded with 50-80% silver flakes. The flakes touch each other to form a percolating conductor while the epoxy gives mechanical strength and cures at a temperature the chip and substrate tolerate. Bulk resistivity is about 0.001-0.01 ohm.cm, two to three orders of magnitude worse than a m"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.lead-frame" [label="Gold-plated DIP lead frame" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The substrate is attached to a lead frame by reflow or conductive epoxy, and the frame's pins are plated. Gold is used where the RF signal leaves the module, because a nickel or tin surface has poor conductivity at 60 MHz and tarnishes; in cheap modules only the signal pins are gold and the supply p"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.epoxy-encapsulant" [label="Epoxy encapsulant as RF seal" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A clear or opaque filled epoxy poured or transfer moulded over the finished substrate. In RF modules it does three jobs at once: it excludes moisture and contaminants, it holds the components against vibration, and - because it is a low-loss dielectric with a stable dielectric constant - it fixes th"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.thin-film-resistors" [label="Thin-film and thick-film resistor networks" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A film of nickel-chromium is deposited by vacuum evaporation or sputtering onto the alumina, then patterned by photolithography and etching. Because the sheet resistance of the film is very uniform the as-deposited value is already within a few percent, so a trim brings it to its exact value."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.alumina-substrate" [label="Alumina substrate and its ceramic processing" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A thin, smooth, thermally conductive ceramic sheet. Alumina is made by calcining purified aluminium hydroxide, pressing or tape-casting it with an organic binder, sintering at 1500-1700 C, then lapping and laser-cutting the finished sheets to thickness. It is inert, dimensionally stable and easy to "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.alumina-substrate.sintering-aid" [label="Magnesium oxide sintering aid" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A few tenths of a percent of magnesium oxide is added to 96% alumina before sintering to stop the ceramic over-shrinking and to fire it at a lower temperature, which is what makes 96% alumina affordable for a hybrid substrate."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.hybrid-module.alumina-substrate.sintering-aid.magnesite" [label="Magnesite ore" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A magnesium carbonate rock mined in Austria, Spain, China and India, calcined at 1400-1700 C to magnesia. In the 1980s most ceramic-grade magnesia was calcined from seawater or from brine, but the rock remains the classic source."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.supply" [label="Modulator supply and zener stabiliser" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The modulator runs from the C64's ~9 V unregulated DC rail - the same rail that feeds the cassette motor - not from the clean 5 V. Q6 (2SC2120) buffers the feed and a zener with electrolytics sets the working voltage for the analog sections."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.supply.zener" [label="8.2 V Zener diode" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A heavily doped PN junction reverse-biased into breakdown. The doping is so heavy that the depletion layer is only a few microns wide, which gives the device a very low capacitance and a fast, hard breakdown - ideal for a shunt regulator and for clamping."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-output" [label="RF output network and 75 ohm match" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The picture carrier leaves the tank through a tap, is coupled out through a series capacitor, and is matched to the 75 ohm coax of the television's antenna input by a resistive network with a ferrite bead in the supply feed. Getting the match right is what stops the RF from reflecting back into the "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator.channel-selection.uhf-output" [label="PAL UHF output stage" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="At 591 MHz a single transistor oscillator cannot drive a 75 ohm television input through a long lead, so the PAL modulators add a small tuned output stage and, in the later units, an output amplifier with its own bias network. The extra gain is what makes a PAL C64 usable on a European television wi"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-output.rca-jack" [label="RCA phono socket" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The US-style phono connector that carries the composite antenna signal. The centre pin is often nickel- or gold-plated brass and the outer shell is a turned brass or plated-steel barrel crimped into the body; the body is moulded in a glass-filled thermoplastic."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.audio-chain" [label="Audio intercarrier path" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A separate 4.5 MHz (NTSC) or 5.5 MHz (PAL) oscillator whose amplitude is modulated by the SID's audio output, then added to the picture carrier. Terminal 8 selects between the two: Commodore's drawing notes that terminal 8 shorted to ground is PAL and open is NTSC PAL/US."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.audio-chain.intercarrier-selector" [label="Intercarrier selector terminal" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A single link or jumper on terminal 8 that reconfigures the intercarrier oscillator between 4.5 and 5.5 MHz. It is the PAL/NTSC configuration link for the whole module, and it is why one board could serve both markets with a single part change."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.audio-chain.fm-modulator" [label="FM modulator network" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The audio signal is fed to the intercarrier oscillator's base through a series resistor and a coupling capacitor, so the oscillator's amplitude follows the audio envelope. A varactor or a reactance modulator would be the higher-fidelity route; amplitude modulation is cheap and adequate because the t"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.audio-chain.fm-modulator.audio-amplifier" [label="SID audio amplifier transistor" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="On the C64 mainboard the SID's pin 27 output is amplified by Q2 before it reaches CN5 pin 3 and the modulator; the modulator's audio input then loads that amplifier. The stage is a common-emitter NPN with a bias divider and an emitter resistor, running from the CAN +5 V rail."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator" [label="Picture carrier oscillator" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A Clapp or Colpitts oscillator built around Q4 (2SC1730, a VHF transistor with an f_T above 300 MHz) and a miniature RF transformer, with a capacitive divider setting the feedback ratio. Composite video is injected into the oscillator's base network, so the video signal frequency-modulates the carri"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator.varactor" [label="MA57 tuning varactor diode" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="On the earlier 251696 drawing the tuning is done by D3, an MA57: a junction varactor whose capacitance falls as the reverse bias rises. Lifting the tuning out of the tank into a varactor keeps the temperature coefficient and the stray capacitance low. Later drawings use a mechanical trimmer in the s"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator.channel-trimmer" [label="Channel trimmer capacitor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The channel tuning element brought out to terminal 10 of the module: a small screw-adjusted trimmer in the 1-10 pF range, most often a ceramic plate or disc trimmer with a barium titanate or glass-ceramic dielectric, sometimes a stacked mica trimmer. It sits across the tank and is set once, at the t"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator.channel-selection" [label="Channel and region switching" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Three different ways of reaching the right channel appear across the revisions. The US machines use a two-position H-L switch on the C64's own rear panel, wired to change a component value in the oscillator tank or level network; the switch is a low-voltage contact on the mainboard, not the brick's "];
  "mb.rf-modulator.rf-oscillator.rf-transistor" [label="2SC1730 / 2SC2778 VHF transistor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A VHF small-signal NPN in TO-92, chosen because its unity-gain bandwidth is several times the 60 MHz operating frequency so the oscillator starts easily and stays linear, and because its output capacitance is low enough not to detune the tank."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.video-chain" [label="Video amplifier and clip network" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Three small-signal transistors that take the VIC-II's pin 15 (sync + luminance) and pin 14 (colour), clip them with back-to-back diodes, and provide the two separate buffered outputs that go to the monitor connector as S-Video plus the summed composite that drives the RF section. Commodore called Q1"];
  "mb.rf-modulator.video-chain.clip-diodes" [label="1SS119 / MA151K clip diodes" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Small-signal switching diodes, typically 50 mA class in a DO-35 glass can, wired back to back across the sync input. They conduct only on the tips of the sync pulses and the colour burst, which clips the signal to the amplitude a television's AGC expects and stops the modulator over-modulating."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.video-chain.ssc-transistor" [label="2SC1694 small-signal NPN" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A TO-92 epoxy-potted silicon NPN with a 100-150 V collector rating, used as a common-emitter amplifier with a collector resistor and an unbypassed emitter resistor. The 2SC-prefix parts were the standard Japanese equivalents of the 2N series, and Commodore specified them as 'or equivalent'."];
  "mb.rf-modulator.video-chain.ssc-transistor.packaging" [label="Wafer processing and TO-92 moulding" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The die is a small epitaxial NPN on a 100 mm P-type \<111\> wafer, sawn with a diamond blade, die-attached with a silver-glass preform, wire-bonded with 50 um aluminium and transfer-moulded in an epoxy-filled TO-92 tool at 150-175 C."];
  "mb.logic" [label="74-series glue logic" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The off-the-shelf logic chips on a Commodore 64 ASSY 250407 that are not Commodore's own designs - thirteen positions according to the service-manual parts list, plus an LM556 timer alongside them. They do no arithmetic and no video: they multiplex addresses, latch bytes, decode chip selects, count,"];
  "mb.logic.economics" [label="What a 74LS is made of, and what it cost" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The whole point of the comparison. A 74LS chip is a few milligrams of silicon, a few milligrams of metal, a third of a gram of epoxy, and a couple of hundred person-minutes of work spread across a fortnight of factory flow time. It sold for a few tens of cents. The 6510 on the same board, a thousand"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing" [label="Merchant logic supply to Commodore" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Commodore did not fab logic. It bought merchant 74-series parts from the American and Japanese vendors, and because a machine built in millions cannot wait for a part number to be on allocation, it also ran second sources of its own - internal house-numbered equivalents made at other plants, marked "];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.distribution" [label="How the chips physically arrived in 1983" fillcolor="#9aa5ce22" color="#9aa5ce" tooltip="Nobody at Commodore's Pennsylvania assembly plant picked up a telephone and ordered from Texas Instruments. There was a distribution chain, and where a part sat in that chain decided both its price and whether it was genuine. Buying from the manufacturer's own sales arm or a franchised distributor g"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.second-source" [label="Commodore's house-numbered second sources" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="To avoid single-supplier risk Commodore had equivalents of several glue parts made for it and marked with its own house numbers: 7701 and 8701 in place of the PLA family, 7707 for the 7406 hex inverter, 7708 or 7711 for the 74LS139 decoder, and 7712 for the 74LS08 quad AND. These were contracted fro"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers" [label="The merchant logic vendors" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="Five or six companies made the overwhelming majority of the world's 74-series logic in the early 1980s. Between them they ran the same public process to the same design rules, which is why a 74LS08 from any of them was interchangeable. What they did not share was quality: this is the core of the C64"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.japan" [label="Hitachi, Toshiba and the Japanese suppliers" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="By the mid-1980s Japanese and then Korean vendors had taken a large share of world logic volume, and they were undercutting American silicon hard. C64 boards are also found with Hitachi-branded parts. Commodore bought early and often, and the rising Japanese share was one reason the C64's own bill o"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.signetics" [label="Signetics" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="An early Silicon Valley logic house that second-sourced the 74 series through the 1960s and 1970s, and that had been owned by Philips since 1975. It is not attested by name on surviving ASSY 250407 boards. It appears in this branch only because it shared a licensing and cross-supply arrangement with"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.motorola" [label="Motorola" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The dominant supplier of the 4000-series CMOS parts on this board: the MC4044 phase comparator at U32 and the two MC4066 bilateral switches. The MC prefix on those parts is Motorola's, which is why the C64's glue logic is not entirely a 74-series story. Motorola also ran one of the merchant 74LS lin"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.national" [label="National Semiconductor" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A major second-source supplier of 74LS logic throughout the period, and the company that published the 74HC/74HCT databooks that turned high-speed CMOS into a genuine 74-series replacement. It later absorbed much of Fairchild."];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.fairchild" [label="Fairchild" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The company where Faggin invented the silicon-gate technology that made CMOS practical, in 1968, and which shipped the first commercial silicon-gate MOS ICs. Fairchild went bankrupt in 1985 and its remaining operations were absorbed by National and by a French group, so by 1983 a Fairchild-branded 7"];
  "mb.logic.sourcing.makers.ti" [label="Texas Instruments" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The company that invented the 7400 series in 1964 and the Schottky TTL in 1964-65, and the largest supplier of the 74LS catalogue. It ran its own fabs (among them Dallas, Sugar Land in Texas, and Niceville in Florida) and also did foundry work for others - the fab-forum business was a real cash gene"];
  "mb.logic.cmos" [label="The CMOS half: 4066, MC4044, 74LS629" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Three of the C64's glue chips are CMOS, and they were made on a completely different line from the 74LS parts. Instead of four diffusion cycles and an epitaxial collector, a CMOS line does a p-well and an n-well implant, grows a field oxide between them by LOCOS, grows a hundred-nanometre gate oxide"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.now" [label="What CMOS became" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The same n-well/p-well/twin-tub idea, shrunk by a factor of thousands. Every design rule changed. The bird's beak - the way LOCOS field oxide grew sideways and swallowed the active area - became a scaling killer, and shallow trench isolation, in which a narrow trench is etched into the substrate and"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.switch" [label="CMOS transmission gate (bilateral switch)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 4066's four bilateral switches are the canonical CMOS cell: two MOSFETs of opposite polarity in parallel between two nodes, each with its gate driven by one of the two control inputs. When the control is high the pair conducts in both directions; when it is low the pair is off and the terminals "];
  "mb.logic.cmos.litho" [label="The photolithography is the same, but the resist is positive" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A CMOS line of 1983 reused the same mask aligners, coaters and developers as the bipolar line next door. The one substantive difference is the resist: by the end of the 1970s the NMOS and CMOS lines had already switched to positive resists such as Shipley's AZ-1350, while bipolar logic was still oft"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process" [label="The twin-tub CMOS flow" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The order of a CMOS process is dictated by one rule: you can never put anything back into silicon after a high-temperature step, so everything that must survive later furnace work is done first, in descending order of temperature. Wells, then field oxide, then thin gate oxide, then polysilicon, then"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.passivation" [label="Passivation, and why CMOS needed less of it" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="An interlayer oxide and a final nitride cap, opened only at the pads and scribe lanes. A CMOS device is far less sensitive to mobile ions than a bipolar one, because a CMOS gate draws no gate current and its threshold is set electrostatically rather than by charge state; so the passivation job is ea"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.contact" [label="Contact and aluminium interconnect" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The field oxide is etched away over the source, drain and gate to expose silicon, then aluminium is deposited and patterned into one single layer of interconnect. Exactly the same metallisation as the bipolar line, exactly the same problems (step coverage over the oxide steps, electromigration under"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.metal" [label="Aluminium metallisation" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The final mask. A layer of aluminium about 1 micrometre thick is deposited over the whole wafer - sputtered or evaporated - and a mask removes it everywhere except the wires. Those wires connect the transistor terminals, the resistors, and the bonding pads around the edge of the die. One single meta"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.sd" [label="The self-aligned source and drain" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="With the gate already patterned in polysilicon, the source and drain are implanted straight through the exposed silicon beside it, and the gate itself blocks the implant everywhere it is. No alignment mask is needed: the gate defines the gap between source and drain by its own edge. Two implants, ph"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.emitter" [label="The emitter diffusion - and the gold in it" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The third diffusion. Through a finer mask, a very heavily doped n+ region is driven into the boron base, forming the emitter. Because the n+ emitter is so much more heavily doped than the p base, the emitter-base junction is forward-biased at only 0.7 V and injects enormous current gain - but only a"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.emitter.gold" [label="The gold in the emitter" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A fraction of a percent of gold, introduced by co-doping the emitter source and driven in with the emitter diffusion. Gold is a deep acceptor-diffusing substitutional impurity in silicon; it behaves in the base like a recombination centre. In the emitter-base junction it forward-shifts the junction "];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.sd.anneal" [label="The source/drain anneal" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="After the implant the wafer goes back into a furnace briefly to heal the crystal damage and to activate the dopant atoms into substitutional positions. This is the one remaining high-temperature step, and it is why CMOS still used a furnace in the era when bipolar was moving to implantation."];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.poly" [label="The polysilicon gate" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A layer of polycrystalline silicon is deposited over the whole wafer, then doped (usually with phosphorus from phosphine, or with POCL3 in a furnace) so that it conducts, then patterned. That patterned polysilicon is the gate. Crucially, it is also used as the mask for the source and drain implant i"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.gateoxide" [label="The gate oxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Inside the active windows the thin gate oxide is grown, by dry oxidation in oxygen at about 1000 C. On a 3-5 um part it is 80-120 nm thick - a few hundred atom layers. This film is the transistor: its thickness sets the threshold voltage and its capacitance sets the drive current, and it is the thin"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.locos" [label="LOCOS isolation" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Locally Oxidised Silicon. Where two transistors must not touch, the field oxide is simply grown. A layer of silicon nitride is deposited and patterned; the wafer is oxidised in steam, so oxide grows everywhere the nitride is absent; the nitride is stripped off in hot phosphoric acid, leaving a thick"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.locos.phosphoric" [label="Hot phosphoric acid" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Silicon nitride is attacked by hot concentrated phosphoric acid and by nothing else practical, so a boiling phosphoric acid bath is the standard way to remove it - which is why LOCOS needs an acid tank that no other step in the line does. The acid was made by burning phosphorus, itself from phosphat"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.locos.nitride" [label="The silicon nitride masking film" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A layer of amorphous silicon nitride, deposited by low-pressure chemical vapour deposition from silane and ammonia at about 800 C. It is used in three places in a CMOS line: as the mask that protects active areas during field oxidation, as the encapsulant under the nitride passivation at the end, an"];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.wells" [label="n-well and p-well implants" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Two masked implants. Phosphorus (or arsenic) goes into the n-well; boron into the p-well. At 3-5 um these were thermal diffusions or, increasingly, implantations - by the early 1980s medium-current implanters were displacing diffusion in CMOS, the opposite trend to bipolar, where diffusion survived "];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.wells.implanter" [label="The medium-current ion implanter" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A big steel vacuum chamber with a source, an analysing magnet, an accelerating column and a beamline down to a scan platter that holds the wafer. The dopant is ionised, selected by mass, accelerated to tens of keV, and swept across the wafer. Implanters were expensive, and CMOS was the process that "];
  "mb.logic.cmos.process.substrate" [label="p-type substrate (and the tub)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer is p-type, lightly doped. On top of it two wells are implanted: a deep n-well and a somewhat shallower p-well. The n-well holds every p-channel transistor; the p-well (or the substrate itself, on simpler parts) holds every n-channel transistor. Two tubs, two polarities, one chip - that is "];
  "si.wafer" [label="Polished 100 mm CZ silicon wafer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The starting substrate every die in the machine is cut from: a 4-inch (100 mm) Czochralski-grown, boron-doped p-type silicon wafer, 10-20 ohm-cm, mirror-polished on one face, 300-350 um thick, with a primary flat and a small secondary orientation flat. 125 mm was not standard until about 1986, and b"];
  "si.clean" [label="Wafer cleaning: Piranha, RCA SC-1 and megasonic" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="A 1982 fab cleaned more often and washed harder than a modern one, because particles rather than yield models set the pace. The sequence before a furnace step was megasonic at 1.6 MHz in deionised water, a hot Piranha of 3:1 H2SO4:H2O2 at 120 C to strip organics and metals, a dilute HF dip to remove"];
  "si.wafer.finish" [label="Lapping, chemical polish and edge rounding" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Saw-cut wafers are lapped double-sided on a rotating cast-iron plate against a conditioning plate, in a slurry of colloidal silica or alumina in water, taking them to about 300 um with several micrometres of total thickness variation left across the wafer. They are then polished chemically in hot al"];
  "si.wafer.slicing" [label="Sawing the ingot and laser-scribing the flats" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The boule is ground round, a sapphire-orientation-reference crystal is used to find the (100) direction, and the wafer flats are laser-scribed with a ruby or Nd:YAG laser before cutting. Slicing was by OD saw, with blades up to 2.2 m in diameter turning at 2000 rpm for a 0.30 mm kerf, or on more adv"];
  "si.wafer.saw" [label="Diamond-impregnated saw blade" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A thin steel or resin-bonded wheel whose rim carries a diamond grit. Slicing 100 mm silicon at two to three wafers a minute relies on that grit: 20-40 um for a roughing cut and 3-5 um for the finish cut. Resin-bonded blades were introduced in the mid 1970s and gave a thinner kerf and less bow than t"];
  "si.wafer.diamond" [label="Industrial diamond from graphite" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Saw-grade industrial diamond in 1982 was mostly synthetic: graphite pressed and sintered with a diamond-metal catalyst (Fe, Co or Ni) at 5-6 GPa and 1300-1600 C in a belt press. Natural diamond - the harder, scarcer gem and industrial grade - came from kimberlite pipes in South Africa, Botswana and "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar" [label="How a 74LS chip is actually made (bipolar TTL)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 74LS family was not built on the same kind of line as the Commodore custom chips. This is a junction-isolated, collector-diffused bipolar process: an n+ buried-and-collector structure grown epitaxially on p-type silicon, four thermal diffusion cycles, a sputtered NiCr resistor level, one level o"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.now" [label="What happened to bipolar logic afterwards" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 74LS line described here is essentially extinct as a manufacturing process. CMOS won because it drew almost no static power, scaled, and could hold a whole microprocessor on one die, which bipolar could never do economically. Bipolar survives only in niches - power regulators, high-voltage contr"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.fab" [label="The merchant logic fab that ran the process" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A 1983 merchant logic line was a long building with a linear flow: wafers came in at the front and moved one way through cleaning, oxidation and diffusion, photolithography, etch and metallisation, then out to probe, then to assembly and test at the back. Because a logic process was so repeatable, o"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.fab.utilities" [label="Fab utilities: water, power, scrubbers" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A logic fab in 1983 drew tens of megawatts and consumed tens of millions of gallons of ultrapure water a year, and every acid vapour had to be scrubbed. The ultrapure water plant - reverse osmosis then ion exchange, then a final 0.2 um filter - was as large and as capital-intensive as any single mac"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.fab.baymetal" [label="Metallisation bay" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The vacuum tool room: the sputterers that lay down the NiCr resistors and the aluminium metal, the evaporation stations, the sinter furnace, and the ion-implantation machines. Vacuum and cleanroom in one room; argon flows constantly."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.fab.baydiffusion" [label="Oxidation and diffusion bay" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The hot end of the fab: tube furnaces for oxidation, source drive-in and anneal, plus the epitaxial reactors. This bay determined the cycle time of the whole line, because a diffusion push is hours and a furnace boat takes 20-40 wafers. An operator loaded boats in and out at a slow, deliberate rhyth"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.cleanroom" [label="Cleanroom air, filtration and the wet bench" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="The whole front end runs in filtered air with positive pressure, and the wet benches - the sinks where wafers are etched and cleaned - were the dirtiest place in the fab and the reason it needed a Class 100 (ISO 5) hood over every one. HEPA-filtered air was blown down continuously; nitrogen and dry "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.cleanroom.wetbench" [label="The wet bench" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A row of acid-resistant sinks - quartz or polypropylene - where wafers are rinsed and wet-etched. Because wet chemistry releases acid mist and organic solvent vapour, every wet bench sat under a Class 100 laminar-flow hood and exhausted to a scrubbed stack. The wet bench is the reason a 1980s logic "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.cleanroom.ffu" [label="The HEPA fan filter unit" fillcolor="#565f8922" color="#565f89" tooltip="A box of pleated glass-fibre or PTFE media in a sheet-metal housing, blowing filtered air down over the tool at 20-40 air changes an hour. It is the single most numerous object in a 1980s fab - one every few square metres of ceiling - and it is why the whole building was pressurised and why the elec"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho" [label="Eight masks, eight photolithography cycles" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A full bipolar logic cycle is repeated for each of its eight mask levels, and each cycle is the same eight or nine operations: clean, dehydrate, spin-coat resist, soft-bake, align and expose, develop, rinse and dry, hard-bake, then inspect - before the wafer goes back to the furnace, the etcher or t"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.strip" [label="Stripping the resist" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="With the etch done, the resist has served its purpose and is removed. In this era, because resist was often negative and organic, a chlorinated solvent strip in a hot bath was standard - historically xylene, then trichloroethylene, boiling at 87 C. Solvent stripping leaves ionic residue, which had t"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.etch" [label="Etching the oxide window" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The patterned resist is the stencil; the wafer goes into an etchant. Buffered oxide etch - hydrofluoric acid buffered with ammonium fluoride - attacks the silicon dioxide where the resist is gone, opening the window, and stops at the silicon or at the oxide-nitride beneath. The wafer comes out with "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.develop" [label="Developing the image" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The exposed wafer is sprayed with an alkaline developer - for a positive resist, a solution of tetramethylammonium hydroxide or potassium hydroxide; for a negative resist, an organic solvent. The unexposed (positive) or exposed (negative) resist dissolves, leaving the pattern in hardened resist. The"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.align" [label="Aligning the mask to the wafer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer is dried, loaded onto a vacuum or magnetic chuck, and the mask is laid directly on top of it - contact printing - or held just above it with a controlled air gap - proximity printing - or imaged one-to-one through a lens. The machine's job is to align each feature on the mask to the featur"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.align.lamp" [label="The mercury lamp" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 1000 W high-pressure mercury arc with a quartz envelope. Its spectrum peaks at 365, 405 and 436 nm; optical filters select one line, almost always 436 nm (g-line) for the finest resolution the era could reach. Newer deep-UV lamps added 248 nm. The lamp is run at constant power because the resist's"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.align.stepper" [label="The 1:1 projection stepper" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A mask aligner that images the mask through a fixed 1:1 lens onto the wafer with a small gap, so there is no contact and no wear, and no mask-defect printing. TI's Micralign and Perkin-Elmer's units of the late 1970s and early 1980s brought this to the logic lines. It was the technology that eventua"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.align.printer" [label="The contact/proximity mask aligner" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A granite or cast base, a Chuck/Hamble wafer stage with micrometer knobs for theta and X-Y, a vacuum frame to pull the mask down onto the wafer, a mercury lamp below, and a pair of binocular microscopes to watch the alignment marks. One operator could run one or two of these, spending most of the cy"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.dehydration" [label="Dehydration bake before the resist" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Resist will not stick to a wet wafer, so before every coat the wafer goes on a hot plate or in a dehydration oven at 150-250 C to drive off the adsorbed water from the previous wet step. Skipping it causes the single most common resist defect - the film lifting off in patches during development. Eve"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.dehydration.hotplate" [label="The dehydration hot plate" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A temperature-controlled aluminium or ceramic plate the cassette sits on for a minute or two at 150-200 C. Cheap, essential, and present at every wet bench in the bay."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.coat" [label="Spinning on the resist" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer is put on a vacuum chuck in a bowl, a few millilitres of resist is dispensed, and the bowl spins at several thousand rpm, flinging the resist into a film of a chosen thickness - thicker where a thick layer is needed to etch deep, thinner where fine resolution is needed. The film is then so"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.coat.spinner" [label="The coater/developer track" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A machine with hot plates and spray-heads that automates spin-coat, soft-bake, develop, rinse and bake for a whole cassette of wafers in one pass. The track was the workhorse of the photomask bay: an operator loaded a cassette and pushed a button, and the bay's lithography throughput was set by how "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.masks" [label="The mask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A set of eight photomasks, one per level, each a square of glass about 15 cm on a side with the pattern for that level in opaque chrome. They are made once from the circuit layout, then used to print thousands of wafers. Making the mask set - patterning, mask inspection, and a few days of lead time "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.masks.writer" [label="The mask writer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A computer-controlled plotter that draws the pattern onto the chrome layer - a direct-write electron-beam or laser writer, or a mask stepper using photographic reduction for the simpler logic geometries. The mask set for one part number was the output of days of the mask shop's finest-resolution too"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.masks.reticle" [label="The reticle (1:1 mask)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A bipolar logic wafer of 1983 was almost always patterned 1:1 by contact or proximity printing, so the mask carried the pattern at full size - a 100 mm wafer printed from a 100 mm mask. Reticles in the sense of 4x or 5x reduction masks were for steppers and mostly for the finer NMOS and DRAM process"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.masks.chrome" [label="The opaque chrome layer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A layer of chromium, evaporated or sputtered onto the glass and then patterned by a mask writer. Chrome is used because it is opaque to the deep-UV and because it survives repeated contact printing without wearing. The clear areas are just bare glass, which is why a mask is easy to inspect: you look"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.litho.masks.blank" [label="The mask blank (soda-lime glass)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A plate of low-expansion borosilicate or soda-lime glass, polished flat to a few micrometres over 150 mm. Low-expansion glass (LE glass) was preferred for masks because it did not change size with temperature. Both the blank and the pattern ultimately derive from fused silica."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process" [label="The junction-isolated bipolar flow" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The sequence that turns a plain slice of silicon into a chip of interconnected bipolar transistors. Four diffusion cycles do all the work: an n+ isolation diffusion to separate each transistor into its own island; a p base diffusion under the island to form the transistor base; an n+ emitter diffusi"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.passivation" [label="Passivation over the metal" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A final film - silicon dioxide, usually with a nitride cap - deposited over the finished aluminium, opened again only at the bonding pads and along the scribe lanes. It seals the chip against moisture and mobile ions, which is what lets a 1983 logic part hold a 15-year life in a consumer television."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.sinter" [label="Sintering the metal into the contacts" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="After aluminium is deposited, the wafer is pushed briefly to 400-450 C in hydrogen or forming gas. This melts the aluminium into the contact windows, welding it to the silicon and lowering the contact resistance, and it anneals out the sputter damage in the silicon. It is a short, cheap step and it "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.contact" [label="The contact window" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="One more mask, which cuts small windows in the field oxide wherever a metal film needs to reach the silicon underneath - the collector, the base, the emitter. Each window is a hole a few micrometres across. The oxide elsewhere is untouched, and it is the oxide that keeps the aluminium from shorting "];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.nicr" [label="The NiCr thin-film resistor level" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 74LS is full of resistors - the pull-ups, the current-limiting elements, the bias network. On a junction-isolated bipolar logic line they were made as a deposited thin film of nickel-chromium alloy, sputtered onto the wafer, patterned by one extra mask, and annealed. This is the one mask level tha"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.nicr.sputter" [label="The RF/DC sputterer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A vacuum chamber pumped to a low argon pressure, a cathode holding the NiCr target, the wafer as the anode. An RF or DC glow discharge strikes the target, argon ions knock nickel and chromium atoms loose, and they deposit onto the wafer. It is a slow, line-of-sight, unidirectional process - which is"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.nicr.target" [label="The nickel-chromium sputtering target" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A block of 80/20 nickel-chromium alloy that the sputter gun erodes. High-purity nickel and chromium are the two metals that dominate this one thin film; the chromium is what gives the film its near-zero temperature coefficient, and the nickel is what makes it resistive rather than metallic."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.schottky" [label="The Schottky clamp - the S in LS" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="In standard 7400-series TTL a transistor saturates when it is driven hard: both junctions forward-biased, charge stored in the base, and the transistor slow to turn off. A 74LS puts a small Schottky diode - metal to lightly doped n-type, with no forward drop - from each transistor's base to its coll"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.base" [label="The base diffusion" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A second diffusion. Through a new mask, boron is driven into each n-island to make the p-type base of the transistor. The base is the thinnest and most tightly controlled region in the whole structure - roughly half to one micrometre deep - because the transistor's gain and its maximum speed both co"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.base.boron" [label="Boron as the p dopant" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Boron, the p-type dopant, came either as diborane gas or as a thin boron-doped oxide or boron nitride wafer, and was the standard acceptor for base diffusions throughout the era. It is an ingredient of the diffusion, so it hangs off `inputs` (chem.fab-chemicals.dopants.diborane) rather than as a chi"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.base.boron.borax" [label="Boron oxide (B2O3) solid source" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="For thermal diffusion, boron was often carried in a boron-doped oxide or a boron nitride wafer placed face-to-face with the silicon in the diffusion boat. Cheaper and easier to handle than gas. The active species is boron oxide, B2O3; the name previously used here said 'borax', which is the sodium t"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation" [label="Collector-collector isolation" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The first diffusion. Where the field oxide has windows, phosphorus is driven in to make a heavily n-doped region that runs all the way down to the p-type substrate. Everything inside a window is now n-type; the n+ walls around and between windows cut the wafer into separate n-type islands, each with"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation.trench" [label="Deep trench isolation (later, and now extinct in logic)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="For the highest-performance bipolar processes - the ones behind linear amplifiers and the last power logic - isolation was cut as a deep trench etched into the substrate and filled with oxide or polysilicon, then buried. This gives near-vertical isolation walls and far lower capacitance than the lat"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation.dielectric" [label="Dielectric isolation (Fairchild's alternative)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Instead of relying on reverse-biased isolation diodes, the islands can be sunk into a solid thermally grown oxide so each is completely surrounded by insulating oxide. The wafer is oxidised, then diffusion (or epitaxy) is done into small windows in the oxide; the oxide under the silicon is never rem"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation.guardring" [label="Guard rings around the islands" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A ring of n+ material, often tied to the most positive supply, laid around each sensitive island. It intercepts stray minority carriers and surface charge before they can reach the isolation junction, and it raises the breakdown voltage of the isolation. Guard rings are one of the oldest tricks in t"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation.guardring.how" [label="What a guard ring physically is" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Not a new material - just the same n+ diffusion, but drawn as a closed loop around the transistor instead of a small dot. The mask for the isolation diffusion draws the guard ring at the same time as the island, so it costs one more shape on one mask and no extra process step. That is the whole eleg"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.isolation.diffusion" [label="Diffusion into the window" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer is coated with a phosphorus-bearing dopant source - a spun-on emulsion or a deposited glass - then pushed into a furnace at 1000-1150 C. Heat alone moves the phosphorus atoms down into the silicon, opening up and closing the junction as it goes. Diffusion, not implantation, because diffusi"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.oxidation" [label="Growth of the field oxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Silicon dioxide grown by passing steam over the wafer at 900-1050 C. This thick oxide is the field oxide: it covers everything except the windows that are later cut for diffusion and metallisation, and it is what electrically and physically isolates one transistor island from the next, in the same w"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.oxidation.quartz" [label="Fused quartz tube and boat" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The tube the wafer sits in is fused quartz, because quartz holds up to about 1100 C and is chemically inert to the oxidising atmosphere. Quartz is itself manufactured from high-purity silicon, so the tube and the silicon it processes came from related chemistry."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.oxidation.steam" [label="Deionised process steam" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="Ultra-pure water turned to vapour in the furnace inlet. The water must be essentially free of dissolved alkali metals, because sodium diffuses fast through hot oxide and shifts a transistor's threshold. Fab water came from municipal supply through a chain of reverse osmosis and ion exchange. Steam i"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.oxidation.furnace" [label="The oxidation furnace" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A horizontal tube furnace, quartz or silicon-carbide, heated to 1000 C, with wet oxygen or steam flowing through. The wafer rides on a quartz boat pushed in and out by a loader. Oxidation is the slowest step in a bipolar line and the furnaces are the bottleneck."];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.epi" [label="Epitaxial growth of the collector" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The single most distinctive step of the bipolar process. The wafer is placed in a horizontal reactor and a few micrometres of lightly doped n-type silicon is grown on top of it in a hydrogen atmosphere at about 1100-1200 C. The growing layer inherits the crystal lattice of the wafer below, so the n-"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.epi.reactor" [label="The horizontal epitaxial reactor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A quartz or silicon-carbide tube with a graphite susceptor that is heated by a radio-frequency induction coil. Silane or trichlorosilane and hydrogen flow past; the silicon atoms deposit on the hot wafer. Inductors rotate the wafer and provide the radiant heat. Loaded by hand or by a cassette elevat"];
  "mb.logic.bipolar.process.substrate" [label="p-type starting wafer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A slice of p-type silicon, lightly doped so that it is a good insulator between transistor islands and does not leak much reverse current. Everything the tree contains - the transistors, the resistors, the isolation - sits on this one piece of crystal."];
  "mb.logic.package" [label="The black epoxy DIP" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Nearly every 74-series chip Commodore bought arrived in a black moulded dual in-line package: a lead frame carrying sixteen to twenty pins on 0.1 in centres, a piece of die glued to the frame's centre paddle, a fan of 0.5 mil gold wires, and about a third of a gram of filled epoxy poured over the wh"];
  "mb.logic.package.marking" [label="Marking the top of the package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Ink printing was still the norm in 1983: a pad or a dot-matrix printer lays the part number, the manufacturer's mark, a date code (year and week, e.g. 8341) and a lot or assembly code on the moulded surface. Laser marking was beginning to appear, mostly for military parts, and would take over within"];
  "mb.logic.package.marking.ink" [label="Marking ink" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A phenolic or epoxy ink with a pigment, laid on and baked, or cured by UV. Cheap and utterly routine - but it is why an original C64 chip is still readable today and a modern BGA is not. (Ink marking was also curable by laser ablation of the mould compound, which is the mechanism the laser marking r"];
  "mb.logic.package.ceramic" [label="The ceramic DIP (mostly not used here)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="For military and extended-temperature parts the epoxy was replaced by a package made of alumina ceramic, sealed hermetically with a glass frit or a metal lid. It could be baked out, leak-tested, and taken from -55 to +125 C without the epoxy softening. It was also more expensive and much larger, so "];
  "mb.logic.package.ceramic.seal" [label="Glass frit sealing" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A paste of powdered borosilicate glass with a small amount of binder is applied around the rim, the lid is set on it, and the assembly goes through a sealing furnace. The glass melts, wets the metallised ceramic and the metal lid, and on cooling forms a hermetic, dimensionally matched bond. The temp"];
  "mb.logic.package.ceramic.lid" [label="Kovar lid and nickel seal ring" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="For parts sealed with metal rather than glass, a lid of Kovar (iron-nickel-cobalt, chosen for its matching expansion) is brazed or welded to a nickel ring around the ceramic. Kovar is the same idea as the lead frame alloy: pick the expansion coefficient of the metal so it matches the ceramic and the"];
  "mb.logic.package.ceramic.body" [label="Alumina ceramic body" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A package shell made of 95-99 percent alumina, formed by pressing or by tape-casting a green body in a serpentine conductor pattern and firing it at 1500-1600 C. Alumina was chosen because it matches silicon and the glass seal closely in expansion, is cheap, and is a good thermal conductor compared "];
  "mb.logic.package.probe" [label="Probe test before packaging" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Die that fail are thrown away before they are worth a lead frame. The wafer is loaded onto a probe stage, a probe card with one needle per bonding pad is lowered onto it, and a tester runs every pattern and every DC parameter. Only known-good die are cut. This is why probe test and not final test is"];
  "mb.logic.package.probe.tester" [label="The logic tester" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="An automated test system running a stored functional pattern and a set of DC limit tests per device type: propagation delay, fan-out current, input current, output high and low voltage, and the truth table at the functional corners. A 74LS04 has a few hundred such tests. The tester was the expensive"];
  "mb.logic.package.probe.card" [label="The probe card" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A ceramic or printed-circuit substrate carrying one cantilever needle per bonding pad, cantilevered down from an epoxy or quartz shell so the needles survive hundreds of thousands of touches on hard silicon. Building a card for a new part number took days and cost hundreds to thousands of dollars, w"];
  "mb.logic.package.probe.prober" [label="The wafer probe station" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A heavy cast stage that steps the wafer die by die under a fixed probe card: a vacuum or hot chuck on an x-y-z stage, a microscope to line the card up, and a needle interface to the tester. An operator loads a cassette of up to 25 wafers and unloads it at the end; everything in between was automatic"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly" [label="Die attach, wire bond, moulding" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Three operations turn a loose die and a loose lead frame into a component. The die is brazed to the frame's central ledge. Twenty-ish gold wires are ball- or wedge-bonded from pads on the die to the inner ends of the pins. Then about 0.3-0.5 g of filled black epoxy is transfer-moulded over everythin"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould" [label="Transfer moulding the black epoxy body" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A slab of thermosetting epoxy loaded with 75-85 percent spherical silica is melted at 175 C and forced through a small orifice into a multi-cavity steel mould - the transfer mould - that holds the wired frame in one cavity per die. The compound flows around the wire loops, fills voids, and cures in "];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould.tool" [label="The mould cavity and ejector pins" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A hardened, polished steel block with one to hundreds of identical cavities. For a DIP the cavity is a rectangle with fourteen to twenty pin-shaped slots; the mould also carries the moulded cavity number, the vendor's part-number embossing, and ejector pins that push the finished part out without sc"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould.compound" [label="Epoxy moulding compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A blend of roughly 15-20 percent resin system and 80-85 percent filler by weight. The resin is an epoxy-novolac cured by a phenolic novolac; the filler is spherical fused or precipitated silica graded from a few microns up to about 100 microns so the compound can be both thin enough to flow around a"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould.compound.pigment" [label="Carbon black pigment" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="About half a percent by weight of furnace black, dispersed into the compound, to make the package opaque black. Black rather than natural-coloured because a black package hides internal voids, and because it was what the whole industry had standardised on since the first plastic DIP."];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould.compound.resin" [label="Epoxy cresol novolac resin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The workhorse of chip packaging in every decade since 1970. Cresol-formaldehyde epoxy novolac is made from epichlorohydrin and a cresol-formaldehyde novolac; cured with a phenolic novolac and catalysed with a small amount of hexamethylenetetramine, it cures without volatiles and without ionising any"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.mould.press" [label="The transfer press" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A heated platen press with a pot and plunger that transfers melted compound into the mould. Modern machines are multi-cavity and run at thousands of shots an hour; a 1980s line used 20- to 100-shot multi-cavity tools and got through a few hundred thousand DIPs a month."];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.wirebond" [label="Gold wire bonding" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A machine called a wedge bonder presses a hair-thin gold wire onto an aluminium bonding pad with a tungsten or ceramic tool while a hydrogen torch or arc welds them together, then repeats at the inner end of the pin. Each joint is a small aluminium-gold intermetallic wedge, and the wire is parted by"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.wirebond.bonder" [label="The wedge bonder" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A two-station machine that draws wire from a spool, forms a loop, drives a wedge onto the die pad under a hydrogen flame, swings 90 degrees, and drives the second wedge onto the pin, then snaps the wire. Twenty or forty of these sat in a row along a packaging line, each attended by one operator feed"];
  "mb.logic.package.assembly.attach" [label="Die attach to the frame paddle" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The die is placed face-down onto a small ledge in the middle of the lead frame and brazed there with a preform of gold-silicon eutectic at 350-400 C. The eutectic melts before the silicon itself does, so the joint is made without heating the chip to a temperature that would damage it. Cheap parts in"];
  "mb.logic.package.die" [label="The separated, tested die" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Between the diffusion bay and the packaging line, the wafer is scribed into rectangles, diced, and every rectangle is probed while still in wafer form. The die that arrives at the assembly line is a bare rectangle of silicon, perhaps 1.5 by 2.5 mm, with a scratched pattern of aluminium bonding pads "];
  "mb.logic.package.die.hardtest" [label="Why 4164 DRAMs die but 74LS parts mostly do not" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 74LS gate is a handful of transistors with enormous margin; it either works or the diffusion is cracked. A 64Kx1 DRAM stores charge in a capacitor about 100 femtofarads across a transistor with a leakage path, and it has to be refreshed every few milliseconds. That is why the eight 4164s on the C6"];
  "mb.logic.package.die.passivation" [label="The glass over the aluminium" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="After metallisation the whole wafer is coated in a thick film of silicon dioxide, usually with a plasma-deposited nitride cap. This passivation layer is what protects a 1983 logic die from the humidity and mobile ions that would otherwise creep under the aluminium and shift the threshold of a transi"];
  "mb.logic.package.die.scribe" [label="Scribing and dicing the wafer" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A diamond stylus is dragged along the scribe lanes between dies, not to cut through but to fracture the crystal along the damage tracks buried in the oxide. For the narrow lanes the wafer is turned edge-on and run through a rotating diamond blade, or more recently a tensioned wire loaded with abrasi"];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe" [label="Nickel-iron lead frame" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A continuous strip of metal, punched and formed into a repeating chain of DIP skeletons joined by tie-bars. It arrives at the assembler as a reel of maybe 3000 frames; the frames are cut out one at a time, plated, and then become the skeleton of the package - the seventeen or more separate pins, the"];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe.c4" [label="Copper-clad C4 lead frame (later, not 1983)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A lead frame fabricated not from a bulk alloy strip but from an aluminium or copper leadframe clad on both faces with a controlled thickness of copper, so the surface the solder sees is copper rather than a flash plate. Known by the trademark C4. It solved the tin-whisker and solderability problems "];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe.plating" [label="Lead plating: tin, gold flash, nickel" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="Bare nickel-iron leads solder badly and gall when the DIP is pushed into and out of a socket, so every logic lead frame came out of the stamping press already plated. The common 1983 finish was an electroless or electrolytic nickel pre-plate followed by a matte tin or tin-lead overlay; gold flash ov"];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe.plating.tin" [label="Tin plating bath" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A chemical or electrolytic tin deposit, typically 1-3 micrometres thick, over a nickel underlayer. Tin is soft, wets solder readily and costs almost nothing, and it is why a logic chip could be soldered onto a two-layer board with a plain iron in an automated wave-solder machine with no special flux"];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe.stamp" [label="Progressive-die stamping of the frame" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The strip is fed through a sequence of steel dies on a mechanical press. Each station cuts, pierces, forms and bends a little more; by the last station the strip has a repeating row of completed DIP skeletons still joined by their tie bars. Ten to twenty tonnes of press capacity for a strip that is "];
  "mb.logic.package.leadframe.stamp.tool" [label="The progressive die set" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A stack of hardened tool-steel plates, each with apertures and forming punches cut by wire EDM or grinding, bolted into a set between the press bolster and the ram. Making the tooling for one frame number was a days-to-weeks job and was why a lead frame number was never changed casually: the tooling"];
  "mb.logic.package.geometry" [label="Why 0.1 inch and 0.3 inch" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The two dimensions that made the DIP work at all, and the reason for both. The 0.100 in (2.54 mm) lead pitch was chosen so a cheap single-row socket, and a hole in a piece of perforated board on the same grid, would accept the part. The 0.3 in (7.62 mm) row spacing was a compromise: close enough tha"];
  "mb.logic.package.geometry.rowspacing" [label="0.3 inch row spacing and its wider variants" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The distance between the two rows of pins. Standard JEDEC practice in 1983 was 300 mil for packages up to 24 pins and 600 mil for 28-pin and 40-pin parts, so a 40-pin device and a 16-pin device could share a footprint grid. A 900 mil (22.9 mm) body was used by some hermetic and high-current ceramic "];
  "mb.logic.package.geometry.pitch" [label="0.1 inch (2.54 mm) lead pitch" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The distance between adjacent pins on one row of the DIP. It is the same pitch as the holes on a piece of perforated board, and it is the reason a 74LS04 could be dropped into a socket or a perfboard without any special tooling. The pin itself is a 0.25 x 0.5 mm rectangular wire, the narrow dimensio"];
  "mb.logic.not-on-this-board" [label="74-series parts that are NOT on a C64" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A warning about the part list, kept so a reader can trust the sibling nodes. Frequently published lists of '74-series chips in the Commodore 64' include 74LS157, 74LS165, 74LS161, 74LS138, 74LS32, 74LS02, 74LS20, 74LS10, 74LS11, 74LS125 and 74LS181. None of those are on the ASSY 250407. They belong "];
  "mb.logic.cmos-4000" [label="CMOS logic on the board: 4066 switches and MC4044" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Four of the C64's glue chips are CMOS rather than bipolar TTL: two 4066 quad bilateral switches (U16, U28) and one MC4044 phase/frequency comparator (U32), plus the 74LS629 above. The 4066 is four independent CMOS transmission gates used as analogue multiplexers to route luma, chroma and audio and t"];
  "mb.logic.cmos-4000.u20-lm556" [label="LM556 dual timer (U20) - adjacent, not a logic part" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two 555-class monostable timers sharing one package, in a 14-pin DIP. It sits in the analogue paddle-input path, producing the timed pulse whose width the SID measures to report joystick and paddle position. Included here for completeness because it is off-the-shelf, because it is bipolar, and becau"];
  "mb.logic.u31-74ls629" [label="74LS629 dual voltage-controlled oscillator (U31)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two voltage-controlled oscillator sections plus an enable and a range control, in a 14-pin DIP. The C64 has no crystal governing its video dot clock: one half of U31 amplifies the colour-subcarrier reference coming out of the video circuitry, the other half generates a free-running dot clock whose f"];
  "mb.logic.u30-74ls193" [label="74LS193 synchronous 4-bit up/down counter (U30)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="A four-bit presettable binary counter with separate up and down clocks and a clear, in a 16-pin DIP. It sequences the multiplexed dynamic-RAM cycle - stepping through address states and generating the refresh strobes - and helps step the VIC-II's raster counters. This is the chip that turns 'one add"];
  "mb.logic.u29-74ls74" [label="74LS74 dual D-type flip-flop (U29)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two positive-edge-triggered D flip-flops with asynchronous preset and clear, in a 14-pin DIP. In the C64 they sit in the DRAM timing chain: shaping and stretching the column strobe and dividing or retiming the phase clock so the 4164s get a valid /CAS pulse on the right edge. A flip-flop is what tur"];
  "mb.logic.u27-74ls08" [label="74LS08 quad 2-input AND gate (U27)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Four independent two-input AND gates in a 14-pin DIP - the most ordinary chip on the board and the one that does the most unglamorous work. ANDing is how the machine makes qualified control signals: a decode line ANDed with a clock phase becomes /RAS, /CAS or /WE for the DRAMs, and ANDed with the re"];
  "mb.logic.u26-74ls373" [label="74LS373 octal D latch, three-state (U26)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Eight transparent D-type latches with three-state outputs in a 20-pin DIP. The VIC-II has only eight address pins but must reach two different memories - the 1 KB screen/bitmap RAM and the character-generator ROM - so it puts one byte on its own pins while this latch holds the other byte. The three-"];
  "mb.logic.u15-74ls139" [label="74LS139 dual 1-of-4 decoder (U15)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two independent four-way decoders with common active-low enables and active-low outputs, in a 16-pin DIP. Given four address bits and an enable, each half asserts exactly one of four outputs. Commodore uses it to expand the sparse decode coming out of the PLA into individual chip selects - VIC-II ba"];
  "mb.logic.u14-74ls258" [label="74LS258 quad multiplexer with three-state outputs (U14)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="The same four 2-to-1 selectors as the 74LS257 but with outputs that can be switched into a high-impedance state. This is the bus arbiter for VIC-II memory access: when the VIC-II is allowed to take the bus, the multiplexer's outputs are disabled so the VIC-II's own address pins can drive directly in"];
  "mb.logic.u13-u25-74ls257" [label="74LS257 quad 2-to-1 multiplexer (U13 and U25)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Two identical 16-pin packages, each holding four independent 2-to-1 data selectors with non-inverting outputs. They exist for one reason: the 4164 DRAMs are multiplexed parts with only nine address pins, taking a row address first and a column address a few hundred nanoseconds later on the same wire"];
  "mb.logic.u8-7406" [label="7406 hex inverter buffer, open collector (U8)" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="Six inverting buffers whose outputs are open collectors rather than totem poles, in a 14-pin DIP. On the 250407 they invert and buffer the 6510's cassette-port output lines (CASS MOTOR, CASS SENSE, CASS WRITE, CASS DATA) and the shared user-port / serial lines. Open-collector outputs with pull-ups a"];
  "mb.discretes" [label="Discrete semiconductors on the board" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The handful of non-IC semiconductors in the machine: small-signal NPN and PNP planar transistors (2N3904, 2N3906, 2N4124) for the serial/parallel port buffers, the colour-amplifier and video-driver stages and the cassette/joystick input switching; higher-voltage NPN power transistors (2N3055, 2N3792"];
  "mb.discretes.test" [label="Probe, gain binning and final test of the discretes" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Discretes are probed on the wafer with a 3- or 10-needle card, then tested after packaging on a curve tracer or a dedicated bin tester. Bipolar small-signal parts are binned by hFE and by VCEO - the 2N3904's hFE spread runs roughly 100-300 - so a given lot is graded, and the C64's assembly only ever"];
  "mb.discretes.varistor" [label="Zinc oxide varistor disc" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A MOV disc across the mains input: a disc of ZnO powder mixed with a few percent of glass and pressed and sintered at 1000-1200 C in air. Its resistance is nonlinear - about 1 kohm/mm at low voltage, collapsing to tens of ohms at the 275 V clamping point - because the grain boundaries are thin deple"];
  "mb.discretes.varistor.zinc" [label="Zinc oxide powder from zinc concentrate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Sphalerite (ZnS) concentrate is roasted in a fluidised bed at 900-1000 C to zinc oxide, then reduced with carbon in a shaft or electrothermic furnace at 900-1100 C to zinc metal, burned back to ZnO, and purified to high-purity ZnO for the varistor mix."];
  "mb.discretes.led" [label="Indicator LED (red/green/amber only)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's power indicator is a 3 mm or 5 mm epoxy-domed LED: a liquid-phase epitaxially grown GaP or GaAsP junction die, die-bonded into a reflector cup on the cathode post, wire bonded to the anode, and encapsulated in a semi-transparent epoxy lens that also acts as a light pipe. Red (GaAsP, ~660 n"];
  "mb.discretes.led.substrate" [label="GaP or GaAsP substrate wafer" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="The LPE junction layers grow on a polished, Czochralski-grown substrate of n-type or semi-insulating GaAs, or on GaP grown by the same kind of pull used for silicon. GaAs itself is made by liquid-phase or VGF growth from a melt of gallium and arsenic, with the arsenic fed from a boiler of high-purit"];
  "mb.discretes.led.gallium" [label="Refined gallium" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Gallium refined from bauxite residue or from zinc residues by repeated ion-exchange in alkaline solution or by amalgam/hydroxide extraction, to 99.9-99.99% metal, then to GaP by reaction with high-purity phosphorus at 1000-1100 C in a sealed quartz ampoule."];
  "mb.discretes.led.epi" [label="Liquid-phase epitaxy of the GaP junction" fillcolor="#c0caf522" color="#c0caf5" tooltip="A GaP or GaAsP wafer is dipped in a molten, boron-doped Ga/GaP/P solution in a horizontal tilting boat inside a long quartz tube furnace at ~850-900 C. The substrate is swept through many alternating supersaturated melts so that dozens of GaP layers a few microns thick grow in, alternating p-type (Z"];
  "mb.discretes.diodes" [label="Rectifier, switching and zener diodes" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Three diode families with completely different construction. The 1N4001 is a diffused rectifier: an n- epitaxial layer on an n+ substrate with a heavily p-doped anode, rated 1 A / 50 V. The 1N4148 is a planar switching diode: a small npn with a Schottky-clamped base shorted to the cathode, rated 200"];
  "mb.discretes.bjt-package" [label="TO-92 epoxy mould package" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Discretes were diced, attached to the tab of a stamped lead frame, wire bonded or solder dipped, and transfer moulded in a black epoxy (a filled phenolic or epoxy compound loaded with carbon black for colour) with the leads formed to a 0.25 inch pitch. The 2N3904's die is under 1 square mm; the pack"];
  "mb.discretes.leadframe" [label="Discrete lead frame, 0.25 inch pitch" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A transistor's leads are a stamped 0.25-0.30 mm strip of nickel or copper alloy, plated with tin or tin-lead so they solder into a phenolic or epoxy-moulded phenolic board. The C64 mainboard is a solder-through board, so lead-frame plating was the difference between a working assembly and a cold joi"];
  "mb.discretes.isolation" [label="Isolation diffusion and its moat" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A boron diffusion deep enough to reach the subcollector creates a p-type tub around each transistor, isolating it from its neighbours through reverse-biased pn junctions rather than an oxide. The same oxide that covers the tub is later cut away in the base and emitter windows - this is the 'planar e"];
  "mb.discretes.epi" [label="Radiant-heated epitaxial reactor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="After the n+ subcollector diffusion, a 1-3 um n-type epitaxial layer was grown in a radiant-heated vertical reactor: the wafers sat on a graphite susceptor inside a quartz bell jar, inductively heated to 1150-1250 C, with AsH3 or PH3 in H2 flowing over them. Epitaxy gave the collector the uniformity"];
  "mb.discretes.bjt" [label="Planar epitaxial small-signal BJT process" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 2N3904, 2N3906 and 2N4124 are all the same architecture, differentiated by polarity and voltage rating: a p-type substrate, a heavily arsenic- or phosphorus-doped n+ subcollector, then a 1-3 um epitaxial n collector layer grown in an ATCP radiant-heated reactor at ~1150-1250 C with AsH3 or PH3 i"];
  "si.litho" [label="Photolithography: coat, bake, expose, develop" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Every layer of every die is defined the same way. The wafer is coated on a spinner at 2000-5000 rpm to 1-3 um of positive photoresist, soft-baked at 90-100 C, aligned to the mask on a 1:1 contact or proximity aligner, exposed through the chrome plate at 30-50 mJ/cm2, spray-developed in 5-10% tetrame"];
  "si.litho.aligner" [label="Contact and proximity mask aligner" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 1:1 mask aligner: mercury or xenon source, a 20x-100x binocular microscope, a vacuum or magnetic chuck, and either hard vacuum contact with the mask pressed straight down onto the resist, or a 100-500 um proximity gap. Typical 1982 units were the Coburn 2400, the Kasper 2400, the Universal Exposur"];
  "si.litho.spinner" [label="Spin coater and hot-plate track" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A bowl coater dispensing 1-3 mL of resist onto a vacuum-chucked spinning wafer, followed by a convection hot plate or an infrared bake at 90-120 C. By 1982 mask plates and wafers were often run on a coater-developer track that baked, exposed at a fixed dose and developed in one enclosed unit, with a"];
  "mb.pla" [label="U17 PLA 906114-01 (mask-programmed NMOS)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's Programmable Logic Array at U17: it decodes the 6510's address lines and generates the chip-select and bank-switching strobes for every other chip on the board - RAM, ROMs, VIC-II, SID, CIAs, VIA, colour RAM - which makes it the single point of failure for the whole machine. Commodore part"];
  "mb.pla.test" [label="Probe, truth-table test and final test" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A PLA is uniquely testable because its specification is a truth table rather than a waveform. On probe, a stored truth table is stepped through all 16 inputs x /OE combinations on all 8 outputs - a few thousand vectors - which catches a stuck link, a shorted link or a bad decoder in seconds. Assembl"];
  "mb.pla.failures" [label="Why it fails more than anything else in the machine" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Three reasons stack up. First, it is a single point of failure: if any of its eight strobes misbehaves, the symptom is a dead or misbehaving machine rather than one dead chip. Second, its output enable and eight outputs switch hard on every 6510 cycle against the capacitive load of the whole board, "];
  "si.trim" [label="Trim, form, mark and final electrical test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="After moulding the leads are formed on an 8-16 station trim-and-form press - trimmed, bent to the package pitch, flattened, and dipped in a tin or tin-lead solder pot - the body is marked with a dot-matrix ink printer or a Nd:YAG laser with part number, lot code and date code, and the part is droppe"];
  "si.mould" [label="Transfer moulding with silica-filled epoxy" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The bonded frame sits in a multi-cavity transfer press and is encapsulated in silica-filled epoxy moulding compound. A typical cycle is 150-180 C at 700-1000 psi for one to three minutes per cavity, and plaques of flash at the parting line are trimmed off in a second press operation. The alternate r"];
  "si.mould.press" [label="Hydraulic transfer moulding press" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 50-100 ton multi-cavity transfer press with a heated upper platen, a pot and plunger that pre-form the compound pellet, and the encapsulated lead-frame strip in the cavity. Cycle: close the press, 150-180 C, 700-1000 psi, one to three minutes dwell, then open and eject the encapsulated part still "];
  "si.bond" [label="Gold wire wedge bonding" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="A wire bonder pulls 25-30 um diameter gold wire from a spool through a ceramic capillary and welds it to the aluminium bond pads: a first wire from each pad out to the frame lead, then interconnect wires pad to pad for internal bussing. Gold is soft, so a wedge-shaped tool presses the wire flat rath"];
  "si.attach" [label="Die attach to the lead frame" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The die is placed circuit-side-up on a pre-plated gold lead frame tab and swept through a die bonder. The hot-tool bond raises die and frame to about 400-450 C: gold and silicon melt together at 361 C and, on cooling, form a hard Au-Si eutectic weld roughly 25-50 um thick. That is both the mechanica"];
  "si.emc" [label="Silica-filled epoxy moulding compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="What actually seals a 1982 plastic IC. A thermosetting epoxy or phenolic compound of novolac resin plus 60-75% crushed quartz filler, a small amount of silicone release agent, catalyst and pigment, compounded on a two-roll mill under heat and then transfer moulded at 150-180 C and 700-1000 psi for o"];
  "si.emc.silica" [label="Crushed quartz filler from quartz sand" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The single largest mass ingredient in any plastic package: 60-75% by weight of angular fused or ground silica of 0.5-3 um mean size, made by firing and grinding high-purity quartz. It is there to control the shrinkage coefficient and the thermal expansion, and it is why a decapsulated die sits in a "];
  "si.emc.resin" [label="Epoxy novolac resin from phenol and epichlorohydrin" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Phenol and formaldehyde condensed under acid to a novolac resin, which is then reacted with epichlorohydrin and caustic soda to give the epoxy novolac that forms the backbone of the moulding compound. Phenol itself came from cumene-process oxidation of benzene - overwhelmingly a coal-tar derivative "];
  "si.leadframe" [label="Stamped DIP lead frame" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The die and its leads emerge from a stamped, plated strip. Frames were made from FeNi 42/58 (C4-29) alloy 0.15-0.20 mm thick by progressive die stamping on a 400-800 ton press, then plated - a nickel strike and gold for hermetic parts, or a matte tin or tin-lead plate over nickel for plastic parts. "];
  "si.leadframe.plating" [label="Nickel strike and gold or tin plating of the lead frame" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A nickel strike first, because gold will not adhere directly to the iron-nickel alloy and the leads have to solder. Hermetic parts get a heavier gold plate over the nickel; plastic parts get tin or tin-lead over the nickel so the leads solder into the machine's sockets. The bonding tab is always gol"];
  "si.leadframe.alloy" [label="FeNi 42/58 lead frame alloy strip" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The standard low-CTE frame alloy: 42% nickel, 58% iron, with a coefficient of thermal expansion close to that of the die, so the bond survives temperature cycling. Melted in a vacuum induction furnace, cast, hot-rolled, then cold-rolled and annealed to 0.15-0.20 mm. Copper alloy C194 with 2.3% lead,"];
  "si.package.28" [label="28-pin 600 mil DIP package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A wide-body package, because the PLA needs 16 inputs, 8 outputs, an output enable, the unused FE/NE pin, VCC and VSS. Die attach is a gold-silicon eutectic bond on a gold-plated tab, about 28 pad bonds on a 25 um gold wedge bonder, and a transfer-moulded body of silica-filled epoxy, with the leads f"];
  "mb.pla.economics" [label="The economics of one custom mask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 906114-01 encodes the entire C64 memory map in a die smaller than the fingertip, and Commodore sold millions of C64s, so once the mask was paid for the part was dirt cheap per unit. But the flip side is that the function exists nowhere else in the world. Any machine needing a different bank-swit"];
  "mb.pla.provenance" [label="Reverse-engineered from a Signetics 82S100" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Early C64s shipped with a bipolar Signetics 82S100, or the Fairchild 93459 clone of it; Signetics was acquired by Philips, which renamed the part PLS100. Commodore's engineers then decapped the bipolar part, photographed it with a Polaroid camera and rebuilt the function as a mask-programmed NMOS de"];
  "mb.pla.vs-pal" [label="PLA versus PAL: why the name misleads" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's part is called a PLA, but the parts most readers know by that name - the 16L8, 16R8, 20L8 - are PALs, and the two are not interchangeable in sourcing or programming. A PAL is a generic catalogue device: every 16L8 from every vendor is the same silicon, and the buyer programs it. A mask-pro"];
  "mb.pla.alternatives" [label="Fusible-link and antifuse PLAs" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The two field-programmable routes a PLD could have taken instead, and the reason a mask-programmed part is a different commercial animal. A fusible link is a real wire of chromium or nichrome, 1-3 um thick, burned out by a laser programmer through the package's FE/NE pin - this is how the PAL 16L8 a"];
  "mb.pla.mask-programming" [label="Mask programming of the link pattern" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="This is the practical heart of a mask-programmed PLA. The AND array, the OR array and the decoders are all routine NMOS layers, but the pattern of which links exist is frozen into a single additional mask level - drawn as one extra colour plane in the layout database and cut like any other plate. Th"];
  "mb.pla.process" [label="NMOS process for the 906114-01 (node not claimed)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The device-level facts are solid: it is NMOS, mask programmed, made at Norristown, and running a lower VOH than the bipolar original. The process details are not published anywhere. On the evidence of die markings, the era and the same fab, it is almost certainly the house silicon-gate NMOS flow use"];
  "mb.pla.array" [label="What a PLA physically is" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Two arrays of transistors and a set of 'links'. The fixed AND array is a matrix of diode or transistor junctions arranged so that each row implements one product term - every possible AND of a subset of the 16 inputs. The programmable OR array adds up to eight of those product terms per output. Each"];
  "mb.ppi" [label="6522 PPI/VIA, 40-pin DIP" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6522 Versatile Interface Adapter at U15 on the C64 mainboard: two 8-bit parallel ports (the user port at $D420 and the cassette port at $D800), two 1-bit handshake lines, an 8-bit shift register for the cassette serial interface, two 16-bit timers with pulse output, four interrupt inputs and an "];
  "mb.ppi.test" [label="Wafer probe and final test of the 6522" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Probe with a 40-needle card: both 16-bit timers clocked against a reference, the shift register stepped, both ports and the handshake lines cycled, and the interrupt inputs verified. Assembled parts repeat the program in a socket on the automatic tester, then go into the C64's machined-pin socket."];
  "si.probe-card" [label="Probe card: gold-plated needles on a printed board" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Before dicing, every die is probed on the wafer through a probe card: a fibreglass or ceramic substrate with a pattern of gold-plated tungsten or rhenium needles on 0.1 inch centres, hanging on a probe ring over the die and stepping down with a few grams of force. In 1982 cards were hand-drilled or "];
  "si.package.40" [label="40-pin plastic DIP package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The package the 6510, the 8562 VIC-II, the 6581 SID, the 6526 CIA and the 6522 VIA all shipped in: a 0.6 inch pitch, 40-leaded stamped lead frame with the die eutectic-bonded on its tab, 25 um gold wire to the pads, and a transfer-moulded body of silica-filled epoxy. A ceramic hermetic version exist"];
  "si.package.ceramic" [label="Hermetic ceramic DIP sintering" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="For hermetic parts the die went into a 96% alumina ceramic shell, sealed with a devitrifying glass frit in a belt furnace at about 850-950 C, and the metal flange was brazed or glass-sealed to the header. Ceramic was slower and costlier than plastic and was used for military and industrial grades - "];
  "si.package.ceramic.shell" [label="Alumina ceramic DIP shell" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A 96% alumina header: alumina powder pressed and sintered into a gas-tight body with a metallised cavity floor for the die, a glass-frit seal ring, and a flange carrying the frame leads. Alumina powder comes from bauxite, and that chain lives in the metals and packaging trees."];
  "mb.ppi.mask" [label="6522 photomask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Nine or ten levels - n+ field, LOCOS active area, contact, polysilicon, self-aligned diffusion, metal, passivation pad and alignment - printed 1:1 in AZ-1350 on a contact or Perkin-Elmer Micralign proximity aligner from chrome-on-glass plates with grid-broken alignment. As with the CIAs, the process"];
  "si.mask.plate" [label="Chrome-on-glass photomask plate" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The physical plate a 1982 fab printed from: 5x7 inch of 1 mm soda-lime glass or fused quartz, coated with 100-150 nm of sputtered chromium, then exposed and developed in positive photoresist, the exposed chrome etched away in hot perchloric or sulfuric-perchromate acid, washed, inspected on a Zeiss "];
  "mb.ppi.userport" [label="Parallel ports and handshake lines" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6522's two 8-bit ports are the C64's only general-purpose parallel I/O. Port A/B appear on the 12-way cartridge edge as the user port for printers, samplers and third-party cartridges, and as the cassette port on the mainboard. Both ports are quasi-bidirectional TTL with no bus contention protec"];
  "si.front-end" [label="NMOS silicon-gate front end" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The flow every simple logic part in the machine ran: n+ source and drain pre-diffusion, LOCOS field oxide grown in dry O2 and then wet H2/O2 at about 1000-1050 C, LPCVD polysilicon gate from silane at 800-850 C, a self-aligned arsenic source/drain implant with a 1000-1100 C anneal, contact cuts, 1-2"];
  "mb.cia" [label="Two 6526 CIA complex interface adapters" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="CIA 1 at U17 and CIA 2 at U18 (PLA at U17 on some board revisions) provide the C64's two 8-bit parallel ports, the keyboard/joystick matrix scanning, the two 16-bit interval timers with one-shot/underflow interrupt, the shift register used for the serial (IEC) bus, and TOD (time-of-day) clocking der"];
  "mb.cia.test" [label="Wafer probe and final test of the 6526" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Probe with a 40-needle card and a program that clocks both 16-bit timers against a reference, steps the serial shift register through a byte, checks the interrupt priority logic and the port handshake, and measures the TOD chain. Assembled parts repeat it in a socket. Because the TOD is clocked from"];
  "mb.cia.mask" [label="CIA photomask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Nine or ten levels - n+ field, LOCOS active area, contact, polysilicon, self-aligned diffusion, metal, passivation pad and alignment levels - printed 1:1 in AZ-1350 on a contact or Micralign aligner from chrome-on-glass plates, using the grid-broken alignment scheme with dummy structures and stepper"];
  "mb.cia.serial" [label="Serial shift register" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The CIA's 8-bit shift register is what makes the Commodore serial bus work at all. Software sets a bit, shifts it out under CIA timer control with the handshake lines driving the ATN and CLK/CB lines, and reads bits back the same way; the VIC-II's serial output is decoded separately. This is a purel"];
  "mb.cia.tod" [label="Time-of-day clock" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The CIA's time-of-day clock is a 17-bit counter clocked from the serial pin, which the C64 mainboard drives from one side of the AC line through a 470 kohm resistor. The resulting clock is therefore a divided version of the mains frequency, trimmed per unit at final test by selecting a film capacito"];
  "mb.sid" [label="6581 SID sound chip, 40-pin DIP" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's sound generator at U18: three analogue subtractive-synthesis voices (two oscillators plus a noise source through a 12-bit DAC, one extra-noise-only channel for percussion), a ring modulator, a resonant multimode filter with a self-oscillating VCA, external-to-internal feedback volume routi"];
  "mb.sid.revisions" [label="SID revisions as an analogue-process story" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6581 shipped as R4 (1982), R4AR, R5 and R6. Each revision was a change to the analogue block - VCA threshold voltages, filter resonance scaling, DAC linearity - discovered by listening to the parts on probe. The 8580 (R4, 1984; R5 and R6, 1992) moved to a later NMOS process with a redesigned ana"];
  "mb.sid.test" [label="Wafer probe and audio test of the 6581" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Probe testing a SID meant electrical tests (register access, oscillator counters, envelope thresholds, DAC linearity, filter self-oscillation) plus, on the better lines, a functional audio test: a short score stepped through the chip and the output listened to or measured on a spectrum analyser. Fin"];
  "mb.sid.mask" [label="SID photomask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Nine or ten levels for the 6581: n+ field, LOCOS active area, base/well, contact, polysilicon (including the poly resistor pattern that is the DAC), second diffusion, metal, passivation pad, plus alignment. Printed 1:1 in AZ-1350 on a contact or Micralign aligner from 5x7 inch chrome-on-glass plates"];
  "mb.sid.external" [label="External components the SID needs from the board" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6581 does not make sound by itself. The C64 mainboard provides the 11 MHz colour clock derived from the VIC-II, two 4066 CMOS bilateral switches and a 7406 hex inverter to control filter routing and to stabilize the output (a documented MOS workaround that varies with chip revision), the 470 ohm"];
  "mb.sid.process" [label="NMOS hybrid process: digital counters on an analogue poly" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6581 was not a purely digital NMOS part. Its 24-bit phase accumulators, the noise LFSR and the envelope generators are ordinary silicon-gate NMOS with depletion loads, the same technology as the 6510. Its DACs, the filter and the VCA are analogue: long polysilicon resistors laid down in the same"];
  "mb.sid.poly-res" [label="Polysilicon load resistors" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6510 and the SID both use undoped or lightly doped deposited polysilicon as a resistor, deposited in the same LPCVD silane step as the gate and left unetched between two aluminium contacts. Sheet resistance was 30-100 ohm/sq in 1982 and the resistance was trimmed, if at all, by laser or by anodi"];
  "mb.vic" [label="8562 VIC-II video chip, 40-pin DIP" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's video, colour and sprite chip at U19, in a 40-pin DIP. The 8562 is the C64-specific part number for the NTSC VIC-II (the 6567/6569 are the Max Machine / PAL parts); it is an NMOS silicon-gate chip of the same generation as the 6510. The die contains the 40x25 text/bitmap video matrix, the "];
  "mb.vic.test" [label="Wafer probe and pattern test of the 8562" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The VIC-II is probed with a 40-needle card and a test program that exercises the character generator, the sprite pointers, the colour registers and the interrupt logic - and, on the analogue side, checks that all six colour outputs swing the full 0-5 V and that the 18-bit colour latches all decode. "];
  "mb.vic.mask" [label="VIC-II photomask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 8562's mask set was nine or ten levels: n+ field, LOCOS active area, contact, polysilicon, second diffusion, metal, ROM-programming (none here), passivation pad and the alignment/test levels. A 1982 small fab of this sort typically printed 1:1 on a Coburn 2400 contact printer or a Perkin-Elmer M"];
  "mb.vic.palette" [label="The 18-resistor PAL palette on the mainboard" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The VIC-II itself does not know what colour is 'red'. It emits six 4-bit-weighted analogue voltages per colour channel; a resistor ladder and a 74LS175 shift register on the C64 mainboard combine them with the border colour to synthesise the 16-entry NTSC palette and the 20-colour PAL palette. Those"];
  "mb.vic.process" [label="NMOS front end for a large analog-plus-logic die" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The same silicon-gate NMOS sequence as the CPU, with extra care because the VIC-II mixes digital logic, a 6-bit DAC and analogue comparators on one die: 2 um minimum feature, ~400 A poly-oxide, LOCOS field oxide, self-aligned n+ source/drain, single 1-2 um aluminium layer, PSG plus plasma nitride pa"];
  "mb.roms" [label="901226-01 BASIC, 901227-03 KERNAL, 901225-01 character ROM" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The three mask-programmed NMOS ROMs on the C64 mainboard, all in 24-pin DIP: 901226-01 is the 8 KB (2364-compatible pinout) Commodore BASIC V2 interpreter at $A000; 901227-03 is the 8 KB KERNAL at $E000 (901227-01/-02 in earlier lots, and 901229-0x is the 1541 drive's KERNAL, not this one); 901225-0"];
  "mb.roms.revisions" [label="ROM revisions are mask revisions" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Every fix to the C64's firmware cost a new mask set and a new wafer run. The BASIC ROM has exactly one version, 901226-01; the KERNAL went through 901227-01, -02 and -03, the last of which fixed a tape-loading bug and is what nearly every C64 shipped with; the character generator is 901225-01, with "];
  "mb.roms.verify" [label="Verifying every ROM bit" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A mask ROM cannot be re-programmed, so its contents are verified twice. On the wafer, a 24-needle probe card runs a read-only pattern through the 8192 or 4096 addresses and sums the data; after assembly, the part goes into an automatic tester that reads the whole array and checks it against a reside"];
  "si.package.24" [label="24-pin plastic DIP package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The package of the BASIC, KERNAL and character ROMs: a 0.6 inch pitch, 24-leaded stamped lead frame, die eutectic-bonded on its tab, 25 um gold wedge bonding, and a transfer-moulded body of silica-filled epoxy. On the C64 mainboard these three were the only chips soldered in rather than socketed, be"];
  "mb.roms.mask" [label="The ROM-only mask plate" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Because the ROM pattern is a physical edit to the flow, Commodore held a separate chrome-on-glass plate for each ROM number. It was cut from the ROM's bit map by the same pattern generator used for the logic masks, inspected against the database, and stored in a mask library; 901226-01 and 901227-03"];
  "mb.roms.programming" [label="Mask programming: one mask set per ROM revision" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="An NMOS mask ROM of 1982 was an NMOS transistor array with a decoder, programmed by removing or adding contacts/metal at the array intersections. The usual 1980s implementation was contact programming: the programmer and sense-amp metal were left unconnected at a bit=1 location, and the ROM-only mas"];
  "mb.roms.sense" [label="Bit-line sense amplifiers" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A masked bit line is precharged high and then a dummy word is selected to develop a voltage across the bit line; a differential sense amplifier (a latch of four NMOS transistors plus a pair of cross-coupled load devices, exactly the same topology as the 6510's logic) amplifies the millivolt differen"];
  "mb.roms.process" [label="NMOS mask ROM front end" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The same silicon-gate NMOS flow as the CPU - n+ source/drain diffusion, LOCOS field oxide, polysilicon, self-aligned source/drain, contact cuts, 1-2 um aluminium, PSG/nitride passivation - with one extra mask level for the ROM programming pattern. A 24-pin DIP ROM die is small (a few mm2) and yields"];
  "mb.color-ram" [label="U6 2114L colour RAM (1K x 4 static)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's screen colour memory at U6, decoding to $D800-$DBE7: 1024 words of 4 bits each, i.e. one colour nibble for each of the 40x25 text screen's 1000 cells. The VIC-II reads these nibbles during the visible portion of every scanline and presents them to the 4-bit colour DAC, so the memory must b"];
  "mb.color-ram.test" [label="Probe, sort and final test" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Probed on the wafer with an 18-needle probe card running an address/data checker over all 4096 bits in a marching pattern, then retested after moulding. Because the part has no refresh, its main failure modes are stuck cells from particles and defective decoders, both of which a walking-one/walking-"];
  "si.package.18" [label="18-pin plastic DIP package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The package of the 2114L colour RAM: an 18-leaded stamped lead frame at 0.6 inch pitch, about 18 pad bonds plus a handful of internal wires, and a transfer-moulded body of silica-filled epoxy. The lead-frame, bonding-wire and compound stories are the same as every other DIP in the machine."];
  "mb.color-ram.vic-interface" [label="How the VIC-II reads it during raster" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The VIC-II owns an internal video address counter that runs ahead of the beam. For each character cell in the visible portion of a scanline it drives the ten colour-RAM address lines and /CS low, and the chip returns the 4-bit colour on D0-D3 within about 300 ns; those four bits go straight to the 6"];
  "mb.color-ram.mask" [label="The 2114 mask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Eight or nine 1:1 levels: n+ field, LOCOS active area, contact, polysilicon (gate and, if used, the load resistor pattern), self-aligned diffusion, metal, passivation pad, and alignment/test levels, printed from chrome-on-glass plates with grid-broken alignment."];
  "mb.color-ram.cell" [label="Six-transistor NMOS static cell" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Each of the 1024 nibbles is six transistors: two cross-coupled NMOS inverters forming a bistable latch, plus two NMOS pass transistors that connect the latch to the bit line when the address decoder enables that column. Because it is NMOS, each 'inverter' of the latch is a pull-down transistor in se"];
  "mb.color-ram.cell.access" [label="Pass transistors and the two-decoder structure" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 1024 x 4 array is decoded as a 32 x 32 matrix: a six-to-64 row decoder from A0-A5 and a five-to-32 column decoder from A6-A9, each a NAND-type NMOS decoder built from series pull-down strings. Each cell therefore needs two access transistors, one gated by a row line and one by a column line. The d"];
  "mb.color-ram.cell.load" [label="The load device: polysilicon resistor or depletion transistor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="This is the real difference between a 2114 and a CMOS SRAM, and it is an extra process step. A ratioed NMOS latch needs something in series with the pull-down transistor to act as a load: on 1982 parts this was typically an undoped or lightly doped polysilicon resistor deposited in the same LPCVD st"];
  "mb.color-ram.why-static" [label="Why static rather than dynamic" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The DRAMs beside it get a refresh cycle roughly every 2 ms because the 6510 can be held off the bus while the KERNAL's refresh routine runs. The colour RAM has no such luxury. The VIC-II fetches a colour nibble out of this chip in the middle of drawing each character cell, within a few hundred nanos"];
  "mb.ram" [label="Four/eight 4164 64Kbit DRAMs = 64 KB RAM" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The C64's dynamic memory. The breadbin mainboard (PCB 326498-01) carries EIGHT 4164 64Kbit x 1 DRAMs, each holding 8 KB, giving 64 KB total; the address bus is multiplexed row/column through two 74LS257 multiplexers. Early prototypes used 4116 16-pin parts. The 4164 is a single-transistor single-cap"];
  "mb.ram.refresh" [label="RAS-only refresh" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 4164 has no refresh counter: the address must be presented on RAS every cycle to stay alive. The C64's 6510 does the counting in software - the KERNAL refresh routine puts a 7-bit value into $D012 and clocks the CIA's A12 line every seven machine cycles, so all eight chips are refreshed every 7 "];
  "mb.ram.sort" [label="Wafer sort and final test of the 4164" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Every die is probed with an 8-needle probe card and tested on an automated memory tester that runs a march-type pattern (write a checkerboard, read it, invert, repeat) through all 65,536 cells, then a refresh test holding 128 RAS cycles. Assembled parts go back into a socket on the same tester for a"];
  "si.package.8" [label="8-pin plastic DIP package" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="The smallest and most common package in the machine: eight 4164 DRAMs, each a die on an 8-leaded lead frame with about eight frame bonds plus a dozen internal wires for the sense amps, transfer moulded in silica-filled epoxy. A 16-pin version of the same die exists for stackable C128 memory."];
  "mb.ram.protection" [label="Alpha-particle and latch-up protection" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="DRAM soft errors from alpha particles emitted by radon daughters in the mould compound were a real field-failure mode in 1982. Mitigations used then: a sintered alumina or silicon-nitride chip cover potted over the die in a hermetic package; 'getter' compounds in the cavity; and softer, minimum-area"];
  "mb.ram.package.cover" [label="Sintered chip cover over the die" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="On many 64K DRAMs the mould compound was not allowed near the die: instead a small alumina or silicon-nitride lid was diffusion-bonded to the lead frame cavity and the cavity then filled with a low-alpha moulding compound or left in a hermetic ceramic package. Alpha emission rate of the compound was"];
  "mb.ram.mask" [label="4164 photomask set" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 64K DRAM needed eight to ten mask levels: active area, n+ field, contact, poly 1 (word line), poly 2 (capacitor plate), poly 3 (bit line), the second diffusion, metal, and passivation pad - plus gate-2 test structures. The poly 2 and bit-line levels are what forced 5x step-and-repeat printing; mos"];
  "mb.ram.mask.reticle" [label="5x reticle: chrome on quartz" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A 3x3 inch fused-quartz or borosilicate plate coated with 100-150 nm of sputtered chromium, exposed from a 10x or 20x artwork intermediate by a mask stepper, developed, the chrome etched in perchloric/sulfuric acid, inspected against the database on a Zeiss comparison tool, and mounted in a reticle "];
  "mb.ram.process" [label="Two-level polysilicon NMOS DRAM process" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Same basic silicon-gate NMOS flow as the CPU but heavier: thicker field oxide, more polysilicon depositions (three), a heavier arsenic source/drain, a thicker aluminium (2-3 um) for the bit lines that have to survive repeated bootstrapped swings, and usually two mask levels for bit-line precharge an"];
  "mb.ram.photolitho" [label="Projection printing the poly 2 and bit-line levels" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The tight overlays in a 4164 (bit line over capacitor plate, poly 2 over poly 1) were what finally forced 5x reduction step-and-repeat printing rather than 1:1 contact printing, because 1:1 contact printing of a 4 um bit line over a 4 um plate feature was too lossy. Typical 1982 DRAM mask lines ran "];
  "mb.ram.vbb" [label="On-chip -5 V substrate bias charge pump" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 4116 needed +5, +12, -5 and a boosted write supply, which is why it had a VBB pin and why the C64 prototype had extra regulators. The 4164 collapsed this to +5 V alone by generating the negative substrate bias on chip: an on-chip oscillator drives a capacitor, and the resulting AC is rectified t"];
  "mb.ram.cell" [label="The 1T1C memory cell" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Each of the 65,536 bits is one access transistor plus one capacitor. The cell uses THREE polysilicon layers: poly 1 is the word line crossing the device, poly 2 is the overlapping plate of the storage capacitor (the plate is doped n+ and shared with the bit line), poly 3 is the bit line itself. Arou"];
  "mb.ram.cell.doublepoly" [label="Second polysilicon layer: the storage capacitor" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The key DRAM process problem in 1982. A second conformal polysilicon layer had to be deposited and patterned so that its edge was razor sharp and its overlap onto the access transistor was tight. Deposition was LPCVD silane at ~800-850 C; the plate was doped n+ with a phosphorus implant and annealed"];
  "mb.cpu" [label="MOS 6510 CPU, 40-pin DIP" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="8-bit NMOS microprocessor at U7 of the C64 mainboard, clocked at 1.022727 MHz NTSC (0.985248 MHz PAL). It is the 6502 core plus an 8-bit I/O port on pins 9/10 (address 0000 data-direction, 0001 output latch). The MOS/CSG datasheet calls it 'HMOS, silicon gate, depletion load technology' - i.e. an N-"];
  "mb.cpu.fab" [label="MOS Technology / CSG plant, 950 Rittenhouse Road" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The place these chips were born. MOS Technology was founded in 1969 in Valley Forge, Pennsylvania, moved to 950 Rittenhouse Road in the Valley Forge Corporate Center off Trooper Road near Norristown, and was bought by Commodore Business Machines in 1976, becoming the Commodore Semiconductor Group. I"];
  "mb.cpu.fab.assembly" [label="Assembly and test lines in the same building" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Die sort, sawing, die bonding, wire bonding, transfer moulding, trim-and-form, marking and final test all ran on the site behind the fab. The gold bonder room was kept dry and at constant humidity (roughly 40% RH) because gold wire and epoxy bonding are moisture-sensitive; the moulding presses were "];
  "mb.cpu.fab.maskshop" [label="In-house or commissioned mask shop" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A device plant of this size either cut its own masks or bought them from a regional mask shop. The work involved taking the layout database to a numerically controlled pattern generator, exposing and etching 1:1 chrome-on-glass plates, inspecting them on a comparison tool, and repairing defects - a "];
  "mb.cpu.fab.cleanroom" [label="Class 10 cleanroom bays with HEPA filtration" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Wafer processing in yellow-lit laminar-flow bays fed by fan filter units, with a class 1000 corridor outside; 70% RH and +/-1 C. Gases distributed on stainless steel panels from bulk nitrogen, hydrogen and oxygen tanks; resist and wet chemistry in stainless or quartz benches; photomasks brought in t"];
  "si.burnin" [label="Burn-in and final test" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="Assembled parts were run at elevated voltage - typically 7-9 V on a 5 V part - at 100-125 C for 24 to 168 hours in an oven loaded with sockets on a board, to weed out early-failure parts with contaminated junctions before they reached the customer. The C64 itself needed no burn-in: the chips were si"];
  "mb.cpu.dicing" [label="Die separation: scribe and break, or diamond-wheel saw" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The wafer is flipped onto a vacuum chuck, scored along the scribe lines with a diamond scriber or, in the more expensive method, cut with a resin-bonded diamond wheel on a saw, leaving a kerf of 50-100 um. Small dies like the 6510 (about 3.5 x 3.9 mm) were often diced by scribing and snapping, then "];
  "mb.cpu.dice-tool" [label="Diamond scriber and dicing saw" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small die like the 6510 (roughly 3.5 x 3.9 mm) was separated either by scoring the wafer's back-side scribe lines with a diamond scriber and snapping the wafer along them, or by cutting with a resin-bonded diamond wheel leaving a 50-100 um kerf. The wafer was then washed dry, and a final microscop"];
  "mb.cpu.probe" [label="Wafer probe (electrical die sort)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Before dicing, each die is probed on a wafer with a probe card: a printed-circuit board with 40 microscopic tungsten or rhenium needles, one per 6510 bond pad. A Teradyne L-120 or Fairchild SICTEST automated tester drives a test program and logs wafer coordinates, so only known-good dice go to assem"];
  "mb.cpu.front-end" [label="2 um NMOS front end: the layer stack" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A silicon-gate, double-polysilicon NMOS process flow on p-type (100) CZ silicon: heavily doped n+ source/drain first, LOCOS field oxide, heavily n-doped polysilicon gate, self-aligned arsenic source/drain, contact cuts, aluminium metallization, then phosphosilicate glass plus a plasma nitride seal. "];
  "mb.cpu.fe.implanter" [label="Low-energy ion implanter" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A small Van de Graaff or mass-separated implant source (High Voltage Engineering, Extrion, or Veeco) accelerating arsenic to 50-150 keV, with a beam scanning and dose measurement unit. Implantation of the source/drain had largely replaced diffusion by 1982 in a well-equipped shop."];
  "mb.cpu.fe.etcher" [label="Wet etch bench and plasma etcher" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Wet etches in quartz or PVDF tanks: HF for oxide, hot H3PO4 for aluminium, buffered oxide etch (HF:NH4F) for fine oxide patterning. By 1982 many fabs had added a barrel-type CF4/CF4+O2 plasma etcher (e.g. the Plasma Technologies PT etch, or an ASM) for aluminium and for poly etching."];
  "mb.cpu.fe.deposition" [label="Polysilicon LPCVD furnace and aluminium sputterer" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A second tube furnace, plumbed for silane/phosphine or dichlorosilane, for poly deposition. Aluminium went on in a separate vacuum tool: a DC magnetron sputter chamber (Balzers, Perkin-Elmer, Leybold) or a resistive evaporation chamber, both with turbomolecular pumps and a rotary fixture."];
  "mb.cpu.fe.furnace" [label="Oxidation and diffusion tube furnaces" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Quartz or fused-silica tube furnaces, horizontal, 5-6 inch ID, with 2-4 zone radiant or resistance heating to 1100 C, a boat of silicon-carbide or quartz carrying 25-50 wafers, and gas flow panels with wet/dry mixing valves. These did the field oxide, gate oxide, poly anneal and the source/drain dri"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.photoresist" [label="Positive photoresist: spin, soft-bake, expose, develop" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Wafer coated on a spinner (2000-4000 rpm) to 1-2 um of AZ-1350, dried 90-100 C for 20-30 min on a hot plate, aligned to the mask in a Coburn 2400 contact printer or a Perkin-Elmer Micralign MA-2 optical proximity aligner, exposed through the chrome plate, then spray-developed in 5-10% tetramethylamm"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.passivation" [label="7. Passivation and pad opening" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The aluminium is sealed under a phosphosilicate glass overcoat grown by CVD from TEOS and phosphine at 700-800 C, and in 1982-84 plants increasingly by plasma-enhanced CVD silicon nitride (~700 C, SiH4/NH3 or SiCl4/NH3 in a low-frequency glow discharge). Pad windows are opened with a hot phosphoric "];
  "mb.cpu.fe.contacts" [label="6. Contact cut and aluminium metallization" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Contact windows are cut in the field oxide with an HF dip masked by photoresist (silicon dioxide etches fast in HF), then the wafer is coated by vacuum evaporation or DC sputtering with 1-2 um of aluminium containing 0.5-1% silicon to stop aluminium spikes punching through thin junctions. The alumin"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.self-aligned" [label="5. Self-aligned source/drain implant" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="With the poly gate and field oxide standing up, the wafer is ion-implanted with arsenic (or phosphorus) at 50-150 keV. The gate poly and the field oxide act as their own masks, so the source and drain edges are formed automatically under the poly edges with no alignment tolerance at all. The implant"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.polyoxide" [label="4. Poly gate oxidation (the real '3.5 V' gate dielectric)" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="After the undoped polysilicon is patterned, the whole wafer is re-oxidised: the bare silicon is left capped by the 400 A screen oxide, while the poly itself grows its own oxide. Dry O2 at 1000-1100 C for 30-90 minutes grows ~350-450 A of silicon dioxide on the poly, converting it into a depletion-mo"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.poly" [label="3. Deposition and doping of the polysilicon" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Polysilicon was deposited by low-pressure chemical vapour deposition in a heated-wall tube furnace from silane (SiH4) at 800-850 C or from dichlorosilane (SiH2Cl2) at ~1000 C, giving 4000-8000 A of polycrystalline film. For gates it was deposited heavily n-doped in the same step by adding phosphine "];
  "mb.cpu.fe.locos" [label="2. LOCOS field oxide" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The active-area mask is defined in photoresist over the grown oxide, and field oxide grown by two-step thermal oxidation: a dry O2 pass at ~1000 C to lay down the thin masking oxide, then steam at the same temperature for ~2 hours to reach 6000-8000 A of field oxide. Field oxide was the isolation, t"];
  "mb.cpu.fe.nplus" [label="1. n+ source/drain pre-diffusion" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="A thermal phosphorus diffusion: the wafer is coated with a phosphosilicate glass (PSG) doped by PH3 or by a PBN (pyrolytic boron nitride) solid phosphorus source, then held at 900-950 C for 30-60 minutes to push phosphorus about 0.5-1 um into the silicon. The result is a heavily doped (10^20 cm^-3) "];
  "mb.cpu.mask-set" [label="Photomask set, ~10 levels" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The set of 1:1 chrome-on-glass plates the 6510 pattern was cut into: nine or ten levels - n+ source/drain, field, contact, poly, second contact, metal, passivation and pad, plus alignment and test structures. For a device this small the fab printed 1:1 with contact or proximity printing; 10x stepper"];
  "mb.cpu.mask.stepper" [label="The 10x stepper arriving at the end of the period" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Reduction steppers (GCA, Canon, Magnifire, and later ASML) that printed a 10x-reduced reticle with a +-0.2-0.3 um overlay and broke the industry below the 1 um limit. By 1982 a 1:1 contact printer could just barely hold a 2 um line, so a 2 um 6510 was still printable 1:1; the steppers were arriving "];
  "mb.cpu.mask-set.fabrication" [label="Cutting, inspecting and repairing the mask plates" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="The 6510 layout existed as polygons on a Calma or Computervision workstation. It was checked against the design rules, extracted to a mask database and written to tape, and a mask data preparation program merged the layout with the process layers, the alignment marks and the die boundary to produce "];
  "mb.cpu.mask.inspection" [label="Mask inspection and defect repair" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="Masks were checked on a Zeiss or KLA comparison mask inspection tool against the design database, printing defect-free chrome checkouts. Opaque defects on clear areas were repaired with a fine gallium or indium needle or by lifting the film and re-exposing; clear defects in a chrome line were patche"];
  "mb.cpu.mask.plotter" [label="Pattern generator driving a mask exposure" fillcolor="#7aa2f722" color="#7aa2f7" tooltip="In 1982 most masks of this size were made by a numerically controlled pattern generator that flashed a xenon-mercury lamp through an aperture and shutter wheel in a raster of rectangles into a camera-reduction stage, or by a 1:1 mask writer that exposed a blank plate directly. Electron-beam mask wri"];
  "mb.pcb" [label="C64 mainboard ASSY 250407 - bare double-sided FR-4 board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The unpopulated logic board of a 1983 Commodore 64. A 1.6 mm two-layer epoxy-glass laminate about 390 x 180 mm carrying 35 um copper on both faces, roughly 1200 unplated through-holes for component leads, NO plated-through holes and NO vias at all, green epoxy solder mask on both sides and a white s"];
  "mb.pcb.bareboard" [label="Finished bare board, fab no. 250406" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A drilled, etched, stripped, masked, legended and finished blank ready for component insertion. At 1983 commodity prices a two-layer 1.6 mm consumer board of this size cost on the order of one to two dollars."];
  "mb.pcb.inspection" [label="Electrical test of the bare board" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Before any component goes on, the blank is checked for shorts between nets and for opens in the artwork. In 1983 this was a continuity and insulation-resistance tester plus a careful visual check under a lamp and a loupe. Automated optical inspection existed by 1983 but was a line investment rather "];
  "mb.pcb.legend" [label="White silkscreen legend and compartment boxes" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Reference designators, block outlines and assembly notes are printed in white epoxy ink over the cured mask through a tensioned fine-mesh screen, then baked to cure the ink onto the mask. Commodore drew boxes around functional blocks, the so-called compartments, and used extra ink colours in bands a"];
  "mb.pcb.legend.colour" [label="Coloured silkscreen bands" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A separate pass in each extra colour, with its own screen, to draw the colour bands and outlines that mark functional regions and the PAL or NTSC variant. A modern cosmetic imitation of this is a coloured solder mask; in 1983 it was extra ink passes."];
  "mb.pcb.legend.screen" [label="Screen printing and bake" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A fine-mesh screen coated with photosensitive emulsion is stretched and tensioned over the board, epoxy ink squeegeed through, and the board then baked to cure the ink onto the mask."];
  "mb.pcb.mask" [label="Epoxy solder mask on both sides" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Green epoxy ink is screen-printed over the whole board with apertures left over every pad and hole. It is the green the C64 has always been. Without it the board shorts from a fingerprint, a solder splash or a screw, and the white legend would disappear under bare copper. Period inks were solvent-ba"];
  "mb.pcb.finish" [label="Surface finish: pads and edge contacts" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="After etching the copper is protected, made solderable, and given a wear-resistant finish where it will be touched. On this board, because there are no plated barrels, the finish job is far simpler than on a modern via board: a solderable coating on the pads and a hard plating on the contact surface"];
  "mb.pcb.finish.pth" [label="No plated-barrel line on this board" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A deliberate absence, recorded here so it does not read as an omission. The C64 mainboard skips the entire through-hole plating sequence a via board runs: hole desmear, electroless copper seed, electrolytic copper build, nickel, gold. None of that equipment ever saw this board, and any account that "];
  "mb.pcb.finish.selective" [label="Selective plating of the edge contacts" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Only the contact surfaces get a hard, wear-resistant metal. The board is either plated overall and the gold stripped back from everywhere except the contacts, or the contacts alone are exposed to the bath with the rest shielded by a fountain-plater weir or a plating rack. The usual route is a nickel"];
  "mb.pcb.finish.hasl" [label="HASL solder level on the pads" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Exposed copper is coated in molten solder by carrying the board through a wave or a drag of 63/37 tin-lead and blowing or brushing off the excess, leaving a flat solderable layer that will not corrode. This is the period default for a two-sided consumer board with no vias, and it lines up with the s"];
  "mb.pcb.vias" [label="Layer changes without vias" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The most distinctive fact about the C64 mainboard, and the reason its copper is shaped the way it is. There are no vias and no plated-through holes at all. A connection that has to move from the component side to the solder side goes through the hole of a component lead: every resistor, capacitor, d"];
  "mb.pcb.vias.plane" [label="Ground pour instead of a ground plane" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="With no internal plane layer, the two signal layers have to carry return current on their own copper, so the layout used wide ground pours and heavy copper wherever return current had to flow. Modern boards solve the same electromagnetic problem with a dedicated plane, which is the main reason vias "];
  "mb.pcb.drill" [label="Drilling every component hole" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The board is drilled on a numerical-control drill for every component lead, jumper, jack and mounting hole. Because there are no vias, the drill programme is just a component position list; there is no barrel to plate afterwards and no second drill file to reconcile."];
  "mb.pcb.drill.bit" [label="Carbide drill bit" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Tungsten-carbide or high-speed-steel bits, ground and resharpened. Laminated glass plus copper is abrasive, and the hole is the one feature that cannot be reworked once drilled wrong."];
  "mb.pcb.drill.ncd" [label="NC drill and tool change" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A tape-fed drill head steps through the tool table, swapping bits for the several diameters the board needs, over pinned or vacuum-held fixtures. A bit-management and drill-jig system holds sub-millimetre position accuracy; drill wander is what breaks a fine-pitch DIP row."];
  "mb.pcb.mill" [label="Routing and outline cutting" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="In 1983 the board outline, the internal cut-outs and the expansion-port routing slot were milled mechanically on a CNC router with a carbide cutter. The slot is cut so that a thin projecting ear of laminate is left standing with copper on both faces: that ear is the board's edge-connector receptacle"];
  "mb.pcb.mill.cutter" [label="Carbide router cutter" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A tungsten-carbide end mill, often with a diamond-like coating, cutting laminate that is mostly glass fibre. Glass dust is abrasive, so tool life is short and the cutter is changed on a schedule."];
  "mb.pcb.strip" [label="Resist stripping and clean-up" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The cross-linked resist film is dissolved away in an aqueous caustic or ammoniated stripper, then the board is rinsed, given a mild acid clean to knock back any last residue, dried, and inspected."];
  "mb.pcb.etch" [label="Ferric chloride etching" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The masked laminate goes into warm ferric chloride solution, which dissolves the unmasked copper and leaves traces, pads and ground pour standing. Copper removed along the way is recovered, and the etchant is periodically replenished or dumped. Because copper in solution poisons the bath, an etching"];
  "mb.pcb.exposure" [label="UV exposure through the artwork film" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The artwork negative is laid against the resist and the board is flood-exposed in a vacuum-frame unit. In 1983 the lamp was a mercury-vapour or metal-halide source rich in the near ultraviolet where the acrylate resists are sensitive; fibre-optic exposure systems that pipe collimated light into a be"];
  "mb.pcb.exposure.lamp" [label="Mercury-vapour exposure lamp" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A quartz envelope filled with mercury vapour struck by an arc, rich in the near ultraviolet where the acrylate resists are sensitive. Needs a water-cooled jacket and a choke."];
  "mb.pcb.exposure.vacframe" [label="Vacuum exposure frame" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A hinged platen with a rubber diaphragm. Film and resist are clamped and then evacuated so the film sits hard against the resist with no bubbles and no shadowing."];
  "mb.pcb.photoresist" [label="Dry-film photoresist, negative working" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A dry film about 25-40 um thick, on a polyester carrier, laminated onto the copper. Where the artwork film lets ultraviolet light through, the acrylate resist cross-links and hardens; the hardened areas stand up to the ferric chloride. Everything else dissolves away and the copper under it etches ou"];
  "mb.pcb.photoresist.coat" [label="Surface clean and tack-free prep" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Before the resist goes on, the board is degreased and dried so the film lies flat. A tack-free prep was unusual on period boards; wet resist had an easier time of it, which is one reason modern processes moved."];
  "mb.pcb.photoresist.laminate" [label="Film lamination of the resist" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The board is baked dry, the film is fed from a spool, heated, and pressed onto the copper by rubber-covered nip rollers. The rubber hardness matters: too hard and it embosses the fragile 35 um foil."];
  "mb.pcb.photoresist.carrier" [label="PET carrier film" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The polyester backing the resist is laminated from, peeled off after lamination and before exposure, leaving the resist on the copper. It is the same dimensionally stable polyester film as the artwork film stock, and it exists so the 25 um resist can be handled and rolled without touching."];
  "mb.pcb.laminate.press" [label="Hot platen press and the lamination cycle" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The machine that turns loose foil and cloth into a sheet. A hydraulic press pack carries several heated platens so several laminate books can be pressed in one loading, and a stack of foil, prepreg, core and prepreg goes between them. Pressure squeezes out the excess resin and forces it into the wea"];
  "mb.pcb.laminate.press.heat" [label="Press heat source" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Steam coils or electric resistance elements in the platens. Gas- or oil-fired central steam was the norm in a laminate plant of this era, which is what makes the press the largest single fuel load on such a site."];
  "mb.pcb.laminate.press.release" [label="Release paper between books" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Bleached or unbleached kraft paper laid between each laminate book so resin does not bond one book to the next and the platen stays clean. The same sheet doubles as the cushion that distributes pressure evenly across the book."];
  "mb.pcb.laminate.press.hydraulic" [label="Hydraulic press pack" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The pressure side of the press: cylinders and platens arranged so that several laminate books are pressed in one loading. The circulating hydraulic oil is a petroleum-derived lubricating fluid, refined from crude alongside almost every other lubricant on the line."];
  "mb.pcb.laminate.press.platen" [label="Heated platens" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Cast iron platens, machined and surface-ground flat, heated by embedded steam coils or electric resistance elements. The cast iron is chosen for dimensional stability and thermal mass rather than for strength, and the grind is what guarantees that a stack of books comes out of the press uniformly 1."];
  "mb.pcb.laminate" [label="FR-4 copper-clad laminate panel, 1.6 mm" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The structural core of the board: woven E-glass cloth in a brominated epoxy matrix, faced with 35 um copper foil on both sides. A merchant laminate plant presses it as a panel roughly 1.2 x 2.4 m, which the printed-circuit-board house cuts into working blanks. The cloth is pulled through epoxy, sque"];
  "c64.io-panel" [label="Connectors and operator controls" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Everything that plugs into the machine, and the three things a person touches to run it. The rear edge of the board carries the 44-pin cartridge expansion socket, the integral RF modulator with its channel adjust and RCA antenna socket, an 8-pin DIN for composite video plus separate luma and chroma "];
  "c64.switches" [label="Switches and slide potentiometers" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Three human-operated controls on a C64: the mains-free power slider SW1 on the right-hand case panel, and the two slide potentiometers for volume/tone and brightness on the top surface. The power switch gates the incoming 5 V rail; the two potentiometers are wired directly into the SID audio output "];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders" [label="Volume and brightness slide potentiometers" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Two miniature slide potentiometers on the mainboard whose sliders protrude through the top of the case. One sets the audio level at the SID output; the other sets the analogue video brightness level. Both use a printed resistive track and a spring-loaded wiper."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.section" [label="Single and ganged (twin) sections" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A potentiometer is made in two or three mechanically independent sections that share a common wiper shaft, so one slider knob moves the end terminal, the wiper and the middle terminal together. A 'ganged' pair means two complete potentiometers on one shaft; the C64's two sliders are physically separ"];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.section.bushing" [label="Self-lubricating bushing" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A moulded nylon or PTFE-impregnated bushing in the chassis that the shaft runs in. Dry, it is what makes a slider pot feel smooth rather than gritty."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.section.bushing.ptfe" [label="PTFE-impregnated bushing" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A nylon or bronze-impregnated bushing with a PTFE (Teflon) lubricant. PTFE is made by polymerising tetrafluoroethylene, whose monomer is made from fluorspar via hydrofluoric acid and chloroform."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.section.shaft" [label="Common wiper shaft" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A drawn steel shaft pressed through the moulded wiper carriers, so all the potentiometer sections move together. Drawing the shaft from steel rather than moulding it in plastic is what keeps the alignment over millions of slider cycles."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.housing" [label="Moulded housing and slider knob" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small injection-moulded thermoplastic body with the moulded detent rails for the slider, and a moulded knob with the finger rib that protrudes through the case cut-out. The same polymer families as the switch body: nylon, ABS or a filled phenoxy."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.housing.knob" [label="Moulded slider knob" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small moulded knob with a moulded-in guide rib that engages the body's rails, protruding through the case cut-out. The knob and the body were often moulded in the same tool family so the fit is guaranteed."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.housing.injection" [label="Injection moulding of the pot body" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The body is injection moulded with the three terminal slots, the detent rails and the wiper guide all in one shot, in a multi-cavity tool shared with the rest of the world's miniature slide pot production."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.housing.injection.polymer" [label="Moulded housing polymer (ABS or PBT)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Either the same ABS as the keycaps or a glass-filled PBT. Both are chosen for stiffness at a thin wall - a pot body has to hold a wiper alignment over millimetres of travel without creeping."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.wiper" [label="Spring-loaded wiper" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A small sprung metal clip or blade, usually a copper-nickel-plated spring steel or nickel strip, presses onto the carbon track and taps the voltage off at whatever point along it the slider has dragged it to. It must carry current through a sliding contact, so its plating and its spring force are th"];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.wiper.precontact" [label="Pre-contact break and noise" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A resistive track and a sliding contact inherently generate noise, and a cheap carbon track generates a lot. C64 volume pots are a well-known source of a faint hiss or crackle in the audio output - a real consequence of the printed-carbon technology, not a fault."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.wiper.nickel-strip" [label="Plated spring-steel strip" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The wiper is stamped from spring strip and plated in a nickel or precious-metal bath so that it both springs correctly and carries current across a sliding contact without galling the carbon track."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.wiper.nickel-strip.nickel" [label="Nickel plating bath" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The same nickel chemistry as the resistor lead plating and the connector underplate: a sulfamate or pyrophosphate bath, or electroless nickel-phosphorus where the deposit had to be hard."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.track" [label="Printed carbon track on a ceramic substrate" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The resistive element is a track of carbon ink printed onto a flat ceramic or phenolic substrate, with metal end lands at each end. Printing is what makes a slide pot cheap: the geometry of the track, its length and its taper, sets both the total resistance and the law."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.track.curvature" [label="Fired substrate and end lands" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The carbon track is printed between two metallic end lands, usually fired silver, one at each end of the travel. The wiper bridges them; the centre terminal taps the wiper. All three terminals are solder pins on the pot's tail."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.track.curvature.substrate-firing" [label="Firing of the printed substrate" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The carbon or cermet track is fired into the ceramic substrate in a tunnel oven at several hundred degrees, fusing the binder and the glass frit so the track cannot be scratched off by the wiper."];
  "c64.switches.level-sliders.track.carbon-ink" [label="Printed carbon ink" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Carbon black ground into a binder to a printable ink, screen-printed along the substrate and cured in a tunnel oven. The track length sets the nominal resistance and its taper sets the law; the two C64 sliders are linear, so the printed geometry is constant width."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider" [label="SW1 power slider switch" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A single-pole, double-throw or single-throw slide switch on the mainboard, actuated by a moulded slider that protrudes through a rectangular cut-out in the right-hand side of the case. Its only job in the C64 is to interrupt the incoming +5 V rail - everything else on the board runs off the switch's"];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.contacts" [label="Leaf contacts and contact plating" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A pair of formed leaf springs - phosphor bronze or stainless - carry the current; one is fixed and one moves with the slider, wiping against its mate over a small spring pressure so the electrical contact area is always fresh. The wipe is the whole reason slide switches do not corrode: an oxide film"];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.contacts.phosphor-bronze" [label="Phosphor bronze leaf stock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A copper-tin alloy with a few tenths of a per cent of phosphorus, valued for springiness, fatigue resistance and a low and stable contact resistance. Drawn strip, blanked, pierced and formed in a progressive die; the standard contact alloy of the whole electronics industry."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.mechanism" [label="Slide mechanism, actuator and detent" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The actuator is a moulded slider with ribs that engage moulded detent ramps in the body, so it clicks into a definite position at each end rather than resting ambiguously. A moulded spring or a bent leaf spring inside supplies the return force and the over-centre snap that gives the switch its tacti"];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.mechanism.detent-ramp" [label="Moulded detent ramp" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A stepped ramp moulded into the nylon body or actuator over which the slider rides, giving a definite position at each end. Moulding the detent rather than machining it is what keeps the part cheap at the volumes a consumer switch sold in."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.mechanism.leaf-spring" [label="Return leaf spring" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A bent spring steel leaf that provides the over-centre force and the return to OFF. It is the part that fatigues: after enough actuations it loses tension and the switch no longer snaps crisply."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.mechanism.leaf-spring.steel" [label="Spring steel strip" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A high-carbon strip steel, hardened and tempered, cut and blanked. Blade and coil spring steel of this quality came from the same integrated steel plants as the razor blades and the automotive leaf springs."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body" [label="Moulded switch body (melamine / phenolic / nylon)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The insulator the whole mechanism lives in. Small slide switches of this generation used a filled thermoset (melamine-formaldehyde or glass-filled phenolic) or a glass-filled nylon; both mould well in simple two-plate tools and stand a soldering iron's heat. Melamine was the industry default because"];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.terminal" [label="Solder terminals and anchor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The pins of the switch are brass, either solid or a brass tube over a steel mandrel with a solder tail. Brass is used because it wets tin-lead solder reliably and does not need nickel underplate for a low-voltage contact."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.terminal.brass-stock" [label="Brass terminal stock" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Solid brass wire or a brass tube over a steel mandrel, cut and headed. Brass is chosen because it is springy enough to survive the assembly line and soft enough to take a solder joint."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.nylon.nylon-6-6" [label="Nylon 6,6 (PA66)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The base polymer of PA66, a condensation polymer of hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid - not of caprolactam, which makes nylon 6, a different nylon with a lower melting point. The 6,6 grade was preferred for mechanical parts because of its moisture stability and its higher melting point, and both "];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.nylon" [label="Glass-filled nylon (PA66-GF)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A tough, self-lubricating engineering thermoplastic used where a moving slider needs low friction and good wear resistance against the moulded track. Injection-moulded into the actuator and often into the switch's own guide body."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.nylon.filler" [label="Glass filler in the nylon" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="30 % chopped glass fibre in the PA66, for stiffness at a thin wall and for dimensional stability against the moulded detent. The same filler as in the connector housings."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.melamine" [label="Melamine-formaldehyde thermoset" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A thermoset built from melamine and formaldehyde, filled with cellulose or glass fibre, and compression-moulded hot. It is rigid, arc-resistant, dimensionally stable and cheap - the standard insulator for mains switches and domestic electrical accessories of the period."];
  "c64.switches.power-slider.body.melamine.melamine-resin" [label="Melamine resin from urea and formaldehyde" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Melamine is made by condensing urea with formaldehyde. Urea comes from ammonia and carbon dioxide, both cheap industrial gases; formaldehyde comes from oxidising methanol, which comes from the catalytic conversion of synthesis gas. Both halves were pure petrochemical by 1982."];
  "c64.connector-jacks" [label="Front and rear I/O connectors" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Every plug-in interface on a C64: the 24-pin user port, the 2x6 cassette port, the 44-pin cartridge socket, the 8-pin DIN video/audio and 6-pin DIN serial connectors, the two DE-9 control ports, the 7-pin PSU inlet and the keyboard ribbon header. They share one manufacturing sequence: stamped-and-fo"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.keyboard-header" [label="Keyboard ribbon header" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A soldered pin header at the front-left edge of the mainboard carrying the keyboard's flat ribbon cable. The keyboard's matrix lines and the shift-lock return run out of the machine this way; it is the one connector on the board that is not an industry-standard part but a plain 2.54 mm row of pins."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.keyboard-header.solder-joint" [label="Wave-soldered pin header" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The header's pins were pushed into the board and wave-soldered in one pass with the rest of the components, in 63/37 lead-tin solder. That is why the pin material is brass with a tin plate rather than nickel: it wets tin-lead, and it does not need the corrosion resistance a gold-plated control-port "];
  "c64.connector-jacks.power-inlet" [label="Power inlet, 7-pin round DIN" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The rear-panel socket the external PSU brick plugs into. A 7-pin round connector in a large 'type C' shell, following the DIN 45329 / IEC 10 form: pins 1 and 5 carry the regulated +5 V DC and pins 6 and 7 the 9 V AC from the transformer, with the switched side feeding the board through L5, C97, C100"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.power-inlet.friction-lock" [label="Friction-lock and pull-proof contacts" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A mains-adjacent DC supply connector needs its contacts to survive being tugged by the cable. The mating pins are a friction type with a deliberately stiff crimp barrel so a pull is taken by the metal, not the solder fillet; some Commodore supplies additionally used a friction-lock retention ring on"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.power-inlet.pin-5-fault" [label="The pin 5 damage fault" fillcolor="#ff007c22" color="#ff007c" tooltip="One of the best-known Commodore power faults: a partially populated or incorrectly wired supply lead damages the 5 V pin in the machine's socket, so the machine will not power up even with a known-good supply. It is a wear-and-misalignment problem on a friction contact carrying a DC load that the co"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.power-inlet.friction-lock.crimp" [label="Crimp barrel construction" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The pin is a hollow brass tube with an interference crimp onto the conductor rather than a solder joint. That is the actual meaning of a friction-lock contact: the metal grips the metal, so a tug on the cable is carried by a mechanical interference fit and not by the solder fillet."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports" [label="Control ports, 2 x DE-9 subminiature D" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The two nine-pin D-sub sockets on the rear panel for joystick or paddle controllers, a light pen or a mouse. Each is a DE-9 female in a plastic D-shaped shell with gold-flashed brass contacts dimpled for reliable mating - the connector everyone remembers from Atari, Commodore and Amiga alike."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports.shroud" [label="Metal shell shroud and strain relief" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="On the machine side the socket is a plastic-shell type with no metal shroud; the shroud is on the cable plug, where it is what provides the plug's shell ground and its mechanical strength. Both were stamped and plated the same way."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports.insulator" [label="DE-9 insulator" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A black glass-filled thermoplastic insulator carrying the nine contacts, keyed to the metal shell. It has to hold position to a few hundredths of a millimetre because the contacts must all engage simultaneously in a stiff nine-pin plug."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports.insulator.nylon" [label="Glass-filled nylon insulator" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Glass-filled PA66 holds nine contact positions to a few hundredths of a millimetre over a plastic D-shell, and takes the insertion force of a stiff nine-pin plug without cracking. The base polymer is nylon 6,6 from hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid; caprolactam is the route to nylon 6, a differen"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports.d-sub-standard" [label="D-subminiature (MIL-DTL-24308) standard" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The D-sub was standardised for military/aerospace use in the 1950s and became universal electronics furniture. Its 2.77 mm pitch, D-shaped polarised shell and 2.5 mm panel-mount screw pattern are defined by the standard; the C64's control ports are ordinary commercial DE-9 sockets."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.control-ports.dimple" [label="Dimpled socket contact" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Each female contact carries a dimple on its mating face. Dimpling pushes the two contact surfaces into a defined line contact rather than a random point contact, which lowers the contact resistance and stops the surfaces rolling on each other. The plug's outer shell also contacts the socket shroud, "];
  "c64.connector-jacks.serial-din" [label="Serial bus connector, 6-pin round DIN" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The IEC serial bus that daisy-chained the 1541 disk drive, printers and the rest of Commodore's peripheral family. Same body and same stamping technology as the video connector, smaller pin count, and the same sprung centre pin."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.serial-din.protocol" [label="IEEE-488-style handshake on 6 wires" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Commodore's bus ran clock and data in both directions with attention lines and handshaking on every transfer - an IEEE-488 descendant. That is why a C64 could sit between two 1541 drives and still address the right one: the addressing is on the bus, not in the drive."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din" [label="Video/audio connector, 8-pin round DIN" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="One round DIN on the rear panel carrying composite video, separate luma and chroma (for a Commodore 1701 monitor's RGB-with-separate-luma input), mono audio, and the RF modulator's composite and audio out. Very early 1982 boards used a 5-pin version instead, with luma and chroma combined."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.shell" [label="Nickel-plated steel shell and shield can" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The outer barrel is a drawn steel shell, nickel-plated for corrosion resistance and solderability, and crimped around the insulator. On a metal-panel or rear-case mounting it doubles as the shell ground. Nickel plate on steel is the standard 1982 finish for a mains-adjacent chassis connector. The kn"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.shell.steel-strip" [label="Drawn steel shell stock" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The barrel is deep-drawn from nickel-plated steel strip - a two-and-a-half type can be drawn in a few progressive strokes. The nickel plate carries the shell ground and resists the corrosion a rear-panel connector sees."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.tulip-pin" [label="Sprung split-tulip centre pin" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The centre contact is a thin slotted pin that is sprung into a slight taper, so it plugs into a matching conical socket and self-aligns. The slot lets it flex elastically and also means a slightly undersized mating hole still makes contact at two lines rather than at a point - which is why DIN conne"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.tulip-pin.contact-plating" [label="Contact plating of the DIN pin" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The centre pin is nickel-plated with a gold or silver flash; the signal level here (composite video and line-level audio) is high enough that nickel alone would work, but gold was used for consistency with the other connectors. It is the same nickel-then-gold sequence as every other contact on the m"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.tulip-pin.mating-socket" [label="Conical mating socket" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The socket the tulip pin plugs into has a matching taper, usually machined or stamped with a broach, so the two surfaces self-align on insertion. The taper is the reason a DIN plug feels forgiving and a plain pin-and-socket does not."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.av-din.tulip-pin.mating-socket.broach" [label="Broached taper" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The mating socket's cone is broached rather than turned, so the surface finish is controlled by a single multi-form tool. The broach was hardened steel, so this was another tool-steel consumer."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port" [label="Cartridge / expansion port, 44-pin dual card edge" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The slot along the bottom of the case into which cartridges plug. A 44-position dual female card-edge socket in a black glass-filled polyester body, with phosphor-bronze contacts gold-flashed over nickel. It is the single most-scrutinised connector on the machine: its contacts carry the entire addre"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.contacts-alloy" [label="Card-edge contact alloy (phosphor bronze)" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Phosphor bronze was the standard card-edge contact alloy for this connector, gold-flashed over nickel. It is springy, fatigue-resistant and takes a good plating - the combination a card-edge socket needs, because it is wiped hard on every insertion."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.contacts-alloy.plating-bath" [label="Selective gold plating of the contact area" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The contact is selectively plated by tabbing: the terminal is masked or wired as a cathode so only the mating area is gold-flashed."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.spec" [label="Commodore expansion port specification" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The 44-pin socket's pin assignment, pitch and card thickness were Commodore's own definition rather than a JEDEC standard, which is why cartridges for the C64, C128 and VIC-20 are interchangeable and C64C cartridges are not."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.contact-wear" [label="Contact wear and the 'dead cartridge' problem" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A card-edge socket is a wear item: each insertion plucks the leaf contacts and sweeps the mating gold edge. Dust, oxide from the cartridge's board and accumulated plasticiser eventually make one or two contacts intermittent, which is the classic 'dead cartridge' symptom. Contact cleaning and, on a w"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.contact-wear.cleaning" [label="Contact cleaning regime" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The routine service answer to a marginal cartridge socket: an alcohol swab on the contacts, a foam rod through the slots, and a bend of the leaf if it has lost tension. It is one of the few repairs on a 1982 machine that needs no parts."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.card-guide" [label="Moulded card guide and polarising key" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The socket body doubles as the mechanical guide: a moulded channel sets the card's insertion depth, and an asymmetric keyway stops a cartridge being inserted the wrong way round. Because the body is a thermoset, the guide is machined or moulded in one piece and cannot flex."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.expansion-port.card-guide.machining" [label="Machined guide features" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Because the socket body is a thermoset, the card channel, the stop face and the polarising key cannot be melted into place - they have to be present in the compression tool or machined afterwards. Both were normal for this part in 1982."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.cassette-port" [label="Cassette (datasette) port, 2 x 6 card edge" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The port that reads and writes Commodore DATAsette tapes. It is a 12-position card-edge socket - six contacts on each face, 3.96 mm pitch - that mates with a 6-pin connector on the datasette lead. Carrying analogue audio, motor control and a sense line, it is a reminder that in 1982 'I/O' still mean"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.cassette-port.pull-up" [label="Cassette read pull-up resistor" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="One leg of the datasette sense line is held high by a single 3k3 1/4 W resistor on the mainboard - the same value and the same carbon-film part as the rest of the discrete resistors. It is a tiny detail that shows the whole board's design logic: discrete resistors where few are needed, a printed arr"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.cassette-port.pull-up.signal" [label="Why the port exists at all" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The port carries analogue audio plus three DC control lines. The C64 read FSK-encoded data by timing transitions on the read line and generating the tones on the write line - the whole tape interface is three transistors, a resistor and two electrolytics, which is why it is so sensitive to capacitor"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.user-port" [label="User port, 24-pin card edge" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The C64's expansion bus. A 24-position double-row card-edge socket, 3.96 mm pitch, carrying the address, data and control lines plus regulated and unregulated power out to user-port peripherals. Spare parts for this connector are specified as gold-over-nickel contacts in a PBT UL94 V-0 insulator, wh"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.user-port.pinout" [label="User-port pin functions" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Pins 1-12 carry +5 V out, ground, /RESET, /PPASS (the CPU-select line), the cassette sense/write/read lines and the cassette motor line, plus 9 V AC; pins 13-24 carry the user port's serial bus lines (/EOT, /SERCLK, /ATN IN, /ATN OUT) and the speaker and cassette-slot lines. The C64 6510's on-chip I"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.user-port.contacts" [label="Gold-over-nickel card-edge contacts" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Each contact is a formed spring leaf whose nose protrudes from the insulator and sweeps the mating card's copper edge as it slides in. The wiping action means the gold never has to be thick to stay clean, and the nickel beneath carries the solder joint to the board."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.user-port.contacts.solder-tail" [label="Solder tail and wave-solder attachment" fillcolor="#73daca22" color="#73daca" tooltip="The tail is formed flat and pierced so it takes a fillet in the wave-solder bath, giving a mechanically strong joint that also resists the repeated flexing of a plugged-in peripheral cable."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.user-port.contacts.leaf-alloy" [label="Spring leaf alloy" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The leaf has to both wipe the mating edge and hold a contact force, so it is a hard-worked copper alloy - phosphor bronze or a brass-tempered strip. It is the same material family as the switch contacts and the cartridge socket."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding" [label="Connector housing: injection-moulded thermoplastic or phenolic" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The insulator every connector sits in. Card-edge and card-guide style connectors used black glass-filled polyester or phenolic because it machines cleanly, has almost no moulding flow and stands soldering temperatures; D-sub and DIN shells used glass-filled nylon or PBT. All were injection-moulded i"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.injection-press" [label="Injection moulding press and tool" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A multi-cavity tool clamped between the platens of a screw injection moulder. The housing is a commodity: the same part is made in hundreds of thousands for the entire electronics industry, which is why connector housings cost less than the contacts inside them."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.injection-press.tool-steel" [label="Mould tool steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="P20 stainless tool steel was the industry standard cavity steel in 1982, hardened to ~48-52 HRC and polished to a fine finish. A connector housing's surface is entirely a function of the cavity polish."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.injection-press.screw" [label="Injection screw and barrel" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A nitrided steel screw and barrel, heated to 230-280 C, plastifying the polymer and injecting it into the tool. The screw is itself a wear part made from tool steel, so the moulding industry is a steady consumer of alloy steel."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.pbt" [label="PBT thermoplastic housing" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polybutylene terephthalate is moulded at normal injection temperatures, so a connector body can carry snap-fit features, pivot pins and cable strain-relief arms that a thermoset cannot. Glass-filled PBT was the standard D-sub and DIN shell material of the era, chosen for stiffness and low creep."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.pbt.uv-stabiliser" [label="UV stabiliser and glass filler" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="PBT needs an ultraviolet absorber because it yellows and embrittles in light, plus a glass filler for stiffness. The absorber is a benzotriazole, and both additives are minor mass but were cost-relevant in a moulded part sold in tens of millions."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.pbt.dmt" [label="Dimethyl terephthalate and butanediol" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="PBT is a condensation polymer of DMT and 1,4-butanediol. DMT comes from paraxylene; butanediol came in the 1980s largely from maleic anhydride hydrogenation (a coal/route) or from butanediol dehydrogenation. Both are petrochemical or coal-chemical intermediates."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.pbt.dmt.px" [label="Paraxylene and DMT" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Paraxylene is oxidised to terephthalic acid, esterified with methanol to DMT, then condensed with butanediol. Methanol was itself a cheap synthesis-gas product of that period, so DMT carries coal and gas chemistry as well as oil."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.phenolic" [label="Glass-filled phenolic (thermoset)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Phenol-formaldehyde resin reinforced with chopped glass fibre and moulded under heat and pressure. Extremely dimensionally stable, heat resistant and flame retardant, which is why black glass-filled phenolic is the classic black body of terminal blocks, card-edge sockets and relay bases. The disadva"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.phenolic.resin-source" [label="Phenol and formaldehyde" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Phenol comes from cumene or chlorobenzene, both benzene derivatives; formaldehyde from methanol oxidation. So a black phenolic connector body is, like everything else on this board, two or three steps downstream of a steam cracker."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.phenolic.resin-source.phenol" [label="Phenol from cumene" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Phenol was made almost entirely by the cumene process: cumene oxidised to cumene hydroperoxide, then acid-split to phenol and acetone. Both products of that split fed the phenolic and the epoxy chains."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.moulding.phenolic.compression-press" [label="Phenolic compression press" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A heated platen press with matched metal dies, pressing the thermoset compound to shape and curing it in the same operation. Compression rather than injection is what makes phenolic bodies cheap: the flow is short and the die is simple."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking" [label="Shared contact manufacturing: stamping, forming, plating" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Almost every connector here is made the same way at the front end: a brass or phosphor-bronze strip is blanked, pierced, bent and formed in a multi-slide tool, then plated in a nickel bath and given a thin gold flash on the contact areas."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.gold" [label="Refined gold for contact plating" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Gold is deposited from a cyanide or sulphite bath, by immersion displacement onto the nickel or by electroplating with a gold strike. It is the one precious metal on an entire C64's connector set, applied as a sub-micron skin over several square centimetres - a deliberate economics of just enough go"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.gold.dore" [label="Doré bullion as the metal feed" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Most industrial gold plating in 1982 was fed from dore bullion - a semi-pure gold-silver alloy - refined to the fine gold the plating shops bought. The dore itself was mostly refinery production from anode slimes and mined doré bullion, so the plating gold is not a separate supply chain from jewelle"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.gold.immersion" [label="Immersion gold flash" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A displacement process: the nickel surface is briefly attacked by a cyanide solution and gold plates itself out by substitution, in a few tenths of a micron, with no current. It is fast, cheap and exactly the right thickness for a connector flash."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.gold.cyanide-bath" [label="Gold cyanide plating bath" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Gold is plated from an alkaline cyanide bath with a gold anode that dissolves to replenish the bath. The bath is deliberately kept extremely low in free cyanide and gold so that the gold itself is plated preferentially rather than the underlying base metal."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.gold.cyanide-bath.cyanide" [label="Sodium cyanide" fillcolor="#2ac3de22" color="#2ac3de" tooltip="The plating chemistry itself: gold is plated from an alkaline cyanide bath held deliberately low in free cyanide, so that the gold deposits preferentially rather than the base metal underneath. The cyanide is made from brine by chlor-alkali electrolysis and then from ammonia and carbon, and it is wh"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.plating" [label="Nickel underplate then gold flash" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Two plating baths in sequence. First a nickel underplate (electroless nickel-phosphorus on some parts, barrel or rack electrolytic nickel on others) is laid down; this is the barrier layer that stops the base copper or brass diffusing into the gold, and nickel is also what the solder actually wets. "];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.plating.electroless-nickel" [label="Electroless nickel-phosphorus deposition" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="A hypophosphite bath plates nickel onto the parts without an external current, by catalytic reduction on the brass surface. Because the deposit is amorphous nickel containing a few per cent of phosphorus, it is harder and more corrosion-resistant than pure electrolytic nickel and it does not need to"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.plating.electroless-nickel.phosphorus" [label="Phosphorus from phosphate rock" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="The hypophosphite reducing agent is made from white phosphorus, which was still produced in the 1980s by the electric-furnace reduction of phosphate rock - a route that consumed very large tonnages of low-grade apatite for a few tens of kilograms of elemental phosphorus."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.brass-stock" [label="Brass strip (Cu-Zn)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Cartridge brass, usually 70/30 copper-zinc, drawn into strip. Brass is used because it is easy to form, springs usefully, and takes nickel plating well; a strip's mechanical properties are set by the zinc content and by work hardening from the drawing die."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.brass-stock.brass-copper.electrorefining" [label="Copper electrorefining" fillcolor="#9ece6a22" color="#9ece6a" tooltip="Blister copper from the smelter is refined electrolytically to 99.99 % cathode, which is then cast to wire rod and rolled into foil. Electrorefining is what makes the uniform, sulphur-free copper that both brass contact strip and solderable connector plating require."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.brass-stock.brass-zinc" [label="Zinc of the brass" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Zinc is recovered from sphalerite ore, roasted to the oxide and either smelted to crude zinc or leached and electrowon; it is the element that makes copper springy and hard-working."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.brass-stock.brass-zinc.roasting" [label="Roasting and calcination" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The concentrate is fluid-bed roasted, oxidising ZnS to ZnO with SO2 recovered as sulphuric acid. The oxide is then either smelted with coke to crude zinc or leached and electrowon."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.four-slide-die" [label="Four-slide die forming (blank, pierce, form, cut)" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Contacts are made on a multi-slide machine: a brass or copper-alloy strip is fed as a continuous coil, and each station in the slide performs one operation - piercing the terminal slot, forming the spring barrel, bending the solder tail, then cutting off the finished contact. Hundreds of thousands o"];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.four-slide-die.coil-stock" [label="Coiled contact strip" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The blank comes off the coil as a continuous ribbon of brass strip, the cheapest way to feed a high-speed press. The whole 44-position cartridge socket's 88 contacts are stamped from one coil."];
  "c64.connector-jacks.metalworking.four-slide-die.tool-steel" [label="Tool steel of the stamping die" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The progressive die is made from hardened tool steel - P20 for the body, D2 or H13 for the cutting edges - with the contact geometry machined and ground. A stamping die outlasts tens of millions of strokes, which is why the contact is a commodity."];
  "c64.keyboard" [label="Keyboard module: 66 keycaps on hybrid switches" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The C64's keyboard is a separate wired module that drops into the bezel: a steel backplate, a single-sided printed circuit board carrying the matrix, sixty-six individually moulded ABS keycaps, and beneath each cap a rubber-dome switch - a moulded plunger riding on a return spring and pressing a car"];
  "c64.keyboard.cable" [label="Keyboard ribbon cable and connector" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="A short flat ribbon or discrete-lead cable from the keyboard to the 24-pin header (CN1) on the front-left edge of the mainboard, terminating in a moulded connector housing. It carries the eight matrix rows, the eight columns, the RESTORE line and the power rails - twenty-four conductors."];
  "c64.keyboard.cable.conductor" [label="Ribbon conductors" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="Either a flat laminated ribbon of stranded copper conductors or a bundle of discrete stranded leads; the ribbon is thinner, discrete leads are more flexible at the fold. Whatever the construction, the keyboard cable is the link to the mainboard and has nothing to do with the switch matrix: the matri"];
  "c64.keyboard.body" [label="Keyboard body and its fasteners" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A moulded ABS body that holds the plungers, the PCB and the backplate, and carries the cable. It is retained by about twenty small gold-coloured screws, and on the shift-lock position it has a black-backed keyswitch with one wire running diagonally to the PCB."];
  "c64.keyboard.body.screws" [label="Keyboard retaining screws (gold-coloured)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Roughly twenty small self-tapping screws with a gold or brass finish hold the keyboard backplate and body together. The gold colour is a zinc-nickel or brass-look plating, chosen because it reads as 'quality' on a part the customer never sees."];
  "c64.keyboard.body.keyswitch" [label="Shift-lock keyswitch" fillcolor="#7dcfff22" color="#7dcfff" tooltip="The shift-lock position carries a tiny mechanically latching slide switch: a black plastic body with a metal leaf contact that holds itself in the shifted position until pressed again. It is not a matrix key at all - it is a separate latch that simply closes the shift lines, which is why the firmwar"];
  "c64.keyboard.backplate" [label="Steel backplate" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A sheet-steel plate under the printed circuit board with openings punched for each plunger and rolled stiffening beads pressed into it. It carries the return springs, holds the plungers' travel against the board, and is screwed to the keyboard body with about twenty small gold-coloured screws. The o"];
  "c64.keyboard.pcb" [label="Keyboard PCB (matrix and contacts)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="A single-sided printed circuit board carrying the eight row and eight column traces of the matrix, drilled and plated through in the usual way, with one pair of plated pads terminating each key position. It plugs into a 24-pin header on the mainboard at CN1 and carries no logic of its own: it is a s"];
  "c64.keyboard.pcb.contacts" [label="Contact pads (gold or carbon)" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="Each key position terminates in two pads on the rigid board. The carbon-loaded rubber dome that bridges them is the moving conductor: pressed, it closes the circuit between the pads; released, the pads are open. On gold-plated keyboards the pads are gold over nickel; on cheaper sets they are a carbo"];
  "c64.keyboard.pcb.matrix" [label="How the 8 x 8 matrix and the two odd keys work" fillcolor="#e0af6822" color="#e0af68" tooltip="The CIA drives one of eight rows low and reads the eight columns; a pressed key connects one row to one column, and sixty-four positions are scanned. RESTORE is wired straight to the NMI line, bypassing the matrix entirely, which is why RESTORE works even while the CPU is jammed. SHIFT LOCK is a mec"];
  "c64.keyboard.switches" [label="Hybrid switches (Mitsumi-style plunger, rubber dome, spring)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The C64 does not use a rubber-dome-only membrane like a calculator, nor a mechanical switch like a typewriter. It uses a dome-and-plunger hybrid: a moulded plastic plunger carrying or driving a carbon-loaded rubber pad which bridges two plated pads on the rigid keyboard board when the cap is pressed"];
  "c64.keyboard.switches.force-curve" [label="The snap: force-displacement curve of the switch" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The feel of a C64 key is a curve, not a number. The plunger rides down the spring, then the rubber dome on the board has to collapse, producing a peak in force; past the peak the force drops as the dome flattens, then rises again into the bottom-out. The peak-to-valley difference is what the finger "];
  "c64.keyboard.switches.spring" [label="Return spring (music wire)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A small coil spring under each key that returns the plunger. It is drawn from music wire (a high-carbon, low-carbon-content steel with a tight tensile range) and wound on a automatic spring coiler. Type 1 keyboards spring every key; Type 2 springs only the space bar and relies on the rubber dome els"];
  "c64.keyboard.switches.plunger" [label="Switch plunger (moulded, with conductive rubber)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A small moulded body with a cross-post on top that the keycap clips onto, one or more arms that carry a conductive rubber pad, and a stem that slides in the keyboard body. Type 2 plungers have four prongs and carry a loose grey 'bubble' contact."];
  "c64.keyboard.switches.plunger-polymer" [label="Plunger polymer: acetal or filled nylon" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The plunger has to be dimensionally stable over millions of strokes and low-friction against the spring and the body. Acetal (POM) is the classic choice for its low friction and stiffness; glass-filled nylon or polycarbonate are also used. All three are moulded on small multi-cavity tools, and the f"];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps" [label="Keycaps (66 individual moulded ABS caps)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Not a single dished sheet: 66 separate rectangular caps, each moulded in dark brown-grey ABS with a slightly concave top, each dropping through its own square opening in the bezel onto its own plunger. Some breadbin sets had tan-coloured function keys, others grey."];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps.cross-point" [label="Cross-point fitting and cap underside" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The underside of the cap carries a moulded cross that clips onto the matching cross-post on the plunger - about 5 mm on early breadbin caps, about 3 mm on later ones. The two are not interchangeable. The underside also has guide ribs that keep the cap square as it travels."];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps.colours" [label="Keycap colour: no coding on the breadbin keyboard" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Breadbin C64 caps are almost uniformly a dark brown-grey, with the function keys sometimes a lighter grey or tan. There is no per-number colour coding on the breadbin keyboard - that belongs to some other machines, and the VIC-20 had differently coloured function keys. What a C64 keycap has is a mou"];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps.legend" [label="Printed front (PETSCII) legends" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The graphics characters on the small FRONT wall of the cap - the PETSCII and shifted-symbol set - are printed, not moulded. This is the other half of the keycap story from the double-shot top legends (c64.keyboard-switches.keycap.double-shot), and the difference is visible: these characters wear and"];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps.tool" [label="Keycap tool (small multi-cavity, hardened steel)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Keycaps are made in small high-cavity tools - 4, 8 or 16 cavities in one block - because a cap is cheap and the tool has to be small enough to fit a small press. The tool is hardened tool steel and the caps come off a common gate, so the visible faces are identical across the set. A cap is the short"];
  "c64.keyboard.keycaps.abs" [label="ABS grade for keycaps" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A housing-grade ABS, low rubber content so the moulded texture stays crisp and the cap does not feel gummy. Keycap ABS is usually a slightly higher-gloss, higher-flow grade than the case resin because the caps are thin and have to fill a short, tight flow path."];
  "c64.case" [label="Breadbin case: two-piece ABS shell" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The C64 'breadbin' housing is a rear shell and a front bezel, both injection-moulded general-purpose ABS in a warm beige-brown. The rear shell carries the ventilation ribs, moulded screw bosses, connector cut-outs and the rubber feet; the bezel carries the keyboard window with its square key opening"];
  "c64.case.other-polymers" [label="Other polymers in the C64" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The ABS case is not the only plastic in the machine. This branch gathers the second- and third-generation polymers that appear in the C64's plastic parts so that their feedstocks are traceable alongside the case resin. Several of them - PVC in the cable jackets, POM in the keyboard plungers, nylon i"];
  "c64.case.other-polymers.methanol" [label="Methanol (from synthesis gas)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Made by reacting carbon monoxide and hydrogen from natural-gas reforming, a few hundred C and 50-100 bar over a copper/zinc oxide catalyst. Formaldehyde comes from methanol oxidation over silver or iron-molybdenum catalyst."];
  "c64.case.other-polymers.polyester-film" [label="Polyester / PET film (insulation, overlay)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Polyester film is used as thin insulation, as overlay film on some keycap legends, and as the amber capacitor sleeve material in the power supply. It comes from terephthalic acid and ethylene glycol, both of which come out of the aromatics and olefin chains already used elsewhere in the machine."];
  "c64.case.moulding" [label="Injection moulding of the case" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The bezel and rear shell are moulded on a large toggle or hydraulic press in a multi-cavity family tool. Dried ABS is melted in a three- or four-zone barrel at 200-260 C, injected through a gate on the parting line at 1000-1500 bar, packed at 50-100 bar until the gate freezes, cooled against water c"];
  "c64.case.moulding.press" [label="Injection press (300-600 t)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A toggle or direct-hydraulic injection moulding machine with a screw plasticiser, a heated barrel with three or four zones, an oil or water temperature controller, and a mould base. Toggle machines clamp faster and are what most housing tools ran on. Oil hydraulics drive the toggle or the platen, pr"];
  "c64.case.moulding.hydraulics" [label="Hydraulic clamp unit" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Oil hydraulics drive the toggle or the platen, provide injection and holding pressure, and drive the ejector. A hydraulic press is the reason injection pressure reached 1000+ bar - compared with the ~500 bar of the older toggle-and-screw machines."];
  "c64.case.moulding.injection-screw" [label="Plasticising screw and barrel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A flighted screw turns in a heated barrel, conveying and melting pellets on the way back and injecting on the way forward. The screw is nitrided 33CrMoV or 40Cr steel, bimetallic-lined at the tip in later designs."];
  "c64.case.tooling" [label="Case tooling: H13 family mould with EDM texture" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The two breadbin case moulds are large two-plate tools in pre-hardened or hardened hot-work steel, with the leather-grain texture cut by electrical discharge machining and the cavity polished and hard-chromed. A single mould produces thousands of C64 cases; its cost is amortised over millions of par"];
  "c64.case.tooling.p20" [label="P20 pre-hardened tool steel" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 0.38% C, 0.9% Cr, 0.4% Mo, 0.2% V steel supplied at HRC 30-35. Because it arrives hard, the whole cavity can be machined and polished with no risk of distortion from a later heat treatment, which is why it dominated housing tooling in 1982."];
  "c64.case.tooling.mould-assembly" [label="Mould assembly: guide pins, ejectors, cooling" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The finished plates are set into a mould base with guide pillars or bushes, an ejector-plate assembly with pins under every feature that would imprint on the visible face, and water lines carrying coolant through the cores and plates to remove the heat of plasticisation."];
  "c64.case.tooling.plating" [label="Hard chrome plating of the cavity" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The polished cavity is electroplated in a hexavalent chromium bath to a few microns. Chrome is much harder than the tool steel, so the tool wears far better; it is also what gives the moulded ABS its character - the gloss of a C64 case is the gloss of chrome, not of steel."];
  "c64.case.tooling.chromium-acid" [label="Chromic acid" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Made by roasting chromite with soda ash and limestone, leaching the sodium chromate, and acidifying. So the chromium in the plating bath comes from the same chromite that came into the tool steel."];
  "c64.case.tooling.polishing" [label="Lapping and polishing the cavity" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The cavity is progressively polished - progressively finer abrasives on cloth and stone - to a mirror. On a textured tool the polish is done to the whole surface and the texture simply stays; on flat surfaces it goes to Ra 0.05-0.1 um, which is what makes the plated ABS read as glossy."];
  "c64.case.tooling.lapping-compound" [label="Abrasive polishing compounds" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Aluminium oxide for the bulk stock removal and green chromium oxide for the final mirror, both graded by grain size and applied on cloth and stone. This is the most labour-intensive step in the whole case tool: a large textured housing cavity takes days of hand polishing on cast-iron blocks, which i"];
  "c64.case.tooling.synthetic-alumina" [label="Synthetic alumina abrasive" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Bauxite is refined to alumina and sintered or fused into corundum, then crushed and graded into abrasive powders. Hardness ~9 on the Mohs scale, second only to diamond, and the workhorse of metal finishing."];
  "c64.case.tooling.machining" [label="Machining of the cavity and core" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="A 1982 tool shop would rough the cavity with large carbide or HSS milling cutters, leaving 0.3-0.5 mm, then finish-mill, then grind the critical faces flat and parallel to 0.01-0.02 mm across the plate. The parting plane is lapped to the same figure and put exactly at the joint line of the two halve"];
  "c64.case.tooling.edm" [label="EDM cutting of the leather grain" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The texture is the one feature of the case that cannot be milled: a random stochastic pattern of overlapping spherical dimples, 0.3-0.8 mm pitch and 0.05-0.15 mm deep. It is cut by sinking a shaped graphite or brass electrode into the hardened, ground cavity, or by wire-EDM scanning. Sinking EDM giv"];
  "c64.case.tooling.brass-electrode" [label="Brass electrode for fine detail" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Copper-zinc alloy electrodes are used where the texture has fine features, because brass machines more accurately than graphite and can be reworked. Brass comes from copper and zinc ores smelted together."];
  "c64.case.tooling.graphite-electrode" [label="Graphite electrode for EDM texturing" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Isostatic-pressed fine-grain graphite, turned or milled to the negative of the texture panel, or a CNC-shaped graphite block. Graphite is used because it is easy to machine, wears predictably as the electrode erodes, and does not damage the workpiece the way a metal electrode would."];
  "c64.case.tooling.graphite-ore" [label="Natural graphite flake" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Flake graphite is mined in Sri Lanka, Madagascar, China and Brazil, purified by acid (or in the 1980s by chlorination) and pressed with pitch binder into tooling graphite."];
  "c64.case.fasteners" [label="Case fasteners" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The screws that hold the C64 together: a handful of case-assembly screws plus the motherboard and keyboard mounting screws. They are cheap zinc-plated self-tapping screws driven into plain plastic bosses. The wire arrives as EBSD or BQ grade, is drawn, cut to length and cold-headed on a multiple-spi"];
  "c64.case.fasteners.brass-insert" [label="Moulded-in brass insert (later practice)" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Modern repro cases and late Commodore tools mould a brass or stainless insert into the boss and use a real machine screw. The insert is placed by hand or by gravity feed and captured by the ABS on the next shot, giving a reusable metal thread."];
  "c64.case.shield" [label="RFI shield: aluminium foil on a board spacer" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="The C64 needed a conductive screen over the motherboard because a 1 MHz machine with a video oscillator radiates through a plastic case. Commodore used a die-cut sandwich of aluminium foil laminated to a cardboard or plastic-fibre spacer, bent into a box and stapled or taped to plastic standoffs, ra"];
  "c64.case.shield.spacer" [label="Shield spacer (board core)" fillcolor="#a9b1d622" color="#a9b1d6" tooltip="A rigidising core so the foil does not crumple and short against the board. Cheap versions use thin greyboard; better ones use a plastic-fibre or corrugated spacer. This is the part that makes the shield stand off the motherboard so it cannot touch solder joints."];
  "c64.case.shield.foil" [label="Aluminium foil" fillcolor="#f7768e22" color="#f7768e" tooltip="Household or semi-rugged aluminium foil, 0.03-0.06 mm, slit and die-cut. Its job is not to be a Faraday cage in the strict sense but simply to be a continuous, grounded-ish conductor over the board so the radiated field collapses."];
  "c64.case.feet" [label="Case feet" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Four small anti-vibration feet on the underside of the rear shell, moulded in a soft dark rubber or a flexible PVC, that stop the machine skating on a desk and stop the moulded ABS case from ringing against the desk."];
  "c64.case.feet.elastomer" [label="Foot elastomer compound" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A soft, low-durometer elastomer drop-in-moulded over a small ABS boss, or a moulded-in flexible nub. The chemistry is trivial but the tooling is not: it is a two-shot or insert-mould operation with an elastomer barrel and a much longer cure than the ABS."];
  "c64.case.bezel" [label="Front bezel (top half)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The dished front panel with the keyboard window. It is the visible part of the machine, so it carries the fine leather-grain texture, the rainbow badge recess, the two knurled thumbscrews and the moulded-in 'COMMODORE' lettering on the lower lip."];
  "c64.case.logo" [label="Rainbow Commodore badge" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The rainbow Commodore badge on the bezel: a shallow raised island, moulded as part of the bezel tool so that it is smooth while the surrounding case carries the leather grain, and printed with coloured and metallised pad-print ink. The ink is the badge; the ABS of the bezel is only the substrate it "];
  "c64.case.bezel.thumbscrews" [label="Knurled thumbscrews for contrast/brightness" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Two moulded plastic thumbscrews on the bezel that carry small metal shafts to the contrast and brightness potentiometers on the board. The caps are ABS, the shafts are plated steel, and the knurl is moulded rather than cut."];
  "c64.case.bezel.key-well" [label="Keyboard window and key openings" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A single rectangular opening spanned by thin webs between the key holes; the caps push through and their plungers sit on the keyboard PCB below. The webs are the highest-risk area of the tool for short shots and flash, because the melt has to snake between 66 holes that are only about 10 mm across."];
  "c64.case.rear-shell" [label="Rear shell (bottom half)" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="The larger of the two mouldings. It forms the base pan, the raised side walls, the wrap-over top with its row of ventilation slots, the rear and side connector cut-outs, the internal ribs that stiffen the pan, the moulded screw bosses and standoffs, and the feet pads."];
  "c64.case.rear-shell.connector-cutouts" [label="Rear and side connector openings" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Rectangular and keyed cut-outs for the cartridge port, user port, cassette port, serial port, video DIN, power DIN and the two DE-9 control ports. Cut with knife-edged steel on the tool; the moulded lip around each port also acts as a draft and a finger stop, and the mouldings carry the connector bo"];
  "c64.case.rear-shell.bosses" [label="Moulded bosses and standoffs" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="Integral cylindrical bosses on the pan floor that carry the four case screws, the motherboard mounting screws, the keyboard PCB screws and the RF shield tabs. Each is a thick section relative to the 3 mm pan, so the tool must be cored to keep them from sinking, and each has a blind hole with a draft"];
  "c64.case.rear-shell.vents" [label="Ventilation slots in the top wrap" fillcolor="#bb9af722" color="#bb9af7" tooltip="A row of moulded louvre slots in the curved top panel behind the cartridge port, letting convective air out of the case over the SID and VIC-II. They are negative features cut into the tool, not separate ribs, and because they run across a thick section they leave visible flow lines and are a classi"];
